        "title": "11.2 ELECTRIC FIELD AND ITS INTENSITY",
        "theory": "The SI unit of electric field intensity is newton per coulomb (NC) or volt per\nmetre (Vm&quot;1).\nCoulomb&#x27;s law enables the electric field to be calculated for any point in space around a fixed static charge q. The magnitude of electric field is given\nFIGURE 11.3\nby:\nF=kaq2\nAs is the unit vector directed from charge q to 4. so in vector form\nilab\nSo the field intensity is given by\nElectric field intensity due to a point charge.\n99%\nF\n9.\n9.\nE-k\n...(11.10)\nThe strength of the field is proportional to the magnitude of the charge. Its strength decreases as the test charge moves away (unit charge) from source charge q.\nEXAMPLE 11.2\nGIVEN\nFind electric field at a distance of 30cm from a 3μ C point charge.\nradius r = 30 cm = 30 x 102 m= 0.3 m\ncharge q = 3μ C = 3 x 10° C\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n10\nREQUIRED\nelectric field =E=?\nFIGURE 11.4\nSOLUTION\nUsing electric field intensity\nformulae\n1 q\nE=\n4TE\n= (9x10° Nm2 C2)(3x10 C)\n= 3x103 NC-1\n(0.3m)2\n11.2.1 REPRESENTATION OF\nELECTRIC FIELD BY LINES\nIn\nthe previous section,\nTwo equal and opposite charges\nthe vector nature of the electric\nfield strength was discussed. FIGURE 11.5 An electric field can be used in the pictorial form to describe the overall intensity of the field around it. This pictorial representation is called the electric field lines.\nAn electric field line is an imaginary line or curve drawn through a region of empty space. The direction of the electric field at point A, B, C and D is tangent to the field line through that point.\nThe electric field lines, point in\nE\nC\nD\nB\nein\nTwo equal and positive charges placed\nat certain distance\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\n11\nthe direction that a positive test charge would accelerate if placed upon the\nNOT FOR SALE line. The arrows indicate the electric field at different points. The direction of these arrows is outward for positive charge and is inward for negative charge as shown in Fig: 11.4\nThe field lines starts from positive charge and terminate at negative charge as shown in Fig. (11.5)\nFig: 11.6 show two infinitely large parallel plates separated by a very small distance. The upper plate has a uniform positive charge distribution while the lower plate has a negative one. If the plates are not of infinite length, the field lines at the ends of plates will be a little bulging out, showing that field is not uniform at the ends. The field such as at the ends of plates is called &quot;fringing field&quot;.\nAnother interesting property of field lines can be explained with the help of Fig. 11.7 Suppose that a charge +q is placed near a metal plate. The positive charge will attract the negative charges\nFIGURE 11.6\n+++\nUniform electric field between charged plates. FIGURE 11.7\nElectric lines of force on the surface of metal plate\n(electrons) in the metal plate, resulting in the motion of these charges until some of them reach that surface of the metal which is near to +q charge where they will be at rest. The electric lines of force cannot pass through a conductor. Therefore electric field is zero inside a conductor.\nAPPLICATIONS OF ELECTROSTATICS\nPHOTOCOPIERS: The copying process of photocopier is called xerography. It consists of a drum which is made up of aluminum and is coated with selenium.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n12\nSelenium is an insulator in the dark and become conductor when expose to light thus it is a photoconductor. The drum, charged with static electricity, can attract the toner particles.\nWhen the paper to be\ncopied is laid on the FIGURE. 11.8\nglass plate, then the dark and light areas of the document produce same image on the drum as shown in figure 11.8. The dark areas retain their positive charge but light areas become conducting, lose their, positive charge and become neutral.\nThus the positive charge image of the document remains on the selenium surface.\nPaper A\nLens",
