        "theory": "11.10.2 CAPACITANCE OF A PARALLEL PLATE CAPACITOR\nLet us consider a parallel plate capacitor connected to a voltage source V. The charges on the capacitor plates increases till the potential difference V across the plates build. Let the charges on the plates are +Q and -Q when the potential difference is V. If the positive plate is at potential V, and negative plate is at potential V2, then the electric field strength between the plates is\nE=\n-AV_-(V2-V1\nAr\n(A)\nd\nd\nd\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\n-\nWhere, V = V, V2 is the potential difference and d is the separation between the plates. The strength of the electric field also depends on the number of charges on the plates. The charge density is the total charge per unit area of the plate. i.e.,\nNOT FOR SALE\nFIGURE:11.23\nB\nA\n36\nBy using Gauss&#x27;s law the electric field\nintensity E between the plates is\nPlates of a capacitor separated by a distance d\nσ\nQ\nE=\nE. EA\nFrom Eq (A) &amp; (B) we have\nE\nV\nQ\n무\nOr\nd&amp;A\nQ = SAV\nd\nBy using Cyc\n(B)\nwaz e in\n...(11.30)\nEq(11.30) becomes\nV\nQ\nA\nC\n4-3-54\nV d\n...(11.31)\nWhen a dielectric is inserted between the plates of a capacitor, then it is seen that the charge storing capacity of a capacitor is enhanced by the dielectric which permits it to store &amp; times more charge for the same potential\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n37\ndifference. In case of a parallel plate capacitor completely filled with a dielectric the capacitance is\nCaned = &amp;,&amp;,A\n... (11.32)\nd\nEq11.32: shows the dependence of capacitance upon the area of the plates, the separation between the plates and the medium between them. From Eq(11.31 ) and Eq(11.32) we have\nCmed Cyac\n...(11.33)\nThis relation provides us with the definition of relative permittivity or dielectric constant or specific inductive capacity:\nThe ratio of the capacitance of a capacitor with a given material filling the space between the plates to the capacitance of the same capacitor when the space is evacuated is the relative permittivity E, of the material.\n11.10.3 COMBINATIONS OF CAPACITORS\nWe know that the capacitors FOR YOUR INFORMATION can be connected either in series or in parallel. We want to find out an equivalent capacitor that has the same capacitance as that of the combination of capacitors\nSERIES COMBINATION OF CAPACITORS\nWhen the capacitors are connected plate to plate i.e. the right plate of one capacitor is connected to the left plate of the next capacitor so on as shown in Fig:11.24 then it called series\nAn axon membrane acts as a capacitor. Axon of a resting human nerve cell has a potential difference of 65mV.Current enters the axon (or dendrite) through ion membrane, depolarizing that region. The channels (e.g. Na+ channels) in a region of\nintracellular positive charge is attracted to adjacent negatively charged regions of membrane.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n38\ncombination. A battery of voltage V is connected between points A and B. In this type of combination the equivalent capacitance is given by\n1 1\n... (11.34)\nPARALLEL COMBINATION OF CAPACITORS\nWhen the battery is connected to the FIGURE 11.24 parallel plates of a capacitor as shown in Figure 11.25. The equivalent capacitance C, of this combination is\ngiven by\nC2 =c+c2+c\nC, C1\nB\n非",
