        "theory": "C\n+q\nThe charging and discharging of a capacitor has many applications. Capacitor discharge ignition (CDI) is a type of automotive electronic ignition system which is widely used in motorcycles, lawn mowers, chain saws, small engines, turbine powered aircraft, Discharging a capacitor. and some cars. It can enhance the capability\nof power supply and make the spark much\nstronger.\nFIGURE 11.31\nFIGURE 11.30\nCharge\n0.3689\naz ein\nElectronic Ignition CDI\nTip\nTime Discharging of a capacitor\nElectronic flashguns for cameras have to be left for a short period of time between flashes. There is a capacitor inside that stores energy, it needs to be charged up again by the battery in the flashgun.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nK\nNOT FOR SALE\n45\nCoulomb&#x27;s Law: the magnitude of the force between two point charges is directly proportional to the product of the magnitudes of the charges and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.\nElectric potential at any point: it is equal to the work done in bringing a unit positive charge from infinity to that point without producing any acceleration.\nElectric field: the region around a charge in which an electric test charge would experience an electric force.\nThe electric flux : the number of lines of force that pass through the area placed in the electric field. = E.A = EA cose\n©\nWhen area A is normal to the electric field E then electric flux is maximum. When area A is parallel to the electric field E then electric flux is minimum.\nGauss&#x27;s law: The electric flux through any closed surface is 1/e, times the total charge enclosed in it.\nCapacitor: A device which is used for storing electric charges.\nElectron volt (eV): the unit of energy and is related to joule as\n1eV 1.602 x 10-19 J\nDipole: the system in which two charges of equal magnitude but of opposite sign separated by the distance d.\nPOINT\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE 46\nU\nT\nEXERCISE\nChoose the best possible answer\nA charge Q is divided into two parts q and Q-q and separated by a distance R. The force of repulsion between them will be maximum\nwhen:\nA. q-Q/4\nE\nC. q = Q/2\nUIO-UW BEST-ONS\nB. q = Q/3\nD. q=Q\n2 Three objects are brought close to each other, two at a time. When objects A and B are brought together, they attract. When objects B and C are brought together, they repel. From this, we conclude that\nA.objects A and C possess charges of the same sign.\nB. objects A and C possess charges of opposite sign.\nC. all three of the objects possess charges of the same sign.\nD.one of the objects is neutral\n3 Object A has a charge of +2 pc, and object B has a charge of +6 pc.\nWhich statement is true about the electric forces on the objects?\nA. FAB=-3FBA\nC. 3 FAB == FRA\nB. FAB = -FRA\nD. FAB-3 FBA\naz e/ind\nSome charge is being given to a conductor. Then its potential\nA. Is maximum at surface\nB. Is maximum at centre\nC. Is remain same throughout the conductor\nD. Is maximum somewhere between surface and centre\nThe unit of Electric field intensity is :\nA. Volt/metre\nC. Joule/ Coulomb.metre\nB. newton/Coulomb D. Volt.metre\nA proton is about 1840 time heavier than an electron. When it is accelerated by a potential difference if 1 kV, its kinetic energy will be: A. 1840 keV\nC. 1 keV\nB. 1/1840 keV\nD. 920 keV\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FORSALE\n7 The electric field at a distance of 20cm from 2μC charge is\nA. 3x103N\nB. 3x101N\nC. 9x101N\nD. 9x103N\n47",
