        "theory": "PHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\n3\nNOT FOR SALE\n50\nCharges 2 μC, -3μC, and 4μC are placed in air at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of sides 10 cm. What is the magnitude of resultant force acting on 4uC charge?\n(Answer 15.7N)\nTwo equal and opposite charges of magnitude 2x10 C are placed 15cm apart. What is the magnitude and direction of electric intensity (E) at a point mid-way between the charges? What force would act on a proton (charge = +1.6x10&quot; C) placed there?\n(Answer 0.64x10° N/C along AB, 1.024x10-13 N along AB)\nTwo positive point charges of 15 x1010 C and 13x101°C are placed 12cm apart. Find the work done in bringing the two charges 4 cm closer.\n(Answer 7.31x10J)\nA hollow sphere is charged to 14μC. Find the potential (a) at its surface (b) inside the sphere (c) at a distance of 0.2m from the surface. The radius of the sphere is 0.3m. (Answer 42x10 V, 42x10* V, 25.2×10*V)\nIf 280 J of work is done in carrying a charge of 2C from a place where the potential is -12V to another place where potential is V, calculate the value of V.\n(Answer 128V)\nThe electric field at a point due to a point charge is 26 N/C and the electric potential at that point is 13J/C. Calculate the distance of the point from the charge and magnitude of charge. (Answer 0.722x10°C)\nTwo point charges of BuC and -4uC are separated by a distance of 10cm in air. At what point on the line joining the two charges is the electric potential zero?\n(Answer 6.6 cm from 8μC and, 3.3 cm from -4u F charge)\nPHYSICS-XI\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n51\nAn electron with an initial speed of 29x103ms is fired in the same direction as a uniform electric field with a magnitude of 80 NC. How far does the electron travel before being brought to rest momentarily and turned back?\n(Answer 0.299m)\n10 Three capacitors of capacitance 4uF, 6μF and 8uF respectively are connected in series to a 250V d.c. supply. Find (i) the total capacitance (ii) charge on each capacitor and (iii) P.D. across each capacitor.\n(Answer 1.84μF, 460x10 C, 115 V, 76.6V and 57.7 V)\ni.com\nFind the charge on 5uF capacitor in the circuit shown in Fig..\n3μF\n2UF\n5μF\n(Answer 9 μF)\n12 A 6μF capacitor is charged to a P.D. of 120V and then connected to an un-charged 4 pF capacitor. Calculate the P.D. across the capacitors.\n(Answer 72 V)\nPHYSICS-XII\nUnit\n12\nк\nNOT FOR SALE\n52\nCURRENT ELECTRICITY\nHow does an electric eel catch its prey?\nL After studying this unit the students will be able to\nLEAR\nM\nE\nS\ndescribe the concept of steady current.\nstate Ohm&#x27;s law.\ndefine resistivity and explain its dependence upon temperature. define conductance and conductivity of conductor.\naz\nstate the characteristics of a thermistor and its use to measure low temperatures.\ndistinguish between e.m.f and p.d. using the energy considerations.\nexplain the internal resistance of sources and its consequences for external\ncircuits.\ndescribe some sources of e.m.f.\ndescribe the conditions for maximum power transfer.\ndescribe thermocouple and its function.\nexplain variation of thermoelectric e.m.f. with temperature.\n⚫ apply Kirchhoff&#x27;s first law as conservation of charge to solve problem. ⚫ apply Kirchhoff&#x27;s second law as conservation of energy to solve problem.\nPHYSICS-XII\nila",
