        "theory": "UNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n5\nforce F12 exerted by q2 on q, is equal in magnitude to the force F, exerted by q1 on q2 and in the opposite direction as illustrated in Fig 11.2 (o) This proves that Coulomb&#x27;s law fits into Newton&#x27;s third law.\nThe constant k can be generally expressed in terms of permittivity of the free\n1 space ε, i.e., k=\nΑπε\nExperimentally measured value of\n€ 8.85×10-12 C2 N1m2\n1\nso k =\n9.0 x 10&quot; N m2 C2\n4πε\nCOULOMB&#x27;S LAW IN MATERIAL MEDIA\nEq:(11.2) gives the force between two charges when there is air or vacuum between them. But it is experimentally observed that when an insulator is placed between the electric charges, it reduces the force. Permittivity is the property of a medium which affects the magnitude of force between two point charges.\nThe coulomb&#x27;s force can now be written as\nFred\nраш\n1 9,92 4лε r2\n...(11.5)\nThe quantity &amp; is called the permittivity of the medium. For a particular insulator the permittivity of a material medium compared with the permittivity of vacuum is called relative permittivity or dielectric constant &amp; Its ratio is given by\nPHYSICS-XII\n... (11.6)\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nObviously the &amp;, is dimensionless constant and its value is always greater than unity for various dielectrics. The values of relative permittivity for various dielectrics are given in table 11.1.\nThe force in a medium of relative permittivity E,, is given by\nוד\nF\nmed\n9,921\n1 992 4πε ε, r2\nF\nFred\nE,\nNOT FOR SALE\nTable 11.1 :The permittivity\ndielectrics\n16\nrelative\nof various\nMaterial\nE\nVacuum\n1\nAir\n1.0006\nBenzene\n2.284\nGerminium\n16\nWater\n78.5\n... (11.7)\nGlass\n4.8-10\nMica\n3-7.5\nParaffine paper\n2\n(11.8)\nRubber\n2.94\nAmmonia(liquid) 22-25\nEXAMPLE 11.1\nThree charges q1 = -5 μC, q2+10 μC and q = -12 μC are placed in a line as shown in figure. Calculate the net electrostatic force on charge q2 due to the\nother two charges.\nGIVEN\nCharge (q) = -5 μC = -5 x 10° C Charge (q2) = +10 μC = +10 x 10 C\nCharge (q) = -12 μμC = -12 x 10 C\nk = 9 x 10&quot; Nm2C-2\nawaz\nThe distance between charge q, and q2 (12) = 6 cm = 0.06 m = 6 x 102 m\nThe distance between charge q2 and 43 (23) = 4 cm = 0.04 m = 4 x 102 m\n91\nREQUIRED\n6 cm\n92\n93\n4 cm\nThe magnitude of net electrostatic force on charge q2=Fnet=?",
