        "theory": "The direction of electrostatic force=?\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nSOLUTION\nKOT FOR SALE\n7\nElectrostatic force on charge q2 due to charge q1 Fk99\nx\n= 9.0 × 10° N m2 C2 (5x10 C) (10×10 C)\n(6×10-2 m)2\n=9.0 × 10° N m2 C2 (50x10-12 C2)\nx\n= 125N\n(36x10 m3)\nElectrostatic force on charge qz due to q3=\nib.com\nCa\nF2 = 9.0 x 10 N m2 C (10x10 C)(12×10°C)\n(4×102 m)\n=9.0 x 10 Nm2C2 (120×10-12 c2)\n(16x10 m2)\n(1080x103 )N (16x10)\n=675N\nThe net electrostatic force on charge q-F2 = Fa - Fu\n675 N - 125 N = 550 N.\nThe direction of the net electrostatic force on charge q2 points towards\ncharge q (points to the right).\nASSIGNMENT 11.1\nThree charges +q1 = 5 μC, +qz 10 μC and qs are placed as shown in th figure. What is the electrostatic charge on q3 if the net electrostati\nforce on charges q2 is zero.\n(20x10°C)\n92\nq3\n2 cm\n4 cm\n+\n+\nPHYSICS-XII\nNOT FOR SALE\n8\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\n11.2 ELECTRIC FIELD AND ITS INTENSITY\nThe concept of electric field was first proposed by Michael Faraday, in the 19th century. He stated that the electric field around a charge is like a sphere with in which other charges are influenced by it.\nAn electric field is defined as any region around a charge in which an electric test charge would experience an electric force.\nElectric\nfield\nis\nTIP\n94\nq3\n+\n+\ncharacterized by strength and direction at every point in space. The strength and direction of electric field can be determined by placing a unit positive test charge 92 in that field. The direction in which this unit positive test charge move or tends to move is the direction of electric field. The test charge is so small that it does not distort the original field due to the primary\nsource.\n+\nF13\n+\n12\nF.\n15\nF\n14\n15\nA single vector quantity containing If five charges are placed such that, as\ninformation about the field\nstrength and its direction at that\nshown in figure, then the resultant force exerted by charges qz, q3, q, and q on charges q, is\nF1 = F12 + F13 + F14+ F15\npoint is denoted by Ē and is known as electric field intensity. If unit positive\nd intensity.\ntest charge q, experience a force F due to the electric field of charge q.Then\nthe intensity of an electric field at any point is the force per unit positive test charge placed at that point.\nThe electric field intensity E-F\nOr F=q, E\n9%\n... (11.9)\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n9",
