        "theory": "•; =0, +1⁄2 +❤ ̧ +¶ ̧ +\n+..........\n...(11.12)\nPE=Σsurface EAA\nBut as electric field intensity E is constant\nover the surface of sphere\nTherefore,\nSince,\nDE = EΣsurface AA\nE =\nq 1\n4πε\nܐ\nThus the above equation can be written as\nq 1\n4πE surface AA\nATE\nr2\nEsurface 44= total area enclosed by\nspherical surface=4лr\nFlux through an irregular close surface\nNOT FOR SALE\n20\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nq\nATE\nΦε\nq\nΦε\n금(Azr2)\n...(11.13)\nThe above equation shows that electric flux does not depend upon the shape or geometry of a closed surface. But it depends upon the medium and the charge enclosed by that surface.\nIn order to calculate the electric flux through irregular close surface 5, enclosing point charges 91,92,939, as shown in Fig 11.15 a. Then the total electric flux through that closed surface is FIGURE 11.15\n+92 + 93\nΔΑ\n919293\nΦε\nE. E. E\n1\n-(91+q2+93 +9)\nE\nΦε\n9,0\n9.\n94\n(total charge enclosed by a surface)\nThus Gauss&#x27;s law shows that the electric flux through any closed surface is 1/ε, times the total charge enclosed in it.\n(a) Number of charges in a closed\nsurface\nFIGURE 11.15\nPHYSICS-XII\n(b) Flux through an irregular close surface having number of patches\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n21\n11.5.1 APPLICATIONS OF GAUSS&#x27;S LAW\nGauss&#x27;s law provides a convenient method to calculate E in the case of charge distribution.\nA conductor is a material with neutral atoms rather than a volume distribution of charge. Fig shows an imaginary Gaussian surface S1, lies just below the actual surface of the conductor; consequently, there is no net charge inside the conductor.\nTherefore, there is no excess charge inside a conductor. According to Gauss&#x27;s law De=ZĒ.AA=0 as Q=0.\nHence E= 0 inside a conductor.\nIf a conductor possesses excess charge, then the electric field is still zero. Thus E= 0 inside a conductor. Consequently any excess charge must lie on its surface. Another imaginary Gaussian surface S2 is shown by dotted line outside a conductor. At the surface of a charged conductor, the electric field is normal to the surface at every point. All the charge is deposited on the outer surface of the conductor. Thus the excess charge is not inside a conductor but it resides on the conductor&#x27;s surface.\nElectric field E is shown by arrows, dotted pink line shows Gaussian surface S2 outside and dotted blue line shows Gaussian surface S, inside\nS1\nS2\nNOT FOR SALE\n22\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\n11.5.1.3 ELECTRIC FIELD INTENSITY DUE TO AN INFINITE SHEET OF CHARGE\nConsider an infinite plane sheet of FOR YOUR INFORMATION\ncharge with surface charge density σ. Let S1 be the point at a distance r from the sheet and E be the electric field at this point. Gaussian surface is in the form of a cylinder of cross-sectional area A perpendicular to the sheet of charge as shown in Fig11.16. By symmetry, the electric field is at right angles to the end caps and away from the plane. Its magnitude is the same at points S, and S2. The charge enclosed by the surface will be σ A. Since we know by Gauss&#x27;s law\nTotal electric flux=-x(Q)\nE\nQ = charge enclosed by the surface\nAirlines Airbus A380 flew through a storm when 500 peoples were on the board but none of them were injured. As there is no electric field, no potential difference inside a metal shell, so one of the safest way to be inside a metal shell during thunderstorm.\nFIGURE 11.16\n1_x(Q)\nE0\n+\n++\nSince ō-Q/A or, 5A=Q",
