        "theory": "This gives the electric field between oppositely charged parallel plates. We see that the resulting electric field between the plates is uniform, perpendicular to the plates, and independent of the distance from the plates.\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nProject\nNOT FOR SALE\n25\nCalculate the electric flux through each Gaussian surface shown in Figure\n9 +6 με\nC +4 μC\n-2 μC\n-3 μC\n+4 μC\n+ +3 μC\n-2 μC\nC\n+2 μC\n1 με\n-2 μC\n-3 μC\n0-5 με\n+4 μC\n+6 μC\n+2 μC\n+2 μC\n0-4 με\n11.6 ELECTRIC POTENTIAL\nPreviously we studied the electric field due to two parallel plates with space between them. To explain electric potential consider two oppositely charged parallel plates as shown in Fig (11.18 a). If a positive charge +q is placed in between these plates and allowed to move freely in an electric field it will move towards the negative plate, pulled by the electric force and will gain an equivalent amount of kinetic energy.\nFIGURE 11.18\n++\n+q\nPotential difference between point A &amp; B in electric field.\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nKOPFOR SALE\n26\nThe energy is required to move the charge towards the left plate. Let W be the work done by the force F-q,E in carrying a positive charge q, from point B to point A without producing any acceleration as shown in Fig.11.18 b.\nThis work done will be stored as electrical potential energy in the charge.\nIf U and Us are the potential energies at point A and B respectively, then change in potential energy AU is equal to the work done WBA-\nAU=WS U-U, W\n(11.18)\nPotential difference between two points is the work done in moving a unit positive charge from one point to another keeping the charge in electrostatic equilibrium.\nTo get equations for potential difference AV divide Eq (11.18) by q, we get\nWAU\n=V-V=AV\n9%\n9%\n(11.19)\nwhere V, and V, are the electric potentials at point A and B, respectively. Eq (11.19) can be written as\nAU=q,AV=W\nU1 = q2V12 U2 = 92V\n...(11.20)\nחו\nFrom Eq. (11.19) it is clear that potential difference between two points is the difference of potential energy per unit charge. Where AV is potential difference between A and B.\nEq (11.20) can be written as AV = or 1volt=\nW\n9.\nNa\n1Joule\n1Coulomb\nOnie volt is the potential difference between two points in an electric field if one joule of work is done in moving one coulomb of charge from the one point to the other. Other multiples and sub-multiples of volt are\n1 milli volt (mV) = 103 V, 1 micro volt (UV) = 10% V\n1 kilo volt (KV) = 103 V, 1 mega volt (MV) = 10° V I giga volt (GV) = 10° V\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n27\nIn order to give concept of electric potential at a point in an electric field a reference point is required where electric potential is zero and this point is taken at infinity. If point B is at infinity where electric potential is V. =0, while at point A electric potential is V1= W/9o.\nOn dropping the subscripts V\nW\n9.\nThus the electric potential at any point is equal to the work done in bringing a unit positive charge from infinity to that point without producing any acceleration.\n11.8 FIELD AND POTENTIAL GRADIENT\nLet us consider a uniform field, for which the lines of force are parallel and equidistance from each other. Let A and B be two points which are very close to each other so that the electric field intensity is almost constant as shown in Fig 11.18.The exact relationship can easily be derived for the field of a point. charge if a test charge q, is moved a small distance ar from point B to A then\nwork done on test charge force x distance moved\nAW= F Ar\nIn an electric field, the force acting is equal to the charge times the field strength\nF = q, E\nwork done = AW=EqoAr\nUsing Eq 11.20 we have\nAW=-9.4V\nThe negetive sign is applied because the work done on q, is against field force q.E, so\n9.4V = -E qar",
