        "problem": "EXAMPLE 11.5\nNOT FOR SALE\n29\nYou have a 12.0-V motorcycle battery that can move 5000 C of charge, and a 12.0-V car battery that can move 60,000 C of charge. How much energy does each deliver?\nGIVEN\nFor the motorcycle battery, q-5000C and AV-12.0V\nFor the car battery, q-60,000C and AV=12.0V\nSOLUTION\nREQUIRED\nAUcycle? AUcar?\nAUcycle (5000C) (12.0V)-(5000C) (12.0J/C)=6.00×10 J. AUcar (60,000C) (12.0V)=7.20×105J.\nASSIGNMENT 11.2\nWhen a 12.0 V car battery powers a single 30.0-W headlight, how many electrons pass through it each second? Answer (1.56×109 electrons)\n11.7 ELECTRIC POTENTIAL ENERGY AND POTENTIAL DUE TO A\nPOINT CHARGE\nLike earth's gravitational field every charge has electric field which theoretically expands upto infinity. Consider an isolated charge +Q fixed in space as shown in Fig11.20. The electric field intensity E is given by equation Ë= 1 Q.\nΑπε 2\nAs the test charge q is at infinity which is moved towards charge +Q a force of repulsion acts on it. Since external work is required to be done to bring charge from infinity to point A therefore negative sign is inserted in equation. The work done is\nAW=-qE.AF=qEar cos180°\nAW= qEAr\n... (11.22)\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n30\nNow putting the value of E in Eq (11.22) we have\nFIGURE 11.20\n+\nB\n1 Q9 Ar\nAW=\n4TE\n11\nA\nΔη\nWork done on a test charge q in moving it towards source charge +Q\nLet the test charge is at a large distance r, from charge Q. Electric fiel does not remain constant but varies as the square of the distance from the charge.\nWe divide the distance between\nr, and r, into infinitesimally small displacement so that the field.\nintensity over each displacement\nremains constant.\nWork done between point r, and\nr, is\nAW\nQar-r ATE\nAt the beginning of the first displacement E varies as 1/2 and at the end E varies as 1/2 since the average 1/ rover each small displacement is\nover each\n1.\n<r2> <tr>\nTherefore one r is replaced by ra and another b\nA 1 1\nГр\n<r2>\n55\nReplacing 1//2 = 1/rr, and Ar=r-r,in above equation we get:\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS By division\nNOT FOR SALE\n31\nГАГ\nQg1\nAW\nT\nATTE\n..... (11.23)\nSimilarly for second small displacement Ar=r, -r2. Work done is\nQg 1\nAW\nAnd\nQg1\nAW\n... (11.24)\nATE\nThe total work done in moving a charge q from r, to ra can be calculated by taking its sum.\nila\n1\n1111\nAW\n+\n4爬。\n1 5 5 12 12\nAW\nQq 1 4EA.\n... (11.25)\nThe work done to move a test charge q from infnity to a distance r from Qis\nATTE\nQ9 1\nW\nH\nVatra\nQ9\nΑπε\n...(11.26)\nThe electric potential energy at distance r from Qis\nU =\nQ9\n4TE\n(1)\n... (11.27)\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n32\nThe electric potential at a point in an electric field may be defined as the amount of work done in moving a unit positive charge from infinity to that point with\nuniform velocity.\nThe electric potential at distance r from Q is\nV\nW\n1 Q\nq 4π r",
