        "problem": "EXAMPLE 11.6\n... (11.28)\nA point charge of 3μC is placed at point O between M and N, 3cm apart. Point M is 2cm from the charge and N is 1cm from the charge. What is the\npotential difference VM - VM?\nM\nK\n2cm\n0\n1cm\nPotential due to a point charge\nK\nGIVEN\ncharge q= 3μC, distance r, -2cm, distance r2 = 1cm\nREQUIRED\nthe potential difference VM- VN-?\nSOLUTION\nN\nPotential at M due to the charge is\nVM-9×10×3×10%\na\n2x10--13.5x105 V\nPotential at N due to the charge is\n=9×10°×3×10*\n<=9x10°\n1x10-\n=27x105 V\nVM-V=13.5x105-27x10=-13.5x105V\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nASSIGNMENT 11.3\nNOT FOR SALE\n33\nTwo charges 5 x 10° C and -3 x 10° C are located 16 cm apart. At what point(s) on the line joining the two charges is the electric potential zero? Take the potential at infinity to be zero.\nAns(10 cm)\nTHE ELECTRON VOLT (eV)\nThe electron-volt is a unit of energy or work which is defined as the amount of energy acquired or lost by an electron when it is displaced across two points between which potential difference is one volt.\nSince the magnitude of the charge of an electron is about 1.602×10-19 C, it follows that an electron-volt is about 1.602x10-19 J.\nBy definition\nthus\nA(KE) LeV, when q=e= 1.602 x 101 C and AV= 1V.\n=\nI eV = 1.602 x 1019 C x 1 V\n1.602 x 10-19 J\nThis is the smaller unit of energy. Its bigger units are multiples of 1 eV that are in frequent use are given below.\nI Million electron volt = 1 Mev = 10° eV\nI Giga electron volt = | GeV = 10\" eV",
