        "problem": "EXAMPLE 11.6\nA particle carrying a charge (3e) falls through a potential difference of 5V. Calculate the energy acquired by the particle.\nGIVEN\nREQUIRED\nCharge =q=3e, potential difference AV= 5V\nEnergy: K.E=?\nSOLUTION\nThe energy acquired by the charged particle is\nA(KE) q4V (3e) (5 V)=15eV\n= 15×1.602 x 10-19 J= 2.4 x 1018 J\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\n11.10 CAPACITOR\nNOT FOR SALE\n34\nA\nB\nA device which is used for storing FIGURE 11. 21 electric charges is called capacitor. The most common capacitor consists of two parallel metal plates, separated by small distance. The medium between the two plates is air or a sheet of some insulator. This medium is known as dielectric. Figure 11.21 shows a parallel plate capacitor.\nThe capacitor is commonly charged by connecting its plates for a while to the opposite terminals of a battery. In this way some electrons\nParallel plates of a capacitor\nare transferred through the battery from the positive plate to the negative plate. When a charge is transferred to one of the plate say (A) due to electrostatic induction it would induce charge Q on the inner surface of the other plate B. Charge +Q and Q appear on the plates. Mutual attraction between the charges keeps them bound on the inner surface of two plates and thus the charge remains stored in the capacitor even after removal of the battery.\nFIGURE 11.22\nin\nCapacitors use in different electronic devices.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n35\n11.10.1 CAPACITANCE OF A CAPACITOR AND ITS UNIT\nThe capability of a capacitor to store charges is called it capacitance. When a charge Q is transferred on one of the plate of a capacitor it causes the potential difference V between the plates. In other words, the charge \"Q\" on the plate of a capacitor is directly proportional to the potential difference V between them i.e. QC\nQ=CV\n...(11.29)\nOR\n⇒c=2/3\nV\nWhere, C is the capacitance of a capacitor.\nThe capacitance is thus defined as the ratio of magnitude of charge on either plate to the potential difference produced between the plates.\nThe SI unit of capacitance is called farad.\nThe s\nFarad (F) is defined as \"the capacity of that capacitor which stores a charge of 1 coulomb having the potential difference of 1 volt between the plates\".\nConvenient sub-multiples of farad are:\n1μF (micro Farad)=10*F & 1pF (Pico Farad) = 10-11 F\n11.10.2 CAPACITANCE OF A PARALLEL PLATE CAPACITOR\nLet us consider a parallel plate capacitor connected to a voltage source V. The charges on the capacitor plates increases till the potential difference V across the plates build. Let the charges on the plates are +Q and -Q when the potential difference is V. If the positive plate is at potential V, and negative plate is at potential V2, then the electric field strength between the plates is\nE=\n-AV_-(V2-V1\nAr\n(A)\nd\nd\nd\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\n-\nWhere, V = V, V2 is the potential difference and d is the separation between the plates. The strength of the electric field also depends on the number of charges on the plates. The charge density is the total charge per unit area of the plate. i.e.,\nNOT FOR SALE\nFIGURE:11.23\nB\nA\n36\nBy using Gauss's law the electric field\nintensity E between the plates is\nPlates of a capacitor separated by a distance d\nσ\nQ\nE=\nE. EA\nFrom Eq (A) & (B) we have\nE\nV\nQ\n무\nOr\nd&A\nQ = SAV\nd\nBy using Cyc\n(B)\nwaz e in\n...(11.30)\nEq(11.30) becomes\nV\nQ\nA\nC\n4-3-54\nV d\n...(11.31)\nWhen a dielectric is inserted between the plates of a capacitor, then it is seen that the charge storing capacity of a capacitor is enhanced by the dielectric which permits it to store & times more charge for the same potential\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n37\ndifference. In case of a parallel plate capacitor completely filled with a dielectric the capacitance is\nCaned = &,&,A\n... (11.32)\nd\nEq11.32: shows the dependence of capacitance upon the area of the plates, the separation between the plates and the medium between them. From Eq(11.31 ) and Eq(11.32) we have\nCmed Cyac\n...(11.33)\nThis relation provides us with the definition of relative permittivity or dielectric constant or specific inductive capacity:\nThe ratio of the capacitance of a capacitor with a given material filling the space between the plates to the capacitance of the same capacitor when the space is evacuated is the relative permittivity E, of the material.\n11.10.3 COMBINATIONS OF CAPACITORS\nWe know that the capacitors FOR YOUR INFORMATION can be connected either in series or in parallel. We want to find out an equivalent capacitor that has the same capacitance as that of the combination of capacitors\nSERIES COMBINATION OF CAPACITORS\nWhen the capacitors are connected plate to plate i.e. the right plate of one capacitor is connected to the left plate of the next capacitor so on as shown in Fig:11.24 then it called series\nAn axon membrane acts as a capacitor. Axon of a resting human nerve cell has a potential difference of 65mV.Current enters the axon (or dendrite) through ion membrane, depolarizing that region. The channels (e.g. Na+ channels) in a region of\nintracellular positive charge is attracted to adjacent negatively charged regions of membrane.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n38\ncombination. A battery of voltage V is connected between points A and B. In this type of combination the equivalent capacitance is given by\n1 1\n... (11.34)\nPARALLEL COMBINATION OF CAPACITORS\nWhen the battery is connected to the FIGURE 11.24 parallel plates of a capacitor as shown in Figure 11.25. The equivalent capacitance C, of this combination is\ngiven by\nC2 =c+c2+c\nC, C1\nB\n非\n... (11.35)\n반\nSeries combination of capacitors\n11.11 ELECTRIC POLARIZATION\nIn the previous section we explored the dependence of charge storing capacity of a capacitor on the dielectric material. A dielectric is an electrical insulator that can be polarized by an applied electric field.\nWhen a dielectric substance is placed between two parallel plates with an applied electric field. The electric field distorts the negatively charged particles towards the positive plate and the positive charge particles towards the negative plate as shown in figure 11.26. The slight separation of charge makes one side of the atom somewhat positive and the opposite side somewhat negative. This phenomenon is known as electric polarization.\nFIGURE 11.25\nA\nB\nV\nParallel capacitors\ncombination\nof\nPHYSICS-XII\nin\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nELECTRIC DIPOLE\nNOT FOR SALE\n39\nWe know that dielectric materials are made up of the two types of molecules polar and non polar molecules. A polar molecule like water behaves as a permanent dipole; although electrically neutral as a whole.\nOn the other hand, a non-polar molecule like nitrogen has no electric dipole moment in the absence of an external field. In the presence of electric field negative charges in non-polar substance displaces to the opposite of electric field E as shown in Fig11.26.Dipole FIGURE 11.26\nmoments occur when there is a separation of charge. Under the action of external field the centres of negative and positive charges shift and form dipoles. The dipole moment is a measure of the polarity of the molecule.\nThe system in which two charges of equal magnitude but of opposite sign separated by the distance d, are present is termed as a dipole. Electric dipole moment is represented by P, which is equal to of the charge Q present in the dipole and the distance d between the two charges of the\nthe\nproduct\n+\nd\nCĐCĐ\nCĐCĐ\nCDCD\nĐCĐ\nP= 1Qd1\ndipole.\n(11.36)\nWhere P is a vector quantity.",
