        "problem": "EXAMPLE 11.8\nA 6uF capacitor is charged to a P.D. of 200V and then connected in parallel with an un-charged 3uF capacitor. Calculate the P.D. across the parallel plate capacitors.\nPHYSICS-XII\nila\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nGIVEN\nNOT FOR SALE\n41\nCapacitance of charged capacitor C 6μF Capacitance of un-charged capacitor C2= 3pF, P.D. of capacitor C, is: V=200V\nREQUIRED\nSOLUTION\nP.D. across the parallel plate capacitors V=?.\nCharge on capacitor, C, is: Q = C,V (6x10) x 200 = 0.0012C\nThe equivalent capacitance of parallel combination of capacitors is\nCea C-6\nCrq = C1+ C2= 6 + 3 = 9μF.\nThe charge 0.0012 C is distributed between the two capacitors to\nhave a common P.D.\n..P.D. acrossParallel plate capacitors :V\n11.12 ENERGY STORED IN A CAPACITOR\nInitially, the capacitor is uncharged when\nQ 0.0012\n= 133.3V\nC, 9x10\nvoltage is zero. When it is connected to For your Information\nsource of potential difference V it is charged. Finally when charges +Q and -Q are deposited on the plates, the potential difference between the plates becomes V. The average voltage on the capacitor is V / 2. Thus the energy stored in a capacitor is\nEnergy=U=QV\nQv ... (11.37)\n2\nFrom Q = CV\nEnergy stored in the large capacitor is used to preserve the memory of an electronic calculator when its battery is charged.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n42\nU=\nQV CV2 ==\n..... (11.38)\n2\nQ2\nU =\n2C\nIt is also possible to regard the energy as being stored in the electric field between the plates rather than the potential energy of the charges on the plates. Such a view point is useful when the Electric field between the plates is considered rather than the charges on the plates causing the field is to be considered .We know that\nV=Ed\nand\nC = AE, E\nd\nSubstituting these values in Eq. (11.38). We get\n1 AE,Ex(Ed)2\nU=12x\n2 d\nU== &, €, E2 x(Ad) ... (11.39)\n2\nThe product (Ad) is volume between the plates. Let u denote the energy density that is, the energy contained in a unit volume of the field. Then\nU=\nEnergy U\nVolume Ad\n=글2\n... (11.40)\nawaz e in\n11.13 CHARGING AND DISCHARGING OF A CAPACITOR THROUGH A\nRESISTOR\nConsider a circuit having a capacitance C and a resistance R which are joined in series with a battery of voltage V through a key K as shown in the figure 11.27. Let us assume that the capacitor, C has no charge when the key (k) is fully open. These are the initial conditions of the circuit, then t = 0, i=0 and q = 0.\nPHYSICS-XII\nila\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n43\nK\nWhen the key is pressed, the capacitor FIGURE 11.27 begins to store charge. If at any time during charging, I is the current through the circuit and Q is the charge on the capacitor. It can be shown that after time t, as charge builds up on the plates, it repels more charge than is arriving, and the current drops as the charge on the plates increases.\nThe graph between time t and charge q is shown in Figure 11.28. At time t = 0 charge q=0 and at certain time t charge q reaches its maximum value q..\nE-\nRC circuit\nThe graph also shows that the charge. reaches its maximum value sooner when the time constant is small. As the capacitor charges up, the potential difference across FIGURE 11.28 its plates begins to increase with the actual time taken for the charge on the capacitor to reach 63% of its maximum possible fully charged voltage.\nTIME CONSTANT\nThe time taken to charge a capacitor in a given circuit is determined by the time constant of the circuit.\nCharge\n0.632q\nR\nC\nTime\nt\nCharging Of a Capacitor\nThe factor RC is called 'time constant. The time constant is the duration of time for the FOR YOUR INFORMATION\ncapacitor in which 63.2 % of its maximum value charge is deposited on the plates which is illustrated in curve Figure 11.28 Maximum charge on capacitor = capacitance x e.m.f. of battery\nFigure 11.29 shows a resistor capacitor circuit called RC circuit.\nWhen the battery is removed and key (k) is first closed, the capacitor starts to discharge as shown in Fig.11.30.\nIn principle, a capacitor can never charge up fully, because the rate of charging decreases as the charge increases. In practice, after a finite time the charging current becomes too small to measure, and the capacitor is effectively fully charged.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nt\nNOT FOR SALE\n44\nK\nThe capacitor will discharge itself through resistance R. When = RC, the magnitude of charge FIGURE 11.29 remaining on each plate is, q= q. (0.368). Time constant of RC circuit is thus also the time during which the charge on the capacitor falls from its maximum value to 36.8%. The corresponding graph in Fig. 11.30 shows that discharging begins at t=0 when q = q., and decreases gradually.\nR\nC\n+q\nThe charging and discharging of a capacitor has many applications. Capacitor discharge ignition (CDI) is a type of automotive electronic ignition system which is widely used in motorcycles, lawn mowers, chain saws, small engines, turbine powered aircraft, Discharging a capacitor. and some cars. It can enhance the capability\nof power supply and make the spark much\nstronger.\nFIGURE 11.31\nFIGURE 11.30\nCharge\n0.3689\naz ein\nElectronic Ignition CDI\nTip\nTime Discharging of a capacitor\nElectronic flashguns for cameras have to be left for a short period of time between flashes. There is a capacitor inside that stores energy, it needs to be charged up again by the battery in the flashgun.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nK\nNOT FOR SALE\n45\nCoulomb's Law: the magnitude of the force between two point charges is directly proportional to the product of the magnitudes of the charges and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.\nElectric potential at any point: it is equal to the work done in bringing a unit positive charge from infinity to that point without producing any acceleration.\nElectric field: the region around a charge in which an electric test charge would experience an electric force.\nThe electric flux : the number of lines of force that pass through the area placed in the electric field. = E.A = EA cose\n©\nWhen area A is normal to the electric field E then electric flux is maximum. When area A is parallel to the electric field E then electric flux is minimum.\nGauss's law: The electric flux through any closed surface is 1/e, times the total charge enclosed in it.\nCapacitor: A device which is used for storing electric charges.\nElectron volt (eV): the unit of energy and is related to joule as\n1eV 1.602 x 10-19 J\nDipole: the system in which two charges of equal magnitude but of opposite sign separated by the distance d.\nPOINT\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 11 ELECTROSTATICS\nNOT FOR SALE 46\nU\nT",
