    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Electrostatics",
        "def": "In this unit two fundamental processes of electrostatics are introduced: Coulomb&#x27;s law for force between stationary charges and the principle of superposition for electric field configurations.\nElectrostatics is the studies of electric charges which are at rest or static. The electric force between static charged particles is known as the electrostatic\nThe electrostatic force is attractive or repulsive force between particles that are caused due to their electric charges. It is also referred to as Coulomb&#x27;s force. In SI units, charge is measured in coulombs (symbol C). The charge q is quantized and it is an integral multiple of elementary charge e, i.e., q=ne\nAnother quantity being discussed that plays an important role in electrical circuits. is capacitance and its dependence on the dielectrics."
      }
    ],
