        "title": "Learning outcomes & introduction",
        "theory": "UNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nNOT FOR SALE\n53\ndescribe the working of rheostat in the potential divider circuit.\ndescribe what is a Wheatstone bridge and how it is used to find unknown resistance?\ndescribe the function of potentiometer to measure and compare potentials without drawing any current from the circuit.\nCurrent electricity is the study of charges in motion. This unit explores the concept of electric current, the flow of electric charges in a metallic conductor. Simple electrical circuits can be solved by applying Ohm&#x27;s law. For other circuits, Kirchhoff rules are applied.\nWe all enjoy the comforts and benefits of using electricity. Electricity authorities use high-voltage transmission lines and transformers to distribute electricity to homes and industries in a town.\nIn this unit we shall discuss briefly the various features associated with the moving charges.\n12.1 STEADY CURRENT\nThe continuous flow of free electrons is called steady current. The flow of electronic current can be easily explained by referring Figure 12.1. The conductor has a large number of free electrons when electric field or voltage is applied, then free electrons, being negatively charged, will start moving towards the positives terminal around the circuit.\nThis directed flow of\nelectrons is called electric\ncurrent.\nFollowing are the main points:\n1. The actual direction\nof current is from negative terminal to\nFIGURE 12.1\nFree Electrons\n+\n→HHHI\nFlow of free electrons in a conductor\nthe positive terminal through out that part of the circuit external to the cell. However, prior to electron theory, it was assumed that current flows from positive terminal to the negative terminal of the cell via the circuit. This convention is so firmly established that it is still in use. This assumed direction is now called conventional current.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nNOT FOR SALE\n54\n2. Those substances which have larger number of free electrons will permit current flow easily. Such substances are called conductors, for example Copper, Zinc, Silver, Aluminum etc.\nIn different current carrying materials the charges on the moving particles may be positive or negative. In metals the moving charges are always electrons (negative charges). While in gases the moving charges are negative and positive ions. We define the current through the cross-section area A to be the net charge flowing through the area per unit time.\nThus if a net charge &#x27;Q&#x27; flows through an area in a time t, the current / is\n(12.1)\nThe charge Q is measured in coulombs and time t in seconds. Therefore the unit of electric current will be coulombs/sec or ampere (A). One ampere is the current when one coulomb of charge flows across any cross section of a conductor in one second. The submultiples of ampere are:\nTip\n=\n1 milliampere 1 mA 1 x 103 A\n1 microampere = 1 μA = 1 x 10 A\nCurrent is a scalar quantity as it does not follow vector law of addition.\nDo you know?\nNow if electrons drift so slowly, how room light turns on quickly when switch is closed? The answer is that propagation of electric field takes place with the speed of light.\nEXAMPLE 12.1\nEach second 101 electrons flow from right to left across a cross-section of a wire attached to the two terminals of a battery. (a) Calculate the current in the wire, (b) in which direction the current is flowing.\nGIVEN\nHere n = 101;\nCharge on single electron: e = 1.6 x 10&#x27;19 C,\nt=1s\nin\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nREQUIRED\nNOT FOR SALE\n55\n(a) Electric current / =?, (b) direction of current flow=?\nSOLUTION\nElectric current\n101 x1.6x10\n1\nQ_ne t t\n--1.6 x 10-A-160mA\nThe current is flowing from left to right i.e. in opposite direction of electron flow.\nDRIFT VELOCITY IN CONDUCTOR\nEvery conductor is made up of atoms which are in turn composed of electrons. These electrons move in random directions within the atom. Due to their random motion, the net velocity of negatively charged electrons in a conductor is zero.\nBut when a potential difference is applied to the ends of a conductor, an electric field E is set up at every point within the wire. As a result of this force electrons in the conductor acquire a certain amount of velocity which causes a net flow in one direction. This velocity that causes the electrons to move in a certain direction is known as Drift Velocity.\nThe net velocity with which free electrons get drifted in conductor under the influence of electric field is called drift velocity (V).\nFIGURE 12.2\nFree Electron\nAtom\n(a)\n(b)\nDrift of free electrons\nPHYSICS-XII\nNOT FORSALE 56\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nFOR YOUR INFORMATION\nElectroencephalogram\nAn electroencephalogram (EEG) is a test that uses metal discs (called electrodes) to evaluate the electrical activity in the brain. The brain constantly generates minute electrical currents even during sleep and in deep coma. The electrodes with thin wires are attached to your scalp which detects tiny electrical charges that result from the activity of your\nbrain cells.\nThe computer records your brains electrical activity on the screen or on a paper as wavy line. These lines allow neurologists to quickly assess whether there are abnormal patterns. An EEG is one of the main diagnostic tests for epilepsy, Brain tumors, Stroke etc.\n70\nTak C\n12.2 OHM&#x27;S LAW&lt;svg viewBox=&quot;0 0 460 210&quot; role=&quot;img&quot; aria-label=&quot;Current Electricity&quot; style=&quot;width:min(100%,460px);height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #dbeafe;border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M55 170H420M75 185V25&quot; stroke=&quot;#334155&quot; stroke-width=&quot;2&quot;/&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;220&quot; y=&quot;202&quot; fill=&quot;#334155&quot; font-size=&quot;13&quot;&gt;x / independent variable&lt;/text&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;12&quot; y=&quot;22&quot; fill=&quot;#334155&quot; font-size=&quot;13&quot;&gt;y&lt;/text&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M150 90H225M265 90H340M225 90V145H265V90&quot; fill=&quot;none&quot; stroke=&quot;#dc2626&quot; stroke-width=&quot;4&quot;/&gt;&lt;circle cx=&quot;245&quot; cy=&quot;90&quot; r=&quot;24&quot; fill=&quot;#fff&quot; stroke=&quot;#2563eb&quot; stroke-width=&quot;3&quot;/&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;238&quot; y=&quot;96&quot; font-size=&quot;13&quot;&gt;R&lt;/text&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;185&quot; y=&quot;72&quot; font-size=&quot;14&quot;&gt;I →&lt;/text&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;",
