        "title": "12.12 VARIATION",
        "theory": "Cold junction T2\nCopper Iron\nMLUVNTS\nWire B\nCopper Iron\n(a) Variation of thermo emf with temperature\nbetween the two junctions in a Cu-Fe thermocouple.\nThus thermocouple is a temperature measuring device. In other words, it is a type of sensor used for measuring the temperature in the form of an electric current or the EMF.\nThe wire pairs can be composed of noble metals (such as platinum, iridium, silver, osmium, gold, and rhodium) or base metals, (such as copper, iron or\nplatinum nickel-copper alloy, lead and zinc). Thus it remains true for any pair of metals. Thermocouples are the most widely used temperature sensors in industry due to their low cost, simplicity, size and useable temperature range. The electromotive force E is a function of the temperature gradient.\nThe thermo- e.m.f. produced is very small, of the order of mV per every degree of temperature difference. The Seebeck effect is reversible, i.e., if the hot and cold junctions are interchanged, the direction of e.m.f. (and hence current) reverses.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nNOT FOR SAL\nThe greater the separation of the metals forming the thermocouple in series, greater is the thermo e.m.f. produced.\nIts temperature dependence is given by\nE\n8 = aT + 2 BT2\n...(12.16)\nWhere a and B are constants (called thermoelectric coefficients) which depends on the nature of the metals.\n12.12 VARIATION\nVARIATION IN THERMOELECTRIC\nTEMPERATURE\ne.m.f.\nWITH\nKeeping the junction B at 0°C, the temperature of junction A is increased. When both the junctions are at the same temperature, there is no thermo\ne.m.f. when the temperature at one\njunction is cold while at other end FIGURE 12.12\nis increased the thermo e.m.f. also increases with temperature and reaches a maximum value at a certain temperature, called the neutral temperature T.\nila\nneutral\nThe value of the temperature is constant for a thermocouple, depends on the nature of materials and is independent of the temperature of the cold junction. As\nAs the temperature of the hot junction is increased, the thermo e.m.f. starts decreasing instead of increasing.\nThe particular temperature at which, the thermo e.m.f. becomes\nThermo emf\nT\n(b) Variation of thermo emf with temperature T for copper-iron thermocouple\nzero is called the inversion temperature Figure 12.12 (b). The graph shows the variation of the thermo e.m.f. with the temperature of hot junction, with the cold junction.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nNOT FOR SALE\n74\nResistance thermometers\nAccurate temperature measurement is essential for food producers, hospitals, refineries, chemical plants, in research labs, and pharmaceutical manufacturers. That&#x27;s why a great many lab and industrial applications today pick reliable, accurate resistance temperature detectors (RTDs) as the temperature measurement instrument of choice.\nResistance\nthermometers, also\ncalled resistance\ntemperature detectors (&#x27;RTD&#x27;s), are sensors used to measure temperature by correlating the resistance of the RTD element with temperature.\nCommon RTD sensing elements constructed of platinum, copper or nickel have a unique, and repeatable and predictable resistance versus temperature relationship (R vs T) and operating temperature range.\nThe R vs T relationship is defined as the amount of resistance change of the sensor per degree of temperature change.\nPlatinum is the best metal for RTDs because it follows a very linear resistance-temperature relationship and it follows the R vs T relationship in a highly repeatable manner over a wide temperature range. The unique properties of platinum make it the material of choice for temperature standards over the range of -272.5°C to 961.78 °C. They are slowly replacing the use of thermocouples in many industrial applications below 600 °C, due to higher accuracy and repeatability.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\n12.13\nKIRCHOFF&#x27;S LAW\nNOT FOR SALE\n75\nMany complex circuits, where Gustar Robert Kirchhoff&#x27;s (1824-1887)\nsimplification by series and parallel combinations is impossible due to combination of more than one batteries in a circuit. Ohm&#x27;s law cannot be applied to solve such circuits. Circuits like this are known as multi-loop circuits.\nIn order to find the current in all branches of a multi-loop circuit (or the emf of a battery or the value of a resistor) Robert Kirchhoff&#x27;s gave two laws, known as Kirchhoff&#x27;s laws. These laws also apply to very simple circuits.\nKirchhoff was a German physicist who made significant contributions to the\nKIRCHOFF&#x27;S CURRENT LAW fundamental understanding of black-\n(KCL)\nKirchhoff&#x27;s current rule\nbody radiation emitted by heated objects, spectroscopy, and electrical circuits.\n(the junction rule) is manifestation of the conservation of charge to a junction; it is illustrated in Figure 12.13. Current is the flow of charge, and charge is conserved. This law is related to the currents at the junctions of an electric circuit and may be stated as under:\nThe algebraic sum of all FIGURE 12.13\nthe currents meeting at a\njunction in an electrical\ncircuit is zero.Σl=0\nA junction is a point in a circuit where two or more components\nare\nconnected. As algebraic sum is one in which the sign of the quantity is taken into account. example, consider four\nconductors\nFor\ncarrying\ncurrents 11, 12, 13 and 14.\nL\nJunction at O with four branches.\nPHYSICS-XII",
