        "sciNote": "R1\n24V\nつ\n+da\nR, 3k\nExh\n3ΚΩ\n-9b\nD\nComplex circuit solution through Kirchhoff's rule\nTotal circuit resistance,\nCircuit current,\nRT=2+1+3= 6k£2\nE 24V\n-=4mA\nR, 6k0\nApplying Kirchhoff's voltage law to loop ABCDA, we have\n&-IR-8=0\n24-4 mA x 2k-ab=0\n24-8-Eab=0\nEab=24-8=16V\nev\nASSIGNMENT 12.3\nCircuit ABCDEF is shown in Figure. Calculate the current in each branch of the circuit using Kirchhoff's voltage law.\n4V\n50\nAnswer (0.163A and 0.05454 A)\n100\nC\n200\n2300\nPHYSICS-XII\nC\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\n12.14\nWHEATSTONE BRIDGE\nNOT FOR SALE\n79\nThis bridge was first proposed by Wheatstone an English telegraph engineer for measuring accurately the value of an unknown resistance.\nIt consist of four resistors (two fixed resistances P and Q which are known one variable resistance R which is also known and one unknown resistance X whose value is to be found) as shown in figure 12.15. Across one pair of opposite junctions (A and C) battery is connected and across the other opposite pair of junctions (B and D), a galvanometer is connected.\nThe value of P and Q are properly fixed. When the key is switched on, the current / divide unequally between the two branches and the galvanometer shows current.\nThe value of resistance R is FIGURE 12.15\nvaried until the galvanometer shows zero current. Under such conditions, the bridge is said to be balance. The point at which the bridge is balanced is called null point.\nLet I, and I be the currents through P and R respectively when the bridge is balanced. Sin",
