        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 2",
        "theory": "or R=p=\nA\nWhere p (Greek LETTER &#x27;Rho&#x27;) is a constant and is known as resistivity or specific resistance of the material.\nThe specific resistance of a material the resistance offered by 1m length of wire of a material having an area of cross-section of 1m2. A wire of 1 metre length is shown in figure 12.4. The unit of resistivity is ohm-meter (0.m). The resistivity of a substance varies over a wide range. To give an idea to the reader, the following table may be referred.\nFIGURE 12.4.\n1m2\n1m2\nCross-section area\nof a material\nThe resistivity of metals and alloys is very small. Therefore, these materials are good conductors Table 12.1\nof electric current. On the other hand, resistivity of insulators is extremely large. As a result, these materials hardly conduct any current. There is also an intermediate class of material known as semiconductors. The resistivity of these substances. lies between conductors and insulators.\nThe resistivity&#x27;s of some metals.\nMetals\nCopper (hard drawn)\nResistivity(pom)\n0.0178\nAluminium\n0.0285\nTin Silver\n0.114\n0.0163\nn\nBrass\n0.06-0.09\nIron\n0.1\nLead\n0.219\nCONDUCTANCE: The Reciprocal of resistance of a conductor is called conductance (G). If a conductor has resistance R, then its conductance G is given by\nG=TR\n...(12.4)\nThe SI unit of conductance is mho (i.e. ohm spelled back ward) these days, it is a usual practice to use Siemen as the unit of conductance. It is denoted by symbol. S.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nNOT FOR SALE\n61\nCONDUCTIVITY: The reciprocal of resistivity of a conductor is called its conductivity. It is denoted by the symbol o. If a conductor has resistivity p, then its conductivity is given by\n1 L\nP RA\n(12.5)\nThe unit of conductivity is mho m1 or siemen meter (S.m1)\n12.4 EFFECT OF TEMPERATURE ON RESISTANCE\nThe resistance of a material changes with the change in temperature. The effect of temperature upon resistance varies according to the type of material.\ni)\nThus resistance of a conductor (e.g. copper, aluminum) increases with the increase of temperature. The change in resistance is fairly regular for normal range of temperatures.\nThe resistance of electrolytes, insulators (e.g. glass, mica, rubber etc.) and semiconductors (e.g. germanium, silicon etc.) decreases with the increase in temperature.\n12.4.1 TEMPERATURE CO-EFFICIENT OF RESISTANCE/RESISTIVITY\nConsider a conductor having resistance R, at 0°C and R, at T °C. It has been found that in the normal range of temperatures, the increase in resistance (RT- Ro) is\ni) Directly proportional to the initial resistance i.e.\nR-RR\nii) Directly proportional to the rise in temperature i.e.\nR-RT\nCombining the above two relations, we get\nR, -R R2T\nR-Ra RT\n(12.6)\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nNOT FOR SALE\n62\nWhere is the constant and is called temperature co-efficient of resistance. Its value depends upon the nature of material and temperature. Rearranging equation (12.6) we get:\nR1 =R1 (1+α T)\nFrom equation (12.6) we get:\na=\nR-R R.T\n(12.7)\n(12.8)\nTemperature co-efficient of resistance may be defined as increase in resistance per ohm original resistance per degree rise in temperature.\nThe unit of a as derived from the definition is K. Since the resistance of metals increases with the rise in temperature, they have positive temperature co-efficient of resistance, while co-efficient a is negative for semiconductor because they show decrease in resistance as the temperature raises. The resistivity or specific resistance of most materials increases linearly with increasing temperature. The equation, (12.8) also apply to p. At temperature &#x27;T&#x27; the resistivity &#x27;p, of a material is given by\nP=Polat) œ=PT ̄Po\nP.T\n...(12.9)\n...(12.10)\nEXAMPLE 12.3\nA transmission line made of copper has a resistance of 1002 at 0C°. Calculate the change in resistance between summer and winter, knowing that temperature varies from +35C° to -30C°. Assume the temperature co-efficient of copper to be 0.00427/C° at OC°.\nGIVEN\ntemperature T1= +35C, temperature T2 =-30C°, resistance R, 100 temperature co-efficient of copper a=0.00427/C\nREQUIRED\nchange in resistance R=?\nSOLUTION",
