        "theory": "R, R. (1+αT)\n=&gt;R30=100(1+0.00427(-30)\n⇒R_30=87.252\nsimilarly R11 =100(1+0.00427(35)]\n+35\nR35=11502\nChange in resistance = 115 87.2 = 27.80\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nEXAMPLE 12.4\nNOT FORSALE\n63\nCalculate the resistance of copper conductor having a length of 2 km and a cross-section of 22 mm2. Assume the resistivity is 18 x 10.m.\nGIVEN\nLength L = 2 km = 2000m\nCross-section A-22mm2 = 22 x 10 m2\nResistivity p= 18 x 102-m\nREQUIRED\nResistance of copper R= ?\nSOLUTION\nThe resistance of copper wire of length L, cross-sectional area A and resistivity\np is given by\nCO\nR=PL\nA\nThus resistance of copper conductor is\nR=18x10&quot;\n2000\n22x10\nR=1.640\nASSIGNMENT 12.1\n(a) Calculate the resistance per unit length of a 22 nichrome wire of radius 0.321mm. (b) If a potential difference of 10V is maintained across 1m length of nichrome wire, what is the current in the wire? Prichromes 1.5x100.m.\n12.5 WIRE-WOUND VARIABLE RESISTORS\nanswer(4.60/m, 2.2 A)\nHigh stability and high accuracy resistors are always wire-wound. It is then enclosed in an insulating cover.\nGenerally, nickel chromium is used because of its very small temperature co- efficient of resistance. Wire wound resistors can safely operate at higher temperatures than carbon type resistors. Wire wound variable resistor can be used in two ways.\ni) Rheostats ii) Potential divider\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\ni) RHEOSTATS\nNOT FORSALE\n64\nTo use the variable resistor as a current control device one of the two fixed terminals say A and the sliding terminal C are inserted in the circuit as shown in figure12.5. In this\nway the resistance of the wire FIGURE 12.5 between A and the sliding\ncontact C is used. If the\nsliding contact is shifted away Terminal C from the terminal, A the length and hence the resistance included in the circuit increase.\nIf the sliding contact is moved towards A, the resistance decreases. Adjusting the resistance in the circuit controls the current in a circuit.\nii)\nPOTENTIAL DIVIDER\nCoil of wire\nSliding contact\nTerminal B\nTerminal A\nRheostats\nA potential divider provides a convenient way of getting a variable potential difference from a fixed\npotential difference. With the help of FIGURE 12.61\nthe battery, a potential difference V is\napplied across the ends A and B of the resistor (figure 12.6). Let R be the resistance of wire AB. The current / passing through it is\nI-VIR\nIf Rac is the resistance of the portion of the wire BC and the current passing through BC is I. The PD between the points B and C is given by\nVBC = IRBC\nVBC =\nV\nRC V\nRBC\nR\nR\nIV\n10\nwww\nTR\nV\nRec\nVac\nB\nPotential divider\nPHYSICS-XIL\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nNOT FORSALE\n65",
