        "theory": "PHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nBut\nE\nNOT FOR SALE\n68\nIR=E-Ir\nR+r\nIR=V, the terminal voltage of the cell\nV=E-Ir\nNow, if r=0 (OpenCircuit);\nBut, when the circuit is closed, the current move through it experiences an associated drop in potential equal to Ir. Thus when a current is flowing through a source, the Potential difference V between the terminals of the source is V=E-Ir. The Potential V called\nthe terminal voltage, is less than the e.m.f.( Ɛ) because of the term ir representing the Potential drop across the internal resistance r.\n12.9 ELECTRIC POWER\nWhen voltage V is applied to circuit, it causes current / to flow through the resistance R in time t. (Figure 12.10)\nThe total charge Q flows in t\nW\nsecond is Q = /xt and V\nW=QV=Vit\nQ\nClearly work is being done in moving the charges in the circuit. This work done in moving the charges in a unit time is called the\n1=0\nthen V=E\nFIGURE 12.9\nR\nε\nInternal resistance of a battery\nFIGURE 12.10\nZ\nR www\nV\nWork done on a charge\nB\nelectric power or the rate at which work is done in an electric circuit is called electric power.\nWork done in electric circuit\nElectric Power=\nTime\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nElectric power = P==\nt\nW_Vit\nP=VI\n...(12.12)\nusing Eq V=IR weget\nor\nP=IR\n...(12.13)\nor\nP=\nR\n...(12.14)\nNOT FOR SALE\n69\nThe above three formulas are equally valid for calculation of electric power in a D.C. circuit. Which one is to be used depends simply on the known quantities. UNIT OF ELECTRIC POWER: The SI unit of power is watt. A power of 1 watt is said to be consumed in a circuit if a potential difference of 1 volt causes a current of 1 ampere to flow through it.\n1 Watt =1Vx1A\nThe bigger units of power are kilowatt (kW) and mega watt (MW)\n1 kW = 1000 watts = 10&#x27; watts\n1MW = 10° watts\nKILOWATT-HOURS: Electric energy is commonly consumed in very large quantity for the measurement of which Joule is a very small unit. Hence a large unit of energy is required which is called kilowatt-hour. One kilowatt- hour is commonly termed as one commercial unit of electrical energy. It is the amount of energy obtained by a power of 1 kilowatt in one hour.\n1 kWh = 1000 WX3600s=3.6x10° Ws\n1 kWh = 3.6×10° J\n..1J=1Wx15\nEXAMPLE 12.5\nA heating coil has a resistance of 2012. It is designed to operate on 220 V. What electric energy in joules is supplied to the heater in 10 s?\nGIVEN\nR = 202, V = 220 Vand t = 10s\nREQUIRED\nelectric energy W=?\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nSOLUTION\nFrom Ohm&#x27;s law V= IR or I\n220 V\n-11A\n20 Q\nNOT FOR SALE\nV.\n= 10\nR",
