        "theory": "Now applying the formula\nW= 12 Rt\nW = (11A) (202) (10 s)\nW = 24200 J\n70\nEXAMPLE 12.6\n(1)\n(ii)\nThe following are the details of load and a circuit connected through a supply meter.\nSix lamps of 40 watts each working for 4 hours per day.\nTwo fluorescent tubes 125 watts each working for 2 hours per day.\n(iii) 1000 watts heater working for 3 hours per day.\nFind the cost of electrical energy consumed in 30 days of a month when the rate of electricity is 7.0 Rs per unit.\nSOLUTION\nTotal watts for lamps\nTotal watts for tubes\nHeater\nEnergy consumed per day\n40×6=240 watts\n125x2=250 watts\n1000 watts\nex\nze\n(250x-4)+(250x2)+(10\n(250x4)+(250x2)+(1000x3)\n4460 watt hours\n4.46 Kwh\nTotal energy consumed in the month of June\nBill for the month of June\n4.46x30=133.8kwh\n7×133.8 936.6 Rs\nASSIGNMENT 12.2\nA heating coil is rated at 100W,220V. The coil is cut in half and two pieces are joined in parallel to the same source. Now what is the energy liberated per\nAnswer (4×102J)\nsecond?\nPHYSICS-XI\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\n12.10\nNOT FOR SALE\n71\nMAXIMUM POWER OUTPUT\nIn many electronic circuits and systems it is important to have maximum transfer of power from the source to the load. For example, in radio or TV transmitting systems, it is desired to transfer the maximum power possible from the transmitting medium to the antenna systems. We want maximum power transfer from amplifier to speaker system. This is accomplished by proper matching of load resistance R and source resistance r.\nIf the load resistance is less or greater than the source resistance, then the power delivered to the load will be minimum.\nConsider the circuit of figure 12.11, as the current / flows through the load resistance R the charges flow from a point of higher potential to a point of lower potential. In this process, they lose potential energy. If V is the potential difference across R, the loss of potential energy per second is known as power delivered to R by the current I.\nFrom electrical power\nila\nPout IV Pout=FR\nFIGURE 12.11\nE2R\nPout\n(R+r)2\nE1R (R-r)2+4Rr\nPout\nWhen R\nני\nthe\ndenominator of the expression for Pout is minimum and so Pout is maximum.\nThus it can be concluded that\nR\nE\nInternal resistance of a battery\nmaximum power is delivered to a load R when the internal resistance of the source of emf is equal to the load resistance, also called maximum power transfer theorem.\nThe value of the maximum output power is\nɛ2 _ ɛ2\n(Pout)max\n...(12.15)\n4r 4R\nPHY, &#x27;CS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nNOT FURDAY-\n12.11\nTHERMOCOUPLES\nIn early 1821, Thomas Seebeck searched experimentally for a relation between electricity and heat. A thermocouple consists of two dissimilar wires A and B that are joined at one end T, (the &quot;hot&quot; junction). The other end T2 (the &quot;cold&quot; junction) is maintained at a constant reference temperature (usually the melting point of FIGURE 12.12\nice).\nThe device\nconnected at their ends\nshows a deflection. This\nis known as Seebeck Hot junction Wire A Effect. The e.m.f.\ngenerated in the circuit is called thermoelectric e.m.f. In this process energy is directly converted into electrical energy. Figure 12.12 (a) shows an arrangement to study the effect of temperature difference\nT1",
        "content": "Now applying the formula<br>W= 12 Rt<br>W = (11A) (202) (10 s)<br>W = 24200 J<br>70<br>EXAMPLE 12.6<br>(1)<br>(ii)<br>The following are the details of load and a circuit connected through a supply meter.<br>Six lamps of 40 watts each working for 4 hours per day.<br>Two fluorescent tubes 125 watts each working for 2 hours per day.<br>(iii) 1000 watts heater working for 3 hours per day.<br>Find the cost of electrical energy consumed in 30 days of a month when the rate of electricity is 7.0 Rs per unit.<br>SOLUTION<br>Total watts for lamps<br>Total watts for tubes<br>Heater<br>Energy consumed per day<br>40×6=240 watts<br>125x2=250 watts<br>1000 watts<br>ex<br>ze<br>(250x-4)+(250x2)+(10<br>(250x4)+(250x2)+(1000x3)<br>4460 watt hours<br>4.46 Kwh<br>Total energy consumed in the month of June<br>Bill for the month of June<br>4.46x30=133.8kwh<br>7×133.8 936.6 Rs<br>ASSIGNMENT 12.2<br>A heating coil is rated at 100W,220V. The coil is cut in half and two pieces are joined in parallel to the same source. Now what is the energy liberated per<br>Answer (4×102J)<br>second?<br>PHYSICS-XI<br>UNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY<br>12.10<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>71<br>MAXIMUM POWER OUTPUT<br>In many electronic circuits and systems it is important to have maximum transfer of power from the source to the load. For example, in radio or TV transmitting systems, it is desired to transfer the maximum power possible from the transmitting medium to the antenna systems. We want maximum power transfer from amplifier to speaker system. This is accomplished by proper matching of load resistance R and source resistance r.<br>If the load resistance is less or greater than the source resistance, then the power delivered to the load will be minimum.<br>Consider the circuit of figure 12.11, as the current / flows through the load resistance R the charges flow from a point of higher potential to a point of lower potential. In this process, they lose potential energy. If V is the potential difference across R, the loss of potential energy per second is known as power delivered to R by the current I.<br>From electrical power<br>ila<br>Pout IV Pout=FR<br>FIGURE 12.11<br>E2R<br>Pout<br>(R+r)2<br>E1R (R-r)2+4Rr<br>Pout<br>When R<br>ני<br>the<br>denominator of the expression for Pout is minimum and so Pout is maximum.<br>Thus it can be concluded that<br>R<br>E<br>Internal resistance of a battery<br>maximum power is delivered to a load R when the internal resistance of the source of emf is equal to the load resistance, also called maximum power transfer theorem.<br>The value of the maximum output power is<br>ɛ2 _ ɛ2<br>(Pout)max<br>...(12.15)<br>4r 4R<br>PHY, &#x27;CS-XII<br>UNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY<br>NOT FURDAY-<br>12.11<br>THERMOCOUPLES<br>In early 1821, Thomas Seebeck searched experimentally for a relation between electricity and heat. A thermocouple consists of two dissimilar wires A and B that are joined at one end T, (the &quot;hot&quot; junction). The other end T2 (the &quot;cold&quot; junction) is maintained at a constant reference temperature (usually the melting point of FIGURE 12.12<br>ice).<br>The device<br>connected at their ends<br>shows a deflection. This<br>is known as Seebeck Hot junction Wire A Effect. The e.m.f.<br>generated in the circuit is called thermoelectric e.m.f. In this process energy is directly converted into electrical energy. Figure 12.12 (a) shows an arrangement to study the effect of temperature difference<br>T1",
