        "theory": "UNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nNOT FOR SALE\n76\nIf we take the signs of currents flowing towards point O as positive, then currents flowing away from point 0 will be assigned negative sign. Thus applying Kirchhoff&#x27;s current laws to the junction\n(4)+(4)+(-2)+(-4)=0\nOr\n4+4=42+b\n...(12.17)\ni.e. sum of incoming currents = sum of outgoing current.\nHence, Kirchhoff&#x27;s current law may also be stated as the algebraic sum of currents flowing towards the junction in an electrical circuit is equal to the algebraic sum of currents flowing away from that junction.\nKIRCHOFF&#x27;S VOLTAGE LAW (KVL)\nThis law refers to e.m.f and voltage drops in a closed circuit or closed loop and may be stated as under:\nIn any closed electrical circuit, the algebraic sum of all the electromotive force, (e.m.f) and\nvoltage drops in resistor is equal to FIGURE 12.14 zero i.e.\nAlgebraic sum of emf algebraic sum of voltage drops = 0.\nThe validity of Kirchoff&#x27;s voltage law can be easily established by referring to the closed loop ABCDA shown in Figure 12.14. Starting from any point (say point A) in this closed circuit after going around the circuit and go back to this point (i.e. point A). The following convention is adopted to solve the circuit.\n1) A rise in potential is taken\npositive.\nii) A drop in potential is taken negative.\n६-\nB\nA\nwazen\nCI\nR1 www\nD\nSingle loop circuit\niii) For batteries, the positive end is always at higher potential.\niv) Current flows from high potential to the low potential.\nV) Assume the direction of current either clock wise or counter clockwise.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nNOT FOR SALE\n77\nPROCEDURES OF APPLYING KIRCHHOFF&#x27;S LAWS FOR PROBLEM SOLUTION\nMoving along the direction of current flow\nBA\nThe fall in potential is -IR,\nMoving along the direction of current flow\nBA\nThe fall in potential is -IR,\nMoving from the negative terminal to the positive terminal\nilab.com\nB\nC\nFor rise in potential +E,\nMoving from the positive terminal to the negative terminal\nCD\nFor fall in potential -\nThus applying Kirchhoffs law, on figure 12.14 we get\nE-IR-&amp;-IR2 = 0\nሰ ሰ\n&amp;&amp;2 =IR,+IR2\n-=IR,+IR2 Or\nΣε = ΣΙΑ\n...(12.18)\nThe sum of all the emf &amp; in a loop in a circuit is equal to the sum of all the IR voltage drops in that loop. KVL is in fact, a statement of the law of conservation of energy.\nTips\n1. The choice of loops is quite arbitrary, but it should be such that each resistance is included at least once in the selected loop. Kirchhoff&#x27;s voltage law can be applied to these closed loops to get the desired. equations.\n2. The direction in which currents are assumed to flow is not important, since if wrong direction is chosen, it will be indicated by a negative sign in the result.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nEXAMPLE 12.7\nFigure\nshows\nthree resistors and two voltage sources. Use Kirchhoff&#x27;s voltage law\nE\nto find the emf &amp; ab\nGIVEN\nE-24V,\nR1= 2k, R2= 1k and\nR3=3k2\nREQUIRED\ne.m.f. Eob = ?\nSOLUTION\nNOT FORSALE\n78\n2k2\nR1\n1 ΚΩ",
