      "12.6 THERMISTOR",
      "Thermistor is temperature sensing device or resistance thermometer whose prime function is to exhibit change in electrical resistance when subjected to a corresponding change in body temperature. It is made of metallic oxides, pressed into a bead, disk, or cylindrical shape and then encapsulated with an impermeable material such as epoxy or glass. The two main types of the thermistors are NTC (Negative Temperature Coefficient) and PTC (Positive Temperature Coefficient).",
      "NEGATIVE TEMPERATURE COEFFICIENT (NTC) THERMISTOR: These types of thermistor are most commonly used. The resistance of an NTC thermistor decreases with increasing temperature. Conversely, its resistance increases with decreasing temperature. A small changes in temperature can cause significant changes in their electrical resistance. This makes them ideal for accurate temperature measurement and control. Some appliances, such as washing machines, clothes dryers, refrigerators and freezers, as well as small appliances like hair dryers, ovens, toasters, thermostats, air conditioners and fire alarms also have NTCs for temperature control. Figure 12.7 shows some Types of thermistor.",
      "FIGURE 12.7",
      "Ma",
      "Europe",
      "(a) Thermistor Symbol.",
      "(b) Types of thermistor",
      "PHYSICS-XII",
      "UNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY",
      "66",
      "NOT FOR SALE POSITIVE TEMPERATURE COEFFICIENT (PTC) THERMISTOR: This type of thermistor is generally used for fuse purpose. The resistance of PTC thermistor increases with increase in temperature and when temperature decreases, its resistance decreases. One of the properties of PTC thermistors is that when an excessively large current flows, they generate heat by themselves and become highly resistive. With this property, they are used as overcurrent protection devices. They are used as timing devices in televisions. They are also used as heater in automotive industry to provide additional heat inside cabin with diesel engine or to heat diesel in cold climatic conditions before engine injection.",
      "12.7 ELECTROMOTIVE FORCE",
      "An external energy source is required by most electrical circuits to move charge through the circuit. The circuit therefore must include a device that maintains a potential difference between two points in the circuit, just as a circulating fluid requires as analogous device (pump).",
