        "id": "textbook-example-1",
        "title": "EXAMPLE 12.1",
        "problem": "EXAMPLE 12.1\nEach second 101 electrons flow from right to left across a cross-section of a wire attached to the two terminals of a battery. (a) Calculate the current in the wire, (b) in which direction the current is flowing.\nGIVEN\nHere n = 101;\nCharge on single electron: e = 1.6 x 10'19 C,\nt=1s\nin\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nREQUIRED\nNOT FOR SALE\n55\n(a) Electric current / =?, (b) direction of current flow=?\nSOLUTION\nElectric current\n101 x1.6x10\n1\nQ_ne t t\n--1.6 x 10-A-160mA\nThe current is flowing from left to right i.e. in opposite direction of electron flow.\nDRIFT VELOCITY IN CONDUCTOR\nEvery conductor is made up of atoms which are in turn composed of electrons. These electrons move in random directions within the atom. Due to their random motion, the net velocity of negatively charged electrons in a conductor is zero.\nBut when a potential difference is applied to the ends of a conductor, an electric field E is set up at every point within the wire. As a result of this force electrons in the conductor acquire a certain amount of velocity which causes a net flow in one direction. This velocity that causes the electrons to move in a certain direction is known as Drift Velocity.\nThe net velocity with which free electrons get drifted in conductor under the influence of electric field is called drift velocity (V).\nFIGURE 12.2\nFree Electron\nAtom\n(a)\n(b)\nDrift of free electrons\nPHYSICS-XII\nNOT FORSALE 56\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nFOR YOUR INFORMATION\nElectroencephalogram\nAn electroencephalogram (EEG) is a test that uses metal discs (called electrodes) to evaluate the electrical activity in the brain. The brain constantly generates minute electrical currents even during sleep and in deep coma. The electrodes with thin wires are attached to your scalp which detects tiny electrical charges that result from the activity of your\nbrain cells.\nThe computer records your brains electrical activity on the screen or on a paper as wavy line. These lines allow neurologists to quickly assess whether there are abnormal patterns. An EEG is one of the main diagnostic tests for epilepsy, Brain tumors, Stroke etc.\n70\nTak C\n12.2 OHM'S LAW\nThis law was presented by German scientist George Simon Ohm in (1826). It relates the three fundamental electrical quantities: voltage (V), the current (/) and resistance (R). If voltage V is applied across a conductor and current (/) flows through it then according to ohm law\nThe magnitude of the current in a conductor is proportional to the applied voltage as long as the temperature of the conductor is kept constant.\nGeorg Simon OHM (1787-1854)\nGeorg Simon Ohm was a German physicist and mathematician. He defines relationship between voltage, the fundamental\ncurrent, and resistance.\nin\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nNOT FOR SALE\n57\nMathematically\nIxV or I = V\nR\nor\nV=IR\n(12.2)\nWhere R is constant, which is known as the resistance of the conducting material. Resistance depends upon the nature, dimension and physical state of the conductor. For a conductor that obeys Ohm's law, a graph of current / as a function of voltage 'V' is a straight line passing through the origin Figure. (12.3(a).\nThe slope of the line is tane:\n1\nV R\nAs R is constant the slope is constant for ohmic conductors. The Ohm's law is not valid for all conducting material. Those materials for which the slope of I verses V graph is not constant are called non-Ohmic materials. For examples Fig 12.3 (b, c &d) illustrates the non-Ohmic characteristics of the filament of an electric bulb, a thermistor and of a semiconductor diode respectively. The I- V graph for non-Ohmic materials is non linear (curved).\nila\nFIGURE 12.3\n(4) Metals\n(b) Filament bulb\nV-I graph\n(c) thermistor\nPHYSICS-XII\n(d) Semiconductor Diode\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY",
        "given": "",
        "method": "See the source text below.",
        "steps": [],
        "answer": ""
