        "title": "EXAMPLE 12.7",
        "problem": "EXAMPLE 12.7\nFigure\nshows\nthree resistors and two voltage sources. Use Kirchhoff's voltage law\nE\nto find the emf & ab\nGIVEN\nE-24V,\nR1= 2k, R2= 1k and\nR3=3k2\nREQUIRED\ne.m.f. Eob = ?\nSOLUTION\nNOT FORSALE\n78\n2k2\nR1\n1 ΚΩ\nR1\n24V\nつ\n+da\nR, 3k\nExh\n3ΚΩ\n-9b\nD\nComplex circuit solution through Kirchhoff's rule\nTotal circuit resistance,\nCircuit current,\nRT=2+1+3= 6k£2\nE 24V\n-=4mA\nR, 6k0\nApplying Kirchhoff's voltage law to loop ABCDA, we have\n&-IR-8=0\n24-4 mA x 2k-ab=0\n24-8-Eab=0\nEab=24-8=16V\nev\nASSIGNMENT 12.3\nCircuit ABCDEF is shown in Figure. Calculate the current in each branch of the circuit using Kirchhoff's voltage law.\n4V\n50\nAnswer (0.163A and 0.05454 A)\n100\nC\n200\n2300\nPHYSICS-XII\nC\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\n12.14\nWHEATSTONE BRIDGE\nNOT FOR SALE\n79\nThis bridge was first proposed by Wheatstone an English telegraph engineer for measuring accurately the value of an unknown resistance.\nIt consist of four resistors (two fixed resistances P and Q which are known one variable resistance R which is also known and one unknown resistance X whose value is to be found) as shown in figure 12.15. Across one pair of opposite junctions (A and C) battery is connected and across the other opposite pair of junctions (B and D), a galvanometer is connected.\nThe value of P and Q are properly fixed. When the key is switched on, the current / divide unequally between the two branches and the galvanometer shows current.\nThe value of resistance R is FIGURE 12.15\nvaried until the galvanometer shows zero current. Under such conditions, the bridge is said to be balance. The point at which the bridge is balanced is called null point.\nLet I, and I be the currents through P and R respectively when the bridge is balanced. Since there is no current through Galvanometer, the current in Q and X are also I, and 2 respectively. As the Galvanometer reads zero, points B and D are at the same potential. This means that voltage drops from A to B and A to D must be equal.\nB\nA\nG\nC\nX\nAlso voltage drops from B to C and D to C must be equal. Hence\nFor balanced bridge the potential drop across AB= the potential drop across AD\nIP=IR\n(1)\nsimilarly the potential drop across BC= the potential drop across DC\nand 4Q=1X\n(ii)\nDividing Eq: (i)by Eq: (ii), we get\nPR Q X\nPHYSICS-XII\nZu\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nPX = RQ\nNOT FOR SALE\n80\nRQ\nX=\nP\n...(12.19)\nHence, if three resistances on the right side of equation12.19 are known, the fourth resistance X can be calculated. This is the principle of determining unknown resistance by Wheatstone bridge.\n12.15\nPOTENTIOMETER\nA potentiometer is a null type resistance network device for measuring potential differences.\nFor the accurate measurement of potential difference, current and resistance the potentiometer is most useful instrument. Its principle of action is that an unknown emf or potential difference is measured by balancing it, wholly or in part, against a known potential difference.\nA simplest potentiometer consists of wire AC of uniform cross-section stretched alongside a scale and connected across battery of potential V as shown in Figure 12.16. The FIGURE 12.16\nbattery through the\nrheostat and slide\nwire supply\nthe\nworking current. The\nworking current may\nvary by changing the setting of\nthe\nrheostat. A standard cell of known emf & is connected between A A and terminal 1 of a two way switch 5.\nSlider B is pressed momentarily against wire AC and its position is adjusted until\nthe\ngalvanometer\ndeflection is zero\n11°1\n६.\nBattery\nRheostat\ne vaz e\n៩.\n50\n100\nK\nSimplest potentiometer circuit\n150\n200\nC\nwhen B is making contact with AC. Let I, be the corresponding distance between A and B. The fall of potential over length I, of the wire is then the same as the emf &,. Then move the switch to 2, thereby replacing the standard cell by another cell, the e.m.f & of which is to be measured.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nNOT FOR SALE\n81\nAdjust the slider B again to give zero deflection on G. If I be the new distance between A and B,\nthen\n६\nasy as as\n&_k ६ 4\n...(12.20)\nApplications of potentiometer: following are the applications of potentiometer. 1. Measurement of small e.m.f (upto 2V) and high e.m.f( say 250 V). 2. Comparison of e.m.f of two cells.\n3. Measurement of resistance and current. 4. Calibration of ammeter and voltmeter.",
