        "id": "textbook-example-8",
        "title": "EXAMPLE 12.8",
        "problem": "EXAMPLE 12.8\nUsing a Weston cadium cell of 1.0183 V and a standard resistance of 0.10 a potentiometer was adjusted so that 1.0183 m was equivalent to the e.m.f of the cell: when a certain direct current was flowing through the standard resistance, the voltage across it corresponds to 150 cm. What was the value of current?\nGIVEN\nEmf of the cell, E, -1.0183 V\nL2=150 cm = 1.5 m,\nL,-1.0183 m, Resistance R= 0.10\nREQUIRED\ncurrent /2 = ?\nSOLUTION\n& 4\n= 2 &\n&1 = 2xε1 = 1.0183\ncurrent flowing through the standard resistance, ↓ =\n1.5\nx1.0183=1.5V\n1.5\n15A\n0.1\nASSIGNMENT 12.4\nA potentiometer wire of length 1 m is connected to a driver cell of emf 3V. When a cell of 1.5V emf is in secondary circuit the balance point is found to be at 60 cm. On replacing this cell and using a cell of unknown emf the balance point shifts to 80 cm. Calculate unknown emf of the cell.\nAnswer ( 2V)\nPHYSICS-XII\nNOT FOR SALE\n82\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nSteady current: the continuous flow of free electrons\nElectric current: the net charge flowing through the cross- section area A per unit time. 1=\nOhm law the magnitude of the current in metals is proportional to the applied voltage as long as the temperature of the conductor is kept constant. V=IR\nResistance: the opposition offered by the substance to the flow of free electrons.\nConductance (G): the reciprocal of resistance of a conductor.\nTemperature co-efficient of resistance: increase in resistance per ohm original resistance per degree rise in temperature.\nThermistor a heat sensitive device usually made of a semiconductor material whose resistance changes very rapidly with change of temperature.\nva\nSource of electromotive force: a device which converts non- electrical energy into electrical energy.\nElectric power: the rate at which work is done in an electric circuit.\nKirchoff's law: the algebraic sum of the currents meeting at a\njunction in an electrical circuit is zero.\nYEY\nPOINTS\nPHYSICS-XII\nP\nUNIT 12 CURRENT ELECTRICITY\nNOT FOR SALE!\n83",
