        "theory": "putting values Bet\nTm\n40\nx3A+\n0.01pT\n0.01p\n* 4×10-7 T\nx3x\ntherefore\nBoet = 3.4×10 T\nAnswer\nASSIGNMENT 13.2\nSOLENOID\nYou are asked to design a solenoid that will give a magnetic field of 0.10 T, yet the current must not exceed 10.0 A. Find the number of turns per unit length that the solenoid should have.\n(Answer: 7.96 x 103 m)\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT-FOR SALE\n102\n13.6 MOTION OF CHARGED PARTICLE IN MAGNETIC FIELD Moving charge also experience a magnetic field, as shown in figure 13.16 so it can be concluded that the force on the current carrying conductor in magnetic field\nresults from the forces acting on individual moving charges.\nFIGURE 13.16\nA cathode ray will deflect as shown when it is also under the\ninfluence of an external magnetic field.\nA. MAGNETIC FORCE ON A POINT CHARGE\nWhen a point charge is placed in an electric field, it experiences an electric force. When a charge is placed in a magnetic field, it also experiences a force, provided that the following two conditions are met.\n1. The charge must be moving, because no magnetic force acts on a stationary charge.\ne\n2. The velocity of the moving charge must have a component that is perpendicular to the direction of the magnetic field.\nConsider a positive point charge &quot;+q&#x27; moves at velocity &#x27;v&#x27; at a point where the magnetic field is &#x27;B&#x27;, as shown in figure 13.17. The angle between &#x27;v&#x27; and &#x27;B&#x27; is &#x27;e&#x27;. The magnitude of the magnetic force acting on the point charge is the product of\nThe magnitude of the charge &#x27;lql&quot;,\nThe magnitude of the field &quot;B&quot;, and\nThe component of the velocity &#x27;v&#x27; perpendicular to the field\nF2 = q(v sine) B\nin\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT-FORSALE\n103\nIn some cases it is convenient to look at the factor &#x27;sin e FIGURE 13.17 from a different point of view. If we associate the factor &#x27;sin 8&#x27; with the magnetic field instead of with the velocity, then &#x27;B sin O&#x27; is the component of the magnetic field perpendicular to the velocity of the charged particle\nF2 = qv(Bsino)\nThe magnetic force can be written in terms of cross product of two vectors &#x27;y&#x27; and &#x27;B&#x27; as\nor\nF2 = qvBsine ĥ\nF1-qvxB\n13.6\n13.7\nB\nv sin @\nThe force is greatest when the particle moves perpendicular to B (8-90°) because sin 90° = 1:\nFmax=qvB\nThe force is minimum or zero if the particle moves parallel to the field lines (0-0) because sin 0&quot; =0:\nF\nmin\n=0\nB sin 8\nB\nप\nVelocity of particle in magnetic field\nThe direction of the force is perpendicular to the magnetic field B and to the velocity v of the particle. For a positive charge, the Right Hand Rule II and Fleming&#x27;s Left Hand Rule for force on a current carrying conductor are used to determine the direction of force. Where the direction of current is replaced by velocity of charged particle.\nFIGURE 13.18\nThe direction of the magnetic force F, acting on a charged particle moving with a velocity v in the presence of a magnetic field B. (a) The magnetic force is perpendicular to both v and B. (b) Oppositely directed magnetic forces F, are exerted on two oppositely charged particles moving at the same velocity in a magnetic field.\n+q\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT-FOR SALE\n104\nB. THE FORCE ON CURRENT CARRYING CONDUCTOR AND MOTION OF CHARGE PARTICLE IN MAGNETIC FIELD\nWe have seen that a current-carrying wire experiences a force when placed in a magnetic field. Since a current in a wire consists of moving electric charges, we might expect that freely moving charged particles (not in a wire) would also experience a force when passing through a magnetic field. The force on a current carrying conductor is\nF = BILsine\nIf N particles of charge q pass by a given point in time, they constitute a current, then by definition of current\nNg\n|=\n2\nt\nBy definition of velocity v = = =\nor\nL= vt\n3\n* putting equation 2 and equation 3 in equation 1, we get F =Bx",
