        "theory": "or F = BNqv sine\nfor a single particle N = 1 F = Bqv sine\nor F2 = qvBsine\nIn vector form F2 =qVxB or\nNq\nxvxsine\ne in\nF1 = qvBsine n\nC. CIRCULAR MOTION OF CHARGE PARTICLE IN UNIFORM MAGNETIC FIELD The path of a charged particle moving in a plane perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field is a circle. Let us consider a positively charged particle moving in a uniform magnetic field with its initial velocity perpendicular to the field. Let&#x27;s assume the direction of the magnetic field is into the page as in Figure 13.19. As the particle changes the direction of its velocity in response to the magnetic force, the magnetic force remains perpendicular to the velocity.\nQUIZ?\n1. Why wheat flour is usually passed near a magnet before being packed?\n2. Why a steel ship becomes magnetized as it is constructed?\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT-FOR SALE\n105\nThe particle moves in a circle because the FIGURE 13.19 magnetic force &#x27;F,&#x27; is perpendicular to &#x27;y&#x27; and &#x27;B&#x27; and has a constant magnitude &#x27;qvB&quot;. The\ncentripetal force &#x27;F&#x27; is provided by magnetic force &#x27;F,&#x27;, therefore\nFc = Fa\n1\nFor a particle of mass m moving with velocity &quot;y&quot; in a circle of radius &#x27;r&#x27; the centripetal force &#x27;F&#x27; is\nmy2\n2\nB into the page\nx +q\nx\nFax\nx\nX\nCircular trajectory of charged particle in magnetic field.\nIf the charge on particle is +q, which is moving with velocity v perpendicular to magnetic field of strength B the magnetic force F, is maximum which is\nF1 =qvB\n3\nputting equation 2 and equation 3 in equation 1, we get\nmvz\n=q/B\nmv\nqB\nthe relation between linear angular velocity is v = rw, therefore equation 4 can be written as\nm/w\n=qB\nor mo=qB rearranging\nqB\n5\nm\nFrom the above equation we can determine the time period T of the charged particle\nT=\n2л 2π\nqB\n2π m\nsince\nдв\nT\nm\ntherefore f=\nqB\n13.8\n2л m\nThis is often called the cyclotron frequency of a particle in a field because this\nis the frequency at which particles revolve in a cyclotron\nPHYSICS-XII\nNOT-FOR SALE\n106\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nD. DETERMINATION OF CHARGE TO MASS RATIO FOR AN ELECTRON To determine the charge to mass ratio (q./m) for an electron. The beam of electron is projected on a perpendicularly arranged uniform magnetic filed, such that the electron move in a circle.\nFor an electron of charge &#x27;q,&#x27; and mass &#x27;m,&#x27;, the charge to mass ratio (q/m)\nbe calculated by balancing the centripetal and magnetic forces.\nmay\nFc = FB\nΠ\nhence\nme\n4.B\nог\nV\nrearranging\nme\nBr\nPractically the values of &#x27;B&#x27; can be calculated easily from the apparatus shown in the figure 13.20, from ringed coils (called Helmhold&#x27;s Coils). The radius &#x27;r&#x27; can also be calculated by making the electron beam visible through ionization and de- excitation of the gas molecules. However the velocity &#x27;v&#x27; is determined by calculating the kinetic energy of electron by applying the potential &quot;V&quot; of known value. Such that by law of conservation of energy\n2 m,v2 = q. V or",
