        "title": "13.8 GALVANOMETER",
        "theory": "13.8 GALVANOMETER\nNOT FOR SALE\n112\nA device used for detection and measurement of small electric currents is called galvanometer.\nThe principle of moving coil galvanometer is the torque on current carrying coil in the magnetic field.\nModern galvanometer are moving coil galvanometers, which are also called d&#x27;Arsonval galvanometers.\nFIGURE 13.25\nScale spring\npointer\nMoving coil galvanometer consist of pivoted coil wrapped around soft iron core, placed in a concave poles of a permanent magnet as shown in the figure 13.25. The poles of the magnet are made concave to make the field stronger and radial, such that magnetic force is perpendicular and torque is always maximum. Soft iron core concentrates the magnetic field (due to its high permanent\nmagnet permeability) thus giving more inertia to the coil. A light pointer or mirror is used to measure this angular deflection which is placed on a well calibrated scale.\nsoft iron\n110\ncore pivoted\ncoil\nmagnetic field\nGalvanometer\nThe coil is also connected to a spring from other side which produce a restoring torque, bringing the pointer to initial state when the current flow stops. The net torque on the coil is the sum of torque produced due to magnetic force and the restoring torque due to spring. Let the magnetic torque be counterclockwise while the spring coil be the restoring torque opposite to it and is taken as negative.\nThus\nFnet = Tmag +(-Tres) -\nWhen the pointer comes to rest equilibrium is achieved, the net torque is zero Tet=0,\nTnet =0\n2\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT FOR SALE\n113\nWhen current is applied, a magnetic field B sets-up in the coil and iron core assembly which interacts with a permanent magnet producing a torque.\nmog = NIABsine\nWhere N is the number of turns in the coil. Since the magnetic field is radial, therefore vector area is perpendicular to the field 8 = 90° and sin 90° = 1, therefore\nmag = NIAB\nThe restoring torque in its rotational form is\n3\nget\nFres Fres = Cop\n4\nWhere c is the spring constant (restoring torque per unit twist) in rotation and o is the angular displacement. Putting equations 2, 3 and 4 in equation 1, we\nilab\n0 = NIAB-co\nor\nNIAB = cp\nCip\n13.13\nNAB\nThe factor c/NAB is constant, thus the sensitivity of galvanometer can be adjusted by changing deflection angle op, which is done by changing the factor c/NAB. Thus current I is proportional to angular displacement o\nΤαφ\nThis relation shows that angular deflection can be used to detect and measure small currents. The cylindrical soft iron assembly in the core of galvanometer rotates in proportion with the current.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT FOR SALE\n114\nA. CONVERSION OF GALVANOMETER INTO AMMETER\nAn ammeter is a measuring instrument used to measure electric current in the circuit.\nGalvanometer is a very sensitive device even a small current may show full scale deflection. Ammeter is modified form of galvanometer to measure large current. The conversion of galvanometer into an ammeter is done by connecting a low resistance called shunt in parallel with it. When this shunt resistance is connected with galvanometer only small fraction of current flows through the galvanometer, remaining large portion of current passes through the shunt resistance as shown in figure 13.26.\nFIGURE 13.26\n(a)\nAmmeter\nG\n名\n1 R\n4-1-4\n(b)\nstance\n(a) Ammeter (b) Internal circuit with shunt resistance &quot;R,&quot;\nLet &quot;R,&#x27; represents the shunt resistance and &#x27;R,&#x27; represents the internal resistance of galvanometer. Here &#x27;I,&#x27; is the current which produces full scale deflection in galvanometer and I is the total current through Ammeter. Since shunt resistance is connected parallel to the galvanometer, therefore the potential is same for both\nresistence.\nV1 =V,\nAccording to Ohm&#x27;s law the potential drop across R, is V, IR\nPHYSICS-XII\n2\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT FOR SALE\n115\nSimilarly by Ohm&#x27;s law the potential drop across R, is V,-1,R,\nBut here 1,1-1,\ntherefore V, =(I— I1)R ̧\n3\nputting equation 2 and equation 3 in equation 1, we get (1-1)R, -1,R,\nIR\nHence",
