        "title": "13.8 GALVANOMETER",
        "theory": "R1\n(1-1)\n13.14\nThe shunt will convert a galvanometer into an ammeter which will give full scale deflection when current I passes through the main circuit.,Ammeter must have small resistance, so that current change with it is small. Ideally an\nammeter should not have any resistance.\nNote: Ammeter must be connected in series with the circuit.\nB. CONVERSION OF GALVANOMETER INTO VOLTMETER\nila\nVoltmeter is a measuring instrument used to measure potential difference in the circuit.\nVoltmeter is modified form of galvanometer to measure electric potential. The conversion of galvanometer into an voltmeter is made by connecting a high resistance in series with it.\nFIGURE 13.27\nVoltmeter\nR\nG\n(a)\n魚味\n(b)\n(a) Voltmeter (b) Internal circuit with high resistance &quot;R,&quot;\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT FOR SALE\n116\nLet R, represents the internal resistance of galvanometer and current I, is passing through it. The high resistance R, also draws the same current I. Since in series combination the potential splits and the total potential V is the sum of the potential across the galvanometer V, and the potential across the high resistance V\nV = V2+V2\nAccording to Ohm&#x27;s law the potential drop across R, is V, IR2\nSimilarly by Ohm&#x27;s law the potential drop across R, is V1 = R\nbut since I = 1, therefore V1 =1,R\nputting equation 2 and equation 3 in equation 1, we get\n3\n2\n√ = 12R2+12R\nV-IR\nV\nor\nIR-V-IR\nor Rh\nor Rh\nR\nR\nIs Is\nV\ntherefore\nR\nR\n13.15\nThe high resistor will convert a galvanometer into an voltmeter. Voltmeter must have high resistance, because it should not draw any current from the circuit. Ideally an voltmeter should have an infinite resistance.\nNote: Voltmeter must be connected in parallel with the circuit.\nEXAMPLE 13.6\ne\nCONVERSION OF GALVANOMETER\nA galvanometer has a resistance of 100 ohms and gives full scale deflection on 1 mA current. How it can be converted into (a) an ammeter of range 10A, (b) voltmeter of range 10V?\nGIVEN\nGalvanometer resistance &#x27;R&#x27; = 1000\nREQUIRED\nCurrent through galvanometer &#x27;,&#x27; = 1 x 10 A\nShunt resistance R, = ?\ncurrent range &#x27;I&#x27;= 10 A\nHigh resistance R, =?\nvoltage range &quot;V&quot; = 10 V\nPHYSICS-XI\nin\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nSOLUTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n117\n(a) Galvanometer can be converted into an ammeter of range I = 10 A by connecting a shunt resistance &#x27;R,&#x27; in parallel, the shunt resistance is given as\nIR\nR1\n(1-4)\n1x103 Ax1002\nputting values R,\n(10 A-1x10-3A)\ntherefore R1 = 0.010 0\nAnswer\n(b) Galvanometer can be converted into a voltmeter of range V = 10 V by connecting a high resistance R, in series, the high resistance is given as\nab.co\nR\n- R2\n10V\nputting values R",
