        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 3",
        "theory": "A coil wounded in the form of a spiral (helix) forms a solenoid. When current is passed through a solenoid a reasonably uniform magnetic field can be produced. This field is strong along the axis of solenoid and weaker outside as shown in figure 13.12.\nThe magnetic field lines surrounding a loosely wound solenoid, shows that these lines in the interior are nearly parallel to one another, are uniformly distributed, and are close together. For a tightly wound solenoid the field in the interior space is very uniform and strong. The field lines resemble those of a bar magnet, meaning that the solenoid effectively has north and south poles. As the length of the solenoid increases, the interior field becomes more uniform and the exterior field becomes weaker as shown in figure 13.13.\nAn ideal solenoid is approached when the turns are closely spaced and the length is much greater than the radius of the turns. In this case, the external field is close to zero and the interior field is uniform over a great volume.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nFIGURE 13.12\nNOT FOR SALE\nTMILONAI\n99\n(a) Solenoid (b) Iron fillings in a magnetic field due to a single loop of coll (c) iron fillings in a magnetic field due to a solenoid\nFIGURE 13.13\n(a)\nCO\n(b)\nN\nExterior\ninterior\nS\n(a) The magnetic field lines for a loosely wound solenoid and (b) magnetic field lines for a tightly wound solenoid, carrying a steady current.\nWe can use Ampère&#x27;s law to obtain a quantitative expression for the interior magnetic field in an ideal solenoid.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nConsider an closed Amperian path a-b-c-d, with lengths I, I, I, and L, where l, is inside the solenoid and I, outside the solenoid as shown in the figure 13.14. By Ampere Circuital law\nΣB•Al = Ho!\nSince there are four length elements, therefore\nB=L+B=12+B=1, +B+l, H2!\nNOT-FOR SALE\nFIGURE 13.14\n100\nMagnetic field of Solenoid\nor Bl, cose,+Bl2 cose, + Bl, cose, + Bl, cose1 = μl\nfrom the figure 8, 0°, 8, 90°, 6, 180°, and 8,270°, hence\nBi, cos0° + Bl2 cos 90° + Bl, cos180° + Bl, cos 270° = μ2/\nsince cos 0&quot;= 1, cos 90= 0, cos 180°= -1 and cos 270° 0, and since field is weak outside and for ideal solenoid can be taken as zero hence at I,, B=0, therefore\nBl, +0+0+0=μ2l\nor\nBl1 = H Let I, = [\nBI = Hol\nFor N number of turns in a solenoid, the above equation can be written as\nN\nBl=Nμl or\nB=\nHol\nFIGURE 13.15\nRIGHT-HAND RULE III FOR A\nSOLENOID: Curl the fingers in the\nvaz\ndirection of current around the coil or solenoid and the extended thumb will point in the direction of north pole of a magnet. This rule is for direction of conventional current or flow of positive charges. For electronic current flow the same rule is applied but with left hand.\nElectric current\nElectric current\nThe right-hand rule (III) for a solenoid\nPHYSICS-XII\nin\n5\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT FOR SALE\n101\nLet n be the number of turns per unit length (n= N / I), we can write the equation as\nB = nμ2!\n13.5\nThe equation 13.5 can be used to determine the magnetic field of a solenoid.\nEXAMPLE 13.3\nDOUBLE LAYERED SOLENOID\nA solenoid is 10 cm long and is wound with two layers of wire. The inner layer has 50 turns and the outer layer has 40 turns. A current of 3A flows in both layers in the same direction. What is the magnitude of magnetic flux density along the axis of solenoid?\nGIVEN\nnote ==\nLength of a solenoid &#x27;L&#x27; 10 cm = 0.01 m\nTurns of inner layer &#x27;n, 50 turns\nTurns of outer layer &#x27;n,&#x27; = 40 turns\nCurrent &#x27;I&#x27; - 3A\nSOLUTION\nREQUIRED\nMagnetic flux density &#x27;B&#x27; = ?\nThe net magnetic field from inner and outer layer of the solenoid is\nBret = B1+B2\nTo determine the magnetic field of a solenoid, put equation 13.5 we get\nB\n·Hol+\n- Hol\n50",
