        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 11",
        "theory": "1002\n1x101 A\ntherefore R1 = 9,900\n13.9 AVO METER/ MULTI-METER\nAnswer\nA single device which can measure current, voltage and resistance is called AVO meter. Where A stands for amperes, V stands for volts and O stands for ohm. As we know that galvanometer can be converted into ammeter and voltmeter by adding resistors therefore AVO meter is also combination of resistor networks and\na galvanometer. A single galvanometer is added in a circuitry in such a way that all the three quantities can be measured. All the three quantities also has range of values which can also be selected through switch. AVO meter is also capable of measuring alternating quantities as well. AVO meter and its circuit is shown in figure 13.28.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nFIGURE 13.28\nNOT FOR SALE\n10\nG\n118\nSelector Switch\nVoltage\nmeasurement circuit\nCurrent measurement circuit\nResistance\nmeasurement\ncircuit.\n(a) AVO meter (b) Internal circuit\nFor your information\nMAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING\nMagnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) uses a combination of a strong magnetic field and radio waves to produce high resolution images of the interior of the body.\nThe human body is composed primarily of fat and water, it is made up mostly of hydrogen atoms. Hydrogen protons within the body align with the magnetic field.\nBy applying short radio frequency (RF) pulses to a specific anatomical slice, the protons in the slice absorb energy at this resonant frequency, causing them to spin perpendicular to the magnetic field. As the protons relax back into alignment with the magnetic field, a signal is received\nby an RF coil. This signal acts as an antenna, and is processed by a computer to produce diagnostic images of areas of the body.\nRadio Frequency\nCoil\nGradient\nCoil\nMagnet\nvazn\nPHYSICS-XI\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nA. CURRENT MEASUREMENT\nTo measure current the selector switch is turned to measure ampere and proper scale is selected. This circuit contains resistors R,, R., .. of various resistance connected in parallel across the galvanometer as shown in the figure 13.29. These resistance are collectively called universal shunt. Any one of the shunt can be used to measure current in different ranges without making excessive current to flow through the galvanometer.\nB. VOLTAGE MEASUREMENT\nTo measure voltage the selector switch is turned to measure volts and proper scale is selected. This circuit contains resistors R,, R. of various resistances connected in series with the galvanometer as shown in the figure 13.30. The specific range is selected by adding proper resistance for measurement of specific voltage.\nC. RESISTANCE MEASUREMENT To measure voltage the selector switch is turned to measure ohms. The leads\nare connected across the resistance to be\nmeasured as shown in figure 13.31. The battery supplies the current to the galvanometer for deflection. Since the amount of current through the galvanometer now depends upon the external resistance the scale can be calibrated in ohms.\nNOT-FOR SALE\n119\nFIGURE 13.29\nG\nR,\nwww\nR1\nRange selector\nwww\nR1\nR\noff\nLead\nAVO meter switch turned to current measurement\nFIGURE 13.30\nG\nR,\nR,\nRange selector\nR,\nww\nR.\noff\nLeads\nAVO meter switch turned to voltage measurement\nFIGURE 13.31\nLead\nPHYSICS-XII\nG\nBattery\nAVO meter switch turned to resistance measurement\nLead\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM.\nNOT-FOR SALE 120\nhe amount of deflection in the galvanometer will now show the resistance rectly. The ohm-meter reading does not depends upon the polarity of the erminal leads, because the direction of current through the galvanometer is etermined by the polarity of the battery.\nI. DIGITAL MULTI-METERS\nModern multimeters come with digital FIGURE 13.32 lisplay, accurate, reliable, durable",
