        "theory": "A. maximum B. minimum\nThe Earths northern magnetic pole acts like:\nA. the north pole of a magnet\nD. infinite\nB. the south pole of a magnet\nC. it has a positive charge\nD. it has a negative charge\nThe pole pieces of the magnet in galvanometer are made concave to\nmake the field\nA. Radial\nB. Stronger\nC. Weaker D. Both A &amp; B\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT FOR SALE\n123\n11 When a small resistance is connected parallel to galvanometer, the\nresulting circuit behaves as\nA. Voltmeter B.Ammeter C. Velocity Selector D. AVO meter\n12 To measure the voltage the voltmeter is connected with the circuit in\nA. series B. parallel\nC. Perpendicular\n13 The resistance of ideal voltmeter is\nA. Small\nB. Large\nC. Zero\nThe torque in the coil can be increased by increasing\nD. Straight Line\nD. Infinite\nA. Number of turns\nC. Area of coil\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS\nB. Current and magnetic field\nD. All of these\n1 A compass needle is deflected when a charged plastic rod is held near it.\nWhat is the origin of the force that produces the deflection?\n2 What is the difference between permeability and permittivity?\nCan the objects in rotational motion create a magnetic field? Provide an example.\nElectron and proton are projected with the same velocity normal to the magnetic field. Which one will suffer greater deflection? Why?\n5 A charged particle moves in a straight line through a particular region of\nspace. Could there be a non-zero magnetic field in this region?\n6 If a current is passed through an unstretched spring, will the spring\ncontract or expand?\n7 How can neutrons be accelerated in a cyclotron?\n8 Acurrent carrying loop, free to turn, is placed in a uniform magnetic field\nB. What will be its orientation relative to B, in the equilibrium state? How does a current carrying coil behave like a bar magnet?\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nCOMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS\nNOT FOR SALE\n124\nDerive an expression for the force acting on a current carrying conductor in a uniform magnetic field.\nA current carrying loop is placed in a uniform magnetic field. Derive an equation for the torque acting on it.\nDoes a moving charge experiences a force in magnetic field? Explain\nHow charge to mass ratio for electron is determined using magnetic field? Define and explain magnetic flux.\nState Ampere&#x27;s law and use it to derive an expression for the magnetic field of a solenoid.\nWhat is galvanometer? How it is converted into an ammeter and voltmeter?\nNUMERICAL PROBLEMS\nAt what distance from a long straight wire carrying a current of 10 A is the magnetic field is equal to the earth&#x27;s magnetic field of 5 × 10°T?\n(Answer: 0.04 m)\nA long solenoid having 1000 turns uniformly distributed over a length of 0.5 m produces a magnetic field of 2.5 x 10&#x27; T at the center. Find the\n(Answer: 1A) current in the solenoid.\nA proton moving at right angles to a magnetic field of 0.1 T experiences a force of 2 x 10&quot; N. What is the speed of the proton?\n(Answer: 1.3 x 10 m/s) An 8 MeV proton enters perpendicularly into a uniform magnetic field of 2.5 T. (a) Find the force on the electron (b) What will be the radius of the path of proton?\n(Answer: 1.6 x 10&quot; N, 0.17m) What is the time period of an electron projected into a uniform magnetic field of 10 mT and moves in a circle of radius 6 cm?\n(Answer: 3.6 ns)\nn\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT FOR SALE\n125\n6A rectangular coil with 250 turns is 6.0 cm long and 4.0 cm wide. When the coil is placed in a magnetic field of 0.25 T, its maximum torque is 0.20 Nm. What is the current in the coil?\n(Answer: 1.5A)\n7 Crossed electric and magnetic fields are established over a certain region. The magnetic field is 0.105 T and the electric field is 2.00 x 105 V/m. An electron, experiences zero net force from these fields and so continues moving in a straight line. What is the electron&#x27;s speed?\n(Answer: 1.9x 10 m/s)\n8 The full scale deflection for a galvanometer is 10 mA. Its resistance is\n1000. How can it be converted into an ammeter of range 100 A?\n(Answer: R, -0.010)\n(Answer: R,, = 19000)\n9 How a 5 mA, 100 galvanometer is converted into 20 V voltmeter?",
