        "problem": "EXAMPLE 13.1\nMAGNETIC FORCE ON WIRE\nA wire carrying 2 A current and has length of 10 cm between the poles of a magnet is kept at an angle of 30° to the uniform field of 0.6 T. Find the force acting on the wire?\nGIVEN\nCurrent 'I' = 2A\nLength 'L' = 10 cm = 0.01 m\nMagnetic field 'B' -0.6 T\nAngle '0' = 30°\nSOLUTION\nREQUIRED\nMagnetic Force 'F,' =?\nThe force on a current carrying wire in magnetic field is given by\nF2 = ILBsine\nPutting values\nN\nF = 2×0.1×0.6-\nxsin 30°\nAp\ntherefore\nF = 0.06 N\nAnswer\nPHYSICS-XII\nNOT-FOR SALE\n94\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\n13.3 MAGNETIC FLUX\nThe scalar product of magnetic induction B and the area A, is called magnetic flux\n4, = B • Ã = BAcose\nMagnetic induction tells us how close together magnetic field lines are, as it tells us the strength of the magnetic field. The number of magnetic field lines passing through a surface placed perpendicular to it is called magnetic flux.\nA. Maximum Flux: If area vector A is parallel to the magnetic field B, then 'maximum number of magnetic field lines will pass through the surface. Therefore magnetic flux will be maximum,\n•1 = BA cos 0°\nsince cos 0° = 1 therefore, = BA\n0,= BA—\n1\nB. Minimum Flux: If area vector A is perpendicular to the magnetic field B, then no magnetic field lines will pass through the surface. Therefore magnetic flux will be zero or minimum,\n0,- BA cos 90°\nsince cos 90° = 0 therefore\n-\n2\nC. Flux at any Angle: If area vector A is making any angle with magnetic field B, then magnetic flux will depend upon the angle\nFIGURE 13.8\n-BA\nB\n10\nbetween them,\nΦ.\n, = BA cos\n0\n3\nA\nMagnetic flux\n0\nin\nB\n0,= BAcose\nMAGNETIC FLUX THROUGH CURVED OR NON-UNIFORM MAGNETIC FIELD If the magnetic field is non-uniform or the area is curved. Then, we divide the whole curved path into small patches each of area AA, such that they are approximately flat and magnetic field is almost uniform over it as shown in figure 13.9.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT FOR SALE\n95\nFor one of these small patches the magnetic flux is\n=BAA, BAA, cose\nTo calculate the flux through whole surface, we add the flux from each patch, such that\nFIGURE 13.9\nAA,\nB\nAA\nΣΑΦ\nBcose AA=BA cose\n13.3\n0,- 8,44, cose\nΣΑΜΑ\nMal\nUNIT OF MAGNETIC FLUX AND FLUX DENSITY\nThe SI unit of magnetic flux is equal to the unit of magnetic field (1 T) times the unit of area (m2), 1 Wb = 1 Tx m2. This unit is called the weber (1 Wb), in honor of the German physicist Wilhelm Weber.\n1 Wb = 1 Tx m2 (1T-1 N/A m)\nSo 1 Wb = 1 N m/A\nThe unit of magnetic induction can be written as TWb m\n13.4: AMPERE CIRCUITAL LAW\nAmpere's circuital law, given by Andre Marie Peter Ampere in 1826, relates the integrated magnetic field in a loop around a current carrying wire to the current passing through the wire.\nAmpère's circuital law states that, for any closed loop, the sum of the length element multiplied by the component of magnetic field parallel to each element i proportional to the current enclosed by the path.\nΣΒΔΙΝ\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT-FOR SALE\nAmpere law is the relation between magnetic AMPERE (1775-1836) field and current near a current carrying conductor. When no current is present in the wire, all compass needles point in the same direction (toward the Earth's north pole). When the wire carries a strong current, the compass needles deflect in a direction tangent to the circle, which is the direction of the magnetic field created by the current. Now consider a test point P located at distance r from the current carrying conductor, where we want to find the magnitude of magnetic induction B as shown in figure 13.10. At point \"P\", the magnetic induction is directly proportional to the current '/' flowing through the conductor and inversely proportional to the distance 'r' from the\nwire..\nΒα\nог B = Hox!\nπ Γ\n2π\nAmpère, a Frenchman, is credited with the discovery of\nelectromagnetism – the relationship between electric currents and magnetic fields.\nWhere μ,/2m is the constant of proportionality, and ',' is the permeability of a vacuum and its value is 4m x 10' Tm/A or 4π x 10\" Wb/A m. Since, the sum of all the length elements Al gives the circumference of circle 2mг,\nFIGURE 13.10\n1=0\nΣΔΙ = 231\n2\n96\nin\nawaze\nAmpere's circuital law\n(a)\n(b)\nΔΙ\nPHYSICS-XII\n(c)\nΔΙ\nP\nNOT-FOR SALE\n98\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nSOLUTION\nThe net magnetic field is Bet=B,+B2\nas fields are opposite in the middle, therefore Bnet =B-B2\nApplying Ampere Circuital Law Bet Ho\n4\n12\nНо\nputting values Bret =4x10-7\n7Tp 8A\n4× 10-7\nTm 2A\nA 20.03\nA 20.03 p\ntherefore\nBnet = 4×10-ST\nAnswer\nMAGNETIC FIELD OF A LONG WIRE\nASSIGNMENT 13.1\nA long straight wire carries a current of 1 A. Find the magnitude of the magnetic field at a distance 1 m from it.\n(Answer: 2 × 101T)\nA. MAGNETIC FIELD DUE TO A CURRENT CARRYING SOLENOID\nA coil wounded in the form of a spiral (helix) forms a solenoid. When current is passed through a solenoid a reasonably uniform magnetic field can be produced. This field is strong along the axis of solenoid and weaker outside as shown in figure 13.12.\nThe magnetic field lines surrounding a loosely wound solenoid, shows that these lines in the interior are nearly parallel to one another, are uniformly distributed, and are close together. For a tightly wound solenoid the field in the interior space is very uniform and strong. The field lines resemble those of a bar magnet, meaning that the solenoid effectively has north and south poles. As the length of the solenoid increases, the interior field becomes more uniform and the exterior field becomes weaker as shown in figure 13.13.\nAn ideal solenoid is approached when the turns are closely spaced and the length is much greater than the radius of the turns. In this case, the external field is close to zero and the interior field is uniform over a great volume.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nFIGURE 13.12\nNOT FOR SALE\nTMILONAI\n99\n(a) Solenoid (b) Iron fillings in a magnetic field due to a single loop of coll (c) iron fillings in a magnetic field due to a solenoid\nFIGURE 13.13\n(a)\nCO\n(b)\nN\nExterior\ninterior\nS\n(a) The magnetic field lines for a loosely wound solenoid and (b) magnetic field lines for a tightly wound solenoid, carrying a steady current.\nWe can use Ampère's law to obtain a quantitative expression for the interior magnetic field in an ideal solenoid.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nConsider an closed Amperian path a-b-c-d, with lengths I, I, I, and L, where l, is inside the solenoid and I, outside the solenoid as shown in the figure 13.14. By Ampere Circuital law\nΣB•Al = Ho!\nSince there are four length elements, therefore\nB=L+B=12+B=1, +B+l, H2!\nNOT-FOR SALE\nFIGURE 13.14\n100\nMagnetic field of Solenoid\nor Bl, cose,+Bl2 cose, + Bl, cose, + Bl, cose1 = μl\nfrom the figure 8, 0°, 8, 90°, 6, 180°, and 8,270°, hence\nBi, cos0° + Bl2 cos 90° + Bl, cos180° + Bl, cos 270° = μ2/\nsince cos 0\"= 1, cos 90= 0, cos 180°= -1 and cos 270° 0, and since field is weak outside and for ideal solenoid can be taken as zero hence at I,, B=0, therefore\nBl, +0+0+0=μ2l\nor\nBl1 = H Let I, = [\nBI = Hol\nFor N number of turns in a solenoid, the above equation can be written as\nN\nBl=Nμl or\nB=\nHol\nFIGURE 13.15\nRIGHT-HAND RULE III FOR A\nSOLENOID: Curl the fingers in the\nvaz\ndirection of current around the coil or solenoid and the extended thumb will point in the direction of north pole of a magnet. This rule is for direction of conventional current or flow of positive charges. For electronic current flow the same rule is applied but with left hand.\nElectric current\nElectric current\nThe right-hand rule (III) for a solenoid\nPHYSICS-XII\nin\n5\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nNOT FOR SALE\n101\nLet n be the number of turns per unit length (n= N / I), we can write the equation as\nB = nμ2!\n13.5\nThe equation 13.5 can be used to determine the magnetic field of a solenoid.",
