        "problem": "EXAMPLE 13.4\nTIME PERIOD FOR ELECTRON IN MAGNETIC FIELD\nThe path of an electron in a uniform magnetic field of flux density 1.0x101Tina vacuum is a circle of radius 1 cm. Calculate the period of its orbit?\nGIVEN\nRadius 'r' = 1 cm = 1 x 102 m\nREQUIRED\nmagnetic field 'B' = 1.0 x 10 T\nTime period T =?\nElectron Charge 'q,' = 1.6 × 10\" C\nElectron mass 'm,' = 9.11 x 10\" kg\nSOLUTION\nFrom circular trajectory of charged particle, the time period is\nT=\n2π m qB\n1\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nilab.\nPutting values\nNOT FOR SALE\nT-2×3.14×9.11×10-31 kg 1.6×10-19 Cx1×102 T\n10:\ntherefore\nT = 3.57 x 10 s = 3.57 ns\nAnswer\nASSIGNMENT 13.3\nVELOCITY SELECTOR\nA velocity selector in a mass spectrometer uses a 0.100 T magnetic field. What electric field strength is needed to select a speed of 4.00x10 m/s?\n(Answer: 4.00 x 105 N/C)\n13.7 TORQUE ON A CURRENT CARRYING COIL\nFIGURE 13.22\nWhen a current carrying coil or loop is placed in uniform magnetic field i experience a force, this force can exert torque on the coil or loop of wire. Let us consider a rectangular coil carrying a current I in the presence of a uniform magnetic field B directed parallel to the plane of the loop as shown in Figure 13.22. No magnetic forces act on sides 1 and 3 because these wires are parallel to the field = 0; the length of sides 1 and 3 is b, therefore magnetic force is\nF1 = F1 = Blbsin0°\nsince sin0° = 0\n72\nB\nb-\nTherefore F1 = F1 = 0\n1\nA coil in a magnetic field\nHowever maximum magnetic forces act on sides 2 and 4, because these sides are perpendicular to the field = 90\"; the length of sides 2 and 4 is a, therefore magnetic force is\nF2 = F1 = BIL sin 90°\nsince sin 90° 1 Therefore F2 = F1 = Bla\n2\nBy applying Right hand rule II or Fleming's left Hand rule it can be seen that the forces on sides 2 and 4 (F, and F,) are such that they produce a net torque.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM\nIf we view the loop from side 3 and sight along sides 2 and 4, we see the view shown in Figure 13.23. The two magnetic forces F, and F., that the two forces point in opposite directions such that they form a couple. If the loop is pivoted so that it can rotate about point 0, these two forces produce a torque that rotates the loop (in this case clockwise). The magnitude of this torque is\nNOT FOR SALE 110\nFIGURE 13.23\n20\n0\nH-b/2-H\nSide view of a coil in a magnetic field\nputting values of F, and F, from equation 2 in equation 3, we get\nb\nb\nt = IBa=+lBa\nor\nΤ\nt = 2/Ba\n2\nTherefore t = IBab\nSince a is the length and b is the width of the coil, therefore\nab = A\n5\nputting equation 5 in equation 4, we get\nt = IBA\n13.11\nחו\nEquation 13.11 represents the maximum condition for torque on a current carrying loop of wire.\nNow suppose the uniform magnetic field B makes an FIGURE 13.24\nangle 8 < 90' with a line perpendicular to the plane\nof the loop as in Figure 13.24.\nThe torque is given by relation\nt = IBAsine\n13.12\nF2\nकाल\n14\n2\ne\nB\n42\n-sine\nPHYSICS-XII\nTorque at an angle < 90°\nUNIT 13 ELECTROMAGNETISM",
