        "theory": "EXAMPLE 14.3\nA loop of resistance 0.12 is placed in a magnetic field of 2T. If conductor of length 0.2m is sliding along a loop with a velocity of 0.2ms&quot;. Find (a) the e.m.f. produced in the conductor if the motion of a conductor is perpendicular to the field (b) the current through the loop (c) the electrical power generated (d) the input mechanical power.\nGIVEN\nresistance R= 0.12, magnetic field B= 2T, length of conductor (= 0.2m velocity of conductor v=0.2ms&quot;\nPHYSICS-XII\nn\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n137\nREQUIRED\n(a) the e.m.f. produced in the conductor E=?,\n(b) current through the loop I=?,\n(c) power P1=? and\n(d) input mechanical power P2=?\nSOLUTION\n(a)\nThe motional e.m.fЄ\nVBL = 0.2 × 2 × 0.2 = 0.08V\n(b)\nThe current through the loop is == E/R\n0.08V\n-=0.8A\n0.152\n(c)\nElectrical power generated is = P1 = IV\n= 0.8A × 0.08V = 0.064W\nThe magnetic force exerted on the current carrying loop of length L is F = BIL = 2 T x 0.8A x 0.2m = 0.32 N\n(d) The power necessary to move the loop against magnetic force F is\nP2 = Fv = 0.32N x 0.2ms1 = 0.064W\n14.4.2\nSELF-INDUCED e.m.f.\nWe know that in previous article a changing magnetic flux was due to external source i.e. magnet. The relative movement between magnet and coil which causes induced current and hence induced e.m.f. in a coil.\nBut this time the e.m.f. induced in the coil is due to change of the current in the same coil. To explain phenomenon of self inductance consider a coil connected to a battery through a rheostat as shown in figure 14.6. By changing the resistance in the circuit, the current through the coil changes and the magnetic flux through the coil also changes as a result an e.m.f. will be induced in the coil itself. It is concluded that when current in a coil changes, the self-induced e.m.f. opposes the change of current in the same coil which is known as self-inductance or inductance.\nThe e.m.f. induced in a coil due to the change of its own flux linked with it is called self-induced e.m.f.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nFIGURE 14.6\nColl\nRheostat\nLilit\nG\nBattery\nNOT FOR SALE\n138\nSelf-induced e.m.f in a coil\nIn order to find the expression for magnitude of self-induced e.m.f. and self- inductance consider a coil of N turns carrying a current I. When the current in the coil changes then, the flux linking of the coil will also change. This will set up a self-induced e.m.f. E in the coil is given by;\nE = N\nAD ΔΦ A\nAt At\n(NO)\nThe flux linkages due to N turns of coil is proportional to current /\n(NO) ∞ I\nE\nAt\nবাধ\nawaz e in\nE= constantx\nΔΙ Δ\nΔΙ\nε= LA! === (L)\nAt At\n(1)\nWhere L is a constant called self-inductance or inductance of the coil. The unit of inductance is henry (H). Putting &amp; 1V, A/At 1 A/s, in Eq. (1) then L = 1 H.\nAlso we know that\nε = = (ND)\n(2)\nΔΙ\nComparing Eq(1) and Eq (2) we have\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n139\nLI-NO\n14.3\nOr\nL= NO\n14.4.3.1 FACTORS AFFECTING INDUCTANCE\nInductors play a great role in electronic, electrical, electromechanical, wireless circuits etc. The inductance of a coil (figure 14.7) depends upon the following factors:\n(1)\n(ii)",
        "content": "EXAMPLE 14.3<br>A loop of resistance 0.12 is placed in a magnetic field of 2T. If conductor of length 0.2m is sliding along a loop with a velocity of 0.2ms&quot;. Find (a) the e.m.f. produced in the conductor if the motion of a conductor is perpendicular to the field (b) the current through the loop (c) the electrical power generated (d) the input mechanical power.<br>GIVEN<br>resistance R= 0.12, magnetic field B= 2T, length of conductor (= 0.2m velocity of conductor v=0.2ms&quot;<br>PHYSICS-XII<br>n<br>UNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>137<br>REQUIRED<br>(a) the e.m.f. produced in the conductor E=?,<br>(b) current through the loop I=?,<br>(c) power P1=? and<br>(d) input mechanical power P2=?<br>SOLUTION<br>(a)<br>The motional e.m.fЄ<br>VBL = 0.2 × 2 × 0.2 = 0.08V<br>(b)<br>The current through the loop is == E/R<br>0.08V<br>-=0.8A<br>0.152<br>(c)<br>Electrical power generated is = P1 = IV<br>= 0.8A × 0.08V = 0.064W<br>The magnetic force exerted on the current carrying loop of length L is F = BIL = 2 T x 0.8A x 0.2m = 0.32 N<br>(d) The power necessary to move the loop against magnetic force F is<br>P2 = Fv = 0.32N x 0.2ms1 = 0.064W<br>14.4.2<br>SELF-INDUCED e.m.f.<br>We know that in previous article a changing magnetic flux was due to external source i.e. magnet. The relative movement between magnet and coil which causes induced current and hence induced e.m.f. in a coil.<br>But this time the e.m.f. induced in the coil is due to change of the current in the same coil. To explain phenomenon of self inductance consider a coil connected to a battery through a rheostat as shown in figure 14.6. By changing the resistance in the circuit, the current through the coil changes and the magnetic flux through the coil also changes as a result an e.m.f. will be induced in the coil itself. It is concluded that when current in a coil changes, the self-induced e.m.f. opposes the change of current in the same coil which is known as self-inductance or inductance.<br>The e.m.f. induced in a coil due to the change of its own flux linked with it is called self-induced e.m.f.<br>PHYSICS-XII<br>UNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION<br>FIGURE 14.6<br>Coll<br>Rheostat<br>Lilit<br>G<br>Battery<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>138<br>Self-induced e.m.f in a coil<br>In order to find the expression for magnitude of self-induced e.m.f. and self- inductance consider a coil of N turns carrying a current I. When the current in the coil changes then, the flux linking of the coil will also change. This will set up a self-induced e.m.f. E in the coil is given by;<br>E = N<br>AD ΔΦ A<br>At At<br>(NO)<br>The flux linkages due to N turns of coil is proportional to current /<br>(NO) ∞ I<br>E<br>At<br>বাধ<br>awaz e in<br>E= constantx<br>ΔΙ Δ<br>ΔΙ<br>ε= LA! === (L)<br>At At<br>(1)<br>Where L is a constant called self-inductance or inductance of the coil. The unit of inductance is henry (H). Putting &amp; 1V, A/At 1 A/s, in Eq. (1) then L = 1 H.<br>Also we know that<br>ε = = (ND)<br>(2)<br>ΔΙ<br>Comparing Eq(1) and Eq (2) we have<br>PHYSICS-XII<br>UNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>139<br>LI-NO<br>14.3<br>Or<br>L= NO<br>14.4.3.1 FACTORS AFFECTING INDUCTANCE<br>Inductors play a great role in electronic, electrical, electromechanical, wireless circuits etc. The inductance of a coil (figure 14.7) depends upon the following factors:<br>(1)<br>(ii)",
