        "theory": "(iii)\nshape and number of turns (N). the material of the core.\nFIGURE 14.7\ncoil area and length The greater the magnetic permeability of the core which the coil is wrapped around, the greater the inductance\ni.e., by\nsubstituting an iron core with air the inductance\nincreases.\nila\nCoil\n14.4.3\nMUTUALLY INDUCTANCE\nIn order to demonstrate the phenomenon of mutually induced e.m.f. let us place two coils A and B adjacent to each other as shown in Figure 14.8. When the current flows in the coil A then there will be magnetic field produced around it. A part of the magnetic flux produced by coil A passes through or links with coil B. This flux which is common to both the coils A and B is called mutual flux(). The current in the coil A can be changed by a rheostat. A change in the current in coil A induces an e.m.f. in the coil B. The e.m.f. induced in coil A is called self-induced e.m.f. as already discussed. The e.m.f. induced in coil B is known as mutually induced e.m.f.\nThus this phenomenon of the linking flux of the coil A with the other coil B is called Mutual Inductance.\nThe\nproperty of two neighbouring coils to induce e.m.f. in one coil due to the change of current in the other is called mutual inductance.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n140\nMutually induced e.m.f. in coil B is directly proportional to the rate of change of current in coil A i.e.,\nΕκ\nR\nΔΙ\nAt\nE=-MAI\nA(MI)\nAt At\n(1)\nWhere, M is a constant called mutual inductance. The minus\nsign is an expression of Lenz&#x27;s law it show that\nthis e.m.f. will set up a\ncurrent which\nwill\noppose the change of\nflux linking with it.\nmutually\nThe direction of induced\ne.m.f. (by Lenz&#x27;s law) is always such as to oppose the cause producing it. The cause producing the mutually induced e.m.f. in coil B is the changing mutual flux produced by coil A. Hence the direction of induced current in coil B will be such that the flux set up by it will\noppose the changing mutual flux produced by coil A.\nFIGURE 14.8\nB\nA\nGalvanometer\nRheostat\nBattery\nMutually-induced e.m.f in a coil\ne/n\nThe SI unit for mutual inductance M is called the henry (H) in honor of Joseph Henry (1799-1878), an American scientist who discovered induced emf independently of Faraday. Thus, we have 1H-1V-s/A.\nPutting &amp; 1V, A/At 1 A/s, in Eq. (1) then M= 1 H.\nAlso we know that\nε=-=-= (ND)\n(2)\nAt\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nComparing Eq(1) and Eq (2) we have\nNO\nMI-Noor M=\nNOT FOR SALE\n141\n14.4\nLike capacitance, mutual inductance is a geometric quantity. It depends on the shapes and relative positions of the two coils, and it is independent of the currents in the coils.\nSTATICALLY INDUCED e.m.f.: it is defined as &quot;the e.m.f. induced! in a coil due to change of flux linked with it is called statically Induced e.m.f.&quot; In this case, the coils are stationary and the magnetic field is moving around the conductor and produces e.m.f. Transformer is an example of statically induced e.m.f.\nASSIGNMENT 14.2\nThe current flowing in the first coil changes from 2 A to 10 A in 0.4 s. Find the mutual inductance between two coils if an e.m.f. of 60 mV is induced in the second coil. Also determine the induced e.m.f. in the second coil if the current in the first coil is changed from 4 A to 16 A in 0.03 s. Consider only the magnitude of induced e.m.f.\nAnswer(3x10 H, 1.2V)\n14.4.4 EDDY CURRENTS Eddy currents are loops of electrical current induced within conductors by a changing magnetic field in the conductor, due to Faraday&#x27;s law of induction.\nDo you know?\nThe metal detector consists of the oscillator producing the alternating current which passes through a coil producing the alternating magnetic\nconductive metal is close to the coil, field. If the piece of electrically eddy currents will be induced in the metal and this produces the magnetic field of its own. Thus the metallic object is notified with the alarm.\nMagnetic field\nEddy currents are currents induced in Metal conductor moving in a magnetic field or metals that are exposed to a changing magnetic field.\nCoil\nEddy current\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n142\nEddy currents are created when a conductor passes through a magnetic field, which\ncreates FIGURE 14.9 opposing forces that spin inside the conductor. Eddy currents flows in planes perpendicular to the magnetic field shown figure. 14.9. The increasing field at point C induces a counterclockwise",
