        "theory": "as\nin\ncurrent. While the at decreasing field point D inducing a second eddy current in\nclockwise direction\nN\nS\nEddy current brake.\nin the conductor. According to Lenz&#x27;s law, an eddy current produces a magnetic field that is in opposition to the magnetic field that produced it, and therefore eddy currents are an inverse response to the source magnetic field. The two magnetic fields repel each other and a braking force results. This eddy current braking system is generally used in high speed trains and roller\ncoasters.\nProjects\nA bar magnet is dropped inside a long vertical tube. If the tube is made of metal, the magnet quickly approaches a terminal speed, but if the tube is made of cardboard, the magnet falls with constant acceleration. Explain why the magnet falls differently in the metal tube than it does in the cardboard tube ?\n14.5 GENERATING ELECTRICITY\nAlternating current generator\nThe ability to produce an electromotive force by changing the magnetic flux through a coil is used to generate electricity. The generation of electricity is the important application of electromagnetic induction.\nGenerator is a device which converts Mechanical energy into electrical energy.\nPHYSICS-XII\nin\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n143\nIt consists of 2 poles (north and south) of a magnet in order to have uniform magnetic field. There is a coil of rectangular shape CDEF also known\nas armature.\nIt also consists of Slip rings which produces alternating current as shown in figure 14.10. When the FIGURE 14.10\ncoil\nCDEF rotates between the poles of a magnet\nan\nelectromotive force is induced.\nWhen the side CD moves upwards\nFleming&#x27;s Right-hand Rules shows that the direction of the current is from C to D and E to F. Half revolution later FE will be in the position previously occupied by CD and the current direction is reversed, i.e. it is from F to E and D to C. Thus the direction of the induced electromotive force and the current changes every half revolution.\nSlip rings\nCoil\nArde\nD\nE\nF\nB1\nA1\n00000\nAlternating emf\nAO\nLoboog\nB2\nCarbon brushes\nAC generator\nThe graph of electromotive force against time is shown in Fig14.11. The time taken for one revolution T is called the period and frequency f, is the number of revolutions per second. Hence\n1\nT\nThe domestic electricity supply has a frequency of 50 hertz, which means that the generator makes fifty revolutions each second. Figure 14.11 shows the positions of the coil that correspond to various points on the graph.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n144 At position 1 the coil is at 0&quot;. The coil is moving parallel to the direction of the magnetic field and the magnetic flux is zero, so no e.m.f. is induced.\nFIGURE 14.11\nPosition 1 Position 2\nPosition 3\nPosition 4\nPosition 5\nD\nE\nInduced emf\n90°\n180°\n270°\n360&quot;\ntime\nDirection of Magnetic field\n4\nT\n3T4\nA coil rotation in a magnetic field\nShowing different orientations of coil rotation.\nAt position 2 the coil is at 90&quot;. The coil is moving perpendicular to the direction of the magnetic field and the magnetic flux is at its maximum hence induces maximum e.m.f.\nAt position 3 the coil is at 180&quot;. The coil is moving parallel to the direction of the magnetic field and the magnetic flux is zero, so no e.m.f. is induced.\nAt position 4 the coil is at 270. The coil is moving perpendicular to the direction of the magnetic field hence the induced e.m.f. is at its maximum. In this case the direction of induced e.m.f. is opposite to what it was at position\n2.\nAt position 5 the coil is at 360°. The coil is moving parallel to the direction of the magnetic field, hence no e.m.f. is induced. At this position coil has completed full rotation and back to its starting point.\nn\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE",
