        "title": "14.7 TRANSFORMERS",
        "theory": "14.7 TRANSFORMERS\nA transformer is a device which is used to transform electrical power from one voltage and current level to another. The transformer depends on the use of alternating current. There are three main parts of a transformer: primary coil, secondary coil and a FIGURE 14.13 laminated iron core connecting them\nas shown in figure 14.13.\nThe primary coil is connected to the input electrical supply, which has to be AC to work properly. An AC current through the coil creates a changing magnetic field which is concentrated through the iron core.\nN\nLaminated core\nCurrent passing through the primary Step up transformer coil creates a magnetic field. The primary and secondary coils are\nlaminated core.\nN1\nwith\nwrapped around a core of high magnetic permeability, such as iron, so that most of the magnetic flux passes through both the primary and secondary coils. The changing magnetic flux through the primary coil gives rise to an induced e.m.f. V, in the secondary coil.\nThe voltage induced across the secondary coil is given by Faraday&#x27;s law of induction, which states that:\nΔΦ\nV1 = N,\nAt\nPHYSICS-XII\nin\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n149\nSince the same magnetic flux passes through both the primary and secondary coils in an ideal transformer, the voltage across the primary is\nΔΕ\nΔΕ\nTaking the ratio of the two equations for V, and V, gives the basic equation for stepping up or stepping down the voltage\nVN V N\n14.8\nFIGURE 14.14\nLaminated core\nWhere\nV primary voltage,\nV1 = secondary voltage, N,\nturns.\nprimary\nIf NN, then VV, and the transformer Single lamination\nis called a step-up transformer. When\nN,&lt;N, it follows that V,&lt;V, and it is called\nLaminated iron core\na step-down transformer. The amount of energy cannot be stepped up or down. The efficiency of the ideal transformer is 100%. So,\nilab\nV\nV11 = Vplp\n14.9\nIt follows that if the voltage is stepped up the current is stepped down and vice versa. If a high voltage is required a step-up transformer is used, whereas if a high current is required a step-down transformer is used.\nTransformer cores are made FIGURE 14.15\nbut\nof laminated iron, that is, many thin sheets of iron pressed together separated by thin insulating. layers. This limits the circulation of any eddy currents to the thickness of one lamina, rather than the whole core, thus reducing the overall heating effect.\nо\nSolid core with high eddy currents\nEddy currents in an laminated iron core.\nCCCCC\nLaminated core with\nlow eddy currents ordinary iron core, and a\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n150\nThe long thick cables of transmission lines are made of copper or aluminum because they have a low resistance. High voltage transmission lines carry electricity long distances to a substation. The power lines go into Grid station near factories and towns. From these Grid stations, electricity in different power levels is used. Here transformers change the very high voltage electricity back into lower voltage electricity. The voltage is eventually reduced to 220 volts for larger appliances, like stoves and clothes dryers, and 110 volts for lights, TVs and other smaller appliances.\nFIGURE 14.16\nLong distances transmission lines\nDam\nGrid station\nGenerator\nTransformer\nen\nTown\nTransformers enable efficient long distance high voltage transmission of\nelectric energy.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nKEY\nNOT FOR SALE\n151\nSelf-induction: The change of magnetic flux due to a variation in the current flowing in the same circuit.\nMutual induction: The change of magnetic flux due to a variation in the current flowing in the another circuit.\nLenz&#x27;s law: the induced current will flow in such a direction that it opposes the cause that produce it.\nDynamically induced e.m.f. When the conductor is moved in at stationary magnetic field in such a way that the flux linking it changes in magnitude.\nStatically induced e.m.f.: When the conductor is stationary and the magnetic field is moving or changing.\nEddy currents the currents induced in conductor moving in a magnetic field or metals that are exposed to a changing magnetic field.\nGenerator which converts Mechanical energy into electrical energy.\nTransformer: a device which is used to transform electrical power from one voltage and current level to another.",
