        "sciNote": "10000000\nDirection of movement\n(d)\n0000000\na\nMagnetic flux linking the coil\nn\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n133\n14.3.1 LENZ'S LAW AND CONSERVATION OF ENERGY\nLenz's law is a consequence of the law of conservation of energy. In previous example, when the N-pole of the magnet is approaching the coil, the repulsive force acts on the bar magnet due to the current induced in the coil.\nThe result is that the motion of the magnet is opposed. The mechanical energy spent in overcoming this opposition is converted into electrical energy which appears in the coil. Thus Lenz's law is consistent with the law of conservation of energy.\nEXAMPLE 14.1\nAt what rate would it be necessary for a single conductor to cut the flux in order that a current of 1.2 mA flows through it when 10 resistor is connected across its ends?\nGIVEN\nCurrent =/=1.2 mA, Resistor R= ?\nREQUIRED\nSOLUTION\nTime rate of change of flux, A/At = ?\nΔΦ\nE=N\nAt\nHere E=IR=1.2x10x10-1.2x10 V;\nΔΦ ε 1.2×10-2\n=1.2x102 Wb/s\nAt N\n1\nEXAMPLE 14.2\nA coil of mean area 500 cm2 and having 1000 turns is held perpendicular to a uniform field of 0.4 gauss. The coil is turned through 180° in 1/10 s. Calculate the average induced e.m.f.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nGIVEN\nNOT FOR SALE-\n134\nWhen the plane of the coil is perpendicular to the field, 0=0°.\nWhen the coil is turned though 180°, 0 = 180°.\nTherefore, initial flux linked with the coil is P, NBA cose = NBA Flux linked with coil when turned through "
