        "sciNote": "X\nX X\nX\nenclosing a varying area\ndue to the motion of the\nX\nX X\nX\nX\nX X\nX\nrod MN.\nR\nN\nX\nX\nX\nX X\nX X\nX\nH\n-AX\nX,\nA conductor movement in a magnetic field\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\n135\nNOT FOR SALE When rod MN is moved from position x, to position x2 in small time interval At then there is a change in the flux linked with the conductor and a current / will start to flow in the circuit. Therefore, an induced e.m.f. is produced. This induced e.m.f. due to the motion of an electric conductor in the presence of the magnetic field is called motional e.m.f. The distance travelled by the rod in time At is X-X-AX. To find the magnitude of e.m.f. induced along the moving rod, we use Faraday's law of induction\nE=NAD\nAt\nIn this equation, N = 1 and the flux = BA cos 8. We have = 0° and cos 8 = 1, since B is perpendicular to A. Where change in flux ADA(BA) BAA, since B is uniform. The area enclosed by the circuit MNRS is AA = CA.\nPutting the values of N and AO into the above equation we get,\nBAA\nAt\nBCAX\nAt\nThe velocity of the rod is Ax/At = v. Thus\nilah\nE=Bev (B,L, and v perpendicular)\n.. AA = CAX\nThus, the e.m.f. generated in the circuit by the moving rod is simply the product of the magnetic field-strength, the length of the rod, and the velocity of the rod. This equation is valid as long as B, I and v are mutually perpendicular. If they are not, then their mutually perpendicular components are used. Motional e.m.f. is an example of dynamically induced e.m.f. It is so c"
