        "sciNote": "(iii)\nshape and number of turns (N). the material of the core.\nFIGURE 14.7\ncoil area and length The greater the magnetic permeability of the core which the coil is wrapped around, the greater the inductance\ni.e., by\nsubstituting an iron core with air the inductance\nincreases.\nila\nCoil\n14.4.3\nMUTUALLY INDUCTANCE\nIn order to demonstrate the phenomenon of mutually induced e.m.f. let us place two coils A and B adjacent to each other as shown in Figure 14.8. When the current flows in the coil A then there will be magnetic field produced around it. A part of the magnetic flux produced by coil A passes through or links with coil B. This flux which is common to both the coils A and B is called mutual flux(). The current in the coil A can be changed by a rheostat. A change in the current in coil A induces an e.m.f. in the coil B. The e.m.f. induced in coil A is called self-induced e.m.f. as already discussed. The e.m.f. induced in coil B is known as mutually induced e.m.f.\nThus this phenomenon of the linking flux of the coil A with the other coil B is called Mutual Inductance.\nThe\nproperty of two neighbouring coils to induce e.m.f. in one coil due to the change of current in the other is called mutual inductance.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n140\nMutually induced e.m.f. in coil B is directly proportional to the rate of change of current in coil A i.e.,\nΕκ\nR\nΔΙ\nAt\nE=-MAI\nA(MI)\nAt At\n(1)\nWhere, M is a constant called mutual inductance. The minus\nsign is an e"
