        "theory": "145\nThe induced electromotive force, is determined by the rate at which the number of flux linkages changes. The greater the angular velocity &amp; the coil will cut the lines of flux at a faster rate so more induced e.m.f. would be produced. Suppose the coil is rotating with angular velocity w and at any instant t, be the angle which the coil makes with the field B. If N is the number of turns in the coil then the flux through the coil of area A is\n= B.AN = NAB cos@\n= NAB cost\n..0= ot\nThe induced e.m.f is\nΔΦ\nE\nA (NAB cos out)\ncost)\nAt\nAt\nE=-NAB lim (cost)\nAs lim (cos out) A(cost)\n=-(@sin@t)\nAt\nAt-40 At\nNAB(@sinat)\n14.5\nlab\n£= E\nFor maximum value of induced e.m.f. sinwt-1, so Emax = NABO Hence induced e.m.f at any instant of time is:\nsinat\nE = E sin(2mft)\n14.6\nASSIGNMENT 14.3\nAn electric generator consists of 100 turns of wire formed into a rectangular loop 50 cm by 30 cm, placed entirely in a uniform magnetic field of 3.50 T. What is the maximum value of the e.m.f. produced when the loop is spun at 1000 rev/min about an axis perpendicular to B?\n14.6 AC MOTOR\nAnswer (5500 V)\nAn electric motor is a device that converts electrical energy into mechanical energy.\nAn AC motor has two basic electrical parts: a &quot;stator&quot; and a &quot;rotor&quot; as shown in Figure 14.12. The stator is the stationary part of the motor while rotor is the rotating part of the motor. The stator consists of a group of individual electro-magnets arranged in such a way that they form a hollow cylinder, with one pole of each magnet facing toward the center of the group.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nFIGURE 14.12\nNOT FOR SALE\n146\nStator\nRotator\nStator\nRotator\nAC Motor\nThe rotor, obviously, is located inside the stator and is mounted on the motor&#x27;s shaft. The objective of these motor components is to make the rotor rotate which in turn will rotate the motor shaft. It also consists of a group of electro- magnets arranged around a cylinder, with the poles facing toward the stator poles. When the stator windings are connected to an AC supply flux is produced in the air gap. The flux rotates at a fixed speed called synchronous speed. This rotating flux induces voltages in the stator and rotor winding.\nIf the rotor circuit is closed, the current flows through the rotor winding and react with the rotating flux and a torque is produced. In the steady state, the rotor rotates at speed very close to synchronous speed. Eddy currents in iron cores of motors can cause troublesome energy losses. These are usually minimized by constructing the cores out of thin, electrically insulated sheets of iron. The rotor consists of coils wound on a laminated iron armature mounted on an axle. Motors are used in a wide range of applications, such as fans, power tools, appliances, electric vehicles, and hybrid cars etc.\n14.6.1\nBACK e.m.f.\nWhen the coil of electric motor rotates in a magnetic field by applying it with a battery of potential difference V an induced e.m.f. £ is produced. This induced e.m.f. is produced in such a direction so as to oppose the e.m.f. V of battery which is known as back e.m.f. The magnitude of the back or counter e.m.f. is proportional to the product of the flux and the speed.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n147\nThe value of back e.m.f. (E) is always less than the applied voltage, thus there is difference between these two potentials. It is the difference between these two quantities which actually drives current through the resistance of the armature circuit. Thus we have\nV = ε + laRa\n14.7\nWhere R, is resistance of the armature circuit, l, is the current, E is the back e.m.f. and V is the applied voltage.\nThe generator output of a motor is the difference between the supply voltage and the back e.m.f.\nEXAMPLE:14.4\nAn AC motor has an internal resistance of 4 Q. When powered by a 50Hz AC supply of peak voltage 120V it draws a peak current of 5 A. 1. What is the peak back- e.m.f. produced by the motor? 2. What is the peak power delivered to the motor by the AC supply? 3. What is the peak rate of energy loss as heat in the motor?\nGIVEN\nresistance R= 40, f= 50Hz, current = 5 A peak voltage V=120V\nREQUIRED\n1. peak back-e.m.f.\n£ 1,\nand\n2. peak power Ppeak? 3. peak rate of energy loss Pheat=?\nFOR YOUR INFORMATION\n(a)Elevator in the form of load\n(b) Electric motor\nWhen the motor is first turned on, the back e.m.f. is zero and the motor draws maximum current. This effect is noticeable when a water pump is first turned on, lights in the same circuit dim briefly due to the IR drop. As the motor speeds up the eddy currents and the resulting back e.m.f. also increase but reduces the amount of current through the coil. When a mechanical load is placed on the motor, like an Elevator going up a hill, the load opposes the rotation (Figure) and may slow it down, thus reducing back e.m.f. If the coil is prevented from turning by its load, then there is no back e.m.f. The applied potential difference can then drive a current that is large enough to overheat the wires and destroy the motor.\nilz\nPHYSICS-XII\nNOT FOR SALE\n148\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nSOLUTION\n1. The equation for peak back-e.m.f., is given by\nE=V-IR-120-(5×4)= 100 V\n2. The peak power delivered by the AC supply is\nPpeak VI =120x5 =600 w\n3. Energy is lost as heat in the motor at the peak rate\nPheat-/R-(5)x4-100 w",
