        "theory": "(B) 15 V\n(C) 90 V\n(D) 300 V\nPUES\nEddy current is produced when\nA. a metal is kept in varying magnetic field\nB. a metal is kept in steady magnetic field\nC. circular coil is placed in a steady magnetic field D. current is passed through a circular coil.\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTION&#x27;S\n1 What factors limit the size of the back e.m.f.?\n2 Why does back e.m.f. tend to decrease as the rate of doing work\nincreases?\n3. In Faraday&#x27;s experiments, what would be the advantage of using coils\nwith many turns?\n4 &amp;=\nShow that the relationship &amp; =- is dimensionally correct.\nAt\n5 Give the formulae for the flux linkage in terms of angular orientation. 6 How electromagnetic brake works?\n7 How eddy currents can be minimized in transformer?\n8 How electromagnetic induction is used in cook tops in electric ranges?\nPHYSICS-XII\n14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n154\nWhat is meant by the term back e.m.f. in any electric motor operation? Why it is an advantage for the armature to rotate in a radial magnetic field rather than a uniform one?\n(a). Which materials would you prefer while making transformer core; iron, solid soft iron, laminated soft iron, aluminum?\n(b). what is the reason for using this material?\nOMPREHENSIVE QUESTION&#x27;S\n3\nDescribe electromagnetic induction with simple experiments. Explain the factors effecting magnitude and direction of induced e.m.f.\nState Faraday&#x27;s law of electromagnetism. Also explain statically and dynamically induced e.m.f. with experiments.\nDescribe lenses law. Show that this law is a manifestation of conservation of energy.\nExplain the phenomenon of mutual induction. Define the coefficient of mutual induction and units.\n5 Explain the phenomenon of self-induction. Define the coefficient of\nself-induction and units.\n7\nExplain motional e.m.f. Show that motional induced e.m.f. is £ =Blv. What is dc generator? Explain how alternating e.m.f. is generated by a loop of wire rotating in a magnetic field?\nWhat is AC motor? Explain the construction and working of AC motor. What is transformer? Give its principle, mathematical relationship. Also explain why laminated iron core is used instead of solid one.\n10 Explain the back e.m.f. in an electric motor.\nNUMERICAL PROBLEMS\nA wheel with 12 metal spokes each 0.6 m long is rotated with a speed of 180 r.p.m in a plane normal to earth&#x27;s magnetic field at a place. If the magnitude of the field is 0.6 G, what is the magnitude of induced e.m.f. between the axle and rim of the wheel?\n(Answer: 2.035 x 10 V)\n2 A circuit has 1000 turns enclosing a magnetic circuit 20cm2 in section with 4A current, the flux density is 1 Wbm2 and with 9A current, it is 1.4Wbm. Find the mean value of the inductance between these current limits and the induced e.m.f. if the current falls from 9A to 4A\n(Answer: 0.16H, 16V)\nin 0.05s.\nPHYSICS-XI\nin\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n155\n3 A coil of resistance 1000 is placed in a magnetic field of 1mWb. The coil has 100 turns and a galvanometer of 4000 resistance is connected in series with it: find the average e.m.f. and the current if the coil is moved in 1/10th s from the given field to a field of 0.2mWb.\n(Answer: 1.6 mÅ) 4 A horizontal straight wire 10 m long extending from east to west is falling with a speed of 5.0 m s, at right angles to the horizontal component of the earth&#x27;s magnetic field, 0.30 x 10 Wb m2 (a) what is the instantaneous value of the e.m.f. induced in the wire? (b) What is the direction of the e.m.f.?\n(c)Which end of the wire is at the higher electrical potential?\n(Answer: 1.5x1013V)\n5 Current in a circuit falls from 5.0 A to OA in 0.1 s. If an average e.m.f.\nof 200 V induced, give an estimate of the self-inductance of the circuit.\n(Answer: 4 H )\n6 A long solenoid with 15 turns per cm has a small loop of area 2.0 cm2 placed inside the solenoid normal to its axis. If the current carried by the solenoid changes steadily from 2.0 A to 4.0 A in 0.1 s, what is the induced e.m.f. in the loop while the current is changing?\n(Answer: 7.54 x 10V) 7 A rectangular wire loop of sides 8 cm and 2 cm with a small cut is moving out of a region of uniform magnetic field of magnitude 0.3 T directed normal to the loop. What is the e.m.f. developed across the cut if the velocity of the loop is 1 cm s1 in a direction normal to the (a) longer side, (b) shorter side of the loop? For how long does the induced voltage last in each case?\n(Answer: 2.4 x 10 V, 2 s, &amp; 0.6 x10 V, 8 s) 8 A 90-mm length of wire moves with an upward velocity of 35 ms&quot; between the poles of a magnet. The magnetic field is 80 mT directed to the right. If the resistance in the wire is 5.00 mo, what are the magnitude and direction of the induced current? (Answer:50.4 A)\nA pair of adjacent coils has a mutual inductance of 1.5 H. If the current in one coil changes from 0 to 20 A in 0.5 s, what is the change of flux linkage with the other coil?\n(Answer: 30 Wb)\nPHYSICS-XII\nNOT FOR SALE\n156\nUnit\n15\nALTERNATING CURRENT\nHow ultraviolet (UV) radiations are important to us?\nLEARN‐ZQ\nG\nT\nC\nOD-VOSшm\n5\nAfter studying this unit the students will be able to\ne\ndescribe the terms time period, frequency, instantaneous peak value and root mean square value of an alternating current and voltage.\n⚫ represent a sinusoidally alternating current or voltage by an equation of the\nform x = x, sin wt.",
