      "⚫ explain what is meant by motional e.m.f. Given a rod or wire moving through a magnetic field in a simple way, compute the potential difference across its ends.",
      "define mutual inductance (M) and self-inductance (L), and their unit henry.",
      "describe the main components of an A.C. generator and explain how it works.",
      "⚫ describe the main features of an A.C. electric motor and the role of",
      "each feature.",
      "explain the production of back e.m.f. in electric motors.",
      "⚫ describe the construction of a transformer and explain how it works.",
      "identify the relationship between the ratio of the number of turns in the primary and secondary coils and the ratio of primary to secondary voltages.",
      "⚫ describe how step-up and step-down transformers can be used to ensure",
      "efficient transfer of electricity along cables.",
      "In 1831, English scientist Michael Faraday discovered that when the magnetic flux linking a conductor changes, an e.m.f is induced in the conductor. This phenomenon is known as electromagnetic induction.",
      "The interplay of electric and magnetic forces is the basis for electric motors, generators, and many other modern technologies.",
      "Faraday's Law of Induction describes how an electric current produces a magnetic field and, conversely, how a changing magnetic field generates an electric current in a conductor.",
      "In this unit, we will study the various aspects of electromagnetic induction.",
      "14.1 INDUCED E.M.F. AND MAGNETIC FLUX",
      "In order to produce induced e.m.f. consider a coil C of wire connected to a sensitive galvanometer G as shown in Figure 14.1(a). The galvanometer is used to detect any current induced in the coil.",
      "Near the coil is a stationary bar magnet. When the magnet is stationary the galvanometer shows no current. Now if the magnet moved towards the coil C, the galvanometer will show deflection in one direction.",
      "PHYSICS-XII",
      "UNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "128",
      "If the magnet is moved away from the coil, the galvanometer again shows deflection but in the opposite direction. Furthermore, if the magnet is held steady and the coil of wire is FIGURE 14.1",
      "moved toward or away from the",
      "magnet, again an e.m.f is induced and a current flows.",
      "If the magnet is held stationary inside the coil though the flux is linking the coil there is no change in flux and hence no e.m.f is induced in the coil. Therefore the galvanometer shows zero deflection. If the magnet is moved faster, à greater deflection is observed. In each case, an e.m.f is induced in a circuit when the magnetic flux through the circuit changes",
      "(a)",
      "C",
      "I",
      "with time. It doesn't matter",
      "whether the magnet or the coil moves. It is their relative motion that counts.",
      "TIP",
      "The basic requirement for electromagnetic induction is the change in flux linking the conductor (or coil).",
      "When the conductor is stationary and the magnet is moving. Then the e.m.f. induced in this way is called statically induced e.m.f.",
      "n",
      "A similar effect can be produced by using the apparatus shown in Figure 14.1(b). It consists of a coil connected to a switch and a battery. We call this coil the primary coil and the corresponding circuit the primary circuit. A second coil, called the secondary coil, at the right, is connected to an ammeter. The corresponding circuit is called the secondary circuit. There is no source of e.m.f in the secondary circuit.",
      "When the switch in the primary circuit is suddenly closed, the ammeter measures a current in the secondary circuit and then returns to zero! The two coils are magnetically linked together by a common magnetic flux. When the switch is opened again, the ammeter reads a current in the opposite direction",
      "and again returns to zero.",
      "PHYSICS-XII",
      "UNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION",
      "FIGURE 14.1",
      "Switch",
      "Rheostat",
      "Battery",
      "+111-",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "129",
      "Primary coil",
      "Secondary coil",
      "(b)",
      "Electromagnetic induction",
      "Finally, whenever there is a steady current in the primary circuit, the ammeter reads zero. The current produced in the secondary circuit occurs for only",
      "an instant while the magnetic field through the secondary coil is changing. Closing and",
      "opening the switch induces the current.",
      "APPLICATIONS OF ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION To measure the earthquakes we use a device which is called seismometer.",
      "It is the change in magnetic field that creates the current. We therefore say. that a changing magnetic field induces an e.m.f.",
