      "132",
      "The coil resembles as South-pole and repels an approaching magnet. A changing magnetic flux induces the current through the coil which itself will produce its own magnetic field.",
      "According to Lenz's law, the direction of the induced current will oppose the change in flux that created it.",
      "On the other hand, if the South-pole of the magnet is moved away from the coil as shown in figure",
      "14.3(b), the magnetic flux FIGURE 14.3",
      "linked with the coil decreases. The coil resembles as north-pole and attracts the magnet when it is pulled away. The direction of the induced current must be such that its own magnetic field is directed in a way to oppose the changing flux caused by the field of the approaching magnet.",
      "The direction of the induced current can be determined by applying right-hand rule if the magnetic polarity of the coil. is known.",
      "Similarly when the North- pole of the magnet is moved toward or away from the coil repulsion and attraction takes place between the coil and the moving magnet, and always opposes the motion of the magnet as illustrated in Figure 14.3 (c), (d).Thus Lenz's Law states that: the polarity of induced e.m.f. is such that it tends to produce a current which opposes the change in magnetic flux that produced it",
