        "title": "EXAMPLE 14.2",
        "problem": "EXAMPLE 14.2\nA coil of mean area 500 cm2 and having 1000 turns is held perpendicular to a uniform field of 0.4 gauss. The coil is turned through 180° in 1/10 s. Calculate the average induced e.m.f.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nGIVEN\nNOT FOR SALE-\n134\nWhen the plane of the coil is perpendicular to the field, 0=0°.\nWhen the coil is turned though 180°, 0 = 180°.\nTherefore, initial flux linked with the coil is P, NBA cose = NBA Flux linked with coil when turned through 180°-NBA\nChange in flux linking the coil is A = 02-0,=(-NBA)-(NBA)=-2NBA\nN=1000; B=0.4 G=0.4x10 T\n;A=500x100 m2; At=0.1s\nREQUIRED\ninduced e.m.f. =E=?\nSOLUTION\n..Average induced e.m.f., E=\nA 2NBA\nAt At 2x1000x(0.4x10 )x500x10-\n=0.04V\n0.1\nASSIGNMENT:14.1\nIn the initial state (1), the magnetic flux is changed by 5 Wb in 2 s on a coil with 20 loops. In the final state (2), the same flux changed in 8 seconds. Determine the ratio of the initially induced e.m.f. and the final induced e.m.f.\n14.4.1\nMOTIONAL e.m.f.\nConsider a conducting rod FIGURE 14.4\nAnswer (4: 1)\nNM placed in a uniform magnetic field B directed into the page as shown in figure 14.4. The rectangle\nBX X X\nX\nX X\nX\nS\nM\nP\nX\nX\nX X\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX\nMNRS form a closed circuit\n1\nResistor\nV+\nX\nX\nX\nX\nX X\nX\nenclosing a varying area\ndue to the motion of the\nX\nX X\nX\nX\nX X\nX\nrod MN.\nR\nN\nX\nX\nX\nX X\nX X\nX\nH\n-AX\nX,\nA conductor movement in a magnetic field\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\n135\nNOT FOR SALE When rod MN is moved from position x, to position x2 in small time interval At then there is a change in the flux linked with the conductor and a current / will start to flow in the circuit. Therefore, an induced e.m.f. is produced. This induced e.m.f. due to the motion of an electric conductor in the presence of the magnetic field is called motional e.m.f. The distance travelled by the rod in time At is X-X-AX. To find the magnitude of e.m.f. induced along the moving rod, we use Faraday's law of induction\nE=NAD\nAt\nIn this equation, N = 1 and the flux = BA cos 8. We have = 0° and cos 8 = 1, since B is perpendicular to A. Where change in flux ADA(BA) BAA, since B is uniform. The area enclosed by the circuit MNRS is AA = CA.\nPutting the values of N and AO into the above equation we get,\nBAA\nAt\nBCAX\nAt\nThe velocity of the rod is Ax/At = v. Thus\nilah\nE=Bev (B,L, and v perpendicular)\n.. AA = CAX\nThus, the e.m.f. generated in the circuit by the moving rod is simply the product of the magnetic field-strength, the length of the rod, and the velocity of the rod. This equation is valid as long as B, I and v are mutually perpendicular. If they are not, then their mutually perpendicular components are used. Motional e.m.f. is an example of dynamically induced e.m.f. It is so called because e.m.f. is induced in the conductor which is in motion.\nWhen the conductor is moved in a stationary magnetic field in such a way that the flux linking it changes in magnitude. Then the e.m.f. induced in this called dynamically induced e.m.f.\nway is\nThe above Equation can be written as /====\n£ Blv R R\nFrom an energy perspective, F produces power Fv, and the resistor dissipates power I'R. Since the rod is moving at constant velocity, the applied force F must balance the magnetic force Fm-Bvl on the rod when it is carrying the induced current I.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n136\nThus the power produced is\nBlv\nI\nFy= Bilv\nBlV Blv\nB212v2\nR\nR\nR\nB2122\nThe power dissipated is P =\n14.2\nR\nIn satisfying the principle of energy conservation, the produced and dissipated powers are equal. This principle can be seen in the operation of a rail gun. A rail gun is an electromagnetic projectile launcher as shown in Figure 14.5.\nFIGURE 14.5\nMagnet Field B\nCurrent/\nNegatively charged rail\nDriving Current\nMagnet\nField B\nArmature Current (0)\nArmature\nCurrent (1)\nPositively charged rail\nForce F\nA rail gun\nF\nForce\n(F)",
