        "theory": "Inductance L = 60 m H, current / -0.2 sin (377t -25°)A\nMathematical expression for the voltage V= ?\nSOLUTION\nInductive reactance,\nX1 =2πfL=377×60×10=22.622\nmaximum value of an alternating V=X=0.2x22.62=4.5V\nSince the voltage leads the current by 90° therefore 90° is added to the\nphase angle of voltage.\nASSIGNMENT 15.2\nV=V sin(377t-25° +90°)\nV=4.5sin(377t+65°) V\nA pure inductor is connected across a 10 V, 200 Hz supply, and the current flowing through it is measured as 0.4 A. Determine the value of its inductance.\nAnswer(19.9 mH)\n15.9 A.C. THROUGH CAPACITOR\nWa\nConsider an alternating voltage applied to a capacitor of capacitance C as shown in Fig15.12. When an alternating voltage is applied across the plates of a capacitor, the capacitor is charged in one direction and then in the other as the voltage reverses. The result is that electron move to and fro around the circuit, connecting the plates, thus constituting alternating current. The basic relation between the charge q on the capacitor and voltage V across its plates i.e. q=CV holds at every instant. Let the applied alternating voltage is\nV= Vm sin wt\n(1)\nThen, at any instant / be the current and q be the charge on the plates.\nCharge on capacitor, q = CV= C Vm sin wt\nn\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n173\nAq_A(CV, sin(at)) CV (Asin(at))\nAt\nAt\nby using maths formulae\n..Asin(at) = @cos(at)\nAt\n= CV_@cos(at) = CV @sin(at + 2)\nFIGURE 15.12\n90°\n120\n150&quot;\ncfad/s\n180\n210\n240\n270&quot;\n(a)\nCapacitor in AC circuit\n:. CV @=Im\n| = | sin(at +\n+22\n330.\nAA\n(b)\nphasor diagram\n..:(15.14)\n.......(ii)\nEqs. (i) and (ii) shows that current leads the voltage by m/2 radians or 90°. Hence in a pure capacitance, current leads the voltage by 90°. Capacitance opposes the change in voltage and serves to delay the increase or decrease of voltage across the capacitor. This causes the voltage to lag behind the current. This is also illustrated in the phasor diagram shown in Fig:15.12(b):\nLike inductor which opposes the flow of A.C. capacitor also opposes the flow of A.C. in the circuit. From the above:\nIm = wC Vm\n...(15.15)\nOr\n1\n-13\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n174\nJust like ohm law the ratio of V/I is the measure of opposition offered by a resistor to the flow of current. In case of capacitor this opposition is capacitive reactance which opposes the flow of current.\nV V 1\nL I\nCo\n...(15.16)\nClearly, the opposition offered by capacitance to current flow is I/C. This quantity/7@C is called the capacitive reactance X, of the capacitor. It has the same dimensions as resistance and is, therefore, measured in .\nWhere capacitive reactance is\n1 = Vc/Xc\n1\n1\nX=\n@C 2πfC\n...(15.17)\nThe capacitive reactance depends upon frequency of A.C. In case of D.C., X, has infinite value.\n15.9.1\nPOWER LOSS IN A CAPACITIVE CIRCUIT\nIn pure capacitive circuit the current FIGURE 15.13 leads the voltage by 90° in phase",
