        "id": "15.1",
        "title": "Learning outcomes & introduction",
        "theory": "UNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n157\nEARN_Z\nconstruct phasor diagrams and carry out calculations on circuits including resistive and reactive components in series.\nsolve the problems using the formulae of A.C Power.\nexplain resonance in an A.C circuit and carry out calculations using the resonant frequency formulae.\n⚫ describe that maximum power is transferred when the impedances of source.\nand load match to each other.\nG⚫ describe the qualitative treatment of Maxwell&#x27;s equations and production of\nelectromagnetic waves.\nCOME UN\nS\nbecome familiar with electromagnetic spectrum (ranging from radio waves to y- rays).\nidentify that light is a part of a continuous spectrum of electromagnetic waves all of which travel in vacuum with same speed.\ndescribe that the information can be transmitted by radiowaves.\nidentify that the microwaves of a certain frequency cause heating when\nabsorbed by water and cause burns when absorbed by body tissues.\ndescribe that ultra violet radiation can be produced by special lamps and that prolonged exposure to the Sun may cause skin cancer from ultra violet radiation.\nThe electricity produced by most generators is in the form of alternating. current (AC). It is true that in some cases AC holds no practical advantage over DC. However, with AC it is possible to build electric generators, motors and power distribution systems that are far more efficient than DC, and so we find AC being used predominantly across the world in high power applications. A theory describing relation between accelerating charges, circuits and electric and magnetic fields was given by James Clerk Maxwell in 1864 called electromagnetic theory. The theory predicts that accelerating electric charges radiate electromagnetic waves, which propagate at the speed of light.\n15.1\nALTERNATING VOLTAGE AND CURRENT\nThe supply of current for electrical devices may come from a direct current source (DC), or an alternating current source (AC). In direct current electricity, electrons flow continuously in one direction from the source of\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n158\npower through a conductor to a load and back to the source of power. The voltage in direct current remains constant. DC power sources include batteries and DC generators.\nIn alternating current an AC generator is used to make electrons flow first in one direction then in another. A source which produces potential difference of changing polarity with time is called as alternating source. A voltage which changes its polarity at regular interval of time is called an alternating voltage. Fig 15.1: shows an alternating voltage source connected to a resistor R. Where, the upper terminal of alternating voltage source is positive and lower terminal negative so that current flows in the circuit.\nFIGURE 15.1\nV\nFIGURE 15.2\n180\n270\n360\nR\n0\n90°\nAC circuit\nAlternating current and voltage\nAfter some time, the polarities of the voltage source are reversed, so that current now flows in the opposite direction. This is called alternating current. AC voltage has a time-varying waveform known as a Sinusoidal Waveform as shown in Fig.15.2. The instantaneous value of a sine-wave voltage for any angle of rotation is expressed in the equation.\nV = Vm sinut\n...(15.1)\nWhere Vis instantaneous value of alternating voltage, V., is maximum value of alternating voltage and is angular velocity of the coil. Fig15.2: shows that sinusoidal voltage or current not only changes direction at regular intervals but the magnitude is also changing continuously. The change from one polarity to\nPHYSICS-XII\nn\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n159\nthe other is a smooth one, the voltage level changing most rapidly at the zero point and most slowly at its peak.\n15.3\nA.C. TERMINOLOGIES\nAlternating voltage or current changes continuously in magnitude and alternates in direction at regular intervals of time. It rises from zero to maximum positive value, falls to zero, increases to a maximum in the reverse direction and falls back to zero again. The important A.C. terminology is defined below: FIGURE 15.3\nilab.com\n1.\n2.\n3.\n4.\n180 270\n360&quot;\n+of\n90°\n(a): AC waveform for one cycle\nCYCLE. One complete set of positive and negative values of an alternating quantity is known as a cycle. Fig. 15.3 (a) shows one cycle of an alternating voltage. A cycle can also be defined in terms of angular measure. One cycle corresponds to 360° or 2m radians.\nTIME PERIOD. The time taken in seconds to complete one cycle of an alternating quantity is called its time period. It is generally represented by T.\nFREQUENCY. The number of cycle that occurs in one second is called the frequency (ƒ) of the alternating quantity. It is measured in cycle per second or Hertz (Hz). The frequency of power system in Pakistan is 50 Hz. It means that alternating voltage or current completes 50 cycles in\none second.\nINSTANTANEOUS VALUE. The value of an alternating quantity at any instant is called instantaneous value. The instantaneous values of alternating voltage and current are represented by V and / respectively.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n160\nAs an example, the instantaneous values of voltage at 0°, 90° and 270° are 0, + Vm, Vm respectively as shown in Fig 15.3(a).\nThe AC waveform is constantly changing with time. Therefore it can be expressed as, Peak value, Average value or mean value, and R.M.S. value or effective value.\n5. PEAK VALUES: The maximum\nvalue attained by an AC waveform v\nis called its peak value.\nIt is also known as the maximum\nvalue or amplitude. The peak\nFIGURE 15.3&lt;svg viewBox=&quot;0 0 460 210&quot; role=&quot;img&quot; aria-label=&quot;Alternating Current&quot; style=&quot;width:min(100%,460px);height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #dbeafe;border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M55 170H420M75 185V25&quot; stroke=&quot;#334155&quot; stroke-width=&quot;2&quot;/&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;220&quot; y=&quot;202&quot; fill=&quot;#334155&quot; font-size=&quot;13&quot;&gt;x / independent variable&lt;/text&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;12&quot; y=&quot;22&quot; fill=&quot;#334155&quot; font-size=&quot;13&quot;&gt;y&lt;/text&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M75 105 C110 15 150 15 185 105 S260 195 295 105 S370 15 405 105&quot; fill=&quot;none&quot; stroke=&quot;#2563eb&quot; stroke-width=&quot;3&quot;/&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;330&quot; y=&quot;50&quot; font-size=&quot;14&quot;&gt;AC wave&lt;/text&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;",
