        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 2",
        "theory": "I = Im sinwt\n(i)\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n163\nFIGURE 15.6\n90°\n120\n60°\n150\n30°\n360°\n180°-\nprad/s\n60 90\n180\n270\n210\n330\n240&quot;\n300\n270\nPhasor at 60° angle.\nWhere, I is the value of current at that instant, I is the amplitude of current and e=wt is the angle through which the vector rotate. The phasor diagram represents the sinusoidal alternating wave for every instant of time. The wheel like shape represents wave location at 60° as shown in figure 15.6.\nPHASE DIFFERENCE\nThings start to get complicated when we need to relate two or more AC voltages or currents that have phase difference with each other. Fig 15.7(a) shows two waveforms that are not synchronized i.e., their peaks and zero points do not match up at the same points in time.\nThus when two alternating quantities of the same frequency have different zero point, they are said to have a phase difference.\nThe quantity which crosses through its zero point earlier is said to leading while the other is said to be lagging. A few more examples of phase difference are given in the following graphs to better illustrate this concept. The two waves / versus V shown in figure 15.7(a) have same amplitude and frequency, but they have phase difference with each other.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nFIGURE 15.7\n90\n120\n60°\n150&quot;\nbrad/s\n180\n210%\n330\n300\n240\nNOT FOR SALE\n164\n(a): Wave / leads wave V by 45&quot;\n90°\n120°\n60°\n150\nrad/s 1901\n180°\n90\n210\n330°\n240\n300&quot;\n270&quot;\n(b): Wave / leads wave V by 90&quot;\nphase Since both alternating quantities have the same frequency, the phase difference between them remains the same through the cycle. The phase difference between voltage and current at any angle + can be expressed in the equations form as\nV = V sin (wt -)\n15.6 A.C. THROUGH RESISTOR\n(ii)\nConsider a circuit containing a resistance R connected across an alternating voltage source as shown in Fig15.8 (a), then free electrons flow in one direction for the first half-cycle of the supply and then flow in the opposite direction during the next half-cycle, thus constituting alternating current in the circuit. The applied voltage and current pass through their zero values at the same instant and attain their positive and negative peaks at the same instant such that current is in phase with the applied voltage as shown in fig15.8(b).\nPHYSICS-XII\nn\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nThe alternating voltage is given by\nV= Vm sin wt\n(1)\nNOT FOR SALE\n165\nwhere, V., is the peak value of the alternating voltage.\nFIGURE 15.8\nCO\n(a)\n90&quot;\n120\n150\n/ad/s\n180",
