        "theory": "184\n(iii) When XL - Xc =0, the circuit is purely resistive and phase angle -0° the circuit will then have unity power factor.\n+\nIf the equation for the applied voltage is V=V sinot, then equation for the circuit current will be\nEXAMPLE15.7\n1=1sin(cot±) where =V/Z\nA 220V, 50Hz A.C. supply is applied to a coil of 0.06 H inductance and 2.50 resistance connected in series with a 6.8 μ F capacitor. Calculate (a) impedance (b) current (c) phase angle between current and voltage (d) power factor and (e) power consumed.\nGIVEN\nREQUIRED\ninductance L=0.06 H resistance R= 2.50 voltage V-220V Frequency f= 50Hz capacitance C= 6.8 μ F\n(a) impedance Z-?(b) current l=? (c) phase angle between current and voltage + =? (d) power factor cos =? and (e) power consumed P=?.\nSOLUTION\n1\n106\nX1 =2πƒL=2πx50x.06-18.852 Xe:\n=46852\n2π fС 2л×50×6.8\n(a) Circuit impedance, Z =√R2+(X-Xc)2\n√(2.5)2+(18.85-468)2 =449.20\n(b) Circuit Current, I =V/Z=220/449.2=0.4897A\n(c)\nPhase angle,\ntano=\n-179.66\nX-X 18.85-468\nR\n2.5 =tan (-179.66)=-89.7°\nz e\nThe negative sign with shows that current is leading the voltage\n(d) Power factor, cos¢ = =\n(e) Power consumed,\nR 2.5\nZ 449.2\n-=0.00557\nP = Vicos += 220 x 0.4897 x 0.00557 = 0.60007w\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n185\n15.13 RESONANCE IN R-L-C SERIES A.C. CIRCUIT\nIn R-L-C Series AC circuit, inductors, capacitors, and resistors all impede current. There impedance and reactance is given by the equations:\nZ = √√R2+(X1-X1)2\nFIGURE 15.19\nSmall resistance\nX1 = coL and XC\nWhen the frequency is very small, the capacitive reactance is large and X Z. When the frequency is very large, the inductive reactance is large and XL &gt;Z. But when the inductive reactance X is equal to the capacitive reactance Xc, XL = Xc or (XL - Xc = 0)\nLarge resistance\nf.\nFigure 15.19: resonance curve\nThe circuit power factor is unity and R-L-C series circuit is said to be in resonance. The frequency f, at which it occurs is called resonant frequency.\nAt series resonance,\nOr\nResonant frequency,\nXe Xe\n...(15.32)\n1\n2π f, L =\n1\nf\n11 = 2 = √/LC (15.33)\nFrom Eq. (15.33), it is clear that on increasing either the inductance or the capacitance causes the resonant frequency to decrease. For a given value of inductance and capacitance, there is only one resonant frequency at resonant frequency the current is maximum and impedance of circuit is minimum. Z-R The magnitude of the current as a function of frequency is plotted as resonance curve Fig 15.19 which shows that the current I starts at near to zero, reaches maximum value at the resonance frequency f, and then drops again to nearly zero as ƒ becomes infinite.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nAn A/C. circuit containing reactive elements (L and C) is said to be in resonance when the circuit power factor is unity.\n15.14 PRINCIPLE OF METAL DETECTORS\nA coil and capacitor are electrical components, which together can produce oscillations of current. An L-C circuit behaves just like an oscillating mass-spring system. In this case, energy oscillates between a capacitor and an inductor. The circuit is called an electrical oscillator. Two such oscillators A and B are used for the operation of common type of metal detector (Fig 15.20). In the absence of any nearby metal object, the inductances L, and La\nFIGURE 15.20\nC:\nOscillator circuit A\nAdditional\nNOT FOR SALE\nFOR YOUR INFORMATION\n186\nWhen a metal detector comes near a piece of metal, the self-inductance of one of its coils changes. This causes a shift in the resonant frequency of a circuit containing the coil. That shift is detected by the circuitry and transmitted to the diver by means of the headphones.\ncircuit for oscillator A\nAdditional piron for\nvor B\nOverler decl\nLa\nBeat fruques si",
