        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 12",
        "theory": "Speaker\nmetal detectors\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n187\nare the same and hence the resonance frequency of the two circuits is also same. When inductor B, called the search coil comes near a metal object the inductance La decreases and corresponding oscillator frequency increases and thus a beat note is heard in the attached speaker. Such detectors are extensively used not only for various security checks but also to locate buried metal objects.\n15.15 MAXIMUM POWER TRANSFER\nWhen we connect a load resistance, R, across the output terminals of the power source, the impedance of the load will vary from an open-circuit state to a short-circuit state resulting in the power being absorbed by the load becoming dependent on the impedance of the actual power source. The source and load R, are shown in figure 15.21. Then for the load resistance to absorb the maximum power possible it has to be &quot;Matched&quot; to the impedance of the power source and this forms the basis of Maximum Power Transfer.\nila\nFIGURE 15.21\nV\nMaximum\nR1\nB\nww\nA\npower is\n20\ntransferred from a source to a load when the load resistance equals the source resistance.\nThe maximum power transfer theorem states that &quot;the maximum amount of power will be dissipated in the load resistance if it is equal in value to the source resistance of the network supplying the power&quot;.\nIn other words, the load resistance resulting in greatest power dissipation must be equal in value to the equivalent source resistance, then R, Rs but if the load resistance is lower or higher in value than the source resistance of the network, its dissipated power will be less than maximum.\nR1 = R1 or Z1 = Z,\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n188\n15.16 MAXWELLS EQUATIONS\nWe already studied the Gauss&#x27; law, Faraday&#x27;s law and Ampere&#x27;s law. James Clerk Maxwell [1831-1879] unified these experimental laws into a set of Equations known as Maxwell&#x27;s Equations. These equations predict the existence of electromagnetic waves and that such waves are radiated by accelerating charges.\nFIGURE 15.22\nMaxwell&#x27;s Equations are a set of equations that describe how electric and magnetic fields propagate, interact, and how they are influenced by objects.\n(a). Consider the situation where a bar magnet is moving toward a conducting loop with its north pole down, as shown in Figure 15.22). By Faraday&#x27;s law changing magnetic flux induces an induced emf in\nloop.\nS\nEne\nInducing,emf by varying\nΑΦ\nE=\nΠ\n(B.A) = 4 AB\nA\n...(15.34)\nthe magnetic field strength\nAt At\nAt\nLet a unit positive charge completes one circular trip in a loop of radius r, the work done will be\nW = 2лr(F) = 2лr(qE)\naw p\nAn electric field E is induced to drive the current around the loop, and a charge on the loop experiences an electric force F=qE.\nW 2лrgE\n=2лгE\n9\nε=2лrE\n...(15.35)\nW ...(15.36). E=\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n189\ncomparing Eq(15.34) &amp; Eq(15.36) we get\nAB\n2лгE = A\nAt\nE=A AB 2лr At\n...(15.37)\nThis equation shows that a changing magnetic flux gives rise to an electric field. Experiments have shown that the electric field produced by changing magnetic field is present even if the conducting loop is absent.\n(b): Analogues to changing magnetic flux it is found that a changing electric flux gives rise to a magnetic field. In order to arrive at this statement, let a capacitor be connected to a battery as arranged in Fig. (15.23). Current starts growing in the circuit but very quickly decreases to zero when the capacitor is fully charged. An electric field is established between the plates of the capacitor. The charge Q on an air filled capacitor of capacitance C, plate area A, plate separation d and potential difference V is given by\nila\nQ=CV =2+^V=6,AE V=E2AE\n...(15.38)\nV\n=E\nd\nFIGURE 15.23\nSuppose the capacitor is now connected to an alternating e.m.f. source. It is observed that the current flows continuously in the circuit. In this case the electric field E between the plates of the capacitor changes with time AE/At. We saw in former case the current stop while in later case current flow is continue\nV\nT\nUniform electric field between a pair of charged plate.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT-",
