        "title": "EXAMPLE 15.1",
        "problem": "EXAMPLE 15.1\nWaz e in\n...(15.11)\nAn A.C. circuit consists of a pure resistance of 2002 and is connected across A.C. supply of 220V, 50Hz. Calculate (a) current (b) power consumed and (c) equation for voltage and current.\nGIVEN\nREQUIRED\nFrequency f = 50 Hz Resistance R = 200\n(a) current l=? (b) power consumed P =? (c) equation for voltage and current =?\nVoltage V= 220 V\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nSOLUTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n167\nMaximum value of an alternating voltage is V = √2 V = √2 x 220- 311.1V\n(a) Current,\n| = V/R = 220/20= 11A\n(b) Average power P dissipated in the resistor is\nP = VI = 220 x 11- 2420 w\nMaximum value of an alternating current = √21 = √2 x 11 = 15.55 A\nw=2πƒ= 2 mx 50 = 314 rads1\n(c) Equations for voltage and current is\nV=V sin(@t) & /=sin(at)\nputting values\nV = 311.1sin(314t) & /=15.55 sin(314t)\nASSIGNMENT 15.1\nA 60 resistor is connected across A.C. supply of 220V. Calculate the current drawn from the supply and the power consumed by the heating element.\n15.7 A.C. THROUGH INDUCTOR\nAns(3.66A, 860w)\nAn inductor is a two-terminal electrical component which resists changes in electric current passing through it. It consists of a conductor such as a wire, usually wound into a coil. Consider an alternating voltage applied to a pure inductance of L as shown in Fig15.10 when a sinusoidal current / flow in time t then a back e.m.f. (LAI/At) is induced due to the inductance of the coil. This back e.m.f. at every instant opposes the change in current through the coil. As there is no drop in potential, so the applied voltage has to overcome the back e.m.f.\nApplied alternating voltage = Back e.m.f.\nSo the energy which is required in building up current in inductance L, is returned back during the decay of the current. Let the equation for alternating current is:\n1 = Im sin wt\n...(1)\nThe changing current sets up a back e.m.f in the coil.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n168\nΔΙ\nThe magnitude of back e.m.f is E = L\nAt\nTo maintain a constant current the applied e.m.f must be constantly applied. The magnitude of applied voltage is\nV=L=LA, sin at)\nAt\nΔΙ\nA(sinat)\n= Um\nAt\n(ii)\nUsing the result of simple calculus:\nA(sinat)\n@cos at\nAt\n(iii)\nFIGURE 15.10\nputting the values of Eq (iii) in (ii) we get\nV = @LI_cos at\n. (@LI =V)\n(a)\n90°\n120\n60%\n150\ntofad/s\n0000000\n180\n0\n90\n180\n0\n270\n210\n30\n240\n300\n270\"\n(b)\nInductor in AC circuit\nawaz ein\n360\nor V=Vcosat\nV=V_sin(at + 2)\n필\n(iv)\nFrom Eqs (i) and (iv) it is clear that current lags behind the voltage by π/2 radians or 90°. Hence in a pure inductance, current lags behind the voltage by 90°. Fig. 15.10(b) also shows that current lags the voltage in an inductive coil. Inductance opposes the change in current and serves to delay the increase or\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n169\ndecrease of current in the circuit. This causes the current to lag behind the applied voltage which is indicated by the phasor diagram shown in Fig. 15.10(b). Inductance opposes the flow of current in the circuit.\nSo the opposition offered by an inductor to the flow of A.C. is called inductive reactive reactance XL.\nTherefore in analogy to ohm law we can write:\nV12 = LX_\nSince inductive reactance is ratio of voltage to current. So\nV\nV\nX1 =\n= @L\nor X1 = @L\nX1=2πfL\n...(15.12) ....(15.13)\nThe reactance of coil depends upon frequency of A.C. In case of D.C. inductive reactance XL is zero.\n15.7.1 POWER LOSS IN AN INDUCTOR\nFig15.11: shows the power curve for a pure inductive circuit. During the first 90° of the cycle, the voltage is positive and the current is negative, therefore, the power supplied is negative.\nThis means the power is flowing from the coil to the source.\nFIGURE 15.11\nP\nAverage power\n270\n360\nPower curve for an inductor\nDuring the next 90° of the cycle, both voltage and current are positive and the power supplied is positive. Therefore, power flows from the source to the coil. Similarly, for the next 90° of the cycle, power flows from the coil to the source and during the last 90° of the cycle, power flow from the source to the coil. The power curve over one\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n170\ncycle shows that positive power is equal to the negative power. Hence the resultant power over one cycle is zero i.e. a pure inductance consumes no power. The electric power merely flows from the source to the coil and back again. In any circuit, electric power consumed at any instant is the product of voltage and current at that instant. The average power loss in an inductive circuit is,\n15.8\nP =< VI >=< V, cos atx/sinat >\nV<sinat >< cosat >\n= 0\nCHOKE COIL\n<sinat >< cosat >= 0\nA Choke is an inductor used in a circuit. It offers high impedance to frequencies above a certain frequency range, without appreciable limiting the flow of current. In a DC circuit, a resistor is used to restrict the current.\nIf I is the current and R is the resistance, the power loss in the form of heat is R. In ac circuit, inductor is used. Its impedance is X, and is large at high frequencies. In general, a choke is used to prevent electric signals along undesired paths. The choke is used as a filter in power supply to prevent ripple. It also prevents unwanted signals to enter other parts of the circuits, e.g. radio frequency choke (RFC) prevents radio frequency signals from entering audio frequency circuits.\nFOR YOUR INFORMATION\nThe purpose of passing current through a circuit is to transfer power from the source to the circuit. The power which is actually consumed in the circuit is called the true power or active power.\nWe know that current and voltage are in phase in a resistance whereas they are 90° out of phase in L or C. Therefore, we come to the conclusion that current in phase with voltage produces true or active power whereas current 90° out of phase with voltage contributes to reactive power i.e.\nTrue Power = Voltage x Current in phase with voltage\nin\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n171",
