        "problem": "EXAMPLE 15.4\nNOT FOR SALE\n175\nA318uF capacitor is connected across a 220V, 50Hz system. Determine (a) the capacitive reactance (b) r.m.s. value of current and (c) equations for voltage and current.\nGIVEN\nCapacitance, C= 318μF= 318×10* F Frequency ƒ = 50 Hz\nREQUIRED\nVoltage V-220 V\n(a) the capacitive reactance Xc =? (b) RMS value of current I=?\n(c) equations for voltage and current =?.\nSOLUTION\n1\n(a) capacitive reactance, X\n1\n=10.2602\n2xfC 2x50x318x10\n(b)r.m.s.value of current, I=V/Xc=220/10.26-21.44A (c)muximum value of an alternating voltage & current is\nV-220x√2-311.1V, L=1x√2-√2x21.44-30.32A,\nFrequency @=2x50=314Hz\nEquations for voltage and current are:\nV = V sin(at) & /=sin(at+/2)\nputting values\nV = 311.1sin 314t, | = = 30.32 sin (314t+/2),\nASSIGNMENT 15.3\nA perfect capacitor is connected across a 6 V, 5 kHz supply, and the resulting current flow is 88.6 mA. Calculate the capacitance value.\nAnswer (0.47μF)\n15.10 R.L SERIES A.C. CIRCUIT\nIn an RL series circuit, a pure resistance (R) is connected in series with pure inductance (L). To draw the phasor diagram of RL series circuit, the current / (r.m.s. value) is taken as reference vector because it is common to both elements.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nKOT FOR SALE\n176\nVoltage drop V1 (= / R) in resistor is in FIGURE 15.14\nphase with current vector, whereas, the voltage drop VL (= XL) in inductor leads the current vector by 90° since current lags behind the voltage by 90° in the purely inductive circuit. In phasor diagram V is represented by the phasor AD while VR The is represented by phasor AB. vector sum of these two voltage drops is equal to the applied voltage V (r.m.s. value).i.e.\nV2=v22+V2\n...(15.18)\nог\nV=√v2 +V2 = √(IR)2 +(IX1 )2\n=1√√R2+X2\nV\nor 1=\n...(15.19)\nD\n√R2+X2\nThe quantity √√R2+X2\nV1=IX\nis\nis the\nopposition offered to current flow and is called impedance of the circuit. It is represented by Z and is measured in\nA\n(a): RL series circuit\n90°\n(b) phasor diagram\nC\nawaz\nB\nV1-IR\nohms (2)\nI = V/Z\nwhere Z= √R2+X2\n...(15.20)\n...(15.21)\n(c):phasor diagram of RL series\ncircuit\nThe phasor diagram is shown in Fig15.14(a). The phasor diagram is a triangle whose sides represent R, X, and Z. The triangle that is created when adding the resistance to the reactance X, is known as an impedance triangle.\nPHYSICS-XII\nn\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n177\nThe phasor diagram shows that circuit current / lags behind the applied voltage V by 4o. The wave diagram in Fig.15.14 (c) also shows the phase difference between current and voltage V. The value of phase angle determined from the phasor diagram.\ncan be\ntano=V-IX, X, VIR R\nL\n...(15.22)\nSince X, and R are known, can be calculated. If the applied voltage is V=V_sinot, then equation for the circuit current will be;\n|=sin(ot-6) where Im = Vm/Z\nIn an inductive circuit current lags behind the applied voltage. The angle (i.e. +) of lagging is greater than 0° but less than 90°. It is determined by the ratio of inductive reactance to resistance (tano=X, /R) in the circuit. The greater the value of this ratio, the greater will be the phase angle\n15.10.1\nPOWER\nFor purely resistive\ncircuit, the power factor is cos 0°-1 as current and voltage\nin phase.\nare\ninductance\nFor the\nor\ncapacitance\ncircuit, the power factor is cos 90° = 0 as current and\nvoltage are 90° out of phase.\nFIGURE 15.15\nAverage power\n8 = t\nthe waveforms for voltage, current and power in a series R-L circuit.\nTherefore power consumed by pure L or C is zero.\nV\nAverage Power, P=V== cos = xx\nor\n2\ncosxxcos\nP=Vicoso\n√2 √2\n...(15.23)\nWhere V and I are the r.m.s. values of voltage and current. The term cose is\ncalled power factor of the circuit.\nIn Fig:15.15 the negative area of curve is indicated by green colour and positive area of curve is indicated by orange colour. The negative area means that the inductance of the circuit returns the power to the source. The area of positive\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n178\ncurve is greater than negative area of curve so net power over one cycle is positive. This shows that power is consumed in R-L series circuit.",
