        "problem": "EXAMPLE 15.5\nA coil having a resistance of 70 and an inductance of 31.8 mH is connected to 220V, 50Hz supply. Calculate (a) the circuit current (b) phase angle (c) power factor and (d) power consumed.\nGIVEN\nREQUIRED\nSOLUTION\nInductance, L= 31.8 m H= 31.8x10 H Voltage V-220 V coil resistance R=70 Frequency ƒ = 50 Hz\n(a) the circuit current I=? (b) phase angle + =? (c) power factor =? (d) power consumed P=?\n(a) Inductive reactance, X1 =2fL\n=2x50×31.8×10-3-102\nCoil impedance,\nZ=√R2+X2\n√72+102-12.252\nCircuit current,\nI=V/Z\n(b)\n.. Phase angle,\n(c) Power factor\n(d) Power consumed,\nASSIGNMENT 15.4\n=220/12.2=18.03A\ntano=X, /R=10/7\nawaz e\n=tan (10/7)=55° lag\n=cos=cos 55°=0.573lag\nP =VIcoso\n=220×18.03x0.573=2272.8w\n220 V, 50 Hz, AC source is connected to an inductance of 0.2 H and a resistance of 200 in series. What is the current in the circuit?\nAnswer (3.33A)\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n179\n15.11 R.C SERIES A.C. CIRCUIT\nIn an RC series circuit, a pure resistance (R) is connected in series with a pure capacitor (C) as shown in fig15.16(a). The voltage, V across the combination is equal to the phasor sum of the two component FIGURE 15.16 voltages, V, and Vc. Where VR = IR and,\nVc = IXc.\nIn a series AC circuit the current is common and can therefore be used as the reference source because the same current flows through the resistance and capacitance. The individual ivector diagrams for a pure resistance and a pure capacitance is shown in Fig15.16(b).\nVoltage drop (VIR) is in phase with current vector, whereas, the voltage drop in (Vc -IXc) lags behind the current vector by 90°, since current leads the voltage by 90° in the pure capacitive circuit. The vector sum of these two voltage drops is equal to the applied voltage V (r.m.s. value).\n(a) RC series circuit\n90\nV\n(b) phasor diagram\nC\nv2 = V2+V2\n...(15.24)\nV = √V2+V2 = √(IR)2 +(-IX1)2 =I√√R2+X2\nV\nor | =\n√ R2 + X 2\n...(15.25)\nThe quantity R2+X2 offer opposition to current flow and is called\nimpedance of the circuit./= V/Z\nwhere Z=√R2+X2\n...(15.26)\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n180\nThe phasor diagram shows that circuit current / leads the applied voltage V by +. This fact is also illustrated in the wave diagram and impedance triangle (as shown in Fig15.17(a), (b)) of the circuit.\nFIGURE 15.17\nP\nV1=IR\nB\nAverage power A\n0- cot\n-V-IXc\n90°\n80 270 360°\nZ\nD\n(a) phasor diagram\n(b) impedance triangle\nThe value of the phase can be determined as under:\nC\ntano=-\nVR\nIX\nXc\nIR\nR\n...(15.27)\ne in\nSince current is taken as the reference phasor, negative phase angle implies that voltage lags behind the current. This is the same thing as current leads the voltage.\n15.11.1\nPower\naw\nThe power waveform for RC series circuit is shown in the fig15.17(a). The negative area of curve is indicated by green colour and positive area of curve is indicated by orange colour. The area of positive curve is greater than negative area of curve, thus power is consumed by the RC series circuit. But power is consumed in resistance only; capacitor does not consume any power. The equation for voltage and current are:\n.. Average power,\nV=V sinot; sin(wt+)\n(P)=(V)(I)=VIcos\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT",
