        "problem": "EXAMPLE 15.6\nNOT FOR SALE\n181\nA 100V, 50Hz AC supply is applied to a capacitor of capacitance 79.5μF connected in series with a non-inductive resistance of 30 0. Find (1) impedance (2) current (3) phase angle and (4) equation for the instantaneous value of current.\nGIVEN\nREQUIRED\nVoltage V-100 V, resistance R=30\nFrequency ƒ = 50 Hz, capacitance C = 79.5μ F\n(1) impedance Z-? (2) current /-? (3) phase angle =? and (4) equation for the instantaneous value of current list =?\nSOLUTION\n1\n106\n(1)\nCapacitive reactance,\nxc=2xfc\n=400\nilab\nNM\nCircuit impedance,\n(2)\nCircuit current,\n(3)\n2лfС 2лx50x79.5\nZ=√R2+X2=√302+402=502\n/= V/Z = 100/50 = 2 A\ntan Xc/R-40/30 = 1.33\nPhase angle, tan\" 1.33 53°lead\n=\nL=1×√2 = 1=2x√2=2.828A\n=2лƒ=2x50=314rads\"\n(4) equation for current is 2.828sin(314t+53°)\n15.12 R-L-C Series A.C Circuit\nWhen a pure resistor (R), an inductor (L), and a capacitor (C) are connected together in series combination with each other then RLC Series Circuit is formed as illustrated in Fig15.18 (a). The analysis of this circuit is quite easy since all the circuit elements share the same current. We can draw a phasor diagram for the current and voltages across the inductor, capacitor, and resistor. The P.D. across R, is VR = IR in this case V is in phase with I.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n182\nThe P.D. across L, is V, IX, in this case V leads / by 90°. The P.D. across C, is Vc IXc in this case where Ve lags / by 90°.V, and Ve are thus 180° out of phase. In phasor diagram (Fig15.18 (b)), AB represents VR, BE represents V, and BD represents Vc. It may be seen that V, is in phase opposition to Vc.\nFIGURE 15.18\n0000000\nR\nL\nC\nE\nVR\nto\nB\nD\nVL\nV1-VC\nn\n(a) AC circuit of R, L and C connected in series across a supply voltage V (r.m.s.). The resulting circuit current is I (r.m.s.). (b) phasor sum It follows that the circuit can either be effectively inductive or capacitive depending upon which voltage drop (V or Vc) is predominant. If VV then the net voltage drop across L-C combination is V-Vc and their resultant is in the direction V, represented by BC. Therefore, the applied voltage V is the phasor sum of V, and VL-V, and represented by AC.\nV2 = V2 +(V1 −V1)2\nV=√V2+(V1-V1)2 = √(IR)2+(IX, -IX)2\n=I√(R)2 +(X1−X2)2 = IZ\nWhere Z=√(R)+(X-X)2\nawaz\n...(15.28)\n...(15.29)\nThe quantity (X-X) is called the reactance of the circuit, denoted by X\ni.e., X = (XL-X\nFinally we can write\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n183\nZ = √R2+X2 is the opposition offered to current flow and is called impedance\nof the,circuit.\nR\nR\nCircuit power factor, coso\nZ √R2 +(X1-XC)2\n(15.30)\nSince XL, Xc and R, are known, phase angle & of the circuit can be determined.\ntano-V-V\nVR XX\nR R\n(15.31).\nSo if the current is represented by a cosine function, -lcpswit The source voltage leads the current by an angle and its equation is\nl=I_cos(wt+p)\nPower consumed, P=V Icos &\nFIGURE 15.18\nZ\nXL-Xc\nR\ne\nR\nX-X\nZ\n(d): impedance triangle\nThe impedance triangle is shown in Fig 15.18(c,d) and the Equation for impedance of a R-L-C series circuit is given by:\n(1)\nZ= √R2+(X2-Xc)2\nWhen XL - Xc is positive (i.e. X Xc), phase angle op is positive and the circuit will be inductive.\n(ii) When XL - Xe is negative (i.e. Xc XL), phase angle is negative and\nthe circuit is capacitive.\nPHYS CS-XII\nUNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT\nNOT FOR SALE\n184\n(iii) When XL - Xc =0, the circuit is purely resistive and phase angle -0° the circuit will then have unity power factor.\n+\nIf the equation for the applied voltage is V=V sinot, then equation for the circuit current will be",
