        "theory": "e in\nStress and strain take different forms in the situations shown in the Fig. (16.9). For small deformations the stress and strain are proportional to each other. This is known as Hooke&#x27;s law.\nThus,\nStress &lt;&lt; strain Stress=kx(strain)\n...(16.10)\nWhere, k is the proportionality constant and is known as modulus of elasticity. Hooke&#x27;s law is an empirical law and is found to be valid for most materials. However, there are some materials which do not exhibits this linear relationship\n16.3.1\nSTRESS-STRAIN CURVE\nEngineering stress and strain are usually measured using a machine tha\nstrains the material at a fixed linear rate and records the stress.\nThe applied stress is gradually increased in steps and the change in length\nnoted.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 16 PHYSICS OF SOLIDS\nNOT FOR SALE\n214\nThese values are then plotted on a graph. A typical graph for a metal is shown in Fig16.9. The stress-strain curves vary from material to material. These curves help us to understand, how a given material deforms with increasing loads. From the graph, we can see that in the region between 0 to A, the curve is\nUltimate\nlinear. In this FIGURE 16.9 region, Hooke&#x27;s law is obeyed. The body regains its original dimensions when the applied force is removed. In this region, the solid behaves as an elastic body. In the region from A to B, stress and strain\nElastice\nTensile Stress (Ou) region\nYield Point (0)\nStress\n002\nB\nD\nA\nPlastic\nmiyor. 2..\nregion\nsmatrate ne n\nni\nng to gnome new demon\nare not proportional..\nbeaubom\nNevertheless, the\nal nost\nbody still returns\nof\nOlot\nStress-strain curve\nto its original\ndimension when\nthe load is\nremoved. The point\nStraing to oulsy art (5)\nB in the curve is known as yield point (also known as elastic limit) and the corresponding stress is known as yield strength (a,) of the material.\nIf the load is increased further, the stress developed exceeds the yield strength and strain increases rapidly even for a small change in the stress. The region of the plasticity is represented by the portion of the curve between B and C. Where, point C in the Fig16.9: shows the ultimate tensile strength (a) of the material. If the stress is increased beyond point C, the material is said to be permanently changed, and the body does not regain its original dimension, In this case, even when the stress is zero, the strain is not zero. This type of deformation is called plastic deformation. At point D the material cannot be stretched further and smallest increase in stress can result in breaking the sample.\nnis:12\nIf the ultimate strength and fracture points C and D are close, the material is said to be brittle. If they are far apart, the material is said to be ductile.\n=y zuluboм2 gnudy\nOtx25.1 ms112\n(3)\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 16 PHYSICS OF SOLIDS\nEXAMPLE 16.1\nNOT FOR SALE\n212\nforce of 500N is applied to one end of a cylindrical steel rod of diameter 50cm. What is the tensile stress?\nGIVEN\nforce F= 500N, radius of rod r=D/2= 50cm/2=25cm\nREQUIRED\ntensile stress = ?\nSOLUTION\nforce F\nStress=\nSince\narea r 500 (25×102\n=2.5×105Nm2\nEXAMPLE 16.2\nThus the tensile stress in the rod is\n2.5 x105 Nm2\nIn an experiment to measure Young&#x27;s modulus, a load of 500Kg hanging from a steel wire 2.4m long, of cross section 16cm2, was found to stretch the wire 0.30cm from its un-stretched length. What is (a) the stress? (b) the strain (c) the value of Young&#x27;s modulus for the steel of which the wire is composed?\nGIVEN\nREQUIRED\nStretching in wire = x=0.30cm = 3x103m\nCross section of wire =A= 0.16cm2 = 1.6 x10 m2 Load= 500Kg, length of steel wire = L =2.4m\n(a) the stress=? (b) the strain=?\n(c) the Young&#x27;s modulus for the steel y = ?\nSOLUTION",
