        "theory": "Vienino\nIn absance of magnetic field,gm\nano ledet\n(ii) When a magnetizing force is applied to the substance, the molecular magnets are turned and tend to align in the same direction with N-pole of one molecular magnet facing the S-pole of other as shown in Fig.16.13 (b). The result is that magnetic field of the molecular magnets aid each other and two definite N and S-pole are developed near the ends of the specimen; the strength of the two poles being equal. Hence the substance gets magnetized. (iii) The extent of magnetization of the substance depends upon\nthe extent of alignment of molecular magnets. When all the molecular magnets are fully aligned, the substance is said to be saturated with magnetism.\nEULS\n200\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 16 PHYSICS OF SOLIDS\nNOT FOR SALE\n219\n(iv)When a magnetized substance (or a magnet) is heated, the molecular. magnets acquire kinetic energy and some of them loss their arrangement. For this reason, a magnet loses some magnetism on heating.\n16.8 MODERN VIEW ABOUT MAGNETISM\nAccording to modern view, the magnetic properties of a substance are due to the motions of electrons (orbital and spin) in the atoms. We know that an atom consists of central nucleus with electrons revolving around the nucleus in different orbits. This motion of electrons is called orbital motion (Fig 16.14).\nFIGURE 16.14\n+\nN\nN\nMorbital\nS\nS\nAb.com/\nnucleus\ne&quot;\ntulete seria Jera\n12\nady bou\n+M.\nOrbital and spin motion of electronomiźnia „mu\nThe electrons also rotate around their own axis. This motion of electrons is called spin motion (Fig 16.14).\nDue to these two motions, each atom is equivalent to a current loop i.e. each atom behaves as a magnetic dipole.\n1. In the un-magnetized substances, the magnetic dipoles are randomly\noriented so that magnetic fields mutually cancel. When the substance&quot; is- magnetized, the magnetic dipoles are aligned in the same direction. Hence the substance shows net magnetism.\nism\n2. Since the revolving and spinning electrons in each atoms cause magnetism;\nno substance is non-magnetic. 3. It is important to note that spinning motion of electrons in particular is\nresponsible for magnetism of a substance. of sugh) ble badge on to far\nJongen gnorias y baileqan yldsow.\nPHYSICS-XII\n1.\nUNIT 16 PHYSICS OF SOLIDS\n16.8.1\nNOT FOR SALE\n220\nCLASSIFICATION OF MAGNETIC MATERIALS\nWe can classify materials into three categories f.e, diamagnetic, paramagnetic and ferromagnetic. The behaviour of these three classes of substances is different in an external magnetic field.\nPARAMAGNETIC MATERIAL\nIn these substances the orbital and spin axis of the electrons in an atom are so oriented\nthat their field support each other and the atom behaves\nlike a tiny\nFIGURE 16.15\nRandom\nAligned\nmagnet. These\nsubstances are\ncalled\nparamagnetic\nmaterials.\nWhen a\nNo applied magnetic field\nApplied magnetic field\nparamagnetic\nsubstance (e.g.\naluminium, antimony etc.) is placed in a magnetic field, the substance is weakly magnetized in the direction of the applied field (Figure 16.15). Therefore, a paramagnetic substance is weakly attracted by a strong magnet.\n2.\nDIAMAGNETIC MATERIALS\nwaz\nThe substances in which the orbits and spin axis of the electron in an atom are so oriented that their field cancel the effect of each other so that their resultant field is zero. Such substances are called diamagnetic materials. When a diamagnetic substance (e.g. copper, zinc, bismuth etc.) is placed in a magnetic field, the substance is weakly magnetized in a direction opposite to that the applied field (Figure 16.16). Therefore, a diamagnetic substance is weakly repelled by a strong magnet.\nPHYSICS-XII\nFIGURE 16.16\nUNIT 16 PHYSICS OF SOLIDS\nNOT FOR SALE\n221\nOpposing\nNone\nООС\n୦୦୦\nNo applied magnetic field\n3. FERROMAGNETIC MATERIALS\nApplied magnetic field",
