        "title": "16.9 MAGNETIC HYSTERESIS",
        "theory": "FIGURE 16.20\nB+\nNOT FOR SALE\n225\nB+\nH-\nH+\nH-\nH+\nB-\n(a) the hysteresis loop for hard magnetic material suitable for permanent magnet; (b) the hysteresis loop for soft magnetic material suitable for a\ntransformer core.\nMaterials with narrow hysteresis loops are easily magnetised and de- magnetised and known as soft magnetic materials.\nMagnetic Hysteresis results in the dissipation of wasted energy in the form of heat with the energy wasted being in proportion to the area of the magnetic hysteresis loop. The Hysteresis losses will always be a problem in AC transformers.\nHARD MAGNETIC MATERIALS: In order to create a permanent magnet, a material with a very fat hysteresis loop may be used (figure 16.20a). Such materials, once magnetised, are very difficult to demagnetise and when the magnetising force is removed a substantial magnetic flux density remains. These materials are known as hard magnetic materials. Its examples are Tungsten steel, Cunife, Cobalt rare earth 1, Sintered ferrite 3, and Sintered\nalnico 8 etc.,\nMaterials with fat hysteresis loops are difficult to de-magnetise and magnetic flux density remains even magnetising force is removed are known as hard\nmagnetic materials.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 16 PHYSICS OF SOLIDS\nNOT FOR SALE\n226\nK\nYE\nY\nCrystalline solid: it consists of the particles (ion, molecule or atoms) arranged in definite geometric pattern in the three dimensional network.\nPolycrystalline: a material made up of many small single crystals. Amorphous (Non-crystalline) Solid: it is composed of randomly\norientated atoms, ions, or molecules that do not form defined patterns or lattice structures.\nLattice: the collection of infinite number of points in a periodic arrangement.\nP Unit cell: the smallest geometric figure or unit whose periodic\nO repetition in two or three dimension form a crystal.\nN\nElastic modulus: the ratio of stress to strain.\nHooke&#x27;s law: for small deformations the stress and strain are\nT proportional to each other.\nHardness: it is a measure of how easily a material can be scratched. Brittle :a material that has a tendency to break easily.\nStiffness: the ability to resist bending.\nHysteresis: lagging of the magnetization of a ferromagnetic material, behind variations of the magnetizing field.\nawaMaterial\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 16 PHYSICS OF SOLIDS\nNOT FOR SALE\n227\nEXERCISE\nChoose the best possible answer\n1\nThe shear modulus of elasticity G is\n(a) Al/FO (b) FL/AB (c) F/AB\n(d) Al/F\n(a) Knee temperature\n(c) absolute temperature\nThe temperature at which material becomes superconductor is called\n(b) critical temperature\n(d) zero temperature\n(3\nWhich of the following is not a ferromagnetic material?\n(a) Iron\n(b) Nickel\n(c) Cobalt\n(d) Barium\nThe ratio of stress to the strain is\n(a) 0\n(b) &gt;1\n(c) Constant\n(d) &lt;1\nHOTCE\n5 A wire is stretched to double of its length. Its strain is\n(a) 2\n(b) 1\n(c) 0\n(d) 0.5\n6 Which of the modulus of elasticity is involved in compressing a rod\n7\nto decrease its length?\n(a) Young&#x27;s modulus\n(c) Modulus of elasticity\n(b) Bulk modulus (d) Fracture\nA wire is stretched to double of its length. The strain is:\n(a) 2\n(b) 1",
