        "id": "17.1",
        "title": "Learning outcomes & introduction",
        "theory": "UNIT 17 ELECTRONICS\nNOT FORSALE\n231\nElectronics is the science that deals with electronic circuits that operate by controlling the flow of electrons or other electrically charged particles or electronics deals with flow of charge (electron) through non-metal conductors (semiconductors)...\nImagine life without electronic devices. There would be no TV&#x27;s, no computers, and poor medical diagnostic equipment. The developments in semiconductor technology\nduring the past 50 years have made electronic devices smaller, faster, and more reliable. In this unit we will discuss the important applications of\nsemiconductors.\nFOR YOUR INFORMATION\nDUCT\n17.1 SEMICONDUCTOR\nSemiconductors are materials\nhaving an intermediate electric conductivity between conductors and insulators.\nA perfect, pure semiconductor crystal containing no impurities is called an intrinsic semiconductor.\nA semiconductor is considered to be pure when there is less than one impurity atom in a billion host atoms. In a silicon crystal at low temperature, the bonding arrangement is shown in Fig17.1. In the Fig 17.1: the hatched circles represent the core of\n3000\ncalled a chip or microchip, is a\nAn integrated circuit (IC), sometimes\nsemiconductor wafer on which thousands or millions of tiny resistors, capacitors, and transistors are fabricated. An IC can function as an amplifier, oscillator, timer, counter, computer memory, or microprocessor. Any device working under Digital Techniques are called Digital Systems and the Electronic Network used to make them operational is called Digital referred as Modern Digital Electronics. Circuits. The subject as a whole is often Digital calculators, watches, modern\ncommunication systems and computers are\nwidely used in everyday life.\nthe silicon atom. The four valence electrons are shown by the small black dots. The probability of valence electrons being in any place between the bonding atoms is indicated by the dotted curves. When temperature approaches to OK, then all valence electrons are strongly bound to their atoms.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 17 ELECTRONICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n232\nThe electrons completely occupy the valence band leaving the conduction band vacant. The energy band structure of a semiconductor is characterized by a valence band and a conduction band separated by the bandgap E, as shown in the Fig 17.2 a. Electrons in the valence band do not possess enough energy to jump\ninto the conduction band.\nFIGURE 17.1\n+4 Si or Ge\nCovalent\nbonds\nBonding\nelectrons\nTwo dimensional representation\nof Si or Ge structure. +4 symbols indicate\ninner cores of Si or Ge.\nTherefore, an externally applied electric field cannot cause a flow of current and the semiconductor nearly at OK behaves as an insulator.\nFIGURE 17.2\nn\nElectron energy\nEr\nE\n(a)\n(a) bandgap energy E\nEc\nE.\nEv\n(b)\n(b) electron jumping from\nvalence band to conduction band.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 17 ELECTRONICS\n17.2 SEMICONDUCTORS AT HIGH TEMPERATURE\nNOT FOR SALE\n233\nAt temperatures higher than 0 K the bonds start to break due to the absorption of thermal energy. This process results in the creation of mobile electrons and holes. The bandgap energy E, is required to break such a covalent bond where E1 =0.72 eV for Ge and E, -1.1 ev for Si at room temperature (300° k). Those electrons, which acquire potential energy equal to, or in excess of, the bandgap energy E, are excited from the valence band to the conduction band. Thus, the bandgap energy E, is the minimum amount of energy required to excite an electron from valence band to conduction band.\nWhen a covalent bond breaks and electron jumps to the conduction band, an empty state arises in the valence band. Figure 17.3 shows a situation when a covalent bond is broken and one electron departs from the bond leaving a so-\ncalled hole behind.\nFIGURE 17.3\nab.com\nSI\nSI\nSI\nक\nSI\nSI\nFree election\n-Hola\nSi\nSI\n51\nSI\nSI\nSI\nSI\nModel of bond breaking by thermal energy, production\nof free electrons and generation of holes.\nWhen a bond is broken so that a hole is exist, it is easy for a valence -e in a neighboring atom to leave its covalent bond in order to fill this hole, which in turn leaves a hole in its original position. Hence, holes move in opposite direction to that of electron and participate in the conduction of electricity. The electron moves in the conduction band and the hole moves in the valence\nband.\nWe can also define a semiconductor as intrinsic semiconductor in which free electrons and holes are created only by excitation of electrons from the valence band to the conduction band. If N denotes the number density of&lt;svg viewBox=&quot;0 0 460 210&quot; role=&quot;img&quot; aria-label=&quot;Electronics&quot; style=&quot;width:min(100%,460px);height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #dbeafe;border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M55 170H420M75 185V25&quot; stroke=&quot;#334155&quot; stroke-width=&quot;2&quot;/&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;220&quot; y=&quot;202&quot; fill=&quot;#334155&quot; font-size=&quot;13&quot;&gt;x / independent variable&lt;/text&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;12&quot; y=&quot;22&quot; fill=&quot;#334155&quot; font-size=&quot;13&quot;&gt;y&lt;/text&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M175 130L230 45L285 130Z&quot; fill=&quot;#fef3c7&quot; stroke=&quot;#b45309&quot; stroke-width=&quot;3&quot;/&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;204&quot; y=&quot;122&quot; font-size=&quot;14&quot;&gt;p-n&lt;/text&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;",
