        "theory": "212\nN\nØv.\nVO\nLE\nInput signal\nCL\nR\nR\nE\nL\nVac\nOutput signal\n1814\nOutput signal\nInput signal\nA simple circuit of common emitter NPN transistor amplifier\nTransistor as an amplifier\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 17 ELECTRONICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n248\nAn electrical signal can be amplified by using a device which allows a small current or voltage to control the flow of a much larger current from a DC power source. Transistors are the basic device providing control of this kind. The basic configurations of transistor amplifier circuits are the common base, the common emitter, and the common collector. All these configurations can be used as transistor amplifier. Because the common-emitter circuit configurations the most often used, it is described in Fig 17.17 here. The same principles apply to the common-base and common-collector circuits.\n17.7.4 TRANSISTOR AS A SWITCH\nTransistor is used in a great variety of circuits. Transistor switches form the basis of all electronic\ncomputers. With the switch FIGURE 17.18\nclosed, base current\ncausing collector to flow.\nThe output\nvoltage\nflow\nis\n5 vot\nVCE =0V (Fig 17.18. a). The clock Signal battery voltage is dropped across the load causing the collector voltage to fall to a very low value.\n5 vol\nThe transistor is said to be saturated. With the switch open no base current flow, therefore no collector current can flow. Clock Signal The transistor is said to be cut off.VCE +V (Fig 17.18 b). These two states are described as.0 and 1, or low and high.\nEXAMPLE 17.1\n-\nTransistor switched On\n(a) No signal to centre of Transistor\ne in\nTransistor switched Off\n(b) Signal to centre of Transistor\nTransistor ON-OFF switch\nA transistor is connected in a CE configuration. The collector supply voltage is 10V and the voltage drop across the 5002 connected in the collector circuit is 0.6V. If a = 0.96, find the (a) collector-emitter voltage (b) base current, and (c) the emitter current.\nGIVEN\ncollector supply voltage V 10V,\ncollector voltage V,= 0.6V,\nresistance R= 500\nα = 0.96,\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 17 ELECTRONICS\nREQUIRED\nSOLUTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n249\n(a) collector-emitter voltage V. =?\n(b) base current =?, and (c) the emitter current le =?\nTo collector current,\n|c=\nV 0.6 R. 500\n- 1.2 mA\n(1) Collector-emitter voltage V = Vcc - Vc = 10-0.6\n(2)\na = &#x27;s\n1.2\nor l\n1.25mA\na 0.96\n(3)\nle = 18+ Ic or\n=9.4V\n18=IE-Ic=1.25 -1.2-.05mA\nEXAMPLE 17.2\nThe constant o of a transistor is 0.95. What would be the change in the collector current corresponding to a change of 0.4mA in the base current in a common-emitter arrangement?\nGIVEN\nREQUIRED\ncurrent-gain &amp; -0.95, base current /= 0.4mA\nSOLUTION\nchange in the collector current lc = ?",
