        "theory": "The current gain of a transistor in common-emitter arrangement. is B, which is related to its current-gain o in common-base arrangement\nα\nB=-\n1-a\n0.95\nB=\n=19\n1-0.95\nBut, B is the ratio of change in collector current to the change in base current.\nB=\nΔΙ\nE\nAlb\nor Al =BxAl2 = 19×0.4x10 A=7.6mA\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 17 ELECTRONICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n250\nK\n&lt;E&gt;\nY\nPOINTS\nSemiconductors: those materials whose conductivity lying in between conductors and insulators i.e, silicon and germinium.\nIntrinsic semiconductor: Chemically pure semiconductors.\nCarrier in pure semiconductor: Majority charge carrier in N-type are electrons and in P-type are holes.\nAn N-type semiconductor: when a pure semiconductor is doped with a pentavalent impurity\nA P-type semiconductor: when a pure semiconductor is doped with a trivalent impurity.\nT Rectification: the conversion of AC into DC.\nRectifier: a device used for rectification.\nTransistor types: PNP or NPN transistor.\nAmplification: an increase in voltage or current level.\naze.in\naw\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 17 ELECTRONICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n251\nEXERCISE\nChoose the best possible answer\nIn an N-type silicon, which of the following statement is true\n(a) Electrons are majority carriers and trivalent atoms are the dopants. (b) Electrons are minority carriers and pentavalent atoms are the dopants. (c) Holes are minority carriers and pentavalent atoms are the dopants. (d) Holes are majority carriers and trivalent atoms are the dopants.\n2 The barrier potential of silicon diode at room temperature is.\n(a) 0.3V (b) 0.7V (c) 0.9V\n(d) 1.0v\n3 The width of depletion region of a junction\n(a) Increases under forward bias\n(b) is independent of applied voltage\n(c), Increases under reverse bias\nE\n(d) Remains same\nS\nFor common emitter configuration of NPN transistor, current gain is given by\n(a) Ic/ls.\n(b) Ic/le\n(c) le/la\n(d) la/le\nThe reverse saturation current in a PN junction diode is only due to\n(a) majority carriers\n(c) acceptor ions\n(b) minority carriers\n(d) donor ions\n6 PN junction when reversed biased act as:\n(a) Capacitor (b) ON switch (c) Inductor (d) OFF Switch\nImproper biasing of a transistor circuit produces\n(a) Heavy loading of emitter current\n(b) Distortion in the output signal.\n(c) Excessive heat at collector terminal\n(d) Faulty location of load line\n8 When transistors are used in digital circuits they usually operate in the:\n(a) Active region\n(b) Breakdown region\n(c) Saturation and cutoff regions\n(d) linear region\nPHYSICS-XI\nUNIT 17 ELECTRONICS\nM\nNOT FOR SALE\n252\nMost of the electrons in the base of an NPN transistor flow:\n(a) out of the base lead",
