      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "233",
      "At temperatures higher than 0 K the bonds start to break due to the absorption of thermal energy. This process results in the creation of mobile electrons and holes. The bandgap energy E, is required to break such a covalent bond where E1 =0.72 eV for Ge and E, -1.1 ev for Si at room temperature (300° k). Those electrons, which acquire potential energy equal to, or in excess of, the bandgap energy E, are excited from the valence band to the conduction band. Thus, the bandgap energy E, is the minimum amount of energy required to excite an electron from valence band to conduction band.",
      "When a covalent bond breaks and electron jumps to the conduction band, an empty state arises in the valence band. Figure 17.3 shows a situation when a covalent bond is broken and one electron departs from the bond leaving a so-",
      "called hole behind.",
      "FIGURE 17.3",
