        "id": "18.1",
        "title": "Learning outcomes & introduction",
        "theory": "UNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nLEARNING\nOUTCOMES\nNOT FORSALE\n255\ndescribe the concept of black body radiation.\ndescribe how energy is distributed over the wavelength range for several values of source temperature.\ndescribe the Planck&#x27;s hypothesis that radiation emitted and absorbed by the walls of a black body cavity is quantised.\nelaborate the particle nature of electromagnetic radiation.\ndescribe the phenomenon of photoelectric effect.\nsolve problems and analyse information using: Ehf and c = fλ. identify data sources, gather, process and present information to summarise the use of the photoelectric effect in solar cells &amp; photocells. describe the confirmation of de Broglie&#x27;s proposal by Davisson and Germer\nexperiment in which the diffraction of electrons by the surface layers of a crystal lattice was observed.\ndescribe the impact of de Broglie&#x27;s proposal that any kind of particle has both wave and particle properties.\nexplain the particle model of light in terms of photons with particular energy and frequency.\ndescribe Compton effect qualitatively.\nexplain the phenomena of pair production and pair annihilation.\nexplain how the very short wavelength of electrons, and the ability to use electrons and magnetic fields to focus them, allows electron microscope to achieve very high resolution.\n⚫ describe uncertainty principle.\nAt the end of the 19th century, scientists thought that they had discovered nearly everything about physics. Newton&#x27;s laws of motion and his theory of universal gravitation, Maxwell&#x27;s theoretical work in combining electricity and magnetism, and the laws of thermodynamics effectively explain all the\nknown physics.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n256\nHowever, at the turn of the 20th century, two major concepts improved our understanding of nature. In 1900 Planck provided the basic ideas that led to the quantum theory, and in 1905 Einstein formulated his special theory of relativity. These theories inspired new developments and theories in the fields of atomic, nuclear, and condensed-matter physics.\nFor your information\nGPS\nAccording to relativity theory, moving clock appears to run slow with respect to a similar clock that is at rest. The satellites are constantly moving relative to observers on the Earth which causes them to run at slightly faster rate than clocks on the Earth&#x27;s surface.\nA calculation using General Relativity predicts that the clocks in each GPS satellite should get ahead of ground-based clocks by 45 microseconds per day. So the role of special theory of relativity is important in GPS.\n18.1 REFERENCE FRAMES\nWaze\nA coordinate system in which an experimenter makes position and time measurements of physical events is called a reference frame.\nIn the theory of special relativity, an event, is a physical &quot;happening&quot; that occurs at a certain place and time. To record the event, experimenter uses a reference frame that consists of a set of x, y, z axes (called a coordinate system) and time measuring device such as a clock. Reference frames are used to specify the relationship between a moving observer and the event. However, in some physical situations, it is impractical to choose a reference system with a fixed origin.\nPHYSICS-XII\nN\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FORSALE\n257\nConsider, for example, a jet plane landing on an aircraft carrier that is going forward at full throttle at the same time as shown in figure 18.1. You want to describe the plane&#x27;s motion in a coordinate system fixed to the carrier, even though the carrier is moving. The reason why this is important is that the plane needs to come to rest relative to the carrier at some fixed location on the deck.\nFIGURE 18.1\nilab\nA. Inertial Reference Frame\nJet landing on aircraft carrier\nAn inertial frame is one in which an object subjected to no forces moves in a straight line at constant speed.\nFIGURE 18.2\nY\nY\nV=V1\nS1\nZ\nX\nZ\nVelocities observed in different reference frames\nPHYSICS-XI\nX\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n258\nB. Non-inertial Reference Frame\nAn accelerating frame of reference is called a non-inertial frame of reference. Any frame or system moving with constant velocity with respect to an inertial system must also be an inertial, thus there is no single, preferred inertial frame, as shown in figure 18.2\nThe earth-based reference frame is not exactly an inertial reference frame because it is subjected to centripetal acceleration as the earth spins on its axis and revolves around the sun. In most situations, the effects of these accelerations are small, and we can neglect them. Therefore the earth&#x27;s surface can be considered as inertial reference frame in many situations.\nFor example, when analyzing the motion of a cricket ball, the fact that Earth rotates about its axis does not have much effect. But if we want to analyze the motion of a meteor falling from a great distance toward Earth, Earth&#x27;s rotation must be considered.\n18.2 SPECIAL THEORY OF RELATIVITY\nIn 1905 Albert Einstein proposed a theory that completely changed our views of space and time. He based his special theory of relativity on two postulates\n1. The principle of relativity: The laws of physics are the same in all inertial reference frames.\nJa\nThe first postulate declares that the laws of physics are absolute, universal, and the same for all inertial observers. Laws that hold for one inertial observer cannot be violated for any inertial observer. Einstein&#x27;s principle of relativity means that any kind of experiment-mechanical, thermal, optical, or electrical-performed in a laboratory at rest must give the same result when performed in a laboratory moving at a constant speed past the first one.\nFor example the pouring of tea in an aeroplane moving with constant speed is same\nas that on earth.\n2. The principle of constancy of the speed of light: The speed of light in a vacuum has the same value, c = 2.997 924 58 x 10 m/s, in all inertial reference frames, regardless of the velocity of the observer or the velocity of the source\nemitting the light.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n259\nThe second postulate is more difficult to accept because it seems to go against our &#x27;common sense,&#x27; which we observe in everyday experiences.\nFIGURE 18.3\nTorch A\nn\nTorch B&lt;svg viewBox=&quot;0 0 460 210&quot; role=&quot;img&quot; aria-label=&quot;Dawn of Modern Physics&quot; style=&quot;width:min(100%,460px);height:auto;background:#fff;border:1px solid #dbeafe;border-radius:10px;margin:12px 0&quot;&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M55 170H420M75 185V25&quot; stroke=&quot;#334155&quot; stroke-width=&quot;2&quot;/&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;220&quot; y=&quot;202&quot; fill=&quot;#334155&quot; font-size=&quot;13&quot;&gt;x / independent variable&lt;/text&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;12&quot; y=&quot;22&quot; fill=&quot;#334155&quot; font-size=&quot;13&quot;&gt;y&lt;/text&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M100 150H380M130 150V70M220 150V40M310 150V15&quot; stroke=&quot;#7c3aed&quot; stroke-width=&quot;4&quot;/&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;132&quot; y=&quot;65&quot; font-size=&quot;13&quot;&gt;E₁&lt;/text&gt;&lt;text x=&quot;224&quot; y=&quot;35&quot; font-size=&quot;13&quot;&gt;E₂&lt;/text&gt;&lt;path d=&quot;M130 70L220 40&quot; stroke=&quot;#dc2626&quot; stroke-width=&quot;2&quot;/&gt;&lt;/svg&gt;",
