        "title": "18.2 SPECIAL THEORY OF RELATIVITY",
        "theory": "C. LENGTH CONTRACTION\nThe length of an object measured within its rest frame is called proper length AL,. Observers in different frames in relative motion will always measure the length to be shorter called length contraction AL.\nAL = AL\n18.2\nD. RELATIVISTIC MASS\nThe mass of an object measured within its rest frame is called rest mass Am,. Observers in different frames in relative motion will always measure the mass to be greater called relativistic mass Am.\nila\nAm=\nAm\nE. MASS ENERGY EQUIVALENCE\n18.3\nAL\nThe length contraction takes place only along the direction of motion.\nThe mass of an object is equivalent to certain quantity of energy, Thus mass can be converted into energy and conversely energy can be converted into mass. By the Einstein&#x27;s famous equation\nE = Amc2\n18.4\nIn pair production of particle and antiparticle energy is converted into mass, whereas in particle annihilation with antiparticle the entire mass is converted into\nenergy.\nF. SPEED LIMIT\nCalculation of expanded times and contracted lengths involve the expression\nSince times and lengths are real measurements, they must be represented by real numbers, so the value under the square root must be a positive real number. For\nthis to be true,\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n264\nThis implies that v&lt; c. Consider what happens to At when v\napproaches c in (v=c):\nAtp\nAtp\nAt=\nor\nAt=\n1- 2\nSPEED\nLIMIT\n3x 10 m/s\n무\nог\nAtp\nAt=\n√1-1\nThe denominator approaches zero, therefore At = ∞\nDivision of a non-zero real number by zero is undefined so an object&#x27;s speed must be less than the speed of light. This speed limit applies only to material objects.\nALBERT EINSTEIN (1879-1955)\nAlbert Einstein was born on March 14, 1879 in Germany. In 1905, Einstein published three papers. The first was his explanation of the photoelectric effect. The second was on mathematical interpretation of Brownian motion. The third set out special relativity, revising our conceptions of time, length and energy. In 1933, he accepted a post at the Institute for Advanced Studies at Princeton (USA), where he stayed until his death on April 18, 1955.\nAPPLICATION OF TIME DILATION AND LENGTH CONTRACTION FOR SPACE\nTRAVEL\nThe special theory of relativity suggest that due to time dilation and length contraction effects our journey to distant places in universe will take less time. For example if a star is 8 light years away and we move to it in a spaceship with half the speed of light. From the point of view of spaceship-crew the length of the journey will be contracted.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n265\nEXAMPLE 18.1\nSPACE TRAVEL\nAlpha Centauri is nearest star to our sun. It is at 4.37 light years from earth, it means that it takes 4.37 years for light to reach from earth to Alpha Centauri. If a spaceship is sent to Alpha Centauri with a speed of v = 0.96 c. How much time would have passed for the passengers, when they reach it? Assume that the earth and Alpha Centauri are stationary with respect to one another.\nGIVEN\nREQUIRED\ntime for light to travel &#x27;t&#x27; = 4.37 years, Speed &#x27;v&#x27;=0.96c Proper time &#x27;t,&#x27; =?\nSOLUTION\nThe dilated time for person on earth is At=\nThe passenger on ship measures the proper time as\n4.37 years 0.96\n= 4.55 years\nAtp = Atx\nputting values At, = 4.55 years x1-\n(0.96c)\n0.92c2\n4.55 years x\ntherefore\nAt, =1.29 years\nAnswer\nThus, the passengers in spaceship will have aged only 1.29 years when they reach Alpha Centauri, and not the 4.55 years an observer on earth has calculated.\nASSIGNMENT 18.1\nMETER ROD\nHow fast must a meter stick be moving if its length is observed to shrink to one-\nhalf of a meter?\n(Answer: 2.60 x 10 m/s)\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS",
