        "title": "18.4 BLACK BODY RADIATION",
        "theory": "NOT FOR SALE\n266\n18.4 BLACK BODY RADIATION\nThe electromagnetic radiation emitted by objects because of their temperature are called thermal radiation.\nAll bodies, no matter how hot or cold, continuously radiate electromagnetic waves. For example, we see the glow of very hot objects because they emit electromagnetic waves in the visible region of the spectrum. Our sun, which has a surface temperature of about 6000 K, appears yellow, while the cooler star Betelgeuse has a red-orange appearance due to its lower surface temperature of 2900 K. However, at relatively low temperatures, cooler objects emit visible light waves only weakly and, as a result, do not appear to be glowing. The human body, at only 310 K, does not emit enough visible light to be seen in the dark. However the human body does emit electromagnetic waves in the infrared region of the spectrum, and these can be detected with infrared-sensitive devices.\nA perfect black-body at a constant temperature absorbs and re-emits all the electromagnetic radiation that falls on it.\nA blackbody or perfect absorber is also an ideal radiator. In practice, a small opening in any heated cavity, behaves like a blackbody because such an opening traps all incident radiation. If the direction of the radiation is reversed in figure 18.6, the light emitted by a small opening is in thermal equilibrium with the walls, because it has been absorbed and re-emitted many times. The radiations emitted by black body are called black body radiations.\nScience Tidbits Glowing bolt\nThe glowing bolt in this picture radiates primarily In the infrared part of the spectrum, but it is hot enough (a few thousand kelvin) so that a significant portion of its radiation falls within the red end of the visible region. The other bolts are too cool to radiate any detectable amount of visible light.\nPHYSICS-XII\nFIGURE 18.6\nLight\nPractical Black Body\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nFIGURE 18.6\nThe nature of the radiation from the cavity hole of black body depends upon the cavity walls. Experimental data for the distribution of energy of the black body at two different temperatures is shown in the figure. The radiation emitted (the area\nRadiant energy per\nunit wavelength\nNOT FOR SALE\n267\nvisible\n6000 K\n4000 K\nunder the curve) increases by increasing temperature.\n0\n500\n1000 Wavelength (nm)\n1500\nA. WEIN&#x27;S DISPLACEMENT LAW\nWith the increase in the wavelength the peak of distribution shifts toward shorter wavelength as in figure 18.6. This shift is found to obey Wien&#x27;s displacement Law. If &#x27;... is the wavelength at which the curve peak and &#x27;T&#x27; is absolute temperature\nthen\nλ-max\nor max\nConstant\nT\nor T-Constant\n18.5\nWhere constant=0.2898x101m K.\nB. STEPHEN-BOLTSMANN LAW\nThe area under the curve represents the total energy radiated per second per square metre over all wavelengths at particular temperature. It is found that area is directly proportional to the fourth power of absolute temperature.\nEx T1 or E=σ T\n18.6\nWhere the constant σ = 5.67x 10&quot; Wm K is called Stephen constant. And the relation 18.6 is called as Stephen-Boltsmann Law.\nSeveral attempts were made to completely explain the black body spectrum, but all of them failed due to one reason or another. For example Raleigh-Jean presented a theory that could predict long wavelengths but was unable to predict short wavelengths. Similarly Wein presented a theory that explained short wavelengths but failed at longer wavelengths.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n268\n18.5 QUANTUM THEORY OF RADIATION (PLANCK&#x27;S THEORY) In 1900 Max plank presented a theory that was in complete agreement with experiments at all wavelengths, as shown in figure 18.7. In order to explain the spectrum of black-body radiation max plank made the two assumptions:\n1. The vibrating molecules of the black body cavity which emitted these radiations were having only discrete amount of energy E., given by\nE1 = nhf\n&lt;\n18.7\nFIGURE 18.7\nWhere n is the positive integer (n =1, 2, 3,......) called quantum number. is the frequency of oscillations of molecules and h = 6.626 x 10&quot; Js is the plank&#x27;s constant. It means that atoms or molecules cannot emit or absorb radiation in all possible ways, but an oscillating atom or molecule can absorb or emit energy only in discrete bundles. This discreat unit of energy is called quanta or photon.\n2. Atoms or molecules emit or absorb radiation when they jump from one quantum state to another. Hence the energy of one quantum (that is the difference between two adjecent energy levels) is\nE=hf\nAtoms or molecules will emit or absorb energy only when it changes states if it remain in one quantum state no energy is emitted or absorbed.\nIntensity\nExperimental\ndata\nFIGURE 18.8\nEnergy\n8॰ ཙུདྷོཊྛོརྗ॰\nPlanck&#x27;s\ntheory\nClassical\ntheory\n(Rayleigh-Jeans\nWavelength\n།\nlaw)\n5\n4hf\n3hf\n2hf\nhf\nE\n2\nAllowed energy levels according to Planck&#x27;s original hypothesis for an",
