        "theory": "REQUIRED\nCutoff wavelength λ = ?\n=4.52 eV x1.6x10-19 C = 7.232x10-19 J Maximum kinetic energy &#x27;KE&#x27; =?\nStopping potential &#x27;V&#x27; =? · Wavelength &#x27;2&#x27; 198 nm\nSOLUTION\nhc\n(a) The cutoff wavelength λ, is\nλε\nPutting values λ =\n6.626x10-34x2.998 × 108 ms/ 7.232 × 10-19 /\ntherefore λ = 275 nm\nAnswer\n(b) The Maximum kinetic energy &#x27;KE...&quot; at shorter wavelength &#x27;X&#x27; is\ne in\nhc\nKE max\nhf - 4\nог KE\n2.\nPutting values KE\nmax\n6.626×10 J× ph\n6.626 × 10-3⁄4 J × 2.998 × 10 -7.232×10-19 J\n198× 10 gh\ntherefore KEmax = 2.79×10-19 J\nAnswer\n(c) The stopping potential &quot;V.&quot; Vo\nKE\nmax\ne\nPutting values V1 =\n2.79×10-19 J\n1.6×10-19 C\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n2752\ntherefore V, = 1.74V\nAnswer\nASSIGNMENT 18.2\nSODIUM METAL\nila\nA sodium surface is illuminated with light of wavelength 300 nm. The work function for sodium is 2.46eV. Find (a) the K.E of the ejected electrons and (b) The cut-off wavelength for sodium.\n(Answer: (a) 2.7 x 10&quot; J (or 1.68 eV); (b) 5.05x 10 m (or 505 nm)\nScience Tidbits Photocell\nA device in which the photoelectric effect or photoconductivity is used to produce a current or voltage when exposed to light or other electromagnetic radiation.\nA simple photocell is shown in the figure. It consist of evacuated glass tube with thin anode rod and cathode of appropriate surface. The material of cathode is selected whose work function suit the frequency of incident radiation on which the photocell is operated. When this surface is exposed to light electrons are emitted which are collected at cathode. As a result current flows in the external circuit. This current increases with intensity and stops when light beam is interrupted.\nCollecting wire\nPhotocell\nMetal plate\nApplied voltage\nAmmeter\nBurglar alarms and automatic doors often make use of the photocell circuit. When a person interrupts the beam of light, the sudden drop in current in the circuit activates a switch which operates a bell or opens the door. Photocells are used in many other devices, such as absorption spectrophotometers, to measure light intensity.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n276\n18.7 COMPTON EFFECT\nThe scattering of high frequency photon strikes a stationary electron the frequency of photon is decreased after collision. This phenomenon is referred to as Compton&#x27;s effect.\nFIGURE 18.14\nX-ray source\ndetector\nIncident Photon\nΠλ\n0\nӨ\ntarget\nThe change in wavelength\nangle at which\nphotons are\nscattered\ndepends upon the\nThe experimental arrangement of Compton effect\nEXPERIMENTAL ARRANGEMENT\nThe experimental arrangement of Compton effect is shown in the figure 18.14. A beam of X-rays having wavelength &#x27;X&#x27; is directed towards a target of graphite. The scattered photons have wavelength &quot;X&quot; slightly longer, the amount of energy reduction depended upon the angle at which the X-rays were scattered.\nThe change in wavelength AX, between the scattered X-ray photon of wavelength and incident X-ray photon of wavelength A is called Compton Shift.\nThe X-ray photon of frequency &#x27;f&#x27; is incident on a stationary electron.\nCOMPTON (1892-1962)\n7\nArthur H. Compton (America).",
