        "sciNote": "His work on X-ray scattering verified Einstein's photon theory and earned him the 1927 Nobel Prize.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nThe initial energy of photon by Plancks theory is 'hf' and initial rest energy for electron by Eienstein equation is 'm,c''. After collision the energy of photon reduce to hf' and electron is recoiled with total energy 'mc\" as shown in figure 18.15.\nBy law of conservation of energy\nNOT FOR SALE\nFIGURE 18.15\nX-\nIncident Photon\nEphoton) = hf\nY\nElectron at rest\nEelectron) =mc2\n277\nScattered Photon\nEriphoton) = hf\nRecoiling Electron Ef electron) = mc2\nX\nConservation of energy in Compton Scattering\nEphoton) +E(electron) = E(photon) +Ef(electron)\nsubstituting values hf+m,c2 = hf+mc2\nThe initial momentum of photon is 'hf/c' and since electron is considered at rest therefore it have zero momentum. After collision the momentum of photon is 'hf/c' making an angle '0' with its original direction and electron is recoiled with momentum 'my' making an angle o, as shown in figure 18.16.\nFIGURE 18.16\nScattered Photon\nIncident Photon\nhf\nPfxiphoton)\nhf cose\nIncident Photon\nPix photon)\nC\nPly.photon = 0\nScattered Photon\nhf Ply.photon)\n-sine\nC\nElectron at rest\nPix.(electron)=0\nRecoiling Electron Pfx.(electron) = mv cosq\nalong x-axis\nElectron at rest\nPly,electron +0\nRecoiling Electron Pfy.lelectron) = my singo\nmv\nalong y-axis\nConservation of momentum in Compton Scattering\nBy law of conservation of momentum\nAlong x-axis Pix,(photon) +Pix,(electron) =Pfx.",
