        "theory": "C C he\n-\nff mc-cose)\nC C h\n- + ffm.c\n(1-cose)\nλ\nhere- and -&gt;\nTherefore λ=λ+ (1-cose)\nFIGURE 18.17\n0=45&quot;\nX-ray intensity\n8-90°\nh\nm.c\nh\nor 2&#x27;-λ =\n-(1-cose)\nm.c\nhence AX=\nh\nm.c\n-(1-cose)\n18.11\n=35°\nThe quantity h/m,c is constant is called as Compton wavelength 2.43 x 10&quot; m.\n70\n75\nWavelength pm\nCompton&#x27;s results for scattering of X rays with A= 71 pm is shown in figure 18.17. Right-hand peak shows the wavelength shift of the Compton effect. The un-shifted left-hand peak is from photons scattering off tightly bound atomic electrons, which don&#x27;t absorb significant energy. Solid curves are theoretical predictions. These results shows that the Compton&#x27;s theoretical approach agrees with the experimental observations hence the photon concept is a valid concept.\nToday, Compton scattering with gamma rays is a widely used technique for studying the structure of matter.\nPHYSICS-XII\nila\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n279\nEXAMPLE 18.4\nCARBON SCATTERING\nX-ray of wavelength λ= 0.20 nm are scattered from a block of carbon. The scattered X-rays are observed at an angle of 45° to the incident beam. Calculate the wavelength of the scattered X-ray at this angle.\nGIVEN\nAngle 8 = 45°\nPlank&#x27;s constant h = 6.626 x 10°3 Js\nREQUIRED\nrest mass of electron m, 9.11 x 10 kg wavelength after scattering λ =? speed of light c = 3 x 10&quot; ms\nwavelength λ = 0.20 nm = 0.20 x 10&quot; m\nSOLUTION\nBy Compton&#x27;s scattering formula we have λ=λ+\nh\n(1-cose)\nm.c\n6.626×10-34Js\nPutting values = 0.20x10m+\n-(1-cos 45°)\n9.11x 1031 kgx3x 10 ms\ntherefore\nor λ=0.20x10m+7.11x10-13 m\nASSIGNMENT 18.3\nλ=2.00711x10m=0.200711nm\nAnswer\nCARBON SCATTERING\nX-rays of wavelength 0.140 nm are scattered from a very thin slice of carbon. What will be the wavelengths of X-rays scattered at (a) 0°, (b) 90°, (c) 180°? (Answer: (a) 0.140 nm. (b) 0.142 nm (c) 0.145 nm.)\nPHYSICS-XI\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n280\n18.8 PAIR PRODUCTION\nThe creation of an elementary particle and its antiparticle, usually when a photon (or another neutral boson) interacts with a nucleus an electron and its antiparticle, the positron, may be created is called pair production.\ny →e*+e ̄\nPair production is the process in which a photon is converted into matter in accordance with Einstein equation E = mc2. A photon when passes near a nucleus it disintegrates into electron and positron pair as shown in figure 18.18. Positron has the same mass as electron but opposite charge.\nFIGURE 18.18\nIncident photon\nNucleus\nNucleus\nBefore\nAfter\ne+\nPair Production in presence of nucleus\nA photon cannot create an electron alone, because this would violate law of conservation of charge. Thus to conserve charge a positron is created.\nIn pair production for any energy of incident photon an additional massive object (such as atomic nucleus) is required to carry some of momentum. Therefore to conserve momentum the presence of nucleus is required.\ne",
