        "theory": "Scattered\nPhoton\nParticle\nΔ.Χ.\nP + AP,\nIf &#x27;Ax&#x27; is very small, then &#x27;AP,&#x27; is large, and vice versa.\nThe incoming photon has momentum h/2, in collision, the photon can transfer part (or all) of its momentum to the electron (along the x-axis). Therefore, the uncertainty in the electron&#x27;s momentum after the collision is as great as the momentum of the incoming photon, mathematically\nΔΡ.\n1\nFurther, because the photon also has wave properties, we expect to be able to determine the electron&#x27;s position to within one wavelength of the light being used to view it, so\nΔχ πλ\n2\nmultiplying equation 1 and equation 2 we get\nΔ.Χ. Η ΔΡΑ\n火\nand\nAx-AP, h\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n290\nThe value &#x27;h&#x27; represents the minimum in the product of the uncertainties. Because the uncertainty can always be greater than this minimum, we have\nAx⚫ AP, ≥ h\n18.18\nIf &#x27;Ax&#x27; is very small, then &#x27;AP,&#x27; is large, and vice versa.\nAnother form of uncertainty principle can be obtained through similar reasoning. The product of uncertainty in measurement of energy &quot;AE&#x27; and the time &#x27;At&quot; available for the measurement is approximately equal to the Planck&#x27;s constant.\nΔΕ\nAE Ath.\n18.19\nThus our knowledge about a particle is inherently uncertain. There will always be uncertainty in a particle about exact momentum at a given position and exact energy at a given time.\nEXAMPLE 18.6\nUNCERTAINTY IN POSITION\nAn electron moves in a straight line with a constant speed 6.61 x 10 m/s which has been measured to a precision of 0.10%. What is the maximum precision with which its position could be simultaneously measured?\nGIVEN\nspeed &#x27;y&#x27; = 6.61 x 10 m/s\nmass of electron &#x27;m&#x27; 9.11 x 10-31 kg\nREQUIRED\nNaz\nposition precision &#x27;Ax&#x27; = ?\nprecision percentage &#x27;%AV = 0.10%\nPlank&#x27;s constant h = 6.626 x 10&quot; Js\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n291\nSOLUTION\nThe momentum &quot;P&quot; of the electron is P=mv\nPutting values P = 9.11× 103 kg-6.61×106 m/s\ntherefore P=6.02 × 10-24 kgm/s\nThe momentum is &#x27;P= mv&quot;, and the uncertainty in momentum &#x27;%AP&quot; is also = 10% which is AP = 0.0010 × P.\nAP = 0.0010×6.02 × 10:24 kgm/s\nor\nAP = 6.02 x 10:27 kgm/s\nFrom the uncertainty principle, the best simultaneous position measurement will have an uncertainty of\nAx AP h\nh\n6.626 × 10-34 Js\nor Ax\nPutting values Ax =\nAP\n6.02×10-27 kg m/s\ntherefore\nAx = 1.10 x10-7 m\nAnswer\nThis is about 1000 times the diameter of an atom.\nASSIGNMENT 18.5\nUNCERTAINTY IN ENERGY\nThe life time of electron in the excited state is about 10 s. What is the uncertainty in energy during this time?\n(Answer: 6.626 x 10m)\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n292\nPO-\nK Inertial reference frame: a reference frame moving with constant E velocity.\nNon-inertial reference frame: a frame of reference that is accelerating. Relativity: the study of how different observers measure the same event Postulates os Special theory of relativity: the two postulates are\nThe laws of physics are the same in all inertial frames.\nNo The speed of light in free space has the same value for all observers, T regardless of their state of motion\nS Black body: an ideal radiator, which can radiate equally well at all\nwavelengths",
