        "theory": "Quantized: the fact that certain physical entities exist only with particular discrete values and not every conceivable value\nPlank&#x27;s Constant: h=6.626 x 10&quot; Js\nPhoton: a quantum, or particle, of electromagnetic radiation\nPhoto electric effect: the phenomenon whereby some materials eject electrons when light is shined on them\nCompton effect: the phenomenon whereby x rays scattered from materials have decreased energy\nPair Production: the change of very high energy photon into electron -positron pair.\nAnnihilation of matter: when a positron comes close to an electron, they annihilate and produce two photons\nde Broglie wavelength: the wavelength possessed by a particle of matter Wave -particle duality: both radiation and matter exhibit particle as well as wave like properties\nHeisenberg uncertainty principle: fundamental limit to the precision with which pairs of quantities (momentum and position, and energy and time) can be measured\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nMULTIPLE CHOICE\nPUESTIONS\nExercise\nChoose the best possible answer\nNOT FOR SALE\n293\nTriplets A and B board spaceships and head away from Earth in opposite directions, each traveling the same distance at the same speed before returning to Earth. Triplet C remains on Earth. Which expression describes the triplets&#x27; relative ages once they&#x27;re reunited on Earth?\nA. A B C B. A=B&gt;C C. A=B&lt;C D.A=B-C\n2 If a spaceship travels from Earth to a planet in the Alpha Centauri star\nsystem, which observer measures the proper time for the trip?\nA. an observer on Earth\nB. an observer on the planet in the Alpha Centauri star system C. an observer on the spaceship\nD. an observer deep in space far from the planet and Earth\nWhen you compress a spring between your fingers, its mass\nA. increase B. decrease C. stay the same D. turns negative By doubling the absolute temperature of black-body, the total energy radiated will change by a factor of\nA. 0.5\nB. 2\nC. 4\nD. 16\nAccording to Einstein, increasing the brightness of a beam of light without changing its color will increase:\nA. the number of photons.\nC. the speed of the photons.\nB. the energy of each photon.\nD. the frequency of the photons.\n6 Sources of red, blue, and yellow light each emit light with a power of\n50mW. The source which emit more photons is\nA. red\nB. blue\nC. yellow\n7D. They all emit the same number per second.\nOf the following energies for photons, which is the least energy that could result in photoelectron production if the work function is 3.00 eV?\nA. 1.50 eV\nB. 2.90 eV C. 3.50 eV\nD. 6.01 eV\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n294\nIn a Compton scattering experiment at which angle the electron carry maximum energy? (Assume that initially the target electron is at rest).\nM\nU\nL\nT\nA. 0°\nB. 90°\nC. 135°\nD. 180°\n9 If X-rays are scattered from protons instead of electrons, the change in\nits wavelength for a given angle of scattering\nL\nE\nA. increases B. decreases C. is unchanged\nD. is one radian\nC\nH\nO\nC\n10 Pair production cannot take place in vacuum because.\nA. Mass is not conserved\nC. Energy is not conserved\nAccording to de-broglie equation which one has the smallest wavelength\nassociated with it\nB. Momentum is not conserved\nD. Charge is not conserved\nE\nA. proton\nB. neutron\nC. a - particle\nD. electron",
        "content": "Quantized: the fact that certain physical entities exist only with particular discrete values and not every conceivable value<br>Plank&#x27;s Constant: h=6.626 x 10&quot; Js<br>Photon: a quantum, or particle, of electromagnetic radiation<br>Photo electric effect: the phenomenon whereby some materials eject electrons when light is shined on them<br>Compton effect: the phenomenon whereby x rays scattered from materials have decreased energy<br>Pair Production: the change of very high energy photon into electron -positron pair.<br>Annihilation of matter: when a positron comes close to an electron, they annihilate and produce two photons<br>de Broglie wavelength: the wavelength possessed by a particle of matter Wave -particle duality: both radiation and matter exhibit particle as well as wave like properties<br>Heisenberg uncertainty principle: fundamental limit to the precision with which pairs of quantities (momentum and position, and energy and time) can be measured<br>PHYSICS-XII<br>UNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS<br>MULTIPLE CHOICE<br>PUESTIONS<br>Exercise<br>Choose the best possible answer<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>293<br>Triplets A and B board spaceships and head away from Earth in opposite directions, each traveling the same distance at the same speed before returning to Earth. Triplet C remains on Earth. Which expression describes the triplets&#x27; relative ages once they&#x27;re reunited on Earth?<br>A. A B C B. A=B&gt;C C. A=B&lt;C D.A=B-C<br>2 If a spaceship travels from Earth to a planet in the Alpha Centauri star<br>system, which observer measures the proper time for the trip?<br>A. an observer on Earth<br>B. an observer on the planet in the Alpha Centauri star system C. an observer on the spaceship<br>D. an observer deep in space far from the planet and Earth<br>When you compress a spring between your fingers, its mass<br>A. increase B. decrease C. stay the same D. turns negative By doubling the absolute temperature of black-body, the total energy radiated will change by a factor of<br>A. 0.5<br>B. 2<br>C. 4<br>D. 16<br>According to Einstein, increasing the brightness of a beam of light without changing its color will increase:<br>A. the number of photons.<br>C. the speed of the photons.<br>B. the energy of each photon.<br>D. the frequency of the photons.<br>6 Sources of red, blue, and yellow light each emit light with a power of<br>50mW. The source which emit more photons is<br>A. red<br>B. blue<br>C. yellow<br>7D. They all emit the same number per second.<br>Of the following energies for photons, which is the least energy that could result in photoelectron production if the work function is 3.00 eV?<br>A. 1.50 eV<br>B. 2.90 eV C. 3.50 eV<br>D. 6.01 eV<br>PHYSICS-XII<br>UNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>294<br>In a Compton scattering experiment at which angle the electron carry maximum energy? (Assume that initially the target electron is at rest).<br>M<br>U<br>L<br>T<br>A. 0°<br>B. 90°<br>C. 135°<br>D. 180°<br>9 If X-rays are scattered from protons instead of electrons, the change in<br>its wavelength for a given angle of scattering<br>L<br>E<br>A. increases B. decreases C. is unchanged<br>D. is one radian<br>C<br>H<br>O<br>C<br>10 Pair production cannot take place in vacuum because.<br>A. Mass is not conserved<br>C. Energy is not conserved<br>According to de-broglie equation which one has the smallest wavelength<br>associated with it<br>B. Momentum is not conserved<br>D. Charge is not conserved<br>E<br>A. proton<br>B. neutron<br>C. a - particle<br>D. electron",
