        "theory": "Q\n12 An electron and a proton have the same de-broglie wavelength. Then the\nkinetic energy of the electron is\nA. Zero\nB. infinity\nC. equal to the kinetic energy of the proton\nD. greater than the kinetic energy of the proton\n13 In Electron microscope, light source is replaced by a beam of very\nmoving\nA. electron\nB. neutron\nC. proton\nD.\nIn the Photon\nfast\n14 According to Heisenberg, as the uncertainty in the measurement of a particle&#x27;s momentum is reduced by a factor of 2, by what factor is the uncertainty in that same particle&#x27;s position changed?\nA. 1/2\nB. 1\nC. 2\nD. 4\nBUESTIONS\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS\n1\nNOT FORSALE\n295\nImagine a world in which speed of light is only 50 m/s. How would the everyday events appear to us?\n2 Some stars are observed to be reddish, and some are blue. Which stars\nhave the higher surface temperature? Explain.\n3\nIf the photo electric effect is observed for one metal, can you conclude that the effect will also be observed for another metal under the same conditions?\nDoes a light bulb at a temperature of 2500 K produce as white a light as the sun at 6000K?\n6 A beam of red light and a beam of blue light have exactly the same energy.\nWhich light contains the greater number of photons?\n6 Why must the rest mass of a photon be zero?\n7Why don&#x27;t we observe Compton&#x27;s effect with visible light?\n8 If an electron and a proton have the same de Broglie wavelength, which\nparticle has greater speed?\n9 An electron and a proton are accelerated from rest through the same\npotential difference. Which particle has the longer wavelength?\n10 Why ultraviolet radiation is harmful to skin while visible light is not?\nCOMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS\nWhat are inertial and non-inertial reference frames? Explain with examples\nState Einstein&#x27;s postulate of the special theory of relativity. Discuss its\nvarious results.\nWhat are the main features of the thermal radiation from a black body? Discuss Planck&#x27;s quantum theory and its importance in physics.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n296\n7\n8\n9\nWhat is photoelectric effect? Discuss the failure of classical physics and success of photon concept in explaining this effect.\nWhat is Compton&#x27;s effect? Develop a mathematical relation for the Compton&#x27;s wave shift.\nWrite note on pair production and annihilation of matter.\nWhat is de Broglie hypothesis? Describe an experiment to show that particle has wave characteristics.\nWhat is meant by wave-particle duality? Explain.\nState and explain Heisenberg&#x27;s uncertainty principle. Justify the validity of this principle by a thought experiment.\nNUMERICAL PROBLEMS\n1 The length of a space ship is measured to be exactly one -third of its proper length. What is the speed of the space ship relative to the observer?\n2\n3\n5\n(Answer: 0.9428c)\nThe time period of a pendulum is measured to be 3s in inertial frame of the pendulum. What is the period when measured by an observer moving\n(Answer: 9.65) with a speed of 0.95c with respect to the pendulum?\nA metal, whose work function is 3.0 eV, is illuminated by light of wavelength 3 x 10 m. Calculate the (a) threshold frequency, (b) maximum energy of photo-electrons and (c) stopping potential.\n(Answer: (a) 0.72 x 10&quot; Hz, (b) 1.16 eV (c) 1.16 V) A 50 keV X-ray is scattered through an angle of 90°. What is the energy of X-ray after Compton Scattering?\n(Answer: 45.5 keV)\nof\nThe speed of electron is measured to be 5 x 10 ms to an accuracy 0.003%. Find the uncertainty in determining the position of this electron.\n(Answer: 4.84 x 10&#x27; m)\nPHYSICS-XII\nn\nNOT FOR SALE\n297\nUnit\n19",
