        "sciNote": "oscillator with frequency\nf.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n269\nThus the resonator can change its energy only by MAX PLANCK (1858-1947)\nthe difference AE, as\nAE=hf\n18.8\nThat is, it cannot lose just any amount of its total energy, but only a finite amount, hf, called quantum of energy.\ncom\nEXAMPLE 18.2\nسوا\nMax Planck (Germany). His work on the spectral distribution of radiation, which led to the quantum theory, was honored with the 1918 Nobel Prize.\nHUMAN BODY RADIATION\nThe temperature of the human skin is approximately 35 °C. What is the wavelength at which the peak occurs in the radiation emitted from the skin?\nGIVEN\nWien's constant = 0.2898 ×10a mK Temperature T = 35 °C = 35 + 273 K = 308 K\nSOLUTION\nREQUIRED\nWavelength λ = ?\nby Wien's displacement Law\nConstant\nAmaxT=Constant or max\nT\n0.2898x1012 mk\nPutting values mex\nAmax\n308K\ntherefore\nλ=9.409×10* m=9.4μm\nAnswer\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n270\n18.6 PHOTOELECTRIC EFFECT\nWhen light hits the metal surface electrons FIGURE 18.9\nare emitted, the phenomena is called photoelectric effect and the electrons emitted are called photo-electrons.\nWhen a beam of light having short enough wavelength is directed on a metal surface, the light will eject the electrons from the surface of metal, as shown in figure 18.10.\nThis phenomenon, was discovered by\nelectrons\nlight\nLight absorbed by a surface causes electrons to be ejected.\nHeinrich Hertz in 1887, and Einstein FIGURE 18.10\nex"
