        "theory": "In order to create an electron positron pair a minimal of 2m,c&#x27; is needed, the surplus energy is taken by electron and positron as their Kinetic energies.\nn\nhf = 2m,c2 +(KE) + (KE)+e\n18.12\nFor minimum energy the electron and positron will not carry any additional energy as their kinetic energies. Thus (KE), and (KE), must be zero, therefore the minimum energy is\nhf = 2m。c2\nThe rest mass of electron m, -9.11x 10 kg and speed of light c = 3 x 10&#x27; m/s\nhf=2(9.11×10kg)(3x10 m/s)2 = 16.38x10-J\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n281\nhf-16.38×10-14J\n1.6x19-C\n=1.023x10 eV=1.02MeV\nPair production requires at least 1.02 MeV energy photon. The photon in pair production will have a very short wavelength and very high frequency (as with X- ray and gamma-ray photons).\n18.9 PAIR ANNIHILATION\nWhen an electron (e-) and a positron (e+) collide two gamma ray photons are produced and the phenomenon is called pair annihilation.\nWhen a positron and an electron combine together they annihilates as shown in figure 18.19. Both particles disappear, and two (or occasionally three) photons can appear, with total energy of at least. Decay into a single photon is impossible because such a process could not conserve both energy and\nmomentum.\n2hf=2m.c2\nFIGURE 18.19\nila\net\n18.13\nBefore\nAfter\nSo each photon produced must have energy\nPair Annihilation\nhf = m,c2\nThe rest mass of electron m, 9.11 10&quot; kg and speed of light c = 3 10 m/s\nhf = (9.11x101kg)(3x10 m/s) = 8.19x10-J\nhf\n8.19×10-14 J 1.6x10-&quot;C\n-0.51x10° eV 0.51MeV\nAs with any two charged objects, electrons and positrons may also interact with each other without annihilating, in general by elastic scattering.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n282\nThe electron-positron annihilation process is the physical phenomenon relied on as the basis of positron emission tomography (PET) and positron annihilation spectroscopy (PAS). It is also used as a method of measuring the Fermi surface and band structure in metals.\n18.10 WAVE NATURE OF PARTICLES\nWave-particle duality is a theory that proposes that all matter exhibits the properties of not only particles, but also waves, which transfer energy.\nLight and other electromagnetic radiation act sometimes like waves and sometimes like particles. Interference and diffraction. demonstrate wave behavior, whereas in this chapter we saw considerable evidence for the photon theory of light as well, where electromagnetic waves consisted of photons, particle-like pulses that have energy and\nmomentum.\nIn 1924 a French physicist, Louis de-Broglie, made remarkable proposal about the nature of matter. He reasoned that as light is dualistic in nature, behaving in some situations like waves and in others like particles. If nature is symmetric, this duality should also hold for matter. Electrons and protons, which we usually think of as particles, may in some situations behave like waves.\nDE-BROGLIE (1892-1987)\nLouis de-Broglie (France). A member of an aristocratic family, his work contributed substantially to the early development of the quantum theory.\nIf a particle acts like a wave, it should have a wavelength and a frequency. De-Broglie postulated that a free particle with rest mass &#x27;m&#x27;, moving with non-relativistic speed &#x27;v&#x27; should have a wavelength &#x27;X&#x27; related to its momentum &quot;p=my&#x27; in exactly the same way as for a photon.\nThe energy of photon having frequency f by plancks theory is\nE = hf\n0\nE=mc2\n2\nPHYSICS-XII\nAccording to Einstein mass energy relation\nIn\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\ncomparing equation 1 and equation 2 we get\nNOT FOR SALE\nhf = mc2\nDividing equation 3 by c we get hf_mc\nhf\nог\nmc\nC\n283\nSince momentum &#x27;P = my&#x27; for photon can be written for speed of light &#x27;c&#x27; as &#x27;P= mc&#x27; therefore\nhf\nP\n18.14\nEquation 18.14 gives momentum of photon in terms of frequency, by general wave equation for light we also know that &#x27;c=fX&#x27;, therefore equation 18.14 can be\nwritten as\nh\nor\nherice\nh\n18.15\nP\nEquation 18.15 gives momentum of photon in terms of wavelength. De-Broglie argued that we can also assign wavelength to a particle of mass &#x27;m&#x27; moving with velocity &#x27;v&#x27; in the same way by putting &#x27;p=mv&quot;",
