        "theory": "λο\nτην mv\n4\nIf the particle&#x27;s speed is an appreciable fraction of the speed of light &#x27;c&#x27;, we can replace &#x27;my&#x27; in Equation 4 with\nmov\nmv MV =\n18.16\nThus in de-Broglie&#x27;s hypothesis, the relationships of wavelength to momentum and of frequency to energy are exactly the same for free particles as for photons.\nDAVISSON AND GERMER EXPERIMENT\nThe Davisson-Germer experiment demonstrated the wave nature of the electron, confirming de-Broglie hypothesis.\nAn electron diffraction experiment gave the first experimental confirmation of the wave nature of particles (and the quantitative confirmation of the de-Broglie relationship λ=h/p).\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n284\nThis experimental proof was provided by American physicists Clinton J. Davisson (1881-1958) and Lester H. Germer (1896-1971) at the Bell Laboratories in New York in 1927.\nThey designed a vacuum apparatus for measuring the energies of electrons. scattered. The electron beam was accelerated towards nickel target, which could be rotated to observe angular dependence of the scattered\nelectrons as shown in the\nfigure 18.20. At certain angles there was a peak in the intensity of the scattered electron beam. This peak. indicated wave behavior for the electrons, and could be interpreted by the Bragg law. to give values for the lattice spacing in the nickel crystal.\nFIGURE 18.20\nAccelerating voltage\nGlass-vacuum\nvessel\nFilament\nPlate\nMoveable electron detector\nNickel target\nExperimental arrangement by Davisson and Germer\nThe results obtained from the FIGURE 18.21\nexperiment were explained\nby treating electrons as waves of wavelength given by de Broglie&#x27;s equation. The diffraction pattern of electrons from the nickel crystal was found similar to that of X-ray diffraction as shown in figure 18.21.\nWhen the de Broglie wavelength of an electron beam equals the wavelength of the X-rays. The diffraction patterns of thin beam of X-rays and electrons passing through a thin foil are similar as shown in simulation.\nPHYSICS-XII\nด\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FORSALE\n285\nAsimilar demonstration of the wave nature of relativistic electrons was provided in the same year by G. P. Thomson, who observed the transmission of electrons with energies in the range of 10 to 40 keV through thin metallic foils (G. P. Thomson, the son of J. J. Thomson, shared the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1937 with Davisson). The wave properties of particles like protons, neutrons, other atoms and even molecules has also been observed. In all cases the measured wavelengths agree with de Broglie&#x27;s prediction, proving that all matter has wavelike as well as particle like properties.\nEXAMPLE 18.5\nELECTRON WAVELENGTH\nDetermine the wavelength of electron that has been accelerated through a potential difference of (a) 50V (b) 200 V.\nGIVEN\nREQUIRED\nPotential Difference AV, = 50 V\nPotential Difference AV, = 200 V\nwavelength λ, =?\nrest mass of electron m, 9.11 x 10&quot; kg\nwavelength λ, =?\nCharge on electron q.\n1.602 x 10&quot; C\nPlank&#x27;s constant h=6.626 x 10\nJs\nSOLUTION\nThe de-Broglie wave length A is λ =\nh\nm.v\nthe speed of electron can be determined by its kinetic energy gained\nthrough the potential difference.\n1 m. v2 = q. AV or\nv2 - 29, AV\nm.\nTaking square root on both sides v =\n29, AV\n(2)\nm,\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\nh\nPutting equation 2 in equation 1 we get\nλ =\n2q, AV\nme\nh\nrearranging\nλ=\n√2m.9. AV\nh\nh\n(a)",
        "content": "λο<br>την mv<br>4<br>If the particle&#x27;s speed is an appreciable fraction of the speed of light &#x27;c&#x27;, we can replace &#x27;my&#x27; in Equation 4 with<br>mov<br>mv MV =<br>18.16<br>Thus in de-Broglie&#x27;s hypothesis, the relationships of wavelength to momentum and of frequency to energy are exactly the same for free particles as for photons.<br>DAVISSON AND GERMER EXPERIMENT<br>The Davisson-Germer experiment demonstrated the wave nature of the electron, confirming de-Broglie hypothesis.<br>An electron diffraction experiment gave the first experimental confirmation of the wave nature of particles (and the quantitative confirmation of the de-Broglie relationship λ=h/p).<br>PHYSICS-XII<br>UNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>284<br>This experimental proof was provided by American physicists Clinton J. Davisson (1881-1958) and Lester H. Germer (1896-1971) at the Bell Laboratories in New York in 1927.<br>They designed a vacuum apparatus for measuring the energies of electrons. scattered. The electron beam was accelerated towards nickel target, which could be rotated to observe angular dependence of the scattered<br>electrons as shown in the<br>figure 18.20. At certain angles there was a peak in the intensity of the scattered electron beam. This peak. indicated wave behavior for the electrons, and could be interpreted by the Bragg law. to give values for the lattice spacing in the nickel crystal.<br>FIGURE 18.20<br>Accelerating voltage<br>Glass-vacuum<br>vessel<br>Filament<br>Plate<br>Moveable electron detector<br>Nickel target<br>Experimental arrangement by Davisson and Germer<br>The results obtained from the FIGURE 18.21<br>experiment were explained<br>by treating electrons as waves of wavelength given by de Broglie&#x27;s equation. The diffraction pattern of electrons from the nickel crystal was found similar to that of X-ray diffraction as shown in figure 18.21.<br>When the de Broglie wavelength of an electron beam equals the wavelength of the X-rays. The diffraction patterns of thin beam of X-rays and electrons passing through a thin foil are similar as shown in simulation.<br>PHYSICS-XII<br>ด<br>UNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS<br>NOT FORSALE<br>285<br>Asimilar demonstration of the wave nature of relativistic electrons was provided in the same year by G. P. Thomson, who observed the transmission of electrons with energies in the range of 10 to 40 keV through thin metallic foils (G. P. Thomson, the son of J. J. Thomson, shared the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1937 with Davisson). The wave properties of particles like protons, neutrons, other atoms and even molecules has also been observed. In all cases the measured wavelengths agree with de Broglie&#x27;s prediction, proving that all matter has wavelike as well as particle like properties.<br>EXAMPLE 18.5<br>ELECTRON WAVELENGTH<br>Determine the wavelength of electron that has been accelerated through a potential difference of (a) 50V (b) 200 V.<br>GIVEN<br>REQUIRED<br>Potential Difference AV, = 50 V<br>Potential Difference AV, = 200 V<br>wavelength λ, =?<br>rest mass of electron m, 9.11 x 10&quot; kg<br>wavelength λ, =?<br>Charge on electron q.<br>1.602 x 10&quot; C<br>Plank&#x27;s constant h=6.626 x 10<br>Js<br>SOLUTION<br>The de-Broglie wave length A is λ =<br>h<br>m.v<br>the speed of electron can be determined by its kinetic energy gained<br>through the potential difference.<br>1 m. v2 = q. AV or<br>v2 - 29, AV<br>m.<br>Taking square root on both sides v =<br>29, AV<br>(2)<br>m,<br>PHYSICS-XII<br>UNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>h<br>Putting equation 2 in equation 1 we get<br>λ =<br>2q, AV<br>me<br>h<br>rearranging<br>λ=<br>√2m.9. AV<br>h<br>h<br>(a)",
