        "sciNote": "λο\nτην mv\n4\nIf the particle's speed is an appreciable fraction of the speed of light 'c', we can replace 'my' in Equation 4 with\nmov\nmv MV =\n18.16\nThus in de-Broglie's hypothesis, the relationships of wavelength to momentum and of frequency to energy are exactly the same for free particles as for photons.\nDAVISSON AND GERMER EXPERIMENT\nThe Davisson-Germer experiment demonstrated the wave nature of the electron, confirming de-Broglie hypothesis.\nAn electron diffraction experiment gave the first experimental confirmation of the wave nature of particles (and the quantitative confirmation of the de-Broglie relationship λ=h/p).\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n284\nThis experimental proof was provided by American physicists Clinton J. Davisson (1881-1958) and Lester H. Germer (1896-1971) at the Bell Laboratories in New York in 1927.\nThey designed a vacuum apparatus for measuring the energies of electrons. scattered. The electron beam was accelerated towards nickel target, which could be rotated to observe angular dependence of the scattered\nelectrons as shown in the\nfigure 18.20. At certain angles there was a peak in the intensity of the scattered electron beam. This peak. indicated wave behavior for the electrons, and could be interpreted by the Bragg law. to give values for the lattice spacing in the nickel crystal.\nFIGURE 18.20\nAccelerating voltage\nGlass-vacuum\nvessel\nFilament\nPlate\nMoveable electron detector\nNickel target\nExperimental arrangement by D"
