        "theory": "√2m,q, AV,\nPutting values λ=&quot;\n√2m.9. AV1\nPutting values 24\n6.626×10-34Js\n√2x9.11x10-kg-1.602×10-19 Cx50V\ntherefore 0.173x10m=0.173 nm\nAnswer\nh\nh\n(b)\n22\n√2m,q, AV2\nPutting values λ\n√2m,9μ AV2\n6.626×10-34Js\nPutting values λ2 =\n286\n√2×9.11×10-31 kg 1.602×10-19Cx200 V\ntherefore λ2 = 0.087x10 m = 0.087 nm\nAnswer\nASSIGNMENT 18.4\ne in\nCOMPARING DE BROGLIE WAVELENGTH\nCompute the de Broglie wavelength of the following: (a) A cricket ball of mass 160 g, traveling at 45 m/s (b) A smoke particle of mass 10 g moving at 1 cm/s. (c) An electron moving with a typical speed of 10&#x27; m/s.\n[Answer: (a) 9.2 x 10 m, (b) 6.6 x 100 m, and (c) 7.3 x 10&quot; m]\nPHYSICS-XII\nila\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n287\n18.11 ELECTRON MICROSCOPE\nAn electron microscope (EM) is a type of microscope that uses an electron beam with its wave like properties to illuminate a specimen and produce a magnified image. Electron Microscope is based on the wave characteristics of electrons.\nHigh-voltage supply\nCathode (where\nElectron gun\nThe resolving power of EM is greater than optical microscope because electrons have wavelengths about 100,000 times shorter than visible light photons. They can achieve better than 50 pm resolution and magnifications of up to about 10,000,000x (whereas ordinary, light microscopes are limited by diffraction to about 200 nm resolution and useful magnifications below 2000x). The scanning electron microscope FIGURE 18.22 is shown in the figure 18.22, it produces images with a three- dimensional quality. The electron beam produced by an electron gun, is accelerated by an anode. The condenser lenses (electromagnetic coils) controls and defines the size of the electron beam (which set the resolution). The objective lens focus the beam onto the sample. Two types of electrons used for imaging are Back Scattered electron collector\nElectrons (BSE) and Secondary Electrons (SE), which are collected by corresponding detectors. The X-rays generated from the electron-matter interaction is also collected through a detector. Computer is used to compile data from all the detectors and a magnified image\nVacuum chamber\nCondenser lenses\nObjective lens\nBack scatter\nX-Ray Detector\nMotorized Stage\nelectron beam originates\nAccelerating anode\nSecondary electron detector\nSample\nScanning Electron Microscope\nof the object is obtained, as shown in figure 18.23.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n288\nFIGURE 18.23\nBack Scatter Electrons (left) and Secondary Electrons (right) images of Feo, particles. 18.12 THE UNCERTAINTY PRINCIPLE Heisenberg (1901-1976)\nThis principle states that &#x27;the more precisely the position is determined, the less precisely the momentum is known in this instant, and vice\nversa&#x27;.\nIf a measurement of the position of a particle is made with precision &#x27;Ax&#x27; and a simultaneous measurement of linear momentum is made with precision &#x27;AP,&#x27; the product of the two uncertainties can never be smaller than &#x27;h&#x27;, mathematically\nAx = AP = h\n18.17\nWs\nSuppose we design a super microscope and want to accurately measure the position and momentum of an electron at the same time as shown in figure 18.24. As objects can be seen to a precision of about the wavelength of the radiation used due for\ndiffraction.\nWerner Heisenberg (Germany). Best known for the uncertainty principle, he also developed a complete formulation of the quantum theory based on\nmatrices.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n289\nFor us to see the electron and determine its location, at least one photon of light must strike the electron, however, the photon transfers some unknown amount of its momentum to the electron.\nFIGURE 18.24\nShort\nWavelength Photon\nLong Wavelength Photon\nScattered Photon\nParticle\nAx,\nCO\nP+AP,",
