      "18.11 ELECTRON MICROSCOPE",
      "An electron microscope (EM) is a type of microscope that uses an electron beam with its wave like properties to illuminate a specimen and produce a magnified image. Electron Microscope is based on the wave characteristics of electrons.",
      "High-voltage supply",
      "Cathode (where",
      "Electron gun",
      "The resolving power of EM is greater than optical microscope because electrons have wavelengths about 100,000 times shorter than visible light photons. They can achieve better than 50 pm resolution and magnifications of up to about 10,000,000x (whereas ordinary, light microscopes are limited by diffraction to about 200 nm resolution and useful magnifications below 2000x). The scanning electron microscope FIGURE 18.22 is shown in the figure 18.22, it produces images with a three- dimensional quality. The electron beam produced by an electron gun, is accelerated by an anode. The condenser lenses (electromagnetic coils) controls and defines the size of the electron beam (which set the resolution). The objective lens focus the beam onto the sample. Two types of electrons used for imaging are Back Scattered electron collector",
      "Electrons (BSE) and Secondary Electrons (SE), which are collected by corresponding detectors. The X-rays generated from the electron-matter interaction is also collected through a detector. Computer is used to compile data from all the detectors and a magnified image",
