        "title": "EXAMPLE 18.2",
        "problem": "EXAMPLE 18.2\nسوا\nMax Planck (Germany). His work on the spectral distribution of radiation, which led to the quantum theory, was honored with the 1918 Nobel Prize.\nHUMAN BODY RADIATION\nThe temperature of the human skin is approximately 35 °C. What is the wavelength at which the peak occurs in the radiation emitted from the skin?\nGIVEN\nWien's constant = 0.2898 ×10a mK Temperature T = 35 °C = 35 + 273 K = 308 K\nSOLUTION\nREQUIRED\nWavelength λ = ?\nby Wien's displacement Law\nConstant\nAmaxT=Constant or max\nT\n0.2898x1012 mk\nPutting values mex\nAmax\n308K\ntherefore\nλ=9.409×10* m=9.4μm\nAnswer\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n270\n18.6 PHOTOELECTRIC EFFECT\nWhen light hits the metal surface electrons FIGURE 18.9\nare emitted, the phenomena is called photoelectric effect and the electrons emitted are called photo-electrons.\nWhen a beam of light having short enough wavelength is directed on a metal surface, the light will eject the electrons from the surface of metal, as shown in figure 18.10.\nThis phenomenon, was discovered by\nelectrons\nlight\nLight absorbed by a surface causes electrons to be ejected.\nHeinrich Hertz in 1887, and Einstein FIGURE 18.10\nexplained it with photon concept of light in 1905. Let us analyze two basic photoelectric experiments, each using the apparatus shown in figure 18.10, in which light of frequency 'f' is directed onto metallic emitter and ejects electrons from it. A potential difference 'V' is maintained between metallic emitter and collector. This arrangement produces a photoelectric current 'I' that is measured with sensitive ammeter \"A\".\nFirst Photoelectric Experiment: We adjust the potential difference V by moving the sliding contact in figure 18.10 so that collector is slightly negative with respect to metallic emitter. This potential difference acts to slow down the ejected electrons. We then vary V until it reaches a certain value, called the stopping potential V, at which the reading of sensitive ammeter A has just dropped to zero.\nMetallic\nemitter\nLight\nCollector\nelectrons\nPHYSICS-XII\nSensitive\nA\nammeter\nVoltmeter\nV\naw\nwww\nRheostat\nBattery\nApparatus for photo electric effect\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOTE RSALE\n271\nWhen V = V., the most energetic ejected electrons are turned back just before reaching the collector. Then 'KE, the kinetic energy of these most energetic\nelectrons, is\nKE = evo max=ev\n18.9\nwhere 'e' is the elementary charge and V, is the stopping potential.\nMeasurements show that for light of a given frequency, 'KE. does not depend on the intensity of the light.\nThe photoelectric current 'I' as a function of the potential difference 'V' for two different values of the intensity of the light is plotted in figure 18.11. When the intensity is doubled, the current is doubled (twice as many photo-electrons are emitted), but the stopping potential V, remains the same.\nFIGURE 18.11\n1 (WA)\nI, > 1,\nBright light\nDim light\n-5\n0\n5\n10\nBrightness of incident light increases the photo current but the maximum Kinetic energy of the ejected photo-electrons remains the same.\nThis experimental result is a puzzle for classical physics. Classically, the incident light is a sinusoidally oscillating electromagnetic wave. If we increase the amplitude of the wave, the electron should get a more energy as it is being ejected. That is not what happens.\nSecond Photoelectric Experiment: Now we vary the frequency f of the incident light and measure the associated stopping potential V, or maximum Kinetic energy KE of photoelectrons. Figure 18.12 is a plot of KE... versus f. Note that the photoelectric effect does not occur if the frequency is below a certain threshold frequency f, (or equivalently, if the wavelength is greater than the corresponding threshold wavelengthλ=c/f).\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nThis is another puzzle for classical physics. If we view light as an electromagnetic wave, we must expect that no matter how low the frequency may be, electrons can always be ejected by incident light if you supply them with enough energy-that is, if you use a light source that is bright enough. That is not what happens.\nThere is a third puzzle for classical physics in both experiments the wave theory predicts a time delay. However the first photoelectrons are emitted instantaneously after the light source is turned on.\nNOT FOR SALE\n272\nFIGURE 18.12\nMaximum kinetic energy,\n0\nfrequency, f\nKinetic energy of the ejected photo- electron depends upon frequency, f\nEINSTEIN EXPLANATION OF PHOTOELECTRIC EFFECT (PHOTON THEORY OF LIGHT)\nEinstein used Planck's concept of quantization of energy. Einstein proposed that the energy of electromagnetic radiation is not continuously distributed over the wave front, but instead is concentrated in localized bundles or quanta (also known as photons). The energy of a photon associated with an electromagnetic wave of frequency 'f' is\nE = hf\nawlectrom\nEinstein's picture was that a light quantum was so localized that it gave all its energy 'hf' directly to a single electron in the metal. Therefore, according to Einstein, the maximum kinetic energy for emitted electrons is\nKE max = hf - op\n18.10\nwhere q is the work function of the metal, which corresponds to the minimum with which an electron is bound in the metal. Table 18.1 lists values of\nenergy work functions measured for different metals.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nEquation 18.10 beautifully explain the puzzling independence of KE. and intensity. For a fixed light frequency f, an increase in light intensity means more photons and more photoelectrons per second, although KE... remains. unchanged.\nIn addition, Equation 18.10 explain the phenomenon of threshold frequency f Light of threshold frequency f,, which has just enough energy to knock an electron out of the metal surface, causes the electron to be released with zero kinetic energy as shown in figure 18.13. Setting KE... = 0 then ff, and Equation 18.10 gives\nNOT FOR SALE\n273\nTable 18 1 Weak Fruction of\nMetal\nNa\nAL\n235 22 22\nWork Function, ©, (in eV)\n2.28\n4.08\nCu\n4.70\nZn\n4.31\nAg\n4.73\nPt\n6.35\nPb\n4.14\n4.50\nFIGURE 18.13\nPhoton\nKinetic energy\nFe\n0 = hf. - 4\nог\nhf。 = 4\nhence fo\n3\nh\nww\nelectrons\nThus the energy quantization used by Planck in solving the blackbody radiation problem was, in fact, a universal characteristic of light.\nThe\nMetal\ngeneral wave equation y = fλ in terms of threshold frequency f, and the cut off wavelength λ can be written as\nc = foλc or\n4\nputting equation 3 in equation 4, we get\nhc\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n274",
