        "title": "EXAMPLE 18.3",
        "problem": "EXAMPLE 18.3\nTUNGSTEN METAL\nThe work function for tungsten metal is 4.52 eV. (a) What is the cutoff wavelength λ for tungsten? (b) What is the maximum kinetic energy of the electrons when radiation of wavelength 198 nm is used? (c) What is the stopping potential in this case?\nGIVEN\nWork Function '' =4.52 eV\nREQUIRED\nCutoff wavelength λ = ?\n=4.52 eV x1.6x10-19 C = 7.232x10-19 J Maximum kinetic energy 'KE' =?\nStopping potential 'V' =? · Wavelength '2' 198 nm\nSOLUTION\nhc\n(a) The cutoff wavelength λ, is\nλε\nPutting values λ =\n6.626x10-34x2.998 × 108 ms/ 7.232 × 10-19 /\ntherefore λ = 275 nm\nAnswer\n(b) The Maximum kinetic energy 'KE...\" at shorter wavelength 'X' is\ne in\nhc\nKE max\nhf - 4\nог KE\n2.\nPutting values KE\nmax\n6.626×10 J× ph\n6.626 × 10-3⁄4 J × 2.998 × 10 -7.232×10-19 J\n198× 10 gh\ntherefore KEmax = 2.79×10-19 J\nAnswer\n(c) The stopping potential \"V.\" Vo\nKE\nmax\ne\nPutting values V1 =\n2.79×10-19 J\n1.6×10-19 C\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n2752\ntherefore V, = 1.74V\nAnswer\nASSIGNMENT 18.2\nSODIUM METAL\nila\nA sodium surface is illuminated with light of wavelength 300 nm. The work function for sodium is 2.46eV. Find (a) the K.E of the ejected electrons and (b) The cut-off wavelength for sodium.\n(Answer: (a) 2.7 x 10\" J (or 1.68 eV); (b) 5.05x 10 m (or 505 nm)\nScience Tidbits Photocell\nA device in which the photoelectric effect or photoconductivity is used to produce a current or voltage when exposed to light or other electromagnetic radiation.\nA simple photocell is shown in the figure. It consist of evacuated glass tube with thin anode rod and cathode of appropriate surface. The material of cathode is selected whose work function suit the frequency of incident radiation on which the photocell is operated. When this surface is exposed to light electrons are emitted which are collected at cathode. As a result current flows in the external circuit. This current increases with intensity and stops when light beam is interrupted.\nCollecting wire\nPhotocell\nMetal plate\nApplied voltage\nAmmeter\nBurglar alarms and automatic doors often make use of the photocell circuit. When a person interrupts the beam of light, the sudden drop in current in the circuit activates a switch which operates a bell or opens the door. Photocells are used in many other devices, such as absorption spectrophotometers, to measure light intensity.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n276\n18.7 COMPTON EFFECT\nThe scattering of high frequency photon strikes a stationary electron the frequency of photon is decreased after collision. This phenomenon is referred to as Compton's effect.\nFIGURE 18.14\nX-ray source\ndetector\nIncident Photon\nΠλ\n0\nӨ\ntarget\nThe change in wavelength\nangle at which\nphotons are\nscattered\ndepends upon the\nThe experimental arrangement of Compton effect\nEXPERIMENTAL ARRANGEMENT\nThe experimental arrangement of Compton effect is shown in the figure 18.14. A beam of X-rays having wavelength 'X' is directed towards a target of graphite. The scattered photons have wavelength \"X\" slightly longer, the amount of energy reduction depended upon the angle at which the X-rays were scattered.\nThe change in wavelength AX, between the scattered X-ray photon of wavelength and incident X-ray photon of wavelength A is called Compton Shift.\nThe X-ray photon of frequency 'f' is incident on a stationary electron.\nCOMPTON (1892-1962)\n7\nArthur H. Compton (America).\nHis work on X-ray scattering verified Einstein's photon theory and earned him the 1927 Nobel Prize.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nThe initial energy of photon by Plancks theory is 'hf' and initial rest energy for electron by Eienstein equation is 'm,c''. After collision the energy of photon reduce to hf' and electron is recoiled with total energy 'mc\" as shown in figure 18.15.\nBy law of conservation of energy\nNOT FOR SALE\nFIGURE 18.15\nX-\nIncident Photon\nEphoton) = hf\nY\nElectron at rest\nEelectron) =mc2\n277\nScattered Photon\nEriphoton) = hf\nRecoiling Electron Ef electron) = mc2\nX\nConservation of energy in Compton Scattering\nEphoton) +E(electron) = E(photon) +Ef(electron)\nsubstituting values hf+m,c2 = hf+mc2\nThe initial momentum of photon is 'hf/c' and since electron is considered at rest therefore it have zero momentum. After collision the momentum of photon is 'hf/c' making an angle '0' with its original direction and electron is recoiled with momentum 'my' making an angle o, as shown in figure 18.16.\nFIGURE 18.16\nScattered Photon\nIncident Photon\nhf\nPfxiphoton)\nhf cose\nIncident Photon\nPix photon)\nC\nPly.photon = 0\nScattered Photon\nhf Ply.photon)\n-sine\nC\nElectron at rest\nPix.(electron)=0\nRecoiling Electron Pfx.(electron) = mv cosq\nalong x-axis\nElectron at rest\nPly,electron +0\nRecoiling Electron Pfy.lelectron) = my singo\nmv\nalong y-axis\nConservation of momentum in Compton Scattering\nBy law of conservation of momentum\nAlong x-axis Pix,(photon) +Pix,(electron) =Pfx.(photon) +Pfx.(electron)\nsubstituting values\nhf +0 = hr\nhf cose +mv cose\nC\nC\nhf\nhf\ncose +mv cosp\n2\nог\nC C\nBy law of conservation of momentum\nAlong y-axis\nPly.photon) +Ply,(electron)=Pfy.(photon) +Py.(electron)\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nsubstituting values 0+0= sine-mv sing\nNOT FOR SALE\n278\nhf\nC\nor\n0=\nhf sine - my sing\n3\nC\nSolving equation 1, equation 2 and equation 3, simultaneously we get\nh\n-(1-cose)\nffm,c2\nmultiplying equation 4 by c we get\nor\nC C he\n-\nff mc-cose)\nC C h\n- + ffm.c\n(1-cose)\nλ\nhere- and ->\nTherefore λ=λ+ (1-cose)\nFIGURE 18.17\n0=45\"\nX-ray intensity\n8-90°\nh\nm.c\nh\nor 2'-λ =\n-(1-cose)\nm.c\nhence AX=\nh\nm.c\n-(1-cose)\n18.11\n=35°\nThe quantity h/m,c is constant is called as Compton wavelength 2.43 x 10\" m.\n70\n75\nWavelength pm\nCompton's results for scattering of X rays with A= 71 pm is shown in figure 18.17. Right-hand peak shows the wavelength shift of the Compton effect. The un-shifted left-hand peak is from photons scattering off tightly bound atomic electrons, which don't absorb significant energy. Solid curves are theoretical predictions. These results shows that the Compton's theoretical approach agrees with the experimental observations hence the photon concept is a valid concept.\nToday, Compton scattering with gamma rays is a widely used technique for studying the structure of matter.\nPHYSICS-XII\nila\nUNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n279",
