    "definitions": [
      {
        "term": "Dawn of Modern Physics",
        "def": "experiment in which the diffraction of electrons by the surface layers of a crystal lattice was observed.\nAt the end of the 19th century, scientists thought that they had discovered nearly everything about physics. Newton&#x27;s laws of motion and his theory of universal gravitation, Maxwell&#x27;s theoretical work in combining electricity and magnetism, and the laws of thermodynamics effectively explain all the\nHowever, at the turn of the 20th century, two major concepts improved our understanding of nature. In 1900 Planck provided the basic ideas that led to the quantum theory, and in 1905 Einstein formulated his special theory of relativity. These theories inspired new developments and theories in the fields of atomic, nuclear, and condensed-matter physics.\nAccording to relativity theory, moving clock appears to run slow with respect to a similar clock that is at rest. The satellites are constantly moving relative to observers on the Earth which causes them to run at slightly faster rate than clocks on the Earth&#x27;s surface."
      }
    ],
