        "theory": "Γ\nE = hf\n(a)\nV\n(b)\n(a) Coulomb force provides centripetal force and (b) atom radiate energy only when it jump from higher energy level to lower energy level.\nPostulate 3: An electron in stable orbit does not radiate energy. Atom radiates energy only when it jumps from an allowed orbit of higher energy E, to one of lower energy E,. The difference in energy appears as photon of energy E = hf.\nhf = E, -Ep\nA. RADII OF QUANTIZED ORBITS\n3\ne az ei\nBohr&#x27;s model of the hydrogen atom states that electrons can orbit the nucleus only at certain specific distances from the nucleus. These distances are multiples of the radius of the smallest permitted orbit. Thus, the orbits in an atom are quantized.\nmy2\nFrom Bohr&#x27;s first postulate\nor\nmv2 = k22\nh\n5\nFrom Bohr&#x27;s second postulate mvr=n\nor v n\n2π\n2x mr\nh\nputting equation 5 in equation 4 we get\nmn\n=k\n2π mгn\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\nh2\nor mxn2\n42 m2r2\n%\ntherefore n2\nh2\n= ke2\n42 mr\nIsolating r, we get\nh2\n&quot;2 = n2\n42mke2\n19.3\nNOT FOR SALE\n305\nNIELS BOHR (1885-1962)\nHere h = 6.626 x 10&quot; J 5, m = 3.14, m=9.109 x 10 kg, k = 8.988 x 10&#x27; Nm2/C, and e = 1.602 x 10&quot; C, are all constants collectively termed as r, and can be calculated as\nC\nh2 4m2mke\nputting values\n31\n(6.63×10-34 Js)2\nNiels Henrik David Bohr (Denmark) recieved 1922 Nobel prize in physics for the investigation of the structure of atoms and of the radiation emanating from them.\n4(3.14)2(9.11x10 kg) (8.99x10 Nm /C2) (1.67x10-C)2\nог\nTo=0.53×10-10 m\nExpression for the radius of the nth circular allowed orbit in the hydrogen atom can be written as\nr=n2r=n2 (0.53x10-10 m)\n19.4\nThe Bohr orbit with the smallest radius, is known as the Bohr radius, other radii can be calculated from this Bohr radius as\nr1 = (1)2 = 0.53x10-10 m\nг2 =(2)2 = 2.12×10-10m\n=(3)2 = 4.77×10-10 m\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA)\nNOT FOR SALE\n306\nB. ENERGY OF ELECTRON IN QUANTIZED ORBITS\nBoth the kinetic energy and the electric potential energy of an electron in orbit around a nucleus depend on the electron&#x27;s distance from the nucleus. So, the energy of an electron in an atom is also quantized. Each orbit corresponds to a particular energy level for the electron.\nThe total energy E, for an electron in an orbit is sum of its kinetic energy (K.E) and potential energy (P.E)\nE1 = K.E+P.E\n6\nSince kinetic energy can be written as\nK.E = /mv2\nputting value of my from equation 4 in equation 7 we get\n1.e2\nK.E -k\n8\nThe electrostatic potential energy is\nP.E-k\nutting equation 8 and equation 9 in equation 6 we get",
