        "theory": "-0.54 eV $0.85 eV\naw ci\n1.15 eV\nE,\nLyman series (ultra violet)\n- 3.40 eV\nE2\n13.6 eV\nE,\nDetail of Blamer\nseries transitions\nEnergy level diagram for hydrogen atom\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\n(2.17x10-J)\nhc 6.63x10-34 Jsx3x108 m/s\nbut Ry=1.0974x107m21\nNOT FOR SALE\n309\nEo-1.0974x107 mg\nhence\nhc\nEo\n50\nRH\nhc\n19.7\ntherefore\nRH\n= R(-3)\nSince the value of R, in Bohr theory is in agreement with the value determined empirical by Balmer. The equation resembles the general form of the spectral formula for the hydrogen atom. Therefore Bohr model can be used to compute energies or the wavelengths in Hydrogen spectra.\nD. EXCITATION AND IONIZATION POTENTIAL\nThe Bohr model predicts that the total energy of electron in an atom is quantized. The allowed energy states are given by the equation\nKant\nila p\nE\nEo\nwhere n = 1, 2, 3,.... The state n=1 is called ground state and n = 2, 3, 4, ... are called excited states.\nThe energy required to move an electron from its ground state (n = 1) to the excited state (n = 2, 3, 4, ...) is called excitation energy.\nThe\nenergy required to move an electron must be equal to the difference in the energy levels. Thus the first excitation energy that is, an electron making transition from E, to E, in hydrogen atom by Bohr formula is\nor\nth\nE2-E=-틀물 - (-)\nor\nE2-E1\nE2 - E1 = ( - 1 + 1) E。\n(+)\nE2 - E1 = 2 E0\nог\n--E0+ Eo\nhence E2-E1 = (-1+4)E,\nsince E = 2.17x10-18 J=13.6eV\nE2-E, ---E-1.62x10- J-10.2ev\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA)\nNOT FOR SALE\n310\nThus in hydrogen atom 10.2 eV energy is required for an electron to jump from ground state (n=1) to first allowed state (n=2). Similarly the second excitation energy is that is, an electron making transition from E, to E,.\nEs-Ei=-틀 - (특)\nor\nE-E1-(-1+1)α, E = (-1+1) Fo\nor E-E1 = E\ntherefore\nE1 =E, -12.1eV\nHence 12.1 eV energy is required for an electron to jump from ground state (n=1) to allowed state (n= 3) in hydrogen atom.\nThe potential difference required to move an electron from its ground state (n = 1) to the excited state (n = 2, 3, 4, ...) is called excitation potential. The excitation energy required to move an electron must be equal to the excitation potential. Therefore the first and second excitation potential for hydrogen atom are 10.2 eV and 12.1 eV respectively.\nThe minimum energy required to remove an electron from the atom in its ground state (n=1) is called ionization energy.\nWhen an atom absorbs sufficient amount of energy an atom may be raised to a level of n = oo. The electron then become free from the attractive force of the nucleus. An atom which have lost one or more electrons is said to be ionized. Thus anthe ionization energy that is, an electron making transition from E, to E, in\nhydrogen atom by Bohr formula is\nE-E--5-(-4)\nhence E-E1 =0+Ep\nsince\n-18\nor E-E1 =E, -2.17x10-18 J=13.6eV\nThus ionization energy for hydrogen atom is 13.6 eV. This means that ionization energy of an atom is numerically equivalent to ground state energy of an atom.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\nNOT FOR SALE\n311",
