        "title": "19.5 X-RAYS",
        "theory": "PHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\nA typical example of X-ray FIGURE 19.11 spectrum is shown in the figure 19.11. If we look at the spectrum of wavelengths emitted by an X-ray tube, we see that the spectrum consists of two parts: one is series of peaks (K., K., .... ); characteristic X-rays and the second is Bremsstrahlung; continuous X-rays with a cutoff wavelength λ..\nX-Ray intensity per\nunit wavelength\nNOT FOR SALE\n317\nCharacteristic\nK.\nlines\nBremsstrahlung\n0.02 0.04 0.06 0.08 0.10\nTypical X-ray spectrum\nC. CHARACTERISTIC OR INNER SHELL TRANSITION X-RAYS\nIn atoms having many electrons (like molybdenum target in X-ray tube), the electrons are arranged in different orbitals or shells as K, L, M.... When an incoming beam of electrons strike the atom it may remove an inner shell electron from the atom (such as ejecting a K-shell electron). Now an electron from K, L, M shell will rapidly make a transition to fill that vacancy, emitting an X-ray photon in the process. Thus we can explain these characteristic X-rays (the peaks in Figure 19.11) as photons emitted when an electron in an upper state drops down to fill the lower state.\nThe K lines; K., K., .... result from transitions into the K shell from L, M,.... shells respectively. Whereas L lines; L, L., .... result from transitions into the L shell from M, N, .... shells respectively as shown in figure 19.12.\nIn a similar manner, we label the other X-ray series by M, N, and so forth.\nila\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\nA typical example of X-ray FIGURE 19.11 spectrum is shown in the figure 19.11. If we look at the spectrum of wavelengths emitted by an X-ray tube, we see that the spectrum consists of two parts: one is series of peaks (K., K., .... ); characteristic X-rays and the second is Bremsstrahlung; continuous X-rays with a cutoff wavelength λ..\nX-Ray intensity per\nunit wavelength\nNOT FOR SALE\n317\nCharacteristic\nK.\nlines\nBremsstrahlung\n0.02 0.04 0.06 0.08 0.10\nTypical X-ray spectrum\nC. CHARACTERISTIC OR INNER SHELL TRANSITION X-RAYS\nIn atoms having many electrons (like molybdenum target in X-ray tube), the electrons are arranged in different orbitals or shells as K, L, M.... When an incoming beam of electrons strike the atom it may remove an inner shell electron from the atom (such as ejecting a K-shell electron). Now an electron from K, L, M shell will rapidly make a transition to fill that vacancy, emitting an X-ray photon in the process. Thus we can explain these characteristic X-rays (the peaks in Figure 19.11) as photons emitted when an electron in an upper state drops down to fill the lower state.\nThe K lines; K., K., .... result from transitions into the K shell from L, M,.... shells respectively. Whereas L lines; L, L., .... result from transitions into the L shell from M, N, .... shells respectively as shown in figure 19.12.\nIn a similar manner, we label the other X-ray series by M, N, and so forth.\nila\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\nFIGURE 19.12\nO-Nucleus\nK\nK\nL\nM\nNOT FOR SALE\nEjected electron\nN\nM\nL.\nCharacteristic X-rays\nEnergy\nK\nK\nK\n318\nD. CONTINUOUS X-RAYS\nAn electron by interaction with atoms of the target slows down and thus emits radiation, and in this case it is called bremsstrahlung (German for &quot;braking radiation&quot;) as shown in figure 19.13. Because energy is conserved, the energy of the emitted photon hf, equals the loss of kinetic energy of the electron as\nKE - KE&#x27; = hf\nFIGURE 19.13\nKE\nThe deflected electron has less Kinetic energy\nTungsten nucleus\nAn X-ray\nKE\nPhoton is\nemitted\nElectron passes near an atomic nucleus\nElectron slowing down near a nucleus of an atom\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\n319\nAn electron may lose all or a part of its energy in such a collision. Since different\nNOT FOR SALE electrons are slowed down differently in target and thus varying amounts of energies are lost by the electrons in the form of X-rays. Therefore the spectrum obtained is continuous X-ray spectrum. The shortest-wavelength X-ray (the highest frequency) must be due to an electron that gives up all its kinetic energy to produce one photon in a single collision. Since the initial kinetic energy of an electron is equal to the energy given it by the accelerating voltage, V, then\nKE = q,V\n2\nIn a single collision in which the electron is brought to rest KE-0-3\nputting values from equation 2 and equation 3 in equation 1\nilab\nhf\nqV-0=hf",
