        "theory": "CO\nor\nq,V = hf\nsince\nhc\nhence\nλ =\n19.9\n9.V\ntherefore q2,V=h\nThe equation 19.9 agrees with experimentally observed values. This result is further evidence that X-rays are a form of electromagnetic radiation (light) and that the photon theory of light is valid.\nE. APPLICATIONS OF X-RAYS\nThe important practical applications of X-rays can be divided into three categories A. Scientific Applications: The diffraction pattern of X-rays by different. crystals give information about internal structure of crystals. The spacing and dispositions of the atoms of crystals can be determined.\nB. Industrial Applications: X-rays penetrating ability can be used to detect defects in Rial roads, bridges and large machinery. The structure of rubber and plastics can be analyzed. It is also used at airports to check customers and baggage and by art historians to see if a picture has been painted on top of an older one. C. Medical Applications: Since bore is more opaque to X-rays than flesh, therefore if X-rays are allowed to pass through the human body a shadow of bone can be obtained on photographic film. The X-ray film reveal the fractures of bones\npresence of foreign bodies.\nand\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\nNOT FOR SALE\n320\nHigh energetic X-rays can be used for therapeutic purposes as well.\nFor your information\nCT SCANNER\nThe acronym CAT stands for Computerized Axial Tomography or in short CT (Computerized Tomography).\nIn CT scan machine there are large number of X-ray detectors. The source and detectors are arranged on a large ring shape structure. In this technique a number of X-ray beams pass simultaneously through the patient. The source and detector are rotated to get. views from different directions. Large number of images are obtained and are combined by a computer to get detailed 3D images of the patient. In effect, the CAT scanning technique makes it possible to take an X-ray picture of a cross-sectional &#x27;slice&#x27; that is perpendicular to the body&#x27;s long axis.\nEXAMPLE 19.2\nCUTOFF WAVELENGTH\nA tungsten target is struck by electrons that have been accelerated from rest through a 40.0-kV potential difference. Find the shortest wavelength of the\nradiation emitted.\nGIVEN\nApplied voltage &#x27;V&#x27; = 40.0-kV 40.0 x 10&quot; V\nPlank&#x27;s constant &#x27;h&#x27; 6.626 x 10 Js charge on electron &#x27;q.&#x27;= 1.6 x 10&quot; C\nspeed of light &#x27;c&#x27;= 3 x 10&quot; ms\nREQUIRED\nShortest wavelength &#x27;X&#x27; =?\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\nSOLUTION\nNOT FOR SALE\n321\nFor electrons accelerated through a potential difference of 40.0 kV, starting from rest, the kinetic energy is KE = e(AV) e(40.0 kV) 40.0 keV. When these electrons strike the tungsten target, the shortest wavelength possible is produced when the electron loses all its kinetic energy and is brought to rest in a single collision, producing a single photon. The wavelength of the photon produced is\nAmin\nhc\nq.V\n6.626×10-34Jsx3x 10 ms\n1.6 x 10-19 Cx40.0 x 103 V\nPutting values\ntherefore\nAmin = 3.11x10-&quot;m=0.0311nm\nAnswer\nMINIMUM WAVELENGTH\nASSIGNMENT 19.3\nElectrons in an x-ray tube are accelerated through a potential difference of 3000 V. if these electrons were slowed down in a target, what will be the minimum wavelength of X-rays produced?\n(Answer: 0.414 nm)\n19.6 LASER\nA LASER is a device that emits light through a process of optical amplification. based on the stimulated emission of electromagnetic radiation. Intense, monochromatic and coherent beam of light is emitted from LASER source. The term LASER is abbreviation of Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation. Light denotes electromagnetic radiation of any frequency, not only visible light. Therefore LASER operating at microwaves and radio-frequency is\ncalled MASER.\nTo understand how a laser works the terms stimulated emission and population inversion must be understood first.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\nNOT FORSALE\n322\nA. SPONTANEOUS AND STIMULATED EMISSIONS\nThere are three means by which radiation can interact with the energy levels of\natoms.\ni. Spontaneous emission: In the first kind of interaction, an atom in an excited state makes a transition to a lower state, with the emission of a photon.\natom→ atom + photon\nwhere the asterisk (*) indicates an excited state.\nFIGURE 19.14\nE\nE.\nE\nm\nли\nm\nE\nE1\nE1\n(a) Spontaneous\n(b) induced",
