        "theory": "En\n-\nог\nE\n(10)\nsubstituting the value of r, from equation 19.3 in equation 10 we get\ne2\nor\nEn\nh2\nEn=\n24x2mk2e*\nn2h2\nn2\n42mke2\n12x2mk2e*\nhence En\nX\n19.5\nh2\nin\nHere п = 3.14, m = 9.109 × 103 kg, k = 8.988 x 10&#x27; Nm2/C2, e = 1.602 x 10&quot; C and h 6.626 x 10 Js, are all constants and collectively termed as E, can be calculated\nas\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\nNOT FOR SALE\n307\n2x2mke+\nEo\nh2\nputting values E1 = 2(3.14)2(9.11x10-kg) (8.99 x 10° Nm2 / C2)2(1.67 x 10-C)\nог\n(6.63×10-4 Js)2\nE=2.17x10-18 J = 13.6eV\nExpression for the Energy of the nth allowed orbit in the hydrogen atom can be\nwritten as\nE\n--5--\n2.17 x10-18 J\n13.6eV n2\n19.6\nThe negative in 19.6 sign shows that the electron is bound to the nucleus. The lowest possible energy level is called ground state: other allowed energy levels can be calculated from this ground state or stationary state as\nilab\nE1\n=-2.17 × 10-18 J = -13.6eV\nE2\n-0.54 × 10-18 J = −3.4eV\n(2)2\nE\nE1\n=-0.24x101 J=-1.51eV\n(3)2\nC. HYDROGEN EMISSION SPECTRUM\n:.\nAccording to Bohr&#x27;s theory the emission line spectrum of an element tells us that atoms of that element emit photons with only certain specific frequencies &#x27;f&#x27; and hence certain specific energies &#x27;E = hf&quot;. During the emission of a photon, the internal energy of the atom changes by an amount equal to the energy of the photon.\nEach atom has a set of possible energy levels. An atom can have an amount of internal energy equal to any one of these levels, but it cannot have an energy intermediate between two levels. All isolated atoms of a given element have the same set of energy levels, but atoms of different elements have different sets.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA)\nNOT FOR SALE 308\nSuppose an atom is raised, or excited, to a high energy level. (In a hot gas this happens when fast-moving atoms undergo inelastic collisions with each other or with the walls of the gas container.) According to Bohr, an excited atom can make a transition from one energy level to a lower level by emitting a photon with energy eq to the energy difference between the initial and final levels. If E, is the initial energy of the atom before such a transition and E,is its final energy after the transition the photon&#x27;s energy is\nFrom energy of electron in quantized orbits\nhf = E12 - Ep\n1\nE\nEo\nEn\nand Ep\nsince\n1-1\nN-(-5)-(-5)\nEquation 1 can be written as\nhf\ntherefore\nhc Eo Eo or\nhc p\nFIGURE 19.7\n*-5-5 - 1-5(-3)\nPaschen series(infra-red)\nBlamer series (visible)\n0.38 eV",
