        "sciNote": "The minimum potential difference required to remove an electron from the atom in its ground state (n=1) is called ionization potential.\nThe ionization energy required to remove an electron from an atom must be equal to the ionization potential. Therefore ionization potential for hydrogen atom is also 13.6 eV.\nE. ENERGY LEVEL DIAGRAMS\nIt is useful to represent the energy values given by Equation 19.7 on an energy level diagram as shown in figure 19.8. In this diagram, which applies to the hydrogen atom (Z = 1), the highest energy level corresponds to n = oo and has an energy of 0 eV. This is the energy of the atom when the electron is completely removed (r = ∞) from the nucleus and is at rest. In contrast, the lowest energy level corresponds to n = 1 and has a value of -13.6 eV.\nFIGURE 19.8\nTotal energy. E\nOA\n0.54 eV\nila\n1.15 eV\nPaschen Brackett Pfund\nseries\n-3.40 ev\nseries\nseries\nΠ=2\nBlamer\ninfrared\nseries\n(visible)\n13.6 eVl\nLyman series (ultra violet)\nEnergy level diagram for hydrogen atom\nPHYSICS-XII\nn=1\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA)\nNOT FOR SALE\n312\nThe lowest energy level is called the ground state, to distinguish it from the higher levels, which are called excited states.\nThe various lines in the hydrogen atom spectrum are produced when electrons change from higher to lower energy levels and photons are emitted. Electrons can also make transitions in the reverse direction, from lower to higher levels. In this case, an atom absorbs a photon that has precisely the energy needed "
