        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 6",
        "theory": "therefore\nASSIGNMENT 19.1\nLAYMAN SERIES\nThe Layman series for hydrogen atom corresponds to electronic transitions that terminate in the state of quantum number n = 1. Find the (a) shortest and (b) longest wavelength of photon emitted in the Layman&#x27;s series of hydrogen atom?\n[Answer: (a) 91 nm, (b) 121.5 nm]\nASSIGNMENT 19.2\nWhat is the wavelength of the second line of paschen series?\nPASCHEN SERIES\n(Answer: 1281.43 nm)\n19.4 DE-BROGLIE WAVES AND HYDROGEN ATOM\nOf all the postulates of Bohr Model of the hydrogen atom, the most puzzling is the assumption about the angular momentum that an electron cannot revolve around the nucleus in any arbitrary circular orbit. Only those orbits are possible for which the angular momentum is the integral multiple of h/2m.\nIn 1923, ten years after Bohr&#x27;s work, de Broglie pointed out that his own theory for the wavelength of a moving particle could provide an answer to this question.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\nNOT FOR SALE\n314\nAccording to de Broglie\nh\nh\nλ=\n1\nP mv\nThe electron in its circular Bohr orbit must be pictured as a particle wave as shown\nin figure 19.9 (a).\nFIGURE 19.9\nn=2\nn = 3\n(a) Like waves traveling on a string, particle waves can lead to standing waves under resonant conditions.\n(b) Bohr orbits are shown for which 2mr 27, 2πr = 37 and 2mr= 47, these state are possible for electrons orbiting the nucleus all the intermediate stages are not possible.\n2\n2πr\n2x‍ = nλ.\nor 2\nn\nh\n2πr\nn\nComparing equation 1 and equation 2 we get\naz\nnh\nог\nmvr\n19.8\n2π\nwhich is just what Bohr assumed for the angular momentum of the electron. As an example, Figure19.9 (b) illustrates the standing particle wave on a Bohr orbit for which 2mr = 37. Whereas those orbits are not possible where this condition is not\nmet.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\n19.5 X-RAYS\nNOT FOR SALE\n315\nX-rays are electromagnetic waves with wavelengths in the region of an Angstrom Å (100m).\nA. PRODUCTION OF X-RAYS\nFigure 19.10 shows an arrangement of producing X-rays. An X-ray tube is a vacuum tube that produces X-rays. It consist of filament heated by current supplied from battery, which emits electrons. These electrons are accelerated towards rotating anode, anode is rotated to reduce overheating. A high voltage power source, for example 30 to 150 kilovolts (kV), is connected across cathode and anode to accelerate the electrons. When such high energy electrons are suddenly stopped by target, an intense beam of X-rays are produced. The X-ray spectrum depends on the anode material and the accelerating voltage. The range of photon energies emitted by the system can be adjusted by changing the applied voltage, and installing aluminum filters of varying thicknesses.\nFIGURE 19.10\nConnections\nV\nX-rays\nBeam\nRotor\nBearings\nFilament window Anode disk\nX-ray production and X-ray tube\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA\nFor your information\nNOT FOR SALE 316\nX-RAYS DISCOVERY\nRöntgen discovered X-rays in November 1895 in his laboratory at the University of Wurzburg, Germany. Röntgen was investigating cathode rays using fluorescent screen painted with barium platinocyanide and a Crookes tube which he had wrapped in black cardboard. He noticed that when the tube was switched on some invisible rays coming from it were passing through the cardboard to make the screen glow which was placed about 1 meter away.\ncathode ray tube covered with\nblack paper\nglowing fluorescent\nscreen\nRÖNTGEN (1845-1923)\nWilhelm Conrad Rontgen (Germany). In recognition of the extraordinary services he has rendered by the discovery of X rays he was awarded with Nobel Prize for Physics in 1901\nRöntgen studied them throughly and investigated nearly all the properties associated with them. He is also gave them the name &quot;X-rays&quot; (signifying an unknown quantity).\nav\nIt should be noted that about 1% of the energy generated is emitted/radiated, usually perpendicular to the path of the electron beam, as X-rays, the rest is converted into heat. The target becomes very hot and therefore should have high melting point. Typical target materials are a tungsten-rhenium target on a molybdenum core..\nB. X-RAY SPECTRUM\nWe can understand X-rays by studying the spectrum of radiation emitted. It consist of two parts i.e. characteristic X-rays and continuous X-rays.",
