        "sciNote": "(c) stimulated (or\nemission\nabsorption\ninduced) emission\nSpontaneous and Stimulated Emissions\nii. Induced Absorption: The second interaction, induced absorption, is responsible for absorption spectra and resonance absorption. An atom in the ground state absorbs a photon (of the proper energy) and makes a transition to an excited state.\natom + photon → atom\niii. Stimulated emission: The third interaction, which is responsible for the operation of the laser, is stimulated (or induced) emission. In this process, an atom is initially in an excited state. A passing photon of just the right energy (again, equal to the energy difference of the two levels) induces the atom to emit a photon and make a transition to the lower or ground state.\natom + photon atom + 2 photon\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA!\n323\nNOT FOR SALE The two photons that emerge are travel in exactly the same direction with same energy, and the associated electromagnetic waves are perfectly in phase (coherent).\nB. LASER ACTION\nSuppose we have collection of atoms and potential difference is applied to excite the atoms to the same energy level as shown in figure 19.15. These atoms are confined in a cylinder one side of which is having a totally reflecting mirror and the other is partially reflecting to allow the beam of light to escape.\nFIGURE 19.15\nHigh voltage\nActive medium\nTotally reflecting mirror\nPartially reflecting\nmirror\nLASER construction and working\nA photon passes the first atom, causing induced emission"
