        "theory": "Why do the spectral lines in the hydrogen atom become closer together farther away from the nucleus?\nWhy it was necessary to quantize angular momentum in Bohr&#x27;s model of the atom?\n6 Why X-rays have different properties from light even though both originate.\nfrom orbital transition of electrons in excited atoms?\nIf the potential difference in an x-ray tube is increased, how does this affect the wavelengths of (a) bremsstrahlung xrays and (b) characteristic x-rays? ® Crystal lattices can be examined with x-rays but not UV. Why?\nPHYSICS-XII\n5\nUNIT 19 ATOMIC SPECTRA)\nCOMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS\n3\nNOT FOR SALE\n332\nDescribe the spectrum of hydrogen atom in detail.\nWhat are Bohr&#x27;s postulates about hydrogen atom? Hence derive expression for the (a) radii of electron orbit (b) energy of the electron.\nExplain the terms Ground state, Excited state, Excitation energy, lonization\nenergy.\nWhat are X-rays? How these rays are produced? Differentiate between Characteristic and Continuous X-rays.\nWhat is a laser? Explain the principle and operation of a laser.\nNUMERICAL PROBLEMS\n1 Calculate the shortest and longest wavelength of radiation for the Paschen\nSeries.\n(Answer: 1875 nm, 820 nm)\n2 In the fifth energy level determine the radius and energy of electron for\nhydrogen atom?\n(Answer: 1.323 nm, -8.72 x 10 Jor-0.545 eV)\n3 An electron drops from the second energy level to the first energy level within an excited hydrogen atom (a) determine the energy of the photon emitted (b) calculate the frequency of the photon emitted (c) calculate the wavelength of the photon emitted.\n(Answer: (a) 10.2 eV (b) 2.5x1015 Hz (c) 120 nm) Find the wavelength associated with an electron in the state n=4 of hydrogen. An electron is in the first Bohr orbit of hydrogen atom. (a) Find the speed of electron, and (b) the time required for electron to circle the nucleus.\n(Answer: 1.33 nm)\n(Answer: (a) 2.19 x 106 m/s (b) 1.52 x 10-165)\n6 If the shortest wavelength produced by an X-ray tube is 0.46 nm, what is the\nvoltage applied to the tube?\n(Answer: 2.7 kV)\nPHYSICS-XII\nn\nUNIT\n20\nNOT FOR SALE\n333\nNUCLEAR PHYSICS\nHow Sun produces large amount of energy?\nLEARNING\nAfter studying this unit the students will be able to\ndescribe a simple model for the atom to include protons, neutrons and\nelectrons.\nDetermine the number of protons, neutrons and nucleons it contains for the specification of a nucleus in the form &quot;X.\nexplain that an element can exist in various isotopic forms each with a different number of neutrons.\nexplain the use of mass spectrograph to demonstrate the existence of isotopes and to measure their relative abundance.\nO⚫ define the terms unified mass scale, mass defect and calculate binding energy.\nOUTCOMES\nusing Einstein&#x27;s equation.\nillustrate graphically the variation of binding energy per nucleon with the mass\nnumber.\nexplain the relevance of binding energy per nucleon to nuclear fusion and to\nnuclear fission.\nidentify that some nuclei are unstable, give out radiation to get rid of excess energy and are said to be radioactive.\ndescribe that an element may change into another element when radioactivity\n⚫ occurs.\nPHYSICS-XII",
