        "sciNote": "UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n334\nJWARZ-ZU\nidentify the spontaneous and random nature of nuclear decay.\ndescribe the term half life and solve problems using the equation λ=0.693/T12- determine the release of energy from different nuclear reactions.\n⚫ explain that atomic number and mass number conserve in nuclear reactions.\ndescribe energy and mass conservation in simple reactions and in radioactive decay.\nN\nN\nG\ndescribe the fission chain reaction.\nOUTCOMES\ndescribe the phenomena of nuclear fission and fusion.\ndescribe the function of various components of a nuclear reactor.\ndescribe the interaction of nuclear radiation with matter.\n⚫ describe the use of Geiger Muller counter and solid state detectors to detect the\nradiations.\ndescribe the basic forces of nature.\ndescribe the key features and components of the standard model of matter including hadrons, leptons and quarks.\nAn atom has a nucleus. The nucleus is positively charged. The overall dimensions of a nucleus are much smaller than those of an atom. The radius of a nucleus is 10\" times smaller than the radius of an atom. This means the volume of a nucleus is about 10 times the smaller than volume of the atom. For an analogy, if an atom is enlarged to the size of a classroom, the nucleus would just be about the size of pinhead. In other words, an atom is almost empty, as (more than 99.9%) of the mass of an atom is concentrated at the nucleus.\nDoes the nucleus have a structure, just like atoms? Yes there are many sim",
