        "theory": "86\n342\n50\n100\nNucleon Number\n150\n200\nThe graph of binding energy per nucleon in MeV to the nucleon number is shown in the figure 20.6.\na\n1. Intermediate Nuclei: The most stable nuclei lie in the region of greater stability and about A = 50 to 60 this is where binding energy per nucleon is greatest almost\nFe) of 8.7 MeV called intermediate size nuclei. (e. gNi and II. Small Nuclei: The decrease in Packing fraction &#x27;f&#x27; for small &#x27;A&#x27; is due to surface effect. The particles at or near the surface are less strongly bond than the nucleons at the interior. The smaller the nucleus the larger the percentage constituents at the nuclear surface. (e.g&quot;,He and &quot;,O)\nof\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n343\nH. Large Nuclei: The decrease in &#x27;f&#x27; for large nuclei is because of large number of protons. The coulomb repulsion between protons opposes the binding effect of nuclear force. Therefore nuclei with large &#x27;A&#x27; are unstable. (e.gn and The instability shows why a nuclear fission results in large nuclei such as fission fragments of the nuclei undergoing fission lie in the region of stability. The binding energy increases from 7.5 MeV to nearly 8.5 MeV. This difference is the energy liberated in a nuclear fission process.\nEXAMPLE 20.1\nU. The RU).\ngreater\nSODIUM NUCLEUS\nSodium 23Na has an atomic mass of 22.989,769 u. Find the (a) mass defect (b) binding energy and (c) binding energy per nucleon for this nucleus.\nGIVEN\nNumber of protons &#x27;Z&#x27; = 11\nMass of proton &#x27;M,&#x27; = 1.007276 u\nNumber of neutrons &#x27;N&#x27; = 12\nMass of neutron &quot;M, 1.008665 u\nMass of Sodium nucleus M = 22.989769 u\nSOLUTION\n(a) The mass defect is given by\nREQUIRED\nMass defect &#x27;Am&#x27; =?\nBinding Energy &quot;E,&#x27; = ?\nBinding energy per nucleon &#x27;f&#x27; = ?\nAm=ZM2+NMN-M\nPutting values Am=11(1.007825 u) +12 (1.008665 u)-22.989769 u\nor Am=11.086075 u+12.10398u-22.989769u\ntherefore Am=0.200286 u\nAnswer\n(b) The Binding energy is given by E1 = Amxc2 = Amx931.5 MeV\nPutting values E=0.200286x931.5 MeV\ntherefore\nEB = 186.566 MeV\nAnswer\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n344\n(c) The packing fraction (binding fraction) is the binding energy per nucleon, and\nis given as\nEB\nsince A = Z+N=11+12=23\nA\n186.566\nPutting values\nf\nMeV\n23\ntherefore\nf=8.11MeV\nAnswer\nCARBON NUCLEUS\nASSIGNMENT 20.1\nFind the mass defect, expressed in kilograms, and the binding energy for a\ncarbon-12 nucleus.\n20.6 RADIOACTIVITY\n(Answer: 0.098 940 u or 1.6429 x 1028 kg, 92.16 MeV)\nThe spontaneous release of subatomic particles or gamma rays by unstable atoms as their nuclei decay to attain stability is called radioactivity. The element which possess such property is called radioactive element.\nN\nIn order to attain stability element emit three types of radiation: alpha (a), in which the emitted particles are &quot;He nuclei; beta (B), in which the emitted particles are either electrons or positrons; and gamma (y), in which the emitted &#x27;rays&#x27; are high-energy photons. Among about 3000 known nuclides, only 257 are stable. The time scale of these decay processes ranges from a small fraction of a microsecond to billions of years. When the radiation from a radioactive sample is directed into a region in which there is either an electric or a magnetic field as shown in Figure 20.7, we can separate it into three forms of radiation alpha (a), beta (B) and gamma (1). Table 20.3 gives properties of these radiations you studied in grade 10.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nFIGURE 20.7\nPhotographic plate\nB\nNegative Positive\nNOT FOR SALE\n345\nPhotographic plate\nMagnetic field",
