        "id": "20.7",
        "title": "20.6 RADIOACTIVITY",
        "theory": "18F = 180+B++v+Q\nFIGURE 20.9\nAY\nZ41\n234.\n90 Th\nP\nx\n品\n24Th=24Pa+B+5+Q\nThe emission of a beta particle by thorium-234 results in the formation of\nprotactinium-234\nFIGURE 20.10\nov\n23\nMg\n12\n4X\n2.17\nB\n11\n22Th = Na+ B +u+Q\nThe positron emission (B+ decay) is shown with magnesium-23 decaying into sodium-23\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n348\nNotice, in electron emission, an electron antineutrino is also emitted. Whereas the positron emission is accompanied by the emission of an electron neutrino. The electron neutrino is a subatomic particle which has no net electric charge. Like all particles, the electron neutrino has a corresponding antiparticle, the electron antineutrino, which differs only in that some of its properties have equal magnitude but opposite sign.\nScience Tidbits\nWhy a neutrino is emitted in Beta decay?\nBeta decay was a puzzle at first because a continuous spectrum of electron (or positron) kinetic energies was observed. Beta particles are ejected from nucleus at different speeds. In a decay, the definite kinetic energy is same for all particles to conserve energy and momentum. For the same reasons, scientists thought that ẞ particles. emitted in a given decay reaction should also be monoenergetic.\nWolfgang Pauli suggested that not one, but two particles were being emitted in beta decay an electron/positron and neutrino/antineutrino. If a nucleus emits two particles instead of one, then they can conserve both energy and momentum while splitting up the kinetic energy in every possible way. Two momentum vectors that add to zero must The be equal in magnitude and opposite in direction, but three momentum vectors can add to zero in an infinite number of ways and still share the same total kinetic energy. neutrino has the following properties:\nZero electric charge\nAmass much smaller than that of the electron, but probably not zero.\ne in\n(Recent experiments suggest that the neutrino definitely has mass, but the value is uncertain, perhaps less than 1 eV/c.)\nAspin of 1/2\nay\nVery weak interaction with matter, making it difficult to detect\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS-\nC. GAMMA EMISSION\nNOT FOR SALE\n349\nGamma rays are electromagnetic radiation emitted from the nucleus. Gamma radiation emission occurs when the nucleus of a radioactive atom has excess energy. Inside the nucleus the nucleons (like the orbital electrons) exists in discrete energy states or levels. When a nucleus changes from an excited state to a lower energy state, a photon is emitted. The photon has a much greater energy and is called a y ray. In most cases the a or B emission from the nucleus leave it in excited state such nuclei achieve further stability by emitting gamma\nrays.\nenergy\n(x) = x+Y.\n(Co) = CO + Y\nFIGURE 20.11\nilab\n4X\nRa\n226\n(22Ra) = Ra + 7\nThe emission of a gamma ray by radium-226 results radium-226 with\n20.7 SPONTANEOUS AND RANDOM NUCLEAR DECAY\ngamma\nA process of disintegration that occurs when unstable nuclei decay in an attempt to attain stability is called nuclear decay. This process is both spontaneous and\nrandom.\nA process without defined pattern, rule or method is called random process. Nuclear decay is unpredictable because precise moment of disintegration is not known for a particular nuclei. Individual disintegrations occur randomly.\nA\nprocess occurring without apparent external cause is called spontaneous process. Nuclear decay is spontaneous, because the process of radioactive decay cannot be speeded up or slowed down by physical or chemical means.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\n20.8 HALF LIFE AND RATE OF DECAY\nNOT FOR SALE 350\nThe time it takes for half of the radioactive nuclei in a sample to decay is called half life.\nIndividual disintegrations are random, however, the probability of decay for nuclei in a sample has fixed value, which is characteristic of that material.\nThus the amount of radioactive isotope in the sample decrease with time as shown in the figure 20.12 for Half life curve. The number of nuclei present at time t = 0s is N = N,, and the number present at t= T is N = N,/2. The number present at t = 2 T12 is N = N,/4, and so on. For example, &quot;C has a half-life of 15730 years, because it takes this amount of time for one-half of a given quantity of this isotope to disintegrate. In another 15730-years, one-half of the remaining &quot;C atoms will have disintegrated, leaving only one-fourth of the original number intact. The rate of decay or activity of radioactive sample is defined as the number of disintegrations that occur per second.\nIf &#x27;AN&#x27; is the number of radioactive nuclei that disintegrate in time &#x27;At&quot;, then\nAN oc-N\n1\n2\nFIGURE 20.12\n100\nN\nPercentage of Sample\n50\nwaz e in",
