        "theory": "α\ncarbon-14\nB\nDecay type\nHalf Life\n2.0 x 10&quot; seconds\n5.73 x 10&#x27; years\noxygen - 15\nB\ncobalt- 60\niodine-131\nB.Y\nLead - 212\nB.Y\npolonium - 214\nα, Y\n122.24 seconds\n5.3 years\n8.04 days\n10.6 hours\n0.1643 seconds\nradium-226\na. Y\n1.62 x 10 years\neinsteinium-254\nα, B.Y\n275.7 days\nThe activity &#x27;A&#x27; is number of disintegrations per unit time (AN/At) from equation 4 equals the decay constant times the number of radioactive nuclei in the sample\nas\nA\nAN\nAt\n= AN\n20.9\ne\n(H) Units of Activity: A common unit of activity is the curie, abbreviated Ci, which is defined to be 3.70 x 100 decays per second. This is approximately equal to the activity of one gram of radium. The SI unit of activity is the becquerel, abbreviated Bq. One becquerel is one decay per second.\n1 Ci = 3.70 x 10&quot; Bq = 3.70 x 10&quot; decays/s\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS-\nEXAMPLE 20.2\nNOT FORSALE 353\nRADIUM\nThe half-life of the radioactive radium (226Ra) nucleus is 5.0 x 1019 s. A sample contains 3.0 x 1016 nuclei. (a) What is the decay constant for this decay? (b) How many radium nuclei will decay per second?\nGIVEN\nHalf life &#x27;T&#x27; 5.0 x 1010 S\nNumber of nuclei &#x27;N&#x27; = 3.0 x 1016\nSOLUTION\nREQUIRED\n(a) decay constant &#x27;&#x27; =?\n(b) Activity &#x27;A&#x27; =?\n(a) The relation between decay constant A and half life T is T\nVab\n5x1010s\nRearranging λ =\n0.693 T1/2\nPutting values λ= 0.693\nλ=1.4x10111s 1\nAnswer\ntherefore\n(b) The activity is\n0.693\nA = 2N Putting values A=1.4x10&quot;x3x10 A = 4.2×105 decays/&lt;= 4.2x105 Bq\ntherefore A = 4.2x105 Bq = 0.42 MBq Answer\n-\nIn curies this activity can also be written as\n1\n1Bq\n3.70x1010 Ci\nMultiplying both sides by 4.2 x 105 we get\n4.2x10 Bq\n4.2×105 3.70x1010\nCi\nASSIGNMENT 20.2\n4.2x10 Bq=1.135x10-Ci=0.11μCi\nCARBON\nThe radioisotope of carbon 14C has a half-life of 5730 yr. If a sample contains 1.00 x 1022 carbon-14 nuclei, what is the activity of the sample?\n(Answer: 3.83 x 1010 Bq)\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n354",
