        "id": "20.13",
        "title": "20.12 NUCLEAR FISSION",
        "theory": "(a) In a sub-critical mass, too many neutrons escape before colliding (b) in the critical mass, enough neutrons encounter other nuclei to maintain a chain reaction.\nFuel pellets contain enriched uranium-235. Each pellet contains the energy equivalent of I\nton of coal.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\n20.13 NUCLEAR REACTOR\nNOT FOR SALE\n364\nA nuclear reactor is a device that is designed to maintain a self sustaining fission reaction in a controlled manner.\nSince large amount of energy is released in a fission process, therefore it is carried under controlled conditions. This energy can be used for useful purposes such as to produce power and radioisotopes. The Nuclear reactor has five main parts as shown in figure 20.18.\nCORE\nThe core contain nuclear fuel in which fission is achieved by thermal neutrons, for example, the nuclear fuel may be thin rods about 1 cm in diameter. Uranium &quot;&quot;U is a common reactor fuel. Most commercial reactors use uranium in which the amount of &quot;U has been enriched to about 3%.\nFIGURE 20.18\nControl\nrods\nRadiation\nshielding\nPressure\nVessel\nGraphite\nModerator\nFuel rods\nPHYSICS-XII\nHot gas duct\nSteam\nHeat exchanger\nWater circulator\nWater\nGas circulator\nCool gas duct\nUNIT ZU NULLAN ETTEƏNGƏ\nB. MODERATOR\nNOT FOR SALE\n365\nThe material that slows down the neutrons is called a moderator. Neutrons with energies of about 0.025 eV are used in fission of &quot;&quot;U, the neutrons released during the fission process have greater energies of several MeV. Therefore, a nuclear reactor must contain some type of material that decrease or moderate the speed of neutrons so they can fission additional &quot;U nuclei. The material of moderator should be light and should not absorb neutrons, usually graphite and heavy water\nare used as moderators.\nC. CONTROL RODS\nIf the output power from a reactor is to remain constant, only one neutron from each fission event must start a new fission. When each fission leads to one additional fission-no more or no less-the reactor is said to be critical. A reactor normally operates in a critical condition.\nScience Tidbits Sub-critical and Super-critical state of reactors\nWhat happens when reactor is not operated in critical state?\nThe reactor is subcritical when, on average, the neutrons from each fission trigger less than one subsequent fission. In a subcritical reactor, the chain reaction is not self- sustaining and eventually stops. When the neutrons from each fission trigger more than one additional fission, the reactor is supercritical. During a supercritical condition, the energy released by a reactor increases. If left unchecked, the increasing energy can lead to a partial or total meltdown of the reactor core.\nTherefore, a control mechanism is needed to keep the reactor in its normal, or critical, state. This control is accomplished by a number of control rods that can be moved into and out of the reactor core. The control rods contain an element, such as boron, cadmium or hafnium, that absorbs neutrons. These control rods are moved in and out of reactor core to keep reactor in critical state.\nD. COOLANT OR HEAT EXCHANGER\nthe\nThe coolant is used to cool the fuel rods and the moderator, and is capable of carrying away heat generated in fission process. Without Heat exchange system core assembly can melt under enormous heat. The heat generated within the fuel rods is carried away by water that surrounds the rods. The hot water is pumped through a heat exchanger, where heat is transferred to water flowing in a\nsecond, closed system.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n366\nThe heat carried by coolant can run turbine to generate electricity. After exiting the turbine, the steam is condensed back into water that is returned to the heat exchanger.\nF SHIELDING\nSince neutrons and fission fragment in the nuclear reactor produce radiations which are harmful to life and environment. There must be some process of absorbing these radiations. Usually a concrete wall with lead of a few feet thickness is used as shielding.\nF.TYPES OF NUCLEAR REACTOR\nBasically there are two types of nuclear reactors based neutron energy.\nI. Thermal reactors: The reactors in which thermal energy neutrons are used are called thermal reactors. There are many designs of thermal reactor. Pressurized Water Reactor (PWR) is most commonly used reactor in the world. In this reactor the water is prevented from boiling at 100°C, by providing high pressure.\nii. Fast Reactors: The reactors in which fast energy neutrons are used are called fast reactors. Fast reactor make use of U which is about 99% in nature. each atom of &quot;U absorb fast neutron and the following reactions\noccur.\nחו\n1. Neutron absorption by uranium - 238\n2. Beta decay of uranium - 239\n3. Beta decay of neptunium - 239\nFIGURE 20.19\non + 238U → 232U\nU→\n92\n232U→233Np+B+ov\nav\n239 Np-&gt;232 Pu+B+v\n94\non\n238U\n23.5 min\n239U\n239 93\nB\n2.4 days\n239Pu\nAn induced nuclear reaction is shown in which Uranium - 238 is\ntransmuted into the plutonium-239",
