        "sciNote": "A. Proton-proton\nC. Neutron-Neutron\nB. Proton - Neutron\nD. All of these\n14 Which of the following is a boson\nA. proton\nB. neutrino\nC. photon\nD. pion\n15 The hadrons formed by combination of quark and anti-quark pair are\ncalled\nA. baryons B. mesons\nC. meuons\n16 All free particles have a color charge of\nA.0\nB. 2/3\nD. neutrinos\nC. 1/3\nD. 1\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS\nWhy do heavier nuclei have more neutrons than protons?\n2 Radium has a half-life of about 1600 years. If the universe was formed five\nbillion or more years ago, why is there any radium left now?\n3 If we focus our attention on a specific nucleus in a radioactive sample, can\nwe know exactly how long that nucleus will live before it decays?\n4 Why are neutrons such good projectiles for producing nuclear reactions? Why neutron activated nuclides tend to decay by ẞ- rather than B+?\n6 Why are small and large nuclei unstable?\nWhy is a conventional fission nuclear reactor not able to explode as a bomb? 8 Why does the fusion of light nuclei into heavier nuclei release energy?\n9 What factors make a fusion reaction difficult to achieve?\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nCOMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS\nNOT FOR SALE 381\nWhat is meant by natural radioactivity? How are the natural radioactive radiations classified into three types?\nExplain the principle, construction, working and necessary mathematical theory of a mass spectrometer.\nWhat are isotopes? Explain with examples.\n0 Explain the term mass defect and binding energy related to a nucleus.\nD",
