        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 1",
        "theory": "17 Electrons\n(b) The most stable\nartificial isotope of chlorine\nOver 3,000 isotopes either exist in nature or have been made artificially in particle accelerators, out of which only 257 are stable. The remaining isotopes are unstable and are termed as radioisotopes.\n20.3 MASS SPECTROGRAPH\ne\nA device which is used to determine the mass of charged particles is called mass spectrograph. This device can also be used to separate isotopes of chemical element and to determine their relative abundance.\nA mass spectrograph is based upon the principle that a beam of ions moving through electric and magnetic fields is deflected, and this deflection depends upon the charge and mass of ions.\nA mass spectrometer can be used to separate a mixture of ions having different masses. A schematic view of mass spectrograph is shown in the figure 20.4. The atoms or molecules understudy in vapor form are pushed into ionization chamber. Where an electron is removed from them leaving them with a net positive charge. The charged particles are accelerated through acceleration plates through\npotential difference &#x27;V&#x27; applied.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nFIGURE 20.4\nlonization chamber\nAcceleration Plates\nNOT FOR SALE\n337\nvelocity selector\nB out of page\nVacuum Pump\nHeater\nCO\nDetector\nChart recorder\nAmplifier\nDetection Chamber\nThe beam is then passed through a velocity selector which leaves only the same velocity particles. Thus by leaving the velocity selector the kinetic energy of single charge ion will be given by the equation\n+ mv2 = qv\n2qV\nor\n1\nThese ions are then subjected to perpendicular and uniform magnetic field &quot;B&quot;, in the vacuum chamber, where they are deflected in semicircular path towards the detector. The centripetal force is provided by the magnetic force\nmy2\nqB\nqvB\nor V= xr\nm\nComparing equation 1 and equation 2 qB\n2qV\nxr=\nm\nm\nSquaring both sides\nq2B22 24V\nm2\nr2B2q\nSolving for m we get\nM=\n2V\nSo we can obtain the mass if f, B, q and V are known. By rearranging the above\nequation we can also write\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n338\n2Vm\n20.1\nB2q\nThis equation 20:1 shows that if &#x27;V&#x27;, &#x27;B&#x27; and &#x27;q&#x27; are not changed the &#x27;r&#x27; depends upon the square root of mass &#x27;m&#x27;. Thus ions of different masses will strike the detector at different points thus the isotopes can be separated from each other.\n20.4 NUCLEAR MASSES\nNuclear masses are measured in unified atomic mass scale. According to this scale one atomic mass unit is exactly as the mass of 12&quot; part of the mass of carbon 12\natom.\n1u\nmass of one &quot;C atom 12\nOther masses in this unit are then measured by comparison.\nA. UNIFIED ATOMIC MASS UNIT IN KILOGRAM\nAs we know that 1 kg mole of any element contain Avogadro&#x27;s number of atoms. Thus\nог\nMass of 6.023 x10 atoms of &quot;C= 12kg\nMass of 1 atoms of &quot;C=\n12\n6.023 x10\nand\nMass of\n1/24\natoms of 12C\n12\nkg\naz e in\n12 6.023 x10 kg",
        "content": "17 Electrons<br>(b) The most stable<br>artificial isotope of chlorine<br>Over 3,000 isotopes either exist in nature or have been made artificially in particle accelerators, out of which only 257 are stable. The remaining isotopes are unstable and are termed as radioisotopes.<br>20.3 MASS SPECTROGRAPH<br>e<br>A device which is used to determine the mass of charged particles is called mass spectrograph. This device can also be used to separate isotopes of chemical element and to determine their relative abundance.<br>A mass spectrograph is based upon the principle that a beam of ions moving through electric and magnetic fields is deflected, and this deflection depends upon the charge and mass of ions.<br>A mass spectrometer can be used to separate a mixture of ions having different masses. A schematic view of mass spectrograph is shown in the figure 20.4. The atoms or molecules understudy in vapor form are pushed into ionization chamber. Where an electron is removed from them leaving them with a net positive charge. The charged particles are accelerated through acceleration plates through<br>potential difference &#x27;V&#x27; applied.<br>PHYSICS-XII<br>UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS<br>FIGURE 20.4<br>lonization chamber<br>Acceleration Plates<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>337<br>velocity selector<br>B out of page<br>Vacuum Pump<br>Heater<br>CO<br>Detector<br>Chart recorder<br>Amplifier<br>Detection Chamber<br>The beam is then passed through a velocity selector which leaves only the same velocity particles. Thus by leaving the velocity selector the kinetic energy of single charge ion will be given by the equation<br>+ mv2 = qv<br>2qV<br>or<br>1<br>These ions are then subjected to perpendicular and uniform magnetic field &quot;B&quot;, in the vacuum chamber, where they are deflected in semicircular path towards the detector. The centripetal force is provided by the magnetic force<br>my2<br>qB<br>qvB<br>or V= xr<br>m<br>Comparing equation 1 and equation 2 qB<br>2qV<br>xr=<br>m<br>m<br>Squaring both sides<br>q2B22 24V<br>m2<br>r2B2q<br>Solving for m we get<br>M=<br>2V<br>So we can obtain the mass if f, B, q and V are known. By rearranging the above<br>equation we can also write<br>PHYSICS-XII<br>UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>338<br>2Vm<br>20.1<br>B2q<br>This equation 20:1 shows that if &#x27;V&#x27;, &#x27;B&#x27; and &#x27;q&#x27; are not changed the &#x27;r&#x27; depends upon the square root of mass &#x27;m&#x27;. Thus ions of different masses will strike the detector at different points thus the isotopes can be separated from each other.<br>20.4 NUCLEAR MASSES<br>Nuclear masses are measured in unified atomic mass scale. According to this scale one atomic mass unit is exactly as the mass of 12&quot; part of the mass of carbon 12<br>atom.<br>1u<br>mass of one &quot;C atom 12<br>Other masses in this unit are then measured by comparison.<br>A. UNIFIED ATOMIC MASS UNIT IN KILOGRAM<br>As we know that 1 kg mole of any element contain Avogadro&#x27;s number of atoms. Thus<br>ог<br>Mass of 6.023 x10 atoms of &quot;C= 12kg<br>Mass of 1 atoms of &quot;C=<br>12<br>6.023 x10<br>and<br>Mass of<br>1/24<br>atoms of 12C<br>12<br>kg<br>az e in<br>12 6.023 x10 kg",
