        "sciNote": "17 Electrons\n(b) The most stable\nartificial isotope of chlorine\nOver 3,000 isotopes either exist in nature or have been made artificially in particle accelerators, out of which only 257 are stable. The remaining isotopes are unstable and are termed as radioisotopes.\n20.3 MASS SPECTROGRAPH\ne\nA device which is used to determine the mass of charged particles is called mass spectrograph. This device can also be used to separate isotopes of chemical element and to determine their relative abundance.\nA mass spectrograph is based upon the principle that a beam of ions moving through electric and magnetic fields is deflected, and this deflection depends upon the charge and mass of ions.\nA mass spectrometer can be used to separate a mixture of ions having different masses. A schematic view of mass spectrograph is shown in the figure 20.4. The atoms or molecules understudy in vapor form are pushed into ionization chamber. Where an electron is removed from them leaving them with a net positive charge. The charged particles are accelerated through acceleration plates through\npotential difference 'V' applied.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nFIGURE 20.4\nlonization chamber\nAcceleration Plates\nNOT FOR SALE\n337\nvelocity selector\nB out of page\nVacuum Pump\nHeater\nCO\nDetector\nChart recorder\nAmplifier\nDetection Chamber\nThe beam is then passed through a velocity selector which leaves only the same velocity particles. Thus by leaving the velocity selector the kinetic energy of single charge ion"
