        "sciNote": "therefore 1u=1.660×10−27 kg\nB. UNIFIED ATOMIC MASS UNIT IN JOULES\n20.2\nIn nuclear physics we sometime also give the masses in energy units. As by Einstein\nmass energy relation we have\nAE1 = (Am)c2 = (1.6605 x 10-27 kg)(2.9979x10 m/s)2\ntherefore\n1u 1.4924 x 10-10 J\n20.3\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n339\nC. UNIFIED ATOMIC MASS UNIT IN ELECTRON VOLTS\nAlso in nuclear physics we sometime also give the masses in energy units as electron volts, as\n1u= (1.4924x10-10 J) 1eV\n9.315x10 eV\n1.6022x10-19 J\ntherefore\n1u=931.5 MeV\n20.4\nAll the masses of different particles can be converted into this scale as shown in the table 20.1.\nTable 20.1 Rest masses in Kilograms, Unified Atomic Mass Units and MeV\nParticle\nkg\nMeV/c\nElectron\n9.1164 × 10\nx\n0.0005485\n0.511\nProton\n1.67262 x 10\n1.007276\n938.27\nNeutron\n1.67493 x 10\n1.008665\n939.57\nHydrogen\n1.67353 x 10\n1.007825\n938.78\nDeuterium\n3.34449 x 10\"\n2.014102\n1876.12\nTritium\n5.00827 x 10\"\n3.016049\n2809.43\nHelium\n6.64648 x 10\n4.002603\n3728.40\nCarbon\n1.99265x 10\n12.000000\n11177.9\n20.5 MASS DEFECT AND BINDING ENERGY\nThe total mass of a nucleus is less than the sum of the masses of its individual nucleons. For example as shown in the table 20.1 six protons and six neutrons, each having a mass larger than 1 u, combined with six electrons to form a carbon-12 atom having a mass of exactly 12 u. The bound system of \"C has a lower rest energy than that of six separate protons and six separate neutrons. Similarly mass of helium nucleus which c"
