        "id": "20.4",
        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 3",
        "theory": "The binding energy is equal to the mass defect of the nucleus. Since the nucleus is a bound system, its total energy is less than the energy of Z protons and N neutrons that are far apart and at rest.\nin\nE, = (total energy of Z protons and N neutrons) - (total energy of nucleus) If M is the mass of the nucleus, M, is the mass of proton and M, is the mass of Neutron, the Binding energy of the nucleus will be:\nE = (ZMp + NM, Mc2\n20.5\nWhere Z is the number of protons and N is the number of neutron in the nucleus. The mass defect or mass deficit (Am) is given by the relation\nAm = ZMp + NMN-M\n20.6\nThe binding energy per nucleon of a nucleus is called packing fraction. If &#x27;A=N+Z&#x27; is the mass number (number of nucleons), then the packing fraction &#x27;f&#x27; is\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n341\nEB (ZMp +NMN-Mc2\nA\nA\n20.7\nThe packing fraction (binding fraction) is the measure of stability of a nucleus. The greater the binding energy per nucleon, the more stable the nucleus is.\nFor your information STRONG NUCLEAR FORCE\nSince very large repulsive Coulomb forces exists between the close-packed protons in a nucleus, and this force should cause the nucleus to fly apart. Because that does not happen, there must be a counteracting attractive force, this force is called strong nuclear force. The strong nuclear force is a very short range (about 2 fm) attractive force that acts between all nuclear particles. The protons attract each other by means of the nuclear force, and, at the same time, they repel each other through the Coulomb force. The nuclear force also acts between pairs of neutrons and between neutrons and protons. The nuclear force dominates the Coulomb repulsive force within the nucleus (at short ranges), so stable nuclei can exist.\nNucleus\n±\nTotal binding Binding energy\nTherefore, the rest energy of the TABLE 20.2 SOME BINDING ENERGIES bound system (the nucleus) is less than the combined rest energy of the separated nucleons. To separate the protons and neutron from a nucleus work would need to be done to overcome nuclear force, called binding energy. Binding energy is also equal to the energy liberated when a nucleus is created from other nucleons or nuclei. This nuclear binding energy is derived from the nuclear force and is the energy required to disassemble a nucleus into the same number of free, unbound neutrons and protons it is\ncomposed.\nenergy\n(MeV)\nper nucleon (MeV/nucleon)\nH\n2.23\n1.1\n4He\n28.3\n7.1\n12C\n92.1\n7.7\n160\n127.5\n8.0\n5+Fe\n492.3\n8.8\n63Cu\n552.1\n8.8\n1803\n7.6\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nFIGURE 20.6\n62\nNi\nNOT FOR SALE\nBinding energy per nucleon (MeV)\n1\n0\n28\n58\n133\n8 He\nFe\nCs\n2\n26\n55\n7\nㄧˋ\n2\nH\nHe\nRegion of greater stability\n208\n82\n238\nPb\nU\n92\n222\nRn",
