        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 5",
        "theory": "into the paper\nLead cylinder\nEvacuated chamber Radioactive\nEvacuated\nLead cylinder\nchamber\nmaterial\nRadioactive\nmaterial\n(a) Alpha, Beta and Gamma rays in electric field\n(b) Alpha, Beta and Gamma rays in magnetic field\nTABLE 20.3 RADIOACTIVE EMISSIONS\nRelative\nEmission\nDescription\nRest mass (in u)\nPenetrating\nCharge\nionizing\npower\nability\nHelium nuclei\nAlpha (a) (two protons and\n4.002603\n+2\nStopped by sheet of paper\nhighest\ntwo neutrons)\nseveral\nBeta (B)\nelectrons or\n0.000594\n-1, +1\nmillimetres of\nmedium\npositrons\naluminum\nGamma\nshort wavelength\nseveral\n(y)\nelectromagnetic\n0\n0\ncentimetres of\nlowest\nlead\nradiations\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nA. ALPHA (a) EMISSION\nNOT FOR SALE\nAlpha particles are helium nuclei (i.e two protons and two neutrons bound together) emitted from the nucleus. When the ratio of neutrons to protons in the nucleus is too low, certain atoms restore the balance by emitting alpha particles. In alpha decay, the original (parent) nuclide is converted to a &#x27;daughter&#x27; by the emission of an a particle. Balancing the reaction shows that the daughter nuclide has a nucleon number reduced by four and a charge reduced by two.\n2x = ^Y+a+Q\n205g= 5Rf+He (a)+Q\nThe spontaneous emission of an a-particle by some (heavy) nuclei is related to the compactness of the He nucleus whose binding energy is = 7 MeV/nucleon. Many Nuclei with Z&gt; 83 are radioactive in this mode. It is manifestation of quantum tunneling through potential barrier.\nFIGURE 20.8\nAY\n238\n92U\n234\nTh\nHe\n346\n232U= 236Th + 2He (a)+Q\n90\nThe emission of an alpha particle by uranium-238 results in the formation of thorium-234\nawa\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nB. BETA EMISSION\nNOT FOR SALE\n347\nBeta particle emission occurs when the ratio of neutrons to protons in the nucleus is too high. In this case, an excess neutron transforms into a proton and an electron. The proton stays in the nucleus and the electron is ejected energetically. A neutron by itself is unstable; and on average in 890 s will decay into a proton and an electron. Unlike a-decay, B-decay of a radionuclide does not change the number of nucleons. In essence, B-decay changes a neutron into a proton.\nC\nAX = ZAY + B ̄ ̄ +5 +Q\nZ+\n&quot;CN+B+U+Q\nPositron Beta emission: Like beta decay B, positron emission ẞ&quot; does not change the number of nucleons. In essence, ẞ decay changes a proton into a neutron.\n2X = 2AY+B++U+Q",
