        "theory": "Number of nucleons on LHS - Number of nucleons on RHS\nFor example in the reaction\nHe+NO+ H+Q.\nWe can see that on both sides there are (14+4=17+1=18) nucleons.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nCONSERVATION OF MASS ENERGY\nNOT FOR SALE\n358\nSince the mass number differ from atomic masses and difference provides binding energy to nucleons in the nucleus therefore energy conservation should also hold apart from conservation of nucleon number. This also include energy difference due to change in mass from Einstein mass energy relation.\nFor example in the reaction\nHe+NO+H+Q\nnuclear reaction energy Q can be calculated as\nLHS\nHe = 4.00263 u\nN=14.00307 u\n18.005677 u\nRHS H = 1.007825 u\n0 = 16.999133u\n18.006958 u\nSince Q is the rest mass difference, therefore Q = 18.005677u-18.006958 u\nQ=-0.001281 u =-1.192 MeV\nAs Q is negative therefore a-particle having kinetic energy 1.192 MeV can induce such a reaction.\nEXAMPLE 20.3\nNUCLEAR REACTION\nWhat is the least energy does the proton have to make the following nuclear reaction possible?\nH + C→ 1N+ on\n€ 13.00 az\nThe mass of hydrogen 1H is 1.007825 u, carbon 13C is 13.003355 u, neutron &#x27;n is 1.008665 u and nitrogen 13N is 13.005739 u.\nGIVEN\nREQUIRED\nMass of hydrogen &quot;H&quot;\nMass of carbon 13C\n1.007825 u\n13.003355 u\nProton energy Q = ?\nMass of neutron &quot;in&quot;= 1.008665 u\nMass of nitrogen 13N 13.005739 u\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nSOLUTION\nnuclear reaction energy Qcan be calculated as\nLHS\nH = 1.007825 u\n12C =13.003355u\n14.011180 u\nNOT FOR SALE\n359\nRHS &quot;N=13.005739 u\non = 1.008665 u\n= 14.014404 u\nSince Q is the rest mass difference, therefore Q-14.011180u - 14.014404 u,\nthis means that Q=-0.003224 u, therefore\nQ=-0.003224 ux931.5 MeV/u=3.003156 Mev 3.00 MeV Answer The negative sign shows that the reaction is endothermic, and proton would require more than 3.00 MeV energy to give excess energy to neutron and\nnitrogen after formation.\nASSIGNMENT 20.3\nNUCLEAR REACTION\nThe following reaction requires an input of energy equal to 2.438 MeV.\nila\nH+180 → &quot;F+ on\nThe mass of hydrogen &#x27;H is 1.007825 u, oxygen 10 is 17.999160 u and neutron 1n is 1.008665 u.\n20.12 NUCLEAR FISSION\n[Answer: 18.000937 u]\nThe process of splitting of nuclei into intermediate size nuclei is called nuclear fission. The fission process often produces free neutrons and photons (in the form of gamma rays), and releases a very large amount of energy.\nNuclear fission of heavy elements was discovered on December 17, 1938 by Otto Hahn and his assistant Fritz Strassmann, and explained theoretically in January 1939 by Lise Meitner and her nephew Otto Robert Frisch. Frisch named the process by analogy with biological fission of living cells.\nThey found that a uranium nucleus, after absorbing a neutron, splits into two fragments, of intermediate size. The general nuclear reaction of splitting of a massive nucleus into two less massive fragments is given below.\non + 235U → (235U) → X + Y +neutrons\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n360\nWhere (&quot;U)* is an intermediate excited state that Lise Meitner (1878 - 1968) lasts for only about 10&quot;s before splitting into X and Y. The resulting nuclei X and Y are called fission fragments. There are many combinations of X and Y that satisfy the requirements of conservation of mass-energy, charge, and nucleon number as shown in figure 20.15.\nThe process results in the production of several neutrons, typically two or three. On the average, about 2.5 neutrons are released per event. A typical reaction of this type is\n&#x27;n + 235U → (235U) → Ba + Kr+3n +Q\nwhere Q is the nuclear reaction energy.\nLise Meitner (Austrian- Swedish) physicist who contributed to the discoveries of the element protactinium and nuclear fission.\nFIGURE 20.15\n92\n124 Kr\n36",
        "content": "Number of nucleons on LHS - Number of nucleons on RHS<br>For example in the reaction<br>He+NO+ H+Q.<br>We can see that on both sides there are (14+4=17+1=18) nucleons.<br>PHYSICS-XII<br>UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS<br>CONSERVATION OF MASS ENERGY<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>358<br>Since the mass number differ from atomic masses and difference provides binding energy to nucleons in the nucleus therefore energy conservation should also hold apart from conservation of nucleon number. This also include energy difference due to change in mass from Einstein mass energy relation.<br>For example in the reaction<br>He+NO+H+Q<br>nuclear reaction energy Q can be calculated as<br>LHS<br>He = 4.00263 u<br>N=14.00307 u<br>18.005677 u<br>RHS H = 1.007825 u<br>0 = 16.999133u<br>18.006958 u<br>Since Q is the rest mass difference, therefore Q = 18.005677u-18.006958 u<br>Q=-0.001281 u =-1.192 MeV<br>As Q is negative therefore a-particle having kinetic energy 1.192 MeV can induce such a reaction.<br>EXAMPLE 20.3<br>NUCLEAR REACTION<br>What is the least energy does the proton have to make the following nuclear reaction possible?<br>H + C→ 1N+ on<br>€ 13.00 az<br>The mass of hydrogen 1H is 1.007825 u, carbon 13C is 13.003355 u, neutron &#x27;n is 1.008665 u and nitrogen 13N is 13.005739 u.<br>GIVEN<br>REQUIRED<br>Mass of hydrogen &quot;H&quot;<br>Mass of carbon 13C<br>1.007825 u<br>13.003355 u<br>Proton energy Q = ?<br>Mass of neutron &quot;in&quot;= 1.008665 u<br>Mass of nitrogen 13N 13.005739 u<br>PHYSICS-XII<br>UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS<br>SOLUTION<br>nuclear reaction energy Qcan be calculated as<br>LHS<br>H = 1.007825 u<br>12C =13.003355u<br>14.011180 u<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>359<br>RHS &quot;N=13.005739 u<br>on = 1.008665 u<br>= 14.014404 u<br>Since Q is the rest mass difference, therefore Q-14.011180u - 14.014404 u,<br>this means that Q=-0.003224 u, therefore<br>Q=-0.003224 ux931.5 MeV/u=3.003156 Mev 3.00 MeV Answer The negative sign shows that the reaction is endothermic, and proton would require more than 3.00 MeV energy to give excess energy to neutron and<br>nitrogen after formation.<br>ASSIGNMENT 20.3<br>NUCLEAR REACTION<br>The following reaction requires an input of energy equal to 2.438 MeV.<br>ila<br>H+180 → &quot;F+ on<br>The mass of hydrogen &#x27;H is 1.007825 u, oxygen 10 is 17.999160 u and neutron 1n is 1.008665 u.<br>20.12 NUCLEAR FISSION<br>[Answer: 18.000937 u]<br>The process of splitting of nuclei into intermediate size nuclei is called nuclear fission. The fission process often produces free neutrons and photons (in the form of gamma rays), and releases a very large amount of energy.<br>Nuclear fission of heavy elements was discovered on December 17, 1938 by Otto Hahn and his assistant Fritz Strassmann, and explained theoretically in January 1939 by Lise Meitner and her nephew Otto Robert Frisch. Frisch named the process by analogy with biological fission of living cells.<br>They found that a uranium nucleus, after absorbing a neutron, splits into two fragments, of intermediate size. The general nuclear reaction of splitting of a massive nucleus into two less massive fragments is given below.<br>on + 235U → (235U) → X + Y +neutrons<br>PHYSICS-XII<br>UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS<br>NOT FOR SALE<br>360<br>Where (&quot;U)* is an intermediate excited state that Lise Meitner (1878 - 1968) lasts for only about 10&quot;s before splitting into X and Y. The resulting nuclei X and Y are called fission fragments. There are many combinations of X and Y that satisfy the requirements of conservation of mass-energy, charge, and nucleon number as shown in figure 20.15.<br>The process results in the production of several neutrons, typically two or three. On the average, about 2.5 neutrons are released per event. A typical reaction of this type is<br>&#x27;n + 235U → (235U) → Ba + Kr+3n +Q<br>where Q is the nuclear reaction energy.<br>Lise Meitner (Austrian- Swedish) physicist who contributed to the discoveries of the element protactinium and nuclear fission.<br>FIGURE 20.15<br>92<br>124 Kr<br>36",
