        "sciNote": "Number of nucleons on LHS - Number of nucleons on RHS\nFor example in the reaction\nHe+NO+ H+Q.\nWe can see that on both sides there are (14+4=17+1=18) nucleons.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nCONSERVATION OF MASS ENERGY\nNOT FOR SALE\n358\nSince the mass number differ from atomic masses and difference provides binding energy to nucleons in the nucleus therefore energy conservation should also hold apart from conservation of nucleon number. This also include energy difference due to change in mass from Einstein mass energy relation.\nFor example in the reaction\nHe+NO+H+Q\nnuclear reaction energy Q can be calculated as\nLHS\nHe = 4.00263 u\nN=14.00307 u\n18.005677 u\nRHS H = 1.007825 u\n0 = 16.999133u\n18.006958 u\nSince Q is the rest mass difference, therefore Q = 18.005677u-18.006958 u\nQ=-0.001281 u =-1.192 MeV\nAs Q is negative therefore a-particle having kinetic energy 1.192 MeV can induce such a reaction.\nEXAMPLE 20.3\nNUCLEAR REACTION\nWhat is the least energy does the proton have to make the following nuclear reaction possible?\nH + C→ 1N+ on\n€ 13.00 az\nThe mass of hydrogen 1H is 1.007825 u, carbon 13C is 13.003355 u, neutron 'n is 1.008665 u and nitrogen 13N is 13.005739 u.\nGIVEN\nREQUIRED\nMass of hydrogen \"H\"\nMass of carbon 13C\n1.007825 u\n13.003355 u\nProton energy Q = ?\nMass of neutron \"in\"= 1.008665 u\nMass of nitrogen 13N 13.005739 u\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nSOLUTION\nnuclear reaction energy Qcan be calculated as\nLHS\nH = 1.007825 u\n12C =13.003355u\n14.011180 u\nNOT FOR SALE\n"
