        "id": "20.17",
        "title": "Textbook lesson extract 16",
        "theory": "1043\n?\ngravitons\n20.19 BUILDING BLOCKS OF MATTER\nBeginning in about 1945, many new particles were discovered in experiments involving high-energy collisions between known particles. Such particles are highly unstable and have very short half-lives, ranging between 10 and 10&quot; s. So far, more than 400 of these unstable, temporary particles have been catalogued. It is useful to group the known particles into three families-the bosons, the leptons, and the hadrons.\nOSONS\nThe boson family is composed of particles that play central roles in nature&#x27;s fundamental forces. The photon is associated with the electromagnetic force. The W, W&#x27;, and Z&#x27; particles are associated with the weak nuclear force. The gluons are associated with the strong nuclear force, whereas the graviton is thought to be\nassociated with the gravitational force.\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nFIGURE 20.26\nthree generations of matter (fermions)\nNOT FOR SALE\n375\nInteractions / force carriers\n(bosons)\nmass-2.2 MeVie\nm173.1 GaVid\n124 B7 Gevic\ncharge\nu\n%C\n9\nH\nG\nQUARKS\nup\n-47 Movie\nd\ndown\n-\ncharm\ntop\ngluon\nhiggs\ngraviton\n96 MeVic\n»-4.18 GaVic2\nS\nb\nY\nstrange\nbottom\nphoton\ne\nelectron\n– 105.66. MaV\nmuon\ntau\n~1.7768 Gavle!\nτ\nZ\nSCALAR BOSONS\nHYPOTHETICAL\nTENSOR BOSONS\nLEPTONS\nVe\nelectron neutrino\n-0.17 Mavic\nΤμ\nmuon neutrino\nZ boson\n-80:39 GaVI?!\nW\nVT\ntau neutrino\nW boson\nGAUGE BOSONS VECTOR BOSONS\nB. LEPTONS\nStandard Model of Elementary Particles\nThe lepton family consists of particles that interact by means of the weak nuclear force. Leptons can also exert gravitational and (if the leptons are charged) electromagnetic forces on other particles. The four better-known leptons are the electron, the muon, the electron neutrino v,, and the muon neutrino v,. Two other leptons have also been discovered, the tau particle t and its neutrino v,, bringing the number of particles in the lepton family to six. No internal structure for leptons is observed so far, therefore they are considered to be elementary.\nC. HADRONS\nThe hadron family contains the particles that interact by means of both the strong nuclear force and the weak nuclear force. Hadrons can also interact by gravitational and electromagnetic forces, but at short distances the strong pions. The hadrons are subdivided into two groups, the mesons and the baryons. nuclear force dominates. Among the hadrons are the proton, the neutron, and the\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n376\nAs more and more hadrons were discovered, it became clear that they were not all up of elementary particles. The suggestion was made that the hadrons are made smaller, more elementary particles called quarks. In 1963, a quark theory was initiated independently by M. Gell-Mann (1929-) and G. Zweig (1937-).\nQuark Model is based on the following assumptions:\n1. There are six types of quark up (u), down (d), strange (s), charm (c), top (t) and bottom (b).",
