        "theory": "2. Every quark has corresponding antiquark denoted by the same letter with a bar\nover it.\nTable 20.6 QUARKS\nName\nMass (GeV/c&#x27;)\nSymbol\nCharge\nup\n0.004\nU\n+ 2/3\ndown\n0.008\nd\n- 1/3\nstrange\n0.15\nS\n-1/3\ncharm\n1.5\nn\n+2/3\nbottom\n4.7\nb\n- 1/3\ntop\n176\nt\n2/3\nFIGURE 20.27\nd\n1/3 e\n1/3 e\nd\nப\n+ 2/3 e\nū -1/3-2/3 e\nd\nU\n1/3 e +2/3 e\nU\n1/3 e\n+2/3 e\n2/3 e\nProton\nBaryons\nNeutron\nAccording to the original quark model of hadrons, all mesons consist of a quark and an\nantiquark, whereas baryons contain three quarks.\nMesons\nPHYSICS-XII\nUNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\n377\n3. Quarks combine in three to form baryons (proton, neutron, sigma e.t.c.) and antiquarks combine in three to form anti-baryons (anti-proton, anti-neutron, anti-\nsigma e.t.c.) as shown in figure 20.27.\n4. Quarks combine with antiquarks in twos to form mesons.\nD. COLOR CHARGE\nElectric charge comes in only one kind (positive) and its opposite (negative), but strong charge comes in three kinds (called red, blue, and green), each of which has an opposite (called antired, antiblue, and antigreen). Color charge has nothing to do with the colors of light that we perceive visually. They are based on an analogy: just as the red, blue, and green dots produced by different phosphors on a TV screen combine to make white, a red quark, blue quark, and green quark combine to form a colorless (white) combination. All free particles have a color charge of zero. A baryon always contains one quark of each color, an antibaryon always contains one antiquark of each anticolor, and a meson always contains a quark of one color and an antiquark of the corresponding anticolor, such as a red quark and an antired antiquark. In each case, the strong force holds quarks together in colorless combinations, similar to the way the electromagnetic force holds negative and positive charges together to form a neutral atom with zero net electric charge.\nRed + green + blue = white\nr+g+b=0\nAnti-Red (cyan) + Anti-green (magenta) + Anti-blue (yellow) = white\n7+8+5=0\nred + anti-red = white, green + anti-green = white, blue + anti-blue = white\nr+F=g+g=b+b=0\nPHYSICS-XII\nIT 20 HUCLEAR PHYSICS\nNOT FOR SALE\nNucleons: The protons and neutrons present in the nucleus\n378\nIsotopes: Nuclei of an element that have the same charge number Z, but\nhave different mass number A.\nAtomic mass unit (u): the mass of 12&quot; part of the mass of carbon-12 atom.\nBinding energy: the energy needed to separate nucleus into individual protons and neutrons. Binding energy per nucleon gives the stability of the nucleus, larger the value more stable the nucleus.\nRadioactivity: the emission of rays from the nuclei of atoms\nActivity: the number of disintegrations per unit time\nRandom process: A process without defined pattern, rule or method Spontaneous process: A process occurring without apparent external\ncause",
