      "CARBON",
      "The radioisotope of carbon 14C has a half-life of 5730 yr. If a sample contains 1.00 x 1022 carbon-14 nuclei, what is the activity of the sample?",
      "(Answer: 3.83 x 1010 Bq)",
      "UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "354",
      "20.9 INTERACTION OF RADIATION WITH MATTER Interaction of ionizing radiation with matter is divided into three groups.",
      "A. INTERACTION OF CHARGED PARTICLES",
      "Charged particles (electrons (e-), positrons (e+), protons (P), deuterons (d), alphas (a), heavy ions (A> 4)) traveling through matter lose energy in the following ways:",
      "1. In Coulomb interactions with electrons and nuclei",
      "2. By emission of electromagnetic radiation (bremsstrahlung)",
      "3. In nuclear interactions",
      "B. INTERACTION OF PHOTONS",
      "There is a long list of possible interactions of photons (gammas (y) or X-rays), but only the three most important ones are:",
      "1. The photoelectric effect",
      "2. Compton scattering, and",
      "3. Pair production.",
      "INTERACTION OF NEUTRONS",
      "n",
      "Since a neutron (n) has no charge, it interacts with nuclei only through nuclear forces. When it approaches a nucleus, it does not have to go through a Coulomb barrier. As a result, the probability (cross section) for nuclear interactions is higher for neutrons than for charged particles. The interactions of neutrons with nuclei are divided into two categories:",
      "1. scattering and",
      "2. absorption",
      "PHYSICS-XI!",
      "UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS",
      "20.10 RADIATION DETECTORS",
      "NOT FOR SALE 355",
      "Nuclear radiations are measured with the effect they produce when interacting with matter. The function of the detector is to produce a signal for every particle or photon entering into it. Every detector works by using some interaction of particles with matter. There are many types of radiation detectors, however we",
      "would study only two in detail.",
      "1. Geiger-Muller counters",
      "2. Semiconductor detectors",
      "A. GEIGER-MULLER COUNTERS",
      "It is the most widely used device to detect radiation. It consist of cylindrical metal tube filled with gas at low temperature and long wire along the axis of the tube. The wire is maintained at high positive potential with respect to tube. The metal tube is attached with detection system such as counter and audio device as shown in the figure.",
      "FIGURE 20.13",
      "Gas molecules are ionized by radiation",
      "Metal tube negatively charged",
      "Counter and",
      "Electrode positively charged",
      "audio device",
      "Non-ionized gas molecules",
      "lonizing",
      "Window",
      "radiation",
      "When high energy particle or photon enters the tube trough the thin window at one end some of the atoms of the gas ionizes as shown in figure 20.13. Since the wire is at positive potential the electrons removed from atoms are attracted towards the wire.",
      "PHYSICS-XII",
      "UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "356",
      "In the process they ionize other atoms in their path. This results in an avalanche of electrons that produces a pulse at the detection system. Which is amplified and counted by counter and sound is produced.",
      "B. SOLID STATE DETECTORS",
      "A solid state detector is a device that uses a semiconductor (usually silicon or germanium) to detect charged particles or photons. A solid state detector is usually a reverse bias PN junction diode, connected to the pulse detection system.",
      "AP-N junction of semiconductor is placed under reverse bias, thus no current flows. When ionizing radiation is allowed to pass through the depleted region it excites electrons into the conduction band. Thus electrons are moved towards positive terminal and holes towards negative terminal of battery, causing a temporary conduction as shown in figure 20.14. This electric pulse is then measured through an electric counter. The electronics used to analyze the pulses does more than counting.",
      "FIGURE 20.14",
      "OL",
      "Depletion region",
      "N",
      "Reverse Bias PN Junction Diode",
      "There",
      "It separates the pulses into hundreds of groups called channels. Therefore, the equipment is often called multi-channel analyzer.",
      "20.11 NUCLEAR REACTIONS",
      "awa",
      "A nuclear reaction is said to occur whenever an incident nucleus, particle, or photon causes a change to occur in a target nucleus.",
      "Such a structural change can be brought about in the nucleus by bombarding the target by sufficiently energetic particle such as neutrons, alpha particles etc. As a results the nucleons are added, removed or rearranged within a target nucleus. A process in which nuclear transmutation is done by bombarding target nuclei with energetic light particles is known as induced nuclear reaction. In such nuclear",
      "processes the conservation laws must be followed.",
      "PHYSICS-XII",
      "UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "357",
      "When Nucleus X is bombarded with some light particle a the product nucleus Y and light particle b will be obtained by nuclear reaction. Mathematically",
      "a+X→Y+b",
      "In 1919, Ernest Rutherford observed that when an a particle strikes a nitrogen nucleus, an oxygen nucleus and a proton are produced. This nuclear reaction is",
      "written as",
      "He+NO+H+Q",
      "The energy equivalent of the difference between the rest masses of the elements on both sides of nuclear reaction is called nuclear reaction energy 'Q'. If 'Q' is positive energy is released in the reaction, if 'Q' is negative energy is absorbed in the reaction. Few other examples of nuclear reaction are",
      "H + C → N+Y and",
      "CONSERVATION LAWS IN NUCLEAR REACTION",
      "on + B→ Li+He",
      "Aconservation of physical quantity means that for all the process taking place in a system that particular physical quantity remains constant. The following laws of conservation are obeyed for nuclear processes.",
      "ila",
      "• Conservation of charge",
      "• Conservation of Energy (mass inclusive)",
      "• Conservation of linear and angular momentum",
      "• Conservation of nucleon number",
      "The two conservation laws will be discussed.",
      "A. CONSERVATION OF NUCLEON NUMBER",
      "Before and after the reaction the sum of number of protons and neutrons should",
      "remain constant.",
      "Number of nucleons on LHS - Number of nucleons on RHS",
      "For example in the reaction",
      "He+NO+ H+Q.",
      "We can see that on both sides there are (14+4=17+1=18) nucleons.",
      "PHYSICS-XII",
      "UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS",
      "CONSERVATION OF MASS ENERGY",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "358",
      "Since the mass number differ from atomic masses and difference provides binding energy to nucleons in the nucleus therefore energy conservation should also hold apart from conservation of nucleon number. This also include energy difference due to change in mass from Einstein mass energy relation.",
      "For example in the reaction",
      "He+NO+H+Q",
