      "U",
      "The minimum amount of mass required to sustain a fission chain reaction is called critical mass. If the amount of uranium is too small the chain reaction will stop. If a chain reaction is to continue it is necessary that the mass of uranium must be greater or equal to this critical mass.",
      "awauum",
      "PHYSICS-XII",
      "UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "363",
      "The mass less than critical mass FIGURE 20.17 is called sub-critical and mass greater then critical mass is called super-critical as shown in",
      "figure 20.17.",
      "However Natural uranium consists of only about 0.7% of U and 99.3% 23U, which means that most of the available uranium nuclei generally do not participate in the fission process but instead remove. neutrons from being able to produce other fissions. To overcome this problem it is necessary to use enriched uranium, in which the abundance of \"U is increased beyond its natural value of 0.7%.",
      "Fission may take place in any of the heavy nuclei after capture of a neutron. However, low-energy neutrons are able to cause fission only in U-233, U-235, and Pu-239.",
      "(a)",
      "(b)",
      "(a) In a sub-critical mass, too many neutrons escape before colliding (b) in the critical mass, enough neutrons encounter other nuclei to maintain a chain reaction.",
      "Fuel pellets contain enriched uranium-235. Each pellet contains the energy equivalent of I",
      "ton of coal.",
      "PHYSICS-XII",
      "UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS",
      "20.13 NUCLEAR REACTOR",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "364",
      "A nuclear reactor is a device that is designed to maintain a self sustaining fission reaction in a controlled manner.",
      "Since large amount of energy is released in a fission process, therefore it is carried under controlled conditions. This energy can be used for useful purposes such as to produce power and radioisotopes. The Nuclear reactor has five main parts as shown in figure 20.18.",
      "CORE",
      "The core contain nuclear fuel in which fission is achieved by thermal neutrons, for example, the nuclear fuel may be thin rods about 1 cm in diameter. Uranium \"\"U is a common reactor fuel. Most commercial reactors use uranium in which the amount of \"U has been enriched to about 3%.",
      "FIGURE 20.18",
      "Control",
      "rods",
      "Radiation",
      "shielding",
      "Pressure",
      "Vessel",
      "Graphite",
      "Moderator",
      "Fuel rods",
