      "Artificial Sources",
      "All biological damage effects begin with the consequence of radiation interactions with the atoms forming the cells. The cell damage may lead to two effects.",
      "1. Somatic Effect: The effects which appears soon after irradiation. Such",
      "as skin burns, loss of hair and induction of cancer.",
      "ii. Genetic effects: These effects may not appear in the individual life but",
      "damage genes and chromosomes that affect future generations.",
      "PHYSICS-XII",
      "1",
      "UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS-",
      "NOT FOR SALE 371",
      "20.17 BIOLOGICAL AND MEDICAL USES OF RADIATION Although scientists have only known about radiation since the 1890s, they have developed a wide variety of uses for this natural force. Today, radiation is used in medicine, academics, and industry.",
      "A. BIOLOGICAL USES",
      "Nuclear techniques are used in farming and agricultural communities to combat",
      "disease and provide other benefits.",
      "i. Food Preservation: Gamma rays can be used to kill bacteria, mould and insects",
      "in food.",
      "ii. Sterilizing: Gamma rays are also used to sterilize hospital equipment, especially plastic syringes that would be damaged if heated.",
      "FIGURE 20.23",
      "INRADIATED TO PROTECT THE ENVIRONMENT",
      "Preserving food and sterilization of medical equipment",
      "iii. Insect Control: Controlling insects with chemicals can create environmental problems and result in toxic residues in food. The major way of controlling insects without the use of chemicals is the Sterile Insect Technique.",
      "iv. Chemical Study: The chemical changes in living organism can be studied through tracer method. For example the process of photosynthesis and incorporation of carbon atoms in CO, into giant protein or Carbohydrate molecules have been investigated by tracer technique.",
      "PHYSICS-XII",
      "UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS",
      "B. MEDICAL USES",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "372",
      "One of the most important applications of nuclear physics has been in medicine,",
      "for both diagnostic and therapeutic purposes.",
      "1. Medical diagnostics: Radioactive isotopes are used in medical diagnosis as tracers. Radioactive isotopes are introduced in the body in chemical forms that have an affinity for certain organs, such as bone or the thyroid gland. For medical diagnosis, the radionuclide commonly used today is \"Tc long-lived excited state of technetium-99 (the \"m\" in the symbol stands for 'metastable' state). A sensitive detector (called a FIGURE 20.24",
      "\"gamma-ray camera\") can observe the radiations from the isotopes that are concentrated in the organ and can produce an image that shows how the activity is distributed in the patient. These detectors are capable of determining where each gamma-",
      "ray photon originates in the patient",
      "as shown in figure 20.24.",
      "Gamma Camera",
      "For your information",
      "POSITRON EMISSION TOMOGRAPHY (PET)",
      "Another technique that reveals information inside the body is positron emission tomography (PET), in which the patient is injected with a positron-emitting isotope that is readily absorbed by the body. Examples of isotopes used are 150 (t, 2 min), 13N (t12 = 10 min), 11C (t = 20 min), and 18F (t = 110 min). These isotopes are produced with a cyclotron. By surrounding the patient with a ring of detectors, it is possible to determine exactly where the decay occurred, and from a large number of such events, an image is produced that reconstructs the distribution of the radioisotope in the patient.",
      "ii. Radiation therapy: Radiation therapy is the use of high-energy radiation to",
      "damage cancer cells' DNA and destroy their ability to divide and grow.",
      "PHYSICS-XII",
      "UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS",
      "NOT FORSALE",
      "373",
      "The radioactive source such as FIGURE 20.25 cobalt 60 which emit beta particles and high energy gamma rays can also be used to treat various cancers as shown in figure 20.25.",
      "Some radioisotopes are made to absorb by selected organ and radiation is concentrated on the infected tissue. For example cancerous thyroid can be treated with iodine-131.",
      "20.18 BASIC FORCES OF NATURE (Fundamental Interactions) All particles in nature are subjected to four fundamental forces, strong force, weak force, electromagnetic force and gravitational force.",
      "A. STRONG FORCE",
      "ila",
      "The strong force is very short range about 10 m and is responsible for binding of nucleons in the nucleus. It can be considered as an exchange force in which the exchange particles are gluon. It is the strongest of all the forces and have been assigned as relative strength 1.",
      "B. ELECTROMAGNETIC FORCE",
      "Fundamentally, both magnetic and electric forces are manifestations of an exchange force involving the exchange of photons. The electromagnetic force holds atoms and molecules together. It has infinite range and relative strength is",
      "10%.",
      "C. WEAK FORCE",
      "Weak interaction involves the exchange of the intermediate vector bosons, the W and the Z. The weak interaction changes one flavor of quark into another. It is very short range about 0.1% of diameter of proton i.e 10m. It have relative strength of",
      "about 10*.",
      "PHYSICS-XII",
      "UNIT 20 NUCLEAR PHYSICS",
      "D. GRAVITATIONAL FORCE",
      "NOT FOR SALE",
      "374",
      "Gravity is long range force and is the weakest of the four fundamental forces having relative strength of 10\", yet it is the dominant force in the universe for shaping the large scale structure of galaxies, stars, etc. Viewed as an exchange force, the massless exchange particle is called the graviton (however it is not confirmed yet).",
