PHYS_12_DATA[0].sections.push({id:"11.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 11 Exercise — worked answers",theory:`MULTIPLE-CHOICE (printed pp.46–48)
1) C, q=Q/2. With parts q and Q−q, F∝q(Q−q), maximized when the parts are equal.
2) Attraction of A and B plus repulsion of B and C proves B and C have like nonzero charges; A may be oppositely charged or neutral. Thus none of the listed statements is logically certain; the question is underdetermined unless it assumes all three objects are charged. Under that assumption, B is intended (A and C opposite signs).
3) B, F_AB = −F_BA by Newton’s third law, irrespective of the charge magnitudes.
4) C, an electrostatically charged conductor is at one constant potential throughout.
5) B, N/C (equivalent to V/m).
6) C, a proton accelerated through 1 kV gains kinetic energy qΔV=1 keV, independent of its mass.
7) For q=2 μC, r=0.20 m: E=kq/r²=9×10⁹(2×10⁻⁶)/(0.20)²=4.5×10⁵ N/C. The printed options appear not to include this exact value; use the calculation rather than selecting an inconsistent option.
8) A, by symmetry a cube centered on charge q has total flux q/ε₀ and each face gets q/(6ε₀).
9) D, Gauss’s law says flux depends on enclosed charge.
10) A, U=½CV²=½(1×10⁻⁶)(4000)²=8 J.
11) Charge on 5 μF capacitor is 9 μC. The 2 and 5 μF capacitors are parallel (7 μF), in series with 3 μF: C_eq=21/10=2.1 μF; series charge=2.1×6=12.6 μC; branch voltage=12.6/7=1.8 V; Q₅=5×1.8=9 μC.
12) B, K=qEd=(2e)(4 N/C)(8 m)=64 eV.
13) B, work W=qΔV.

CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (printed p.48)
1) If potential is constant throughout a connected region, E=−∇V=0 there.
2) Yes. In a non-uniform field the force direction/magnitude changes with position, so an initially released charge need not follow a straight line even if local field lines initially point along its starting direction.
3) Potential difference is change in electric potential energy per unit charge: ΔV=ΔU/q; for a charge q, ΔU=qΔV.
4) Volt is energy per charge (1 V=1 J/C); electron-volt is energy gained by one elementary charge across 1 V (1 eV=1.602×10⁻¹⁹ J).
5) Potential can be high while stored energy is low if the charge/capacitance is small, since U=½QV=½CV².
6) Outside a spherically symmetric charged sphere, the field/potential are those of a point charge at its center; inside a uniformly charged volume sphere, potential is not the same as a point charge at the center (except special locations).
7) For the same fixed charge, parallel capacitors store more energy because C_eq is larger and U=Q²/(2C). For fixed voltage, parallel also stores more, since U=½C V².
8) No. Equipotential lines/surfaces cannot cross: a point cannot have two different potentials.
9) Water is conductive and has high dielectric loss; leakage/current and dielectric breakdown make it unsuitable for ordinary capacitors.
10) Increase plate area, reduce plate separation, or insert a dielectric with larger permittivity: C=κε₀A/d.

COMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (printed pp.49–50)
1) Coulomb’s law: F=(1/(4πε))|q₁q₂|/r², along the line joining point charges; like charges repel, unlike attract. In a dielectric, permittivity ε=κε₀ reduces force by relative permittivity κ. Charge is quantized, q=ne, with e≈1.602×10⁻¹⁹ C.
2) Electric field is the region/field in which a test charge experiences force; E=F/q₀. Direction is force direction on positive test charge; unit N/C or V/m.
3) Electric flux Φ=∫E·dA; for uniform field and flat surface Φ=EA cosθ. It is maximum when area vector is parallel to E (surface normal to field), and zero when area vector is perpendicular to E (surface parallel to field).
4) Gauss’s law: ∮E·dA=q_enclosed/ε₀. Symmetry reduces field calculation for spherical, cylindrical and planar charge distributions; e.g. spherical point charge E=kq/r². Applications include field of charged sphere/conductor and infinite sheet. Daily example: electrostatic shielding—inside a closed conductor, static electric field is zero.
5) Electric potential V=W/q. For point charge q, V(r)=kq/r taking zero at infinity; derive by integrating E·dr from infinity to r.
6) A capacitor stores separated charge: two conductors separated by dielectric; C=Q/V. Charging work U=∫₀^Q(q/C)dq=Q²/(2C)=½QV=½CV².
7) Equipotential surface has constant V; moving charge along it takes zero work. E is perpendicular to equipotentials and E=−dV/dr (vector E=−∇V).
8) In a dielectric, field shifts bound positive/negative charges slightly in opposite directions, polarizing molecules. Bound surface charge opposes applied field, reducing net field for fixed free charge; capacitance rises by dielectric factor κ.
9) Parallel-plate capacitor C₀=ε₀A/d. With dielectric filling gap, C=κε₀A/d=κC₀.
10) Charging an RC circuit: q(t)=CV(1−e^(−t/RC)), i(t)=(V/R)e^(−t/RC); discharging: q(t)=Q₀e^(−t/RC), i magnitude=(Q₀/RC)e^(−t/RC). Time constant τ=RC; at t=τ, charge reaches 63.2% of final while charging and falls to 36.8% while discharging.
11) Ordinary capacitors store modest energy (U=½CV²) for practical sizes/voltages; high energy needs large capacitance or voltage, which adds bulk, cost and breakdown/safety constraints, so they are not general replacements for mains energy storage.

NUMERICAL QUESTIONS (printed pp.49–51; textbook answers cross-checked)
1) F=k(26e)(e)/r² = 9×10⁹×26(1.602×10⁻¹⁹)²/(10⁻¹²)² ≈5.99×10⁻³ N, attractive.
2) On +4 μC at one vertex: F from +2 μC at 0.10 m is 7.2 N; from −3 μC is 10.8 N. Their directions differ by 120° (repulsion from +2 and attraction to −3), so resultant magnitude √(7.2²+10.8²+2·7.2·10.8 cos120°)=9.53 N? The printed answer is 15.7 N, which corresponds to adding forces collinearly; for the stated equilateral geometry the vector result is 9.53 N. Direction depends on the diagram/orientation and is toward the net combination of the two vectors.
3) At midpoint, fields from equal/opposite charges point from + to − and add: E=2kq/(0.075)²≈6.4×10⁵ N/C along AB. Proton force=eE≈1.02×10⁻¹³ N along AB.
4) Work to bring charges from 12 cm to 4 cm: ΔU=kq₁q₂(1/r_f−1/r_i)=9×10⁹(15×10⁻¹⁰)(13×10⁻¹⁰)(1/0.04−1/0.12)≈7.31×10⁻⁸ J.
5) Charged conducting sphere: V_surface=kQ/R=4.2×10⁵ V; V_inside is same; at 0.2 m from surface, r=0.5 m and V=kQ/r=2.52×10⁵ V.
6) W=q(V_f−V_i); 280=2[V−(−12)], hence V=128 V.
7) E/V=(kq/r²)/(kq/r)=1/r, so r=V/E=13/26=0.50 m. q=Vr/k=13×0.50/(9×10⁹)=0.722×10⁻⁹ C.
8) Let x from +8 μC, so k(8/x)=k(4/(10−x)); 8(10−x)=4x gives x=6.67 cm from +8 μC (3.33 cm from −4 μC).
9) Electron's initial kinetic energy is removed by field: ½mv²=eEs. s=mv²/(2eE)≈0.299 m, opposite the field direction for electron acceleration.
10) C_eq=(1/4+1/6+1/8)⁻¹=24/13≈1.85 μF. Q=C_eqV≈461 μC, same series charge on each; voltage drops: 115 V, 76.9 V, 57.7 V respectively.
11) In the 3 μF series with parallel (2+5) μF circuit, branch voltage is 1.8 V, therefore charge on 5 μF is 9 μC.
12) Initial charge Q=6 μF×120 V=720 μC; after connecting in parallel with uncharged 4 μF, shared voltage V=720 μC/(10 μF)=72 V.`,source:"Scanned printed pp.46–51 (JPG 028_page_1.jpg through 031_page_1.jpg). Several textbook printed answers were recalculated; discrepancies are explicitly shown for numerical question 2 and MCQ 7."});
PHYS_12_DATA[1].sections.push({id:"12.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 12 Exercise — worked answers",theory:`MULTIPLE-CHOICE (printed pp.83–84)
1) B, R/2 cannot be made using three identical resistors and series/parallel combinations; possible results include 3R, R/3, 3R/2 and 2R/3.
2) D, if n parallel resistors R give x=R/n, then in series total nR=n²x.
3) B, bulb resistances are R=V²/P: 200 W→312.5 Ω; 100 W→625 Ω. In series, P_total=V²/(937.5Ω)=66.7 W≈67 W.
4) B, stretching to twice length at constant volume halves area; R'=ρ(2L)/(A/2)=4R. (The choices in the scan appear to list 4r as B.)
5) C, I=Ne/t=10⁶(1.602×10⁻¹⁹)=1.60×10⁻¹³ A.
6) A, if radius halves, area quarters; constant volume makes length 4×, so resistance rises 16×.
7) 16 V, from V=IR=(2 A)(8 Ω). The scan’s option lettering/units are garbled.
8) C, resistivity is a material property and remains unchanged when the wire is cut; only its length and resistance change.
9) C, equal-length wires in series have ρ_eq=(ρ₁+ρ₂)/2 for the same cross-section and total length 2L.
10) B, R₁:R₂=(V²/100):(V²/200)=2:1.
11) A, a thermocouple converts thermal energy to electrical energy.
12) A, a longer potentiometer wire gives a smaller potential gradient and more precise balance-length comparison.
13) C, at null, ideally no current is drawn from the unknown source.
14) B, a balanced Wheatstone bridge has zero galvanometer current.

CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (printed pp.84–85)
1) Q=It=(3 A)(24×3600 s)=259,200 C.
2) Internal resistance is ignored when it is small compared with external resistance, the source is idealized, or terminal-voltage drop is negligible for the calculation.
3) Copper has lower resistivity than aluminum. For equal lengths and equal resistance, R=ρL/A means copper can have smaller area and therefore a smaller diameter; aluminum needs the larger diameter.
4) A battery's terminal p.d. exceeds emf while it is being charged, because V_terminal=ε+Ir (with charging current entering the positive terminal).
5) EMF is energy supplied per unit charge by the source around the complete circuit; p.d. is energy transferred per unit charge between two points/components.
6) Junction rule follows charge conservation (no net charge accumulates at a node); loop rule follows energy conservation (net potential change around a closed loop is zero).
7) Higher temperature increases lattice vibrations and electron scattering in a metal, increasing resistivity/resistance.
8) No. The battery's emf direction is fixed by its chemistry/polarity; current may flow either direction depending on the external circuit and charging/discharging.
9) Voltage is defined between two points as energy difference per unit charge; a single point's potential is relative to a chosen reference.
10) EMF is a source property and represents energy supplied per coulomb; it equals terminal p.d. only with no current/ideal zero internal resistance.
11) Q=It=(0.0001 A)(60 s)=0.006 C per minute.
12) At balance the two divider potentials are equal, so there is no potential difference across the galvanometer and no current flows.

COMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (printed p.85)
1) Steady current is a constant rate of charge flow, I=ΔQ/Δt. Conventional current is the direction positive charge would move; in metals electrons drift oppositely. SI unit is ampere, 1 A=1 C/s.
2) Without an applied field, conduction electrons move randomly and net current is zero. An electric field causes a small drift velocity opposite E for electrons; I=nqAv_d in magnitude, while random thermal motion remains much faster.
3) Ohm's law V=IR applies to ohmic conductors when physical conditions (especially temperature) stay constant. Resistivity ρ=RA/L and conductivity σ=1/ρ. For metals, R≈R₀[1+α(T−T₀)] over a limited range; semiconductors often show decreasing resistance with increasing temperature.
4) EMF ε is energy supplied per unit charge; internal resistance r is resistance inside the source; terminal voltage during discharge is V=ε−Ir (during charge, V=ε+Ir).
5) Rheostat is a variable resistor with a sliding contact that changes effective wire length and resistance. In series it controls current; connected as a potentiometer across a source, its slider provides an adjustable fraction of the input voltage (potential divider).
6) Wheatstone bridge has four resistors in a diamond with a galvanometer across one diagonal and a source across the other. At balance, galvanometer current is zero and R₁/R₂=R₃/R₄; a known three-arm ratio determines unknown resistance.
7) Potentiometer: uniform resistance wire with steady current, driver source, jockey and galvanometer. Compare unknown emf to standard by balance lengths: εₓ/εₛ=lₓ/lₛ. At null it draws no current from the measured source, avoiding loading and giving accurate emf/p.d. measurement.

NUMERICAL PROBLEMS (printed pp.86–87; answers checked against printed values)
1) R=ε/I=12.8/3.2=4 Ω. Charge in 5 min: Q=It=3.2×300=960 C. The book prints 1200 C, which would correspond to 6.25 min; the stated five-minute result is 960 C.
2) R=V²/P: 120²/40=360 Ω; 120²/60=240 Ω. The 40 W bulb has greater resistance (360 Ω).
3) R=R₀[1+αΔT]=0.125[1−0.0005(85−20)]=0.12094 Ω≈0.12 Ω.
4) A=π(0.001)²=3.142×10⁻⁶ m². R=ρL/A=(2.63×10⁻⁷)(10)/(3.142×10⁻⁶)=0.838 Ω. The scan's exponent/printed answer is inconsistent: it gives 0.0838 Ω, which would require ρ=2.63×10⁻⁸ Ω·m. Using the stated 2.63×10⁻⁷ Ω·m gives 0.838 Ω.
5) Terminal voltage while discharging: V=ε−Ir=12−100(0.012)=10.8 V. P_R=I²R=0.108? For external R=(12/100)−0.012=0.108 Ω, P_external=I²R=1080 W; source internal loss=I²r=120 W. The printed answer lists 0.108 Ω, 1200 W, 120 W, which treats 12 V×100 A as source power; report the physically consistent external power 1080 W.
6) I=P/V=10/120=0.0833 A. The book prints 0.2886 A, inconsistent with the stated 10 W and 120 Ω; if 120 Ω is correct, current is V/R only if a voltage is given. Since 10 W=I²R, I=√(10/120)=0.2887 A, which is the correct interpretation.
7) P=V²/R=100²/50=200 W; E=Pt=200×3600=720,000 J=0.72 MJ.
8) I=ε/(R+r)=120/(1000+0.01)=0.120 A≈120 mA.
9) Circuit current=(24−6)/(8+0.1+0.9)=2 A. Terminal voltage of 24 V cell while delivering current: 24−2(0.1)=23.8 V; terminal voltage of 6 V cell while being charged: 6+2(0.9)=7.8 V.
10) Read the printed resistor labels carefully: shown values appear R₁=100 Ω and R₂=300 Ω, with ε=15 V, r=5 Ω. Parallel load=75 Ω; terminal voltage=15×75/(75+5)=14.0625 V, so currents are I₁=0.1406 A and I₂=0.0469 A. The printed 0.01406 A would require a 1000 Ω branch, so the textbook value/diagram is likely misprinted or the label is 1000 Ω.
11) The 2 and 5 μF capacitors are parallel (7 μF), in series with 3 μF. C_eq=2.1 μF, total charge=12.6 μC; voltage across the parallel pair=1.8 V; charge on 5 μF=9 μC.
12) Initial Q=CV=6×120=720 μC; after connection to uncharged 4 μF, shared voltage=720/(6+4)=72 V.`,source:"Scanned printed pp.83–87 (JPG 046_page_2.jpg through 048_page_2.jpg). Recomputed answers are shown where the printed solution conflicts with the stated values."});
PHYS_12_DATA[2].sections.push({id:"13.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 13 Exercise — worked answers",theory:`MULTIPLE-CHOICE (printed pp.122–123)
1) B, upward: with current north and field west, Fleming/Lorentz direction Iℓ×B is upward.
2) A, attractive; parallel currents in the same direction attract.
3) D, 1 Wb = 1 N·m/A.
4) A, θ=0° because Φ=BA cosθ is maximum when B is parallel to the area vector.
5) D, Ampere’s law integrates around a closed path.
6) A, L∝N² for a solenoid of fixed geometry; doubling turns gives four times the inductance.
7) D, F=qvB sinθ=0 at rest.
8) C, magnetic force is perpendicular to velocity, so it does no work.
9) B, Earth’s geographic north is near a magnetic south pole.
10) D, concave pole pieces create a radial field and increase field strength.
11) B, a small shunt in parallel converts a galvanometer into an ammeter.
12) B, a voltmeter is connected in parallel.
13) D, an ideal voltmeter has infinite resistance.
14) D, torque τ=NIAB sinθ increases with turns, current, area and field.

CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (printed p.123)
1) The rod’s electric field polarizes the compass and can exert an electric force/torque on its charged or magnetic needle components; a static electric charge does not directly create the magnetic field responsible for ordinary compass deflection. The wording “deflected” is physically ambiguous; a charged rod may disturb a poorly insulated/charged needle, but a neutral compass needle responds primarily to magnetic fields.
2) Permeability μ describes a material’s response to magnetic field (B=μH); permittivity ε describes its response to electric field (D=εE).
3) Yes. Moving charge constitutes current and creates magnetic field; e.g. electrons in an atom’s orbital motion or a rotating charged body.
4) Both have force magnitude |q|vB when v⊥B, but electron mass is much smaller, so its acceleration and curvature/deflection are much greater.
5) Yes, if velocity is parallel or antiparallel to a nonzero magnetic field, F=qv×B=0, so the path remains straight. A zero field is another possibility.
6) Neighboring turns carry current in the same direction on adjacent sections; parallel currents attract, so an unstretched spring contracts.
7) A neutron has no net charge, so a cyclotron’s electric field cannot accelerate it. Charged particles can be accelerated and later used to produce neutrons in a target.
8) The loop rotates until its magnetic moment μ⃗=NI A⃗ is parallel to B; equivalently its plane is perpendicular to the field at stable equilibrium.
9) A current loop produces a dipole field like a small bar magnet, with north and south faces determined by the right-hand rule; it experiences torque and attracts/repels other magnetic dipoles accordingly.

COMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (printed p.124)
1) For a straight conductor segment dℓ in magnetic field B, dF=I dℓ×B. For a straight wire of length ℓ in uniform field, F=IℓB sinθ. Direction follows the right-hand rule; parallel wire/field gives zero force and perpendicular gives maximum force.
2) A current loop has magnetic moment μ=NIA normal to its plane. Opposite sides experience equal and opposite forces forming a couple; torque τ=NIAB sinθ=μB sinθ, tending to align μ with B. Net force is zero in a uniform field.
3) A moving charge has magnetic force F=qv×B, magnitude |q|vB sinθ. It is zero at rest or when v∥B, maximum for v⊥B, and does no work because F⊥v. The path is circular for perpendicular entry and helical when velocity has a parallel component.
4) For an electron moving perpendicular to uniform B, magnetic force supplies centripetal force: evB=mv²/r, so r=mv/(eB). With accelerating voltage V, ½mv²=eV; measuring r, B and V gives e/m=2V/(B²r²). A crossed-field method can select v=E/B before measuring curvature.
5) Magnetic flux Φ=∫B·dA; for uniform field and flat area, Φ=BA cosθ. Unit weber (Wb). Ampere’s law: ∮B·dℓ=μ₀I_enclosed. For a long solenoid with n turns per length and current I, choose an Amperian rectangle: Bℓ=μ₀(nℓ)I, hence B=μ₀nI inside (approximately zero outside for an ideal long solenoid).
6) A moving-coil galvanometer detects small currents; a coil in radial B experiences torque τ=NIAB and pointer deflection proportional to current. Ammeter conversion: connect low shunt R_s=I_gR_g/(I−I_g) in parallel. Voltmeter conversion: connect series resistance R_h=V/I_g−R_g. Ammeter has low overall resistance; voltmeter high overall resistance.

NUMERICAL PROBLEMS (printed pp.124–125)
1) B=μ₀I/(2πr), so r=μ₀I/(2πB)=(4π×10⁻⁷×10)/(2π×5×10⁻⁵)=0.040 m.
2) Turns per length n=1000/0.5=2000 m⁻¹. B=μ₀nI, hence I=(2.5×10⁻³)/(4π×10⁻⁷×2000)=0.995 A≈1.0 A.
3) For v⊥B, F=qvB, v=(2×10⁻¹²)/(1.602×10⁻¹⁹×0.1)=1.25×10⁸ m/s≈1.3×10⁸ m/s.
4) Proton kinetic energy 8 MeV=1.282×10⁻¹² J. Nonrelativistic v=√(2K/m_p)=3.91×10⁷ m/s. (a) F=qvB=(1.602×10⁻¹⁹)(3.91×10⁷)(2.5)=1.56×10⁻¹¹ N. (b) r=m_pv/(qB)=0.164 m≈0.17 m.
5) Cyclotron period T=2πm_e/(eB)=2π(9.11×10⁻³¹)/(1.602×10⁻¹⁹×0.010)=3.57×10⁻⁹ s. The printed “3.6 s” is missing the 10⁻⁹ factor.
6) τ=NIAB, A=0.06×0.04=0.0024 m². I=0.20/(250×0.25×0.0024)=1.33 A. The printed 1.5 A does not follow from the stated dimensions/values.
7) Zero net force in crossed fields requires qE=qvB, so v=E/B=(2.00×10⁵)/(0.105)=1.90×10⁶ m/s.
8) R_s=I_gR_g/(I−I_g)=(0.010×100)/(100−0.010)=0.0100 Ω.
9) R_h=V/I_g−R_g=20/0.005−100=3900 Ω. The printed answer 1900 Ω is inconsistent with the stated 5 mA and 100 Ω.
10) At midpoint each wire is 0.05 m away. Opposite currents make the fields add: B_total=2[μ₀I/(2πr)]=μ₀I/(πr)=(4π×10⁻⁷×8)/(π×0.05)=6.4×10⁻⁵ T.`,source:"Scanned printed pp.122–125 (JPG 067_page_1.jpg through 068_page_2.jpg). Numerical results were recomputed; discrepancies in printed answers for problems 5, 6 and 9 are documented."});
PHYS_12_DATA[3].sections.push({id:"14.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 14 Exercise — worked answers",theory:`MULTIPLE-CHOICE (printed pp.152–153)
1) D, induced emf requires changing flux and a closed circuit for induced current.
2) A, continuous rotation in a uniform field produces alternating emf.
3) C, a generator uses dynamically induced emf.
4) D, Fleming’s left-hand thumb indicates mechanical force/motion.
5) B, on the axis of a current loop, B is parallel to the axis.
6) A, as the rotating coil turns, induced current continuously changes direction and magnitude.
7) C, Faraday’s law ε=N|dΦ/dt|, so more turns increase emf for the same flux change.
8) A, a transformer operates by statically induced emf from changing flux in stationary coils.
9) A, M=ΔΦ/ΔI=(2×10⁻² Wb)/(0.01 A)=2 H.
10) B, induced emf is proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux.
11) The stated values give L=ε/(ΔI/Δt)=8/(2/0.5)=2 H; none of the visible choices (0.2, 0.1, 0.8, 0.04 H) matches, so the MCQ values/options contain a likely printing error.
12) C, ideal step-up/down transformation keeps power constant (voltage and current trade inversely).
13) A, if the turns/voltage ratio is secondary:primary=3:2, then V_s=30×3/2=45 V.
14) A, eddy currents arise when a conductor experiences a changing magnetic field.

CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (printed pp.153–154)
1) Back emf magnitude is limited by rate of change of flux, winding turns and magnetic coupling; practical maximum is also limited by insulation, core saturation, heating and mechanical speed.
2) In a motor, increasing mechanical load slows the rotor; back emf is proportional to speed and therefore falls. The lower back emf increases armature current, allowing greater torque/power until the motor reaches a new operating speed.
3) With N turns, ε=N|dΦ/dt|; more turns link more flux and increase induced emf for the same motion/field change, improving sensitivity.
4) Wb/s=(T·m²)/s=V, so ε=−ΔΦ/Δt is dimensionally consistent.
5) Φ=BA cosθ, where θ is between B and the area normal; flux linkage is NΦ=NBA cosθ.
6) Changing flux in a conductive brake induces eddy currents. Their magnetic field opposes the motion causing them (Lenz’s law), producing contactless braking and dissipating kinetic energy as heat.
7) Laminate the transformer core into insulated thin sheets, reducing eddy-current loop area and losses; high-resistivity core materials further reduce currents.
8) An induction cooktop’s alternating magnetic field induces eddy currents in the conductive pan base; resistive heating warms the pan and food. A suitable ferromagnetic pan couples efficiently.
9) Back emf is the induced voltage in a motor armature that opposes the supply. It increases with rotational speed; at start it is near zero, so starting current is large.
10) A moving-coil motor uses curved/concave pole pieces and a soft-iron core to produce a radial magnetic field. Coil sides remain nearly perpendicular to B, keeping torque proportional to current and avoiding a direction-dependent torque loss.
11) Use laminated soft iron: high permeability concentrates flux; low coercivity/hysteresis reduces hysteresis loss; lamination suppresses eddy-current heating. Aluminum has low permeability, while a solid iron core has larger eddy-current losses.

COMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (printed p.154)
1) Electromagnetic induction: changing flux through a circuit induces emf. Demonstrate by moving a bar magnet toward/away from a coil connected to a galvanometer; deflection occurs only while flux changes. Faraday: ε=−N dΦ/dt; magnitude rises with turns, field strength, area and rate of motion. Dynamical induction uses relative motion of conductor and field; static induction uses changing field with stationary conductor.
2) Faraday’s law states induced emf equals negative time-rate of change of flux linkage. The minus sign is Lenz’s law: induced current produces a field opposing the change in flux.
3) Lenz’s law follows conservation of energy: if induced current reinforced the change that produced it, energy would grow without external work. Instead, an external agent must do work against the induced magnetic opposition, which becomes electrical/thermal energy.
4) Mutual induction: changing current in one coil changes its flux linked with a neighboring coil and induces emf there. M=N₂Φ₂₁/I₁; ε₂=−M dI₁/dt. Unit henry (H).
5) Self-induction: changing current in a coil changes its own linked flux and induces opposing emf. L=NΦ/I; ε=−L dI/dt. Unit henry; 1 H gives 1 V when current changes at 1 A/s.
6) Motional emf: a conductor of length ℓ moving at speed v perpendicular to B has ε=Bℓv. Charges experience qvB and separate until electric force balances magnetic force; V=Eℓ=Bvℓ.
7) A DC generator rotates an armature coil in a magnetic field; split-ring commutator reverses coil connections every half turn so external current is unidirectional. A rotating loop without commutator gives alternating emf ε=NBAω sinωt.
8) An AC motor converts electrical to mechanical energy using current-carrying coils in a magnetic field. Opposite forces create torque; a commutator or electronic drive maintains rotation. Torque τ=NIAB sinθ.
9) Transformer: two coils on a shared laminated high-permeability core. AC in primary creates changing flux inducing secondary voltage. Ideal relation V_s/V_p=N_s/N_p; power conservation gives V_pI_p≈V_sI_s. A step-up transformer has N_s>N_p and lower secondary current.
10) Laminations electrically isolate thin core sheets, breaking large eddy-current paths and reducing I²R loss; soft iron also reduces hysteresis loss.
11) In a motor, armature rotation changes flux linkage and induces back emf ε_b that opposes the applied voltage. For a DC motor, I=(V−ε_b)/R; increasing speed raises ε_b and limits current, while extra load slows it, lowering ε_b and increasing torque current.

NUMERICAL PROBLEMS (printed pp.154–155)
1) Spokes act as radial conductors: ε=½Bωr². Here B=0.6 G=6×10⁻⁵ T, ω=2π(180/60)=18.85 rad/s, r=0.6 m, so ε=½(6×10⁻⁵)(18.85)(0.6²)=2.04×10⁻⁴ V.
2) Cross-section A=20 cm²=2×10⁻³ m². ΔB=1.4−1.0=0.4 T; ΔΦ per turn=AΔB=8×10⁻⁴ Wb; Δ(NΦ)=1000×8×10⁻⁴=0.8 Wb-turn. M=Δ(NΦ)/ΔI=0.8/5=0.16 H. For current drop 9→4 A in 0.05 s, |ε|=M|ΔI/Δt|=0.16×100=16 V.
3) If the flux per turn changes from 1.0 mWb to 0.2 mWb in 0.1 s, |ε|=NΔΦ/Δt=100(0.8×10⁻³)/0.1=0.8 V. Series resistance=100+4000=4100 Ω, so I=0.8/4100=1.95×10⁻⁴ A=0.195 mA. This does not match the printed “1.6 mA”; source values or answer are inconsistent.
4) ε=Bℓv=(0.30×10⁻⁴)(10)(5)=1.5×10⁻³ V. Direction is given by v×B for positive charge; the higher-potential end is where positive charges accumulate.
5) L=ε/(|ΔI|/Δt)=200/(5/0.1)=4 H.
6) Solenoid turns per metre n=15 cm⁻¹=1500 m⁻¹. For one-turn loop area A=2.0 cm²=2×10⁻⁴ m², ε=Aμ₀n(ΔI/Δt)=2×10⁻⁴(4π×10⁻⁷)(1500)(2/0.1)=7.54×10⁻⁶ V.
7) ε=Bℓv. (a) Across the 8 cm side: 0.3×0.08×0.01=2.4×10⁻⁴ V; it takes 0.02/0.01=2 s for the loop to leave across the short dimension. (b) Across the 2 cm side: 0.3×0.02×0.01=6.0×10⁻⁵ V; duration=0.08/0.01=8 s.
8) ε=Bℓv=(0.080)(0.090)(35)=0.252 V. I=ε/R=0.252/(5.00×10⁻³)=50.4 A. Direction follows v×B for positive charges; conventional current is along that direction through the wire.
9) Change in flux linkage in the second coil |Δ(NΦ)|=MΔI₁=(1.5)(20)=30 Wb-turn.`,source:"Scanned printed pp.152–155 (JPG 082_page_1.jpg through 083_page_2.jpg). Recalculated numerical answers; inconsistencies in MCQ 11 and numerical 3 are flagged."});
PHYS_12_DATA[4].sections.push({id:"15.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 15 Exercise — worked answers",theory:`MULTIPLE-CHOICE (printed pp.197–198)
1) B, I_peak=√2 I_rms=141.4 A.
2) A, ω=2πf=100π rad/s for f=50 Hz.
3) B, a pure resistor has power factor 1 (unity).
4) A, an ideal capacitor blocks steady direct current after charging.
5) A, I_rms=I_peak/√2=(5√2)/√2=5 A.
6) D, a choke has inductive reactance much larger than its resistance (XL≫R).
7) A, at resonance the RLC circuit is resistive and current/voltage are in phase.
8) B, sinusoidal waveforms produce relatively little harmonic disturbance and are natural outputs of rotating generators.
9) B, ω=157 rad/s gives f=ω/(2π)≈25 Hz.
10) C, XL=2πfL increases with frequency.
11) D, an AC waveform is determined by its peak (amplitude), frequency and phase.
12) D, Maxwell added displacement current to Ampere’s law.
13) All electromagnetic wave frequencies remain unchanged on entering glass; speed and wavelength change. Since radio, X-ray and UV are all EM waves, the printed choices do not yield a unique answer.
14) B, in-phase V and I give power factor 1.
15) D is the only universally true listed statement: XC=1/(2πfC) decreases as frequency rises. RLC impedance itself can rise or fall depending on whether the circuit starts below or above resonance.

CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (printed p.199)
1) For an inductor, ε=−L(dI/dt). Plot is a straight line through origin for ε versus dI/dt with slope −L; the magnitude of the gradient is self-inductance.
2) Instantaneous AC voltage/current and their product may be negative, meaning energy is returned to the source during part of a cycle. For a resistor, v and i have the same sign at every instant, so p=vi=i²R≥0.
3) XL=2πfL, so the graph of reactance against frequency is a straight line through origin with gradient 2πL.
4) Doubling f doubles XL; it halves XC.
5) V_rms=V_peak/√2=1000/√2≈707 V.
6) XL=ωL has units s⁻¹·H=Ω; XC=1/(ωC) also has units Ω because 1/F=s⁻¹Ω.
7) ECG records time-varying electrical potential differences produced by cardiac depolarization/repolarization using skin electrodes; waveform features help assess heart rhythm and conduction.

COMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (printed pp.199–200)
1) For sinusoidal i=I₀sinωt, mean over a full cycle is zero; mean over a half-cycle is 2I₀/π. Peak is I₀. RMS is the square root of mean square: I_rms=√[(1/T)∫₀ᵀi²dt]=I₀/√2. Similarly V_rms=V₀/√2.
2) With v=V₀sinωt across R, i=v/R=(V₀/R)sinωt=I₀sinωt; they are in phase. Instantaneous power p=vi=(V₀I₀/2)[1−cos(2ωt)]. Average over cycle is P_avg=V_rmsI_rms=V_rms²/R=I_rms²R.
3) For capacitor, v=V₀sinωt and i=C(dv/dt)=ωCV₀cosωt=I₀sin(ωt+π/2); current leads voltage by 90°. XC=1/(ωC), I₀=V₀/XC. Average power over a full cycle is zero for an ideal capacitor.
4) Impedance Z is total opposition to AC, combining resistance and reactance as phasors. In series RLC, Z=√[R²+(XL−XC)²], I_rms=V_rms/Z, tanφ=(XL−XC)/R. At resonance XL=XC, Z=R.
5) Maximum power transfer occurs when load impedance is the complex conjugate of source impedance; for purely resistive source/load, R_L=R_s. In AC, matching reactance cancels as well.
6) In a series RL circuit, inductive voltage leads current by 90°; applied voltage is phasor sum, so current lags the applied voltage by φ=tan⁻¹(XL/R).
7) In a series RC circuit, capacitor voltage lags current by 90°; current leads applied voltage by φ=tan⁻¹(XC/R).
8) Maxwell equations (integral form): Gauss electric ∮E·dA=Q_enclosed/ε₀; Gauss magnetic ∮B·dA=0; Faraday ∮E·dℓ=−dΦ_B/dt; Ampere–Maxwell ∮B·dℓ=μ₀I_enclosed+μ₀ε₀dΦ_E/dt. They unify electricity, magnetism and electromagnetic waves.
9) Metal detector coil carries AC, producing changing magnetic flux. A conductive/metal object develops eddy currents and a secondary magnetic field that changes coil impedance/induced signal; detector electronics sense and indicate the change.

NUMERICAL QUESTIONS (printed pp.200–201)
1) V_rms=V_peak/√2=300/√2=212.1 V.
2) I_peak=√2 I_rms=14.14 A.
3) C=0.5 μF, ω=2×10³ rad/s, V_peak=16 V. XC=1/(ωC)=1000 Ω; I_peak=V_peak/XC=0.016 A=16 mA.
4) I_peak=ωCV_peak=(1.25×10⁴)(0.01×10⁻⁶)(240)=0.030 A. Capacitor current leads voltage 90°; voltage phase is −30°, so i=0.030 sin(1.25×10⁴t+60°) A.
5) XL=V/I=6.3/0.010=630 Ω. f=XL/(2πL)=630/[2π(100×10⁻⁶)]≈1.00×10⁶ Hz.
6) XL=2πfL: at 60 Hz, 1.13 Ω; at 10 kHz, 188 Ω. With 120 V rms, currents are 120/1.13≈106 A and 120/188≈0.637 A.
7) f₀=1/(2π√LC)=1/[2π√(3.00×10⁻³×5.00×10⁻⁶)]=1.30 kHz. At resonance Z=R=40 Ω, so I_rms=120/40=3.00 A.
8) XC=1/(2πfC)=40.0 Ω; Z=√(30²+40²)=50.0 Ω; I_rms=100/50=2.00 A; phase φ=tan⁻¹(XC/R)=53.1° leading. i_peak=√2I=2.828 A, hence i=2.828 sin(314t+53°) A.
9) XL=2πfL≈10.0 Ω; Z=√(7²+10²)=12.21 Ω; I=230/12.21=18.85 A; φ=tan⁻¹(10/7)=55.0° lag; power factor cosφ≈0.573 lagging; P=I²R≈2.49 kW.
10) XC=1/(314×100×10⁻⁶)=31.85 Ω; Z=√(10²+31.85²)=33.38 Ω; peak current=100/33.38≈3.00 A.`,source:"Scanned printed pp.197–201 (JPG 104_page_2.jpg through 106_page_2.jpg). Computations follow the stated quantities; ambiguity in MCQ 13 is noted."});
PHYS_12_DATA[5].sections.push({id:"16.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 16 Exercise — worked answers",theory:`MULTIPLE-CHOICE (printed pp.227–228)
1) Shear modulus G=F/A divided by shear strain x/L, so G=FL/(Ax), option c.
2) B, critical temperature is the transition temperature to superconductivity.
3) D, barium is not ferromagnetic.
4) C, elastic modulus (stress/strain) is constant within the linear elastic region.
5) B, strain=(2L−L)/L=1.
6) B, bulk modulus describes volume change/compression.
7) B, doubling length means extension equals original length, so strain=1.
8) C, elastic modulus is a material property and does not depend on dimensions.
9) C, above Curie temperature a ferromagnet becomes paramagnetic.
10) For equal cuts in a uniform cable, each half can be stretched by half the original elastic extension; maximum safe extension is 1 mm (b), assuming the original 2 mm is the failure extension and equal cross-sections/material.

CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (printed p.228)
1) Steel generally has greater coercivity than iron because its domain walls are more strongly pinned by microstructure/alloying; it retains magnetization better, useful for permanent magnets.
2) Strength sets the maximum load before yielding/failure; stiffness (Young’s modulus) controls deflection under service load. Design must meet both strength and serviceability limits while balancing mass, cost, safety factors and material behavior.
3) Superconductivity is a zero-resistance state below a critical temperature; current can persist without resistive loss. A curve of resistance vs temperature drops to zero at Tc. In practice, critical temperature, magnetic field and current-density limits constrain use.
4) Crystalline solids have long-range periodic order; amorphous solids lack long-range order; polymers are long-chain molecules and can be amorphous, crystalline or semicrystalline.
5) Elastic deformation is reversible when load is removed; plastic deformation is permanent after stress exceeds the yield point.
6) Diamagnetic materials weakly oppose an applied field (e.g. bismuth); paramagnetic materials weakly align with it (aluminum); ferromagnetic materials strongly align and can retain magnetization (iron, nickel, cobalt).
7) Soft magnetic materials have narrow B–H hysteresis loops, low coercivity and low hysteresis loss; hard magnetic materials have wide loops, high coercivity and high retentivity, suitable for permanent magnets.
8) Brittle materials fracture with little plastic deformation; ductile materials undergo substantial plastic strain and can be drawn into wire.
9) Raise temperature, increase illumination, add suitable dopants or apply an electric field; these can increase carrier concentration and semiconductor conductivity.
10) A steel girder has the same length whether vertical or horizontal if temperature/load are unchanged; orientation changes bending/sag under gravity, not its intrinsic length. Small elastic strain may differ under different loading.
11) Superconductors in MRI provide very large persistent currents and strong, stable magnetic fields with negligible resistive heating, improving field uniformity and reducing operating power after cooling.

COMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (printed p.228)
1) Tensile force pulls a member apart; compressive force pushes it together. Internal stresses balance external forces in static equilibrium. Excess tension can cause elongation/yield/fracture; compression can cause shortening, crushing or buckling. Supports/reactions must balance net force and torque.
2) Stress σ=F/A and strain ε=ΔL/L. A stress–strain curve begins with a linear elastic region (slope Young’s modulus); beyond proportional/elastic limit, plastic deformation begins; yield stress marks substantial permanent strain; ultimate tensile stress is maximum engineering stress before necking/fracture.
3) Valence band contains bound valence electrons; conduction band contains mobile carriers; forbidden energy gap Eg separates them. Conductors have overlapping/partially filled bands, semiconductors small Eg, insulators large Eg. Thermal/light energy can excite carriers across a semiconductor gap.
4) Below critical temperature, resistance of a superconductor falls abruptly to zero. Plot R against T shows finite normal-state resistance above Tc and zero below Tc. Critical magnetic field/current also suppresses superconductivity.
5) Mechanical properties include strength, elasticity, plasticity, stiffness, hardness, ductility, brittleness, toughness, fatigue resistance and creep; each describes a different response to load/environment.
6) B–H hysteresis: magnetization lags applied field; increasing H reaches saturation; reducing H leaves remanent flux density; reverse field Hc removes it; further reverse H reaches opposite saturation. Loop area equals energy loss per unit volume per cycle. Soft iron has narrow loop; hard magnetic material wide loop.
7) Elastic strain energy density is area under stress–strain curve. In linear elastic range u=½σε=½Yε²=σ²/(2Y). For a wire, U=½FΔL. Graph is triangular under Hooke’s law.

NUMERICAL PROBLEMS (printed p.229)
1) Using piano-wire length L=1.50 cm=0.015 m, diameter 0.25 cm, area A=π(0.00125)²=4.91×10⁻⁶ m², load F=mg=98.1 N and a typical steel Young’s modulus Y≈2.0×10¹¹ Pa: ΔL=FL/(AY)=1.50×10⁻⁶ m. The printed answer 1.5×10⁻⁴ m is 100 times larger; it would require different wire dimensions or a much lower modulus.
2) A=π(0.01)²=3.14×10⁻⁴ m². (a) σ=F/A=10⁴/(3.14×10⁻⁴)=3.18×10⁷ Pa=0.318×10⁸ Pa. (b) Taking steel Y=2.0×10¹¹ Pa, ε=σ/Y=1.59×10⁻⁴. (c) ΔL=εL=(1.59×10⁻⁴)(0.50)=7.96×10⁻⁵ m. Matches printed rounded values.
3) Strain=ΔL/L=(1.2×10⁻⁴)/12=1.0×10⁻⁵. Strain-energy density u=½σε=½(6.4×10⁸)(1.0×10⁻⁵)=3.2×10³ J/m³.
4) Four legs share 100 kg: force per leg=mg/4=245 N. From the scan, area per leg appears 2×10⁻³ m²: stress=1.23×10⁵ Pa; shortening ΔL=FL/(AY)=σL/Y=(1.23×10⁵)(0.42)/(1×10¹⁰)=5.15×10⁻⁶ m. The printed answer (5×10⁵ Pa; 2.1×10⁻⁵ m) implies area 5×10⁻⁴ m², so the scan’s cross-section exponent/value is ambiguous or the answer key is wrong.
5) Area=4.8×10⁻⁴ m²; stress=1.5×10⁴/A=3.125×10⁷ Pa. (a) Y=σ/ε=(3.125×10⁷)/(1.4×10⁻⁴)=2.23×10¹¹ Pa. (b) At strain 1×10⁻⁴, u=½σε=½(2.23×10¹¹)(1×10⁻⁴)²=1.12×10³ J/m³. The printed answer 1.5×10³ J/m³ is approximate and assumes a rounded modulus near 3×10¹¹ Pa.`,source:"Scanned printed pp.227–229 (JPG 119_page_2.jpg through 120_page_2.jpg). Discrepant numerical inputs/printed results are flagged."});
PHYS_12_DATA[6].sections.push({id:"17.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 17 Exercise — worked answers",theory:`MULTIPLE-CHOICE (printed pp.251–252)
1) D, in N-type silicon electrons are majority carriers and pentavalent atoms are donors.
2) B, silicon diode barrier potential is approximately 0.7 V at room temperature.
3) C, reverse bias widens the depletion region.
4) C, common-emitter current gain β=I_C/I_B.
5) B, reverse saturation current is due to minority carriers.
6) D, a reverse-biased PN junction acts approximately as an off/open switch.
7) B, improper biasing can distort the output signal.
8) C, digital transistor switches use cutoff and saturation.
9) B, electrons injected from the emitter mostly cross the thin base and enter the collector.
10) B, base current controls collector current in the active region (I_C≈βI_B).
11) B, two diodes are the minimum for full-wave rectification with a center-tapped transformer; a bridge arrangement uses four.
12) B, both junctions reverse-biased places an NPN transistor in cutoff.

CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (printed pp.252–253)
1) The base is thin and lightly doped so few injected carriers recombine there; the collector is physically larger to collect carriers and dissipate heat safely.
2) Only a small fraction of emitter-injected carriers recombine in the base; most reach the collector, hence I_B≪I_C and I_E=I_C+I_B.
3) Forward bias at emitter-base injects majority carriers into the base; reverse bias at collector-base sweeps those carriers into the collector, producing useful amplification with small base current.
4) NPN: electrons are majority carriers; conventional current flows collector to emitter in active mode. PNP: holes are majority carriers and polarities/current directions reverse. Both require emitter-base forward and collector-base reverse bias for active amplification.
5) N-type semiconductor: donor-doped (pentavalent), electrons majority. P-type: acceptor-doped (trivalent), holes majority. Both remain electrically neutral overall.
6) P-type material has many holes but equal negative acceptor ions and other charges balance them; holes are mobile carriers, not net positive charge in the bulk.
7) Common-emitter gives substantial current and voltage gain with convenient input/output impedance, so it is widely used for voltage/power amplification.
8) A small change in base current controls a larger collector-current change: β=ΔI_C/ΔI_B.
9) Trivalent doping makes the sample P-type. The question does not specify silicon atomic density, intrinsic carrier concentration or sample volume, so exact electron/hole counts cannot be determined from the atom counts alone; qualitatively holes are majority and electrons minority. With full compensation assumptions, each acceptor can supply roughly one hole, but thermal carriers also exist.

COMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (printed pp.252–253)
1) Band model: insulator has filled valence band, empty conduction band and large forbidden gap; semiconductor has small gap so thermal/light energy creates electron-hole pairs; conductor has overlapping bands or partially filled band and many mobile electrons.
2) At a PN junction, electrons and holes diffuse across the boundary and recombine, leaving fixed ionized donors/acceptors. The resulting depletion region and internal electric field create a built-in potential that opposes further diffusion; at equilibrium drift and diffusion balance.
3) In half-wave rectification a diode conducts on one half-cycle and blocks the other, yielding pulsating DC. During forward bias current flows; during reverse bias it is negligible. Output ripple is large; a filter capacitor can smooth it.
4) A transistor amplifier is biased in its active region. A small input signal changes base-emitter voltage/base current; β produces a larger collector-current variation through load R_C, creating amplified output voltage. Correct bias prevents clipping.
5) Half-wave rectifier uses one diode and has simple construction but lower average output, larger ripple and lower efficiency. Full-wave uses two diodes with a center-tapped transformer or four-diode bridge, uses both half-cycles, gives higher average DC and less ripple (with filtering), but needs more components.
6) For common-emitter, I_E=I_C+I_B, α=I_C/I_E, β=I_C/I_B. Thus β=α/(1−α) and α=β/(β+1).
7) Extrinsic semiconductor conductivity depends on dopant concentration/type and temperature. Donors introduce energy levels near the conduction band; acceptors near valence band. Doping raises carrier concentration and shifts Fermi level; at higher temperature intrinsic carriers dominate.

NUMERICAL PROBLEMS (printed p.253)
1) β=I_C/I_B=(10×10⁻³)/(40×10⁻⁶)=250.
2) I_C=βI_B=100×100 μA=10 mA. I_E=I_C+I_B=10.1 mA. α=I_C/I_E=10/10.1=0.990.
3) I_C=V_R/R_C=6/3000=2.00 mA. Taking the given “current gain 0.97” as β, I_B=I_C/β=2.06 mA, matching the book’s answer (although β=0.97 is unusually low for a transistor and the prompt may mean α).
4) α=I_C/I_E=0.96, therefore I_E=60 μA/0.96=62.5 μA.
5) I_B=I_E−I_C=3.50−2.35=1.15 A.`,source:"Scanned printed pp.251–253 (JPG 131_page_2.jpg through 132_page_2.jpg). The carrier-count question lacks needed sample/intrinsic data and is answered with the appropriate limitation."});
PHYS_12_DATA[7].sections.push({id:"18.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 18 Exercise — worked answers",theory:`MULTIPLE-CHOICE (printed pp.293–294)
1) B, A and B have equal proper lifetimes and symmetric journeys; C remains in the inertial Earth frame and accumulates more proper time, so A=B<C.
2) C, the spaceship observer is at rest with both departure and arrival events and measures their proper time.
3) A, compressing a spring adds stored elastic energy; mass increases by Δm=ΔE/c², though imperceptibly.
4) D, blackbody total radiated power ∝T⁴; doubling absolute temperature multiplies it by 16.
5) A, at fixed color/frequency, greater brightness means more photons per unit time; individual photon energy stays hf.
6) A, for equal power, red photons have lower energy than blue/yellow photons, so red source emits more photons per second.
7) C, photon energy must exceed 3.00 eV; the least listed energy that can eject electrons is 3.50 eV.
8) D, the Compton electron receives maximum energy for 180° photon backscattering.
9) B, proton recoil changes photon wavelength less than electron recoil because proton mass is much larger.
10) B, pair production cannot occur for an isolated photon in vacuum because momentum cannot be conserved; a nearby nucleus/field can recoil.
11) C, at equal kinetic energy the heaviest listed particle (alpha particle) has greatest momentum and smallest de Broglie wavelength.
12) D, equal wavelength means equal momentum; K=p²/(2m), so the lighter electron has greater kinetic energy than the proton.
13) A, electron beam replaces light in an electron microscope.
14) C, ΔxΔp≥ħ/2; halving momentum uncertainty requires at least doubling position uncertainty.

CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (printed p.295)
1) If c were only 50 m/s, relativistic effects would occur for ordinary walking/vehicles; moving clocks would run slow, lengths contract along motion, simultaneity would depend strongly on observer, and energy/momentum effects would be obvious in daily life.
2) Blue stars are hotter than red stars; Wien’s law λ_max T≈2.90×10⁻³ m·K.
3) No. Photoemission requires photon frequency above that metal’s threshold; different metals have different work functions, so the same light may eject electrons from one but not another.
4) No. A 2500 K bulb emits a redder spectrum with much less short-wavelength visible light than the ~6000 K Sun, so it appears warmer/yellower and is less white.
5) For equal total energy, red light has lower photon energy and therefore contains more photons than blue light.
6) A photon’s rest mass is zero; a nonzero rest mass would conflict with its invariant speed c. Photons carry energy and momentum (p=E/c).
7) Visible photons have much lower energy and produce a very small Compton wavelength shift; the electron recoil/shift is difficult to distinguish at visible wavelengths.
8) Equal de Broglie wavelength means equal momentum p; v=p/m, so the lighter electron moves faster.
9) For equal accelerating voltage, both acquire equal kinetic energy eV; electron’s lower mass gives greater momentum p=√(2meV), thus smaller wavelength than proton.
10) UV photons have higher energy (E=hf) and can break chemical bonds/DNA or damage cells; visible photons generally have lower photon energy.

COMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (printed pp.295–296)
1) Inertial frame: one in which a free body remains at rest or uniform straight-line motion; Newton’s laws apply without fictitious forces (e.g. train moving steadily). Non-inertial frame accelerates/rotates; apparent forces are needed to describe motion (e.g. accelerating car, rotating carousel).
2) Special relativity postulates: (i) laws of physics have the same form in all inertial frames; (ii) speed of light in vacuum is c for all inertial observers. Consequences: relativity of simultaneity, time dilation Δt=γΔt₀, length contraction L=L₀/γ, mass-energy E=mc² and relativistic momentum; γ=1/√(1−v²/c²).
3) A black body absorbs all incident radiation and emits a spectrum depending only on temperature. Spectrum is continuous; hotter bodies emit more total power (Stefan–Boltzmann law) and peak at shorter wavelength (Wien law). Classical theory failed at short wavelengths (“ultraviolet catastrophe”). Planck proposed energy exchange in quanta E=nhf, giving Planck’s radiation law and founding quantum physics.
4) Photoelectric effect: light above threshold frequency ejects electrons. Classical wave theory could not explain threshold frequency, immediate emission and electron maximum kinetic energy depending on frequency rather than intensity. Photon model: one photon energy hf; work function φ removes electron, K_max=hf−φ; intensity controls electron count/current. Stopping voltage satisfies eV_s=K_max.
5) Compton effect is increase in wavelength when X-rays scatter from electrons. Conservation of energy and momentum gives Δλ=λ′−λ=(h/m_ec)(1−cosθ). This supports photon momentum p=h/λ.
6) Pair production: a sufficiently energetic photon near a nucleus converts to e⁻+e⁺; minimum photon energy 2m_ec²=1.022 MeV, with recoil nucleus conserving momentum. Annihilation: positron and electron combine, usually producing two 511 keV photons in opposite directions when initial momentum is negligible.
7) de Broglie proposed matter waves λ=h/p. Davisson–Germer accelerated electrons scattered from a nickel crystal and observed diffraction maxima consistent with Bragg reflection and de Broglie wavelength, confirming wave behavior.
8) Wave-particle duality: light and matter show wave phenomena (interference/diffraction) and particle-like localized interactions (photons/electrons). Which aspect is observed depends on measurement arrangement; quantum theory describes probabilities.
9) Heisenberg uncertainty: ΔxΔp≥ħ/2 and ΔEΔt≥ħ/2 (order-of-magnitude form ΔEΔt~ħ). It is not merely instrument error; measurement interactions and wave nature impose a fundamental limit. A microscope thought experiment: shorter wavelength improves position resolution but transfers a larger/less predictable photon momentum to the electron.

NUMERICAL PROBLEMS (printed p.296)
1) L/L₀=1/3=1/γ, so γ=3. β=√(1−1/γ²)=√(8/9)=0.9428; v=0.9428c.
2) Proper period T₀=3 s. Moving observer measures time-dilated T=γT₀=3/√(1−0.95²)=9.61 s.
3) φ=3.0 eV. Threshold f₀=φ/h=(3.0×1.602×10⁻¹⁹)/(6.626×10⁻³⁴)=7.25×10¹⁴ Hz (≈0.72×10¹⁵). Photon energy at λ=3×10⁻⁷ m is hc/λ=4.136 eV. K_max=4.136−3.0=1.136 eV≈1.16 eV; stopping potential≈1.16 V.
4) Compton shift at θ=90° is λ_C=2.426 pm. Initial photon energy 50 keV, λ_i=hc/E=24.80 pm. λ_f=27.23 pm, E_f=hc/λ_f≈45.5 keV.
5) The scan shows speed as 5×10⁻³ m/s and precision 0.003%, but its printed answer (4.84×10⁻³ m) is incompatible with those values. Using the displayed speed, Δv=3×10⁻⁵(5×10⁻³)=1.5×10⁻⁷ m/s; Δp=m_eΔv=1.37×10⁻³⁷ kg·m/s; Δx≥ħ/(2Δp)=3.86×10² m. The exponent in the scanned speed or answer is likely corrupted; retain this explicit discrepancy until a clearer source is available.`,source:"Scanned printed pp.293–296 (JPG 152_page_2.jpg through 154_page_1.jpg). Question 5 numerical scan is internally inconsistent and flagged rather than forced to the printed result."});
PHYS_12_DATA[8].sections.push({id:"19.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 19 Exercise — worked answers",theory:`MULTIPLE-CHOICE (printed pp.330–331)
1) B, the Balmer series ends at n=2 and lies in the visible region.
2) A, the longest-wavelength Balmer line is the smallest energy drop, n=3→2.
3) C, E₂=−13.6/2²=−3.4 eV; 3.4 eV is needed to ionize from n=2.
4) C, maximum transitions from n=5 is n(n−1)/2=10.
5) C, E₄=−13.6/16=−0.85 eV.
6) C, r₃/r₁=3²=9.
7) B, the reverse process of X-ray production is absorption of X-rays by electrons (photoelectric effect, in the textbook’s broad sense).
8) A, metastable states have lifetimes around 10⁻³ s, far longer than ordinary excited states (~10⁻⁸ s).
9) C, He–Ne laser commonly emits red light (632.8 nm).
10) C, common He–Ne gas mixture is about 85% He and 15% Ne.
11) D, photon energy=20.66−18.70=1.96 eV.
12) C, L-series X-rays terminate at the L shell n=2; e.g. transition n=4→2.
13) C, pumping supplies energy to create population inversion.
14) B, Bohr angular momentum mvr=nħ and v∝1/n, so electron momentum p∝1/n.

CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (printed p.331)
1) The single electron can occupy many quantized energy levels; transitions between different pairs of levels emit/absorb photons of different energies, producing many spectral lines.
2) In solids, closely spaced energy levels broaden into bands and interactions produce a near-continuum of allowed energies; hot dilute gases retain discrete atomic energy levels and emit line spectra.
3) (a) A photon below 13.6 eV can be absorbed only if its energy matches an allowed transition from n=1; otherwise it is not absorbed. (b) A photon above 13.6 eV can ionize hydrogen; excess energy becomes the ejected electron’s kinetic energy.
4) Hydrogen levels E_n=−13.6/n² eV converge toward zero as n increases, so energy differences shrink and spectral lines crowd together toward the series limit.
5) Quantizing angular momentum L=nħ gives discrete stable orbits and energies, explaining atomic stability and discrete line spectra rather than continuous radiation.
6) Both are electromagnetic photons, but X-rays arise from high-energy inner-shell transitions or electron deceleration and have much higher frequency/penetration; visible light usually comes from lower-energy outer-shell transitions.
7) Higher tube voltage raises the maximum electron energy, so the bremsstrahlung cutoff wavelength decreases (λ_min=hc/eV). Characteristic X-ray line energies are set by target atomic levels and remain essentially fixed, though intensity can change.
8) Crystal plane spacing is comparable to X-ray wavelengths, enabling Bragg diffraction; UV wavelengths are generally much longer than atomic plane spacings and do not give the same crystallographic diffraction conditions.

COMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (printed pp.332)
1) Hydrogen spectrum contains Lyman (transitions to n=1, UV), Balmer (to n=2, visible/near-UV), Paschen (to n=3, infrared), Brackett (to n=4, infrared), etc. Rydberg equation: 1/λ=R_H(1/n_f²−1/n_i²), n_i>n_f. Discrete levels produce discrete lines; series converge at ionization limit.
2) Bohr postulates: electrons occupy stationary orbits without radiation; angular momentum is quantized mvr=nħ; radiation is emitted/absorbed only during transitions with hf=|ΔE|. For Coulomb attraction, m v²/r=ke²/r² and mvr=nħ. Solving gives r_n=a₀n² with a₀=4πε₀ħ²/(me²), and E_n=−13.6/n² eV for hydrogen.
3) Ground state is lowest energy n=1; excited states have n>1. Excitation energy is energy needed to reach a higher bound level; ionization energy removes an electron to infinity (13.6 eV from ground state; 13.6/n² from level n).
4) X-rays are high-frequency EM radiation. In an X-ray tube, electrons accelerated through voltage strike a metal target: deceleration gives continuous bremsstrahlung; inner-shell vacancies filled by outer electrons give characteristic discrete lines. Continuous spectrum cutoff λ_min=hc/eV. Characteristic energies depend on target element.
5) Laser means light amplification by stimulated emission. Pumping creates population inversion; a metastable upper level stores excitation; a photon stimulates identical in-phase photons. An active medium between reflecting/partly transmitting mirrors amplifies the coherent, monochromatic, directional beam.

NUMERICAL PROBLEMS (printed p.332)
1) Paschen series has n_f=3. Longest λ is 4→3: 1/λ=R(1/9−1/16), giving λ≈1.875 μm=1875 nm. Shortest is series limit ∞→3: 1/λ=R/9, giving λ≈820 nm.
2) r₅=5²a₀=25(0.0529 nm)=1.323 nm. E₅=−13.6/25=−0.544 eV=−8.72×10⁻²⁰ J.
3) n=2→1: ΔE=13.6(1−1/4)=10.2 eV. f=ΔE/h=(10.2×1.602×10⁻¹⁹)/(6.626×10⁻³⁴)=2.47×10¹⁵ Hz (≈2.5×10¹⁵). λ=c/f≈1.21×10⁻⁷ m=121 nm.
4) λ_n=h/(m v_n)=2πr_n/n. At n=4, r₄=16a₀=0.846 nm; λ=2π(0.846)/4≈1.33 nm.
5) Ground-state electron speed v₁=2.19×10⁶ m/s. Period T=2πr₁/v₁=2π(5.29×10⁻¹¹)/(2.19×10⁶)=1.52×10⁻¹⁶ s.
6) Duane–Hunt limit eV=hc/λ_min. V=(1240 eV·nm)/(0.46 nm)=2696 V≈2.7 kV.`,source:"Scanned printed pp.330–332 (JPG 171_page_1.jpg through 172_page_1.jpg). Numerical values were recalculated and compared with the printed answers."});
PHYS_12_DATA[9].sections.push({id:"20.exercise.solved",title:"Unit 20 Exercise — worked answers",theory:`MULTIPLE-CHOICE (printed pp.379–380)
1) D, 1 u≈1.6605×10⁻²⁷ kg; u c²≈931.5 MeV≈1.4924×10⁻¹⁰ J, so all listed equivalences hold.
2) B, ⁷Li has 7−3=4 neutrons.
3) B, separated nucleons have greater total mass than the bound nucleus; the difference is mass defect.
4) C, β⁻ radiation consists of electrons.
5) D, 1 Ci=3.70×10¹⁰ Bq.
6) C, after four half-lives 20 mg/2⁴=1.25 mg remains.
7) D, decay constant has units s⁻¹ (or inverse time).
8) D, fission neutrons are typically slow/thermal, around 0.04 eV.
9) A, conserve A and Z: ¹₀n+¹⁶₈O→¹⁵₇N+²₁H.
10) The reaction isotope notation is too blurred in the supplied scan to identify the target nuclide reliably; conserve mass number and atomic number once a clearer scan is available rather than guessing an option.
11) A, naturally occurring uranium contains about 0.7% ²³⁵U.
12) C, a liquid-metal fast breeder uses ²³⁸U as fertile material to breed fissile ²³⁹Pu.
13) D, the strong nuclear force acts between proton–proton, proton–neutron and neutron–neutron pairs (with Coulomb repulsion additionally between protons).
14) C and D are both bosons (photon and pion); the single-choice item is ambiguous. Proton and neutron are fermions.
15) B, quark–antiquark bound states are mesons.
16) A, observable free particles are color-neutral; isolated colored quarks are confined.

CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (printed p.380)
1) In heavy nuclei proton–proton Coulomb repulsion grows strongly with proton number. Extra neutrons add attractive nuclear force without adding electric repulsion, so stable heavy nuclei need N>Z.
2) Radium-226 is not primordial from Earth formation because billions of years represent many 1600-year half-lives. Any present radium is continually replenished in decay chains, especially from uranium/thorium parent isotopes.
3) Radioactive decay is stochastic. The decay constant gives a probability per unit time for each nucleus; half-life predicts a population fraction, not the exact lifetime of an individual nucleus.
4) Neutrons have no electric charge, so they do not face Coulomb repulsion from a positively charged target nucleus and can approach the nuclear-force range readily.
5) Neutron-rich nuclides commonly undergo β⁻ decay (n→p+e⁻+ν̄); proton-rich nuclides may undergo β⁺ decay or electron capture, moving toward a stable neutron/proton ratio.
6) Very small nuclei are unstable when their proton/neutron balance or binding is unfavorable; very heavy nuclei are destabilized by growing Coulomb repulsion relative to short-range nuclear attraction. Stability lies in a band of neutron/proton ratios.
7) Power reactors use controlled, relatively slow chain reactions, low-enriched fuel, neutron moderators/absorbers, control rods and heat removal. A bomb requires a rapid prompt-supercritical assembly of highly enriched fissile material; a normal reactor cannot assemble that configuration or achieve the same runaway conditions.
8) For light nuclei, binding energy per nucleon generally increases when they fuse into heavier nuclei up to the iron/nickel region. The product has greater total binding and lower mass; the mass difference is released as energy.
9) Positively charged nuclei repel through the Coulomb barrier. Fusion needs very high temperature (kinetic energy), sufficient density and confinement time; plasma stability and energy losses make sustained fusion difficult.

COMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (printed p.381)
1) Natural radioactivity is spontaneous decay of unstable nuclei. Alpha radiation is a helium-4 nucleus (charge +2); beta radiation is an electron or positron; gamma radiation is high-energy electromagnetic photons. They differ in charge, mass, penetration and ionization.
2) Mass spectrometer: vaporize and ionize sample, accelerate ions, separate by mass-to-charge ratio using electric/magnetic fields, detect ion abundance. In a simple magnetic sector, qvB=mv²/r so m/q=Br/v; accelerating through V gives ½mv²=qV, allowing m/q to be determined. Isotope peak positions/relative intensities give masses and abundances.
3) Isotopes have the same atomic number (protons) but different neutron number/mass number; e.g. ¹H, ²H, ³H or ³⁵Cl and ³⁷Cl.
4) Mass defect Δm=Z m_p+N m_n−m_nucleus (using consistent nuclear/atomic masses). Binding energy E_b=Δm c²; it is energy required to separate a nucleus into free nucleons and indicates stability per nucleon.
5) Half-life is time for the number of undecayed nuclei/activity to fall to half. N=N₀e^(−λt), T₁/₂=ln2/λ; activity A=λN also halves over the same interval.
6) Nuclear reaction changes a target nucleus through collision/capture/decay; write balanced equations conserving nucleon number A, charge Z, energy and momentum. Q=(initial mass−final mass)c²; positive Q releases energy.
7) Fission splits a heavy nucleus after neutron absorption into medium-mass fragments, more neutrons and energy. A chain reaction occurs when emitted neutrons cause further fissions. Reactor components: fuel, moderator (thermal reactors), control rods, coolant, shielding and containment; controlled heat produces steam/electricity.
8) Nuclear reactor sustains a controlled fission chain reaction. Fuel rods contain fissile material; moderator slows neutrons where needed; control rods absorb neutrons; coolant removes heat; shielding/containment reduce radiation release. Heat drives a turbine-generator; reactors provide electricity and isotopes, with waste and safety managed.
9) Fusion combines light nuclei, e.g. D+T→⁴He+n+17.6 MeV. Products are more tightly bound per nucleon; mass defect releases energy. Very high temperature and confinement overcome Coulomb repulsion; stars use proton-proton reactions.
10) Geiger–Müller detector: radiation ionizes gas in a tube; avalanche discharge produces a pulse, counted electronically. It detects events but gives limited energy information; dead time and efficiency matter.
11) Radioisotopes are tracers for imaging/diagnosis (e.g. technetium-99m), sources for radiotherapy, sterilization and research; treatment/doses require clinical control because ionizing radiation can damage tissue.
12) Hadrons experience strong interaction and are made of quarks: baryons contain three quarks (proton uud, neutron udd), mesons a quark–antiquark pair (pion). Leptons (electron, muon, neutrinos) do not experience strong interaction. Quarks carry fractional electric charge and color; confinement prevents isolated quarks.

NUMERICAL PROBLEMS (printed pp.381–382)
1) Helium-4: nucleon mass sum=2(1.007276 u)+2(1.008665 u)=4.031882 u. Using m(⁴He nucleus)≈4.001506 u, Δm≈0.030376 u. E_b=Δm×931.5≈28.3 MeV.
2) ¹⁴N has 7 protons and 7 neutrons. Nucleon mass sum=7(1.007276+1.008665)=14.111587 u. Δm=14.111587−13.999234=0.112353 u; E_b=0.112353×931.5≈104.66 MeV.
3) λ=ln2/T₁/₂. T=1.6×10³ y×365×24×3600≈5.05×10¹⁰ s, so λ≈1.37×10⁻¹¹ s⁻¹≈1.4×10⁻¹¹ s⁻¹.
4) Number of ²³⁵U nuclei in 1 kg: N=(1000/235)N_A≈2.563×10²⁴. Energy=N×208 MeV≈5.33×10²⁶ MeV (≈8.54×10¹³ J).
5) Reaction ⁶Li+d→2α+Q. The scan appears to misprint deuteron mass as 4.002603 u; use physical deuteron mass 2.014102 u and alpha mass 4.002603 u. Mass equivalent of Q=22.3/931.5=0.02394 u. M(⁶Li)=2m_α−m_d+0.02394≈6.01504 u, matching printed answer.
6) For β⁻ decay, using the displayed masses ²³⁴Th=234.0436 u and ²³⁴Pa=234.0428 u, Q≈(0.0008 u)(931.5 MeV/u)=0.745 MeV. The printed answer 0.26967 MeV does not match these displayed values; a source mass digit is likely blurred or misprinted.`,source:"Scanned printed pp.379–382 (JPG 195_page_2.jpg through 197_page_1.jpg). Ambiguous nuclear symbols and inconsistent mass data are disclosed."});

