    "id": "phys12-unit12-current54",
    "title": "12.1 Steady current — definition and units",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>2. Those substances which have a larger number of free electrons permit current to flow easily. Such substances are called conductors; examples include copper, zinc, silver and aluminium.</p><p>In different current-carrying materials, the moving particles may carry positive or negative charge. In metals the moving charges are always electrons (negative charges), while in gases the moving charges are negative and positive ions. We define the current through cross-sectional area A as the net charge flowing through the area per unit time. Thus if a net charge Q flows through an area in time t, the current I is</p><p>\\(I=\\frac{Q}{t}\\) ...(12.1)</p><p>Charge Q is measured in coulombs and time t in seconds. Therefore the unit of electric current is coulomb per second, or ampere (A). One ampere is the current when one coulomb of charge flows across any cross-section of a conductor in one second. The submultiples of ampere are:</p><p>\\(1\\,\\mathrm{mA}=1\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm A\\)</p><p>\\(1\\,\\mu\\mathrm A=1\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm A\\)</p><p>TIP</p><p>Current is a scalar quantity because it does not follow the vector law of addition.</p><p>DO YOU KNOW?</p><p>If electrons drift so slowly, why does room light turn on quickly when a switch is closed? The answer is that propagation of the electric field takes place at the speed of light.</p>",
    "theory": "2. Those substances which have a larger number of free electrons permit current to flow easily. Such substances are called conductors; examples include copper, zinc, silver and aluminium.\nIn different current-carrying materials, the moving particles may carry positive or negative charge. In metals the moving charges are always electrons (negative charges), while in gases the moving charges are negative and positive ions. We define the current through cross-sectional area A as the net charge flowing through the area per unit time. Thus if a net charge Q flows through an area in time t, the current I is\n\\(I=\\frac{Q}{t}\\) ...(12.1)\nCharge Q is measured in coulombs and time t in seconds. Therefore the unit of electric current is coulomb per second, or ampere (A). One ampere is the current when one coulomb of charge flows across any cross-section of a conductor in one second. The submultiples of ampere are:\n\\(1\\,\\mathrm{mA}=1\\times10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm A\\)\n\\(1\\,\\mu\\mathrm A=1\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm A\\)\nTIP\nCurrent is a scalar quantity because it does not follow the vector law of addition.\nDO YOU KNOW?\nIf electrons drift so slowly, why does room light turn on quickly when a switch is closed? The answer is that propagation of the electric field takes place at the speed of light.",
