    "title": "12.4 Effect of temperature on resistance",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>12.4 EFFECT OF TEMPERATURE ON RESISTANCE</p><p>The resistance of a material changes with the change in temperature. The effect of temperature upon resistance varies according to the type of material.</p><p>i) Thus resistance of a conductor (e.g. copper, aluminum) increases with the increase of temperature. The change in resistance is fairly regular for normal range of temperatures.</p><p>ii) The resistance of electrolytes, insulators (e.g. glass, mica, rubber etc.) and semiconductors (e.g. germanium, silicon etc.) decreases with the increase in temperature.</p><p>12.4.1 TEMPERATURE CO-EFFICIENT OF RESISTANCE/RESISTIVITY</p><p>Consider a conductor having resistance R₀ at 0°C and R_T at T °C. It has been found that in the normal range of temperatures, the increase in resistance (R_T − R₀) is</p><p>i) Directly proportional to the initial resistance i.e. \\(R_T-R_0\\propto R_0\\)</p><p>ii) Directly proportional to the rise in temperature i.e. \\(R_T-R_0\\propto T\\)</p><p>Combining the above two relations, we get</p><p>\\(R_T-R_0\\propto R_0T\\)</p><p>\\(R_T-R_0=\\alpha R_0T\\) ...(12.6)</p>",
    "theory": "12.4 EFFECT OF TEMPERATURE ON RESISTANCE\nThe resistance of a material changes with the change in temperature. The effect of temperature upon resistance varies according to the type of material.\ni) Thus resistance of a conductor (e.g. copper, aluminum) increases with the increase of temperature. The change in resistance is fairly regular for normal range of temperatures.\nii) The resistance of electrolytes, insulators (e.g. glass, mica, rubber etc.) and semiconductors (e.g. germanium, silicon etc.) decreases with the increase in temperature.\n12.4.1 TEMPERATURE CO-EFFICIENT OF RESISTANCE/RESISTIVITY\nConsider a conductor having resistance R₀ at 0°C and R_T at T °C. It has been found that in the normal range of temperatures, the increase in resistance (R_T − R₀) is\ni) Directly proportional to the initial resistance i.e. \\(R_T-R_0\\propto R_0\\)\nii) Directly proportional to the rise in temperature i.e. \\(R_T-R_0\\propto T\\)\nCombining the above two relations, we get\n\\(R_T-R_0\\propto R_0T\\)\n\\(R_T-R_0=\\alpha R_0T\\) ...(12.6)",
