    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>Where α is the constant and is called temperature co-efficient of resistance. Its value depends upon the nature of material and temperature.</p><p>Rearranging equation (12.6) we get:</p><p>\\(R_T=R_0(1+\\alpha T)\\) ...(12.7)</p><p>From equation (12.6) we get:</p><p>\\(\\alpha=\\frac{R_T-R_0}{R_0T}\\) ...(12.8)</p><p>Temperature co-efficient of resistance α may be defined as increase in resistance per ohm original resistance per degree rise in temperature.</p><p>The unit of α as derived from the definition is K⁻¹. Since the resistance of metals increases with the rise in temperature, they have positive temperature co-efficient of resistance, while co-efficient α is negative for semiconductor because they show decrease in resistance as the temperature raises. The resistivity or specific resistance of most materials increases linearly with increasing temperature. The equation (12.8) also apply to ρ. At temperature T the resistivity ρ_T of a material is given by</p><p>\\(\\rho_T=\\rho_0(1+\\alpha T)\\) ...(12.9)</p><p>\\(\\alpha=\\frac{\\rho_T-\\rho_0}{\\rho_0T}\\) ...(12.10)</p>",
    "theory": "Where α is the constant and is called temperature co-efficient of resistance. Its value depends upon the nature of material and temperature.\nRearranging equation (12.6) we get:\n\\(R_T=R_0(1+\\alpha T)\\) ...(12.7)\nFrom equation (12.6) we get:\n\\(\\alpha=\\frac{R_T-R_0}{R_0T}\\) ...(12.8)\nTemperature co-efficient of resistance α may be defined as increase in resistance per ohm original resistance per degree rise in temperature.\nThe unit of α as derived from the definition is K⁻¹. Since the resistance of metals increases with the rise in temperature, they have positive temperature co-efficient of resistance, while co-efficient α is negative for semiconductor because they show decrease in resistance as the temperature raises. The resistivity or specific resistance of most materials increases linearly with increasing temperature. The equation (12.8) also apply to ρ. At temperature T the resistivity ρ_T of a material is given by\n\\(\\rho_T=\\rho_0(1+\\alpha T)\\) ...(12.9)\n\\(\\alpha=\\frac{\\rho_T-\\rho_0}{\\rho_0T}\\) ...(12.10)",
