    "title": "Example 12.4 — resistance of a copper conductor",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>EXAMPLE 12.4</p><p>Calculate the resistance of copper conductor having a length of 2 km and a cross-section of 22 mm². Assume the resistivity is 18 × 10⁻⁹ Ω.m.</p><p>GIVEN</p><p>Length \\(L=2\\,\\mathrm{km}=2000\\,\\mathrm m\\); cross-section \\(A=22\\,\\mathrm{mm}^2=22\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm m^2\\); resistivity \\(\\rho=18\\times10^{-9}\\,\\Omega.\\mathrm m\\)</p><p>REQUIRED</p><p>Resistance of copper \\(R=?\\)</p><p>SOLUTION</p><p>The resistance of copper wire of length L, cross-sectional area A and resistivity ρ is given by</p><p>\\(R=\\frac{\\rho L}{A}\\)</p><p>Thus resistance of copper conductor is</p><p>\\(R=18\\times10^{-9}\\times\\frac{2000}{22\\times10^{-6}}\\)</p><p>\\(R=1.64\\,\\Omega\\)</p>",
    "theory": "EXAMPLE 12.4\nCalculate the resistance of copper conductor having a length of 2 km and a cross-section of 22 mm². Assume the resistivity is 18 × 10⁻⁹ Ω.m.\nGIVEN\nLength \\(L=2\\,\\mathrm{km}=2000\\,\\mathrm m\\); cross-section \\(A=22\\,\\mathrm{mm}^2=22\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm m^2\\); resistivity \\(\\rho=18\\times10^{-9}\\,\\Omega.\\mathrm m\\)\nREQUIRED\nResistance of copper \\(R=?\\)\nSOLUTION\nThe resistance of copper wire of length L, cross-sectional area A and resistivity ρ is given by\n\\(R=\\frac{\\rho L}{A}\\)\nThus resistance of copper conductor is\n\\(R=18\\times10^{-9}\\times\\frac{2000}{22\\times10^{-6}}\\)\n\\(R=1.64\\,\\Omega\\)",
