    "title": "12.5 Wire-wound variable resistors — introduction",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>12.5 WIRE-WOUND VARIABLE RESISTORS</p><p>High stability and high accuracy resistors are always wire-wound. It is then enclosed in an insulating cover.</p><p>Generally, nickel chromium is used because of its very small temperature co-efficient of resistance. Wire wound resistors can safely operate at higher temperatures than carbon type resistors. Wire wound variable resistor can be used in two ways.</p><p>i) Rheostats     ii) Potential divider</p>",
