    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>\\(I=\\frac{\\epsilon}{R+r}\\Rightarrow IR=\\epsilon-Ir\\)</p><p>But IR=V, the terminal voltage of the cell</p><p>\\(V=\\epsilon-Ir\\)</p><p>Now, if \\(r=0\\) (OpenCircuit); \\(I=0\\) then \\(V=\\epsilon\\)</p><p>But, when the circuit is closed, the current move through it experiences an associated drop in potential equal to Ir. Thus when a current is flowing through a source, the Potential difference V between the terminals of the source is V=ε-Ir. The Potential V called the terminal voltage, is less than the e.m.f. (ε) because of the term Ir representing the Potential drop across the internal resistance r.</p><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 520 220\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"FIGURE 12.9\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:520px;height:auto\"><rect width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" fill=\"#fff\"/><path d=\"M65 55H455V175H65V55\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"95\" y=\"72\" width=\"100\" height=\"84\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#eff6ff\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M140 91V119M128 119H152M132 127H148\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M200 112l12-14 18 28 18-28 18 28 18-14\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#f59e0b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M295 55V175\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M245 44H355\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M345 38l12 6-12 6\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"104\" y=\"65\" font-size=\"13\">r</text><text x=\"106\" y=\"169\" font-size=\"13\">ε</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"143\" font-size=\"13\">R</text><text x=\"105\" y=\"205\" font-size=\"12\">internal resistance r / emf ε</text><text x=\"390\" y=\"145\" font-size=\"12\">load R</text><text x=\"260\" y=\"33\" font-size=\"13\">current I</text><text x=\"345\" y=\"25\" font-size=\"14\">FIGURE 12.9</text></svg>",
    "theory": "\\(I=\\frac{\\epsilon}{R+r}\\Rightarrow IR=\\epsilon-Ir\\)\nBut IR=V, the terminal voltage of the cell\n\\(V=\\epsilon-Ir\\)\nNow, if \\(r=0\\) (OpenCircuit); \\(I=0\\) then \\(V=\\epsilon\\)\nBut, when the circuit is closed, the current move through it experiences an associated drop in potential equal to Ir. Thus when a current is flowing through a source, the Potential difference V between the terminals of the source is V=ε-Ir. The Potential V called the terminal voltage, is less than the e.m.f. (ε) because of the term Ir representing the Potential drop across the internal resistance r.",
