    "id": "phys12-unit12-kvl-procedure77",
    "title": "Applying Kirchhoff’s laws — potential changes and tips",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>PROCEDURES OF APPLYING KIRCHHOFF’S LAWS FOR PROBLEM SOLUTION</p><p>Moving along the direction of current flow through R₁, the fall in potential is −IR₁.</p><p>Moving along the direction of current flow through R₂, the fall in potential is −IR₂.</p><p>Moving from the negative terminal to the positive terminal, for rise in potential +ε₁.</p><p>Moving from the positive terminal to the negative terminal, for fall in potential −ε₂.</p><p>Thus applying Kirchhoff’s law on Figure 12.14 we get</p><p>\\(\\epsilon_1-IR_1-\\epsilon_2-IR_2=0\\)</p><p>\\(\\Rightarrow\\epsilon_1-\\epsilon_2=IR_1+IR_2\\)</p><p>\\(\\Rightarrow\\epsilon_1-\\epsilon_2=IR_1+IR_2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\sum\\epsilon=\\sum IR\\) ...(12.18)</p><p>The sum of all the emf ε in a loop in a circuit is equal to the sum of all the IR voltage drops in that loop. KVL is in fact, a statement of the law of conservation of energy.</p><p>Tips</p><p>1. The choice of loops is quite arbitrary, but it should be such that each resistance is included at least once in the selected loop. Kirchhoff’s voltage law can be applied to these closed loops to get the desired equations.</p><p>2. The direction in which currents are assumed to flow is not important, since if wrong direction is chosen, it will be indicated by a negative sign in the result.</p><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 540 245\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Voltage change while traversing a circuit\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:540px;height:auto\"><rect width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" fill=\"#fff\"/><rect x=\"15\" y=\"35\" width=\"245\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff7ed\" stroke=\"#c2410c\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M35 70H80l9-10 14 20 14-20 14 20 14-10H235\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M155 52H210\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M202 46l12 6-12 6\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"25\" y=\"31\" font-size=\"12\">R₁ / R₂</text><rect x=\"280\" y=\"35\" width=\"245\" height=\"75\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#eff6ff\" stroke=\"#1d4ed8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M300 70H350M380 70H500\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M350 54V86M365 60V80\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M410 52H480\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M472 46l12 6-12 6\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"310\" y=\"32\" font-size=\"12\">ε₁ / ε₂</text><text x=\"16\" y=\"134\" font-size=\"12\">Voltage change while traversing a circuit</text><text x=\"20\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"12\">Resistor drop</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"158\" font-size=\"12\">Battery rise / Battery fall</text></svg>",
    "theory": "PROCEDURES OF APPLYING KIRCHHOFF’S LAWS FOR PROBLEM SOLUTION\nMoving along the direction of current flow through R₁, the fall in potential is −IR₁.\nMoving along the direction of current flow through R₂, the fall in potential is −IR₂.\nMoving from the negative terminal to the positive terminal, for rise in potential +ε₁.\nMoving from the positive terminal to the negative terminal, for fall in potential −ε₂.\nThus applying Kirchhoff’s law on Figure 12.14 we get\n\\(\\epsilon_1-IR_1-\\epsilon_2-IR_2=0\\)\n\\(\\Rightarrow\\epsilon_1-\\epsilon_2=IR_1+IR_2\\)\n\\(\\Rightarrow\\epsilon_1-\\epsilon_2=IR_1+IR_2\\quad\\text{or}\\quad\\sum\\epsilon=\\sum IR\\) ...(12.18)\nThe sum of all the emf ε in a loop in a circuit is equal to the sum of all the IR voltage drops in that loop. KVL is in fact, a statement of the law of conservation of energy.\nTips\n1. The choice of loops is quite arbitrary, but it should be such that each resistance is included at least once in the selected loop. Kirchhoff’s voltage law can be applied to these closed loops to get the desired equations.\n2. The direction in which currents are assumed to flow is not important, since if wrong direction is chosen, it will be indicated by a negative sign in the result.",
