    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>KEY POINTS</p><p>Steady current: the continuous flow of free electrons.</p><p>Electric current: the net charge flowing through the cross-section area A per unit time. \\(I=\\frac{Q}{t}\\)</p><p>Ohm’s law: the magnitude of the current in metals is proportional to the applied voltage as long as the temperature of the conductor is kept constant. \\(V=IR\\)</p><p>Resistance: the opposition offered by the substance to the flow of free electrons.</p><p>Conductance (G): the reciprocal of resistance of a conductor.</p><p>Temperature co-efficient of resistance: increase in resistance per ohm original resistance per degree rise in temperature.</p><p>Thermistor: a heat sensitive device usually made of a semiconductor material whose resistance changes very rapidly with change of temperature.</p><p>Source of electromotive force: a device which converts non-electrical energy into electrical energy.</p><p>Electric power: the rate at which work is done in an electric circuit.</p><p>Kirchhoff’s law: the algebraic sum of the currents meeting at a junction in an electrical circuit is zero.</p>",
    "theory": "KEY POINTS\nSteady current: the continuous flow of free electrons.\nElectric current: the net charge flowing through the cross-section area A per unit time. \\(I=\\frac{Q}{t}\\)\nOhm’s law: the magnitude of the current in metals is proportional to the applied voltage as long as the temperature of the conductor is kept constant. \\(V=IR\\)\nResistance: the opposition offered by the substance to the flow of free electrons.\nConductance (G): the reciprocal of resistance of a conductor.\nTemperature co-efficient of resistance: increase in resistance per ohm original resistance per degree rise in temperature.\nThermistor: a heat sensitive device usually made of a semiconductor material whose resistance changes very rapidly with change of temperature.\nSource of electromotive force: a device which converts non-electrical energy into electrical energy.\nElectric power: the rate at which work is done in an electric circuit.\nKirchhoff’s law: the algebraic sum of the currents meeting at a junction in an electrical circuit is zero.",
