      "Printed answer: 2 × 10⁻⁵ T.",
      "A. MAGNETIC FIELD DUE TO A CURRENT-CARRYING SOLENOID",
      "A coil wound in the form of a spiral (helix) forms a solenoid. When current is passed through a solenoid, a reasonably uniform magnetic field can be produced. This field is strong along the axis of the solenoid and weaker outside, as shown in Figure 13.12.",
      "Figure 13.12 — (a) Solenoid; (b) iron filings in the magnetic field due to a single loop of coil; (c) iron filings in the magnetic field due to a solenoid.",
      "The magnetic field lines surrounding a loosely wound solenoid show that the lines in its interior are nearly parallel to one another, uniformly distributed, and close together.",
      "For a tightly wound solenoid, the field in the interior space is very uniform and strong. The field lines resemble those of a bar magnet, meaning that the solenoid effectively has north and south poles.",
      "As the length of the solenoid increases, the interior field becomes more uniform and the exterior field becomes weaker, as shown in Figure 13.13.",
      "An ideal solenoid is approached when the turns are closely spaced and its length is much greater than the radius of its turns. In this case, the external field is close to zero and the interior field is uniform over a great volume.",
      "Ampere’s law can be used to obtain a quantitative expression for the interior magnetic field in an ideal solenoid."
