    "paragraphs": [
      "D. DETERMINATION OF CHARGE TO MASS RATIO FOR AN ELECTRON",
      "To determine the charge to mass ratio (q_e/m_e) for an electron, the beam of electron is projected on a perpendicularly arranged uniform magnetic field, such that the electron moves in a circle.",
      "For an electron of charge q_e and mass m_e, the charge to mass ratio (q_e/m_e) may be calculated by balancing the centripetal and magnetic forces.",
      "F_c = F_B",
      "Hence m_e v²/r = q_e vB, or m_e v/r = q_e B.",
      "Rearranging,",
      "q_e/m_e = v/(Br) ...(1)",
      "Practically, the values of B can be calculated easily from the apparatus shown in Figure 13.20, from ringed coils (called Helmholtz’s coils). The radius r can also be calculated by making the electron beam visible through ionization and excitation of the gas molecules. However, the velocity v is determined by calculating the kinetic energy of the electron by applying the potential V of known value. Such that by the law of conservation of energy,",
      "1/2 m_e v² = q_e V, or v² = 2q_eV/m_e",
      "Taking square root on both sides, √(v²) = √(2q_eV/m_e), hence",
      "v = √(2q_eV/m_e) ...(2)",
      "Putting equation 2 in equation 1, we get",
      "q_e/m_e = (1/Br) √(2q_eV/m_e)",
      "Squaring both sides,",
      "(q_e/m_e)² = (1/(Br) √(2q_eV/m_e))², or (q_e/m_e)² = (2V/(B²r²))(q_e/m_e)",
      "q_e/m_e = 2V/(B²r²) ...(13.9)",
      "Thus, knowing the values of potential V, magnetic field B and radius r, the charge to mass ratio can be determined. The accurately known value of e/m for an electron is 1.7588 × 10¹¹ C kg⁻¹.",
      "FIGURE 13.20 — (a) The ringed Helmholtz coils of current-carrying wire produce a nearly uniform magnetic field. (b) The blue ring inside the glass tube is the glow of a beam of electrons that ionize the gas molecules."
    ]
