    "title": "Circular magnetic field around a current-carrying wire",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>When current is passed through a long wire with compass needles placed around it in a circle, the compass needles line up with the magnetic field. Similar observations can be made through a cardboard on which iron filings are sprinkled. These iron filings form concentric circles around the wire, as shown in Figure 13.2.</p><p>FIGURE 13.2 (a) A current-carrying wire with compass needles lining up with the magnetic field in a circular pattern. (b) A current-carrying wire through cardboard shows concentric circles of iron filings around the wire.</p><p>These experiments show the orientation of a magnetic field, which is in the form of a circle around the wire. The direction of the magnetic field can be determined by Right Hand Rule I, as shown in Figure 13.3.</p><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 560 285\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Figure 13.2: compasses and iron filings show circular magnetic field lines around a current-carrying wire.\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:560px;height:auto\"><rect width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" fill=\"#fff\"/><circle cx=\"155\" cy=\"140\" r=\"76\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"155\" cy=\"140\" r=\"48\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M155 140V45M155 140H250M155 140V235M155 140H60\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"155\" cy=\"140\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/><g fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\"><circle cx=\"88\" cy=\"83\" r=\"15\"/><circle cx=\"222\" cy=\"83\" r=\"15\"/><circle cx=\"88\" cy=\"197\" r=\"15\"/><circle cx=\"222\" cy=\"197\" r=\"15\"/></g><path d=\"M77 83h21M211 83h21M77 197h21M211 197h21\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\">(a) compass needles</text><circle cx=\"405\" cy=\"140\" r=\"76\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"405\" cy=\"140\" r=\"53\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"405\" cy=\"140\" r=\"30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M405 58V222\" stroke=\"#7c2d12\" stroke-width=\"8\"/><circle cx=\"405\" cy=\"95\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#b45309\"/><circle cx=\"382\" cy=\"130\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#b45309\"/><circle cx=\"430\" cy=\"170\" r=\"3\" fill=\"#b45309\"/><text x=\"353\" y=\"270\" font-size=\"14\">(b) iron filings</text></svg>",
    "theory": "When current is passed through a long wire with compass needles placed around it in a circle, the compass needles line up with the magnetic field. Similar observations can be made through a cardboard on which iron filings are sprinkled. These iron filings form concentric circles around the wire, as shown in Figure 13.2.\nFIGURE 13.2 (a) A current-carrying wire with compass needles lining up with the magnetic field in a circular pattern. (b) A current-carrying wire through cardboard shows concentric circles of iron filings around the wire.\nThese experiments show the orientation of a magnetic field, which is in the form of a circle around the wire. The direction of the magnetic field can be determined by Right Hand Rule I, as shown in Figure 13.3.",
