    "title": "Direction of magnetic force and tesla unit",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>As force is a vector quantity, its direction should also be determined.</p><p>The direction of magnetic force F_B on a wire can be determined by Right Hand Rule II and Fleming’s left-hand rule, as shown in Figures 13.6 and 13.7 respectively.</p><p>The magnetic induction B will be one tesla if a 1 m long wire carrying a current of 1 A is placed perpendicular to the field and the force between them is 1 N. In SI, 1 T = 1 N A⁻¹ m⁻¹. In CGS, 1 G = 10⁻⁴ Tesla.</p><p>1 T = 1 N/(A m)</p>",
    "theory": "As force is a vector quantity, its direction should also be determined.\nThe direction of magnetic force F_B on a wire can be determined by Right Hand Rule II and Fleming’s left-hand rule, as shown in Figures 13.6 and 13.7 respectively.\nThe magnetic induction B will be one tesla if a 1 m long wire carrying a current of 1 A is placed perpendicular to the field and the force between them is 1 N. In SI, 1 T = 1 N A⁻¹ m⁻¹. In CGS, 1 G = 10⁻⁴ Tesla.\n1 T = 1 N/(A m)",
