    "id": "phys13-example131-force-wire93",
    "title": "Example 13.1 — magnetic force on a wire",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>EXAMPLE 13.1 — MAGNETIC FORCE ON WIRE</p><p>A wire carrying 2 A current and has length of 10 cm between the poles of a magnet is kept at an angle of 30° to the uniform field of 0.6 T. Find the force acting on the wire?</p><p>GIVEN</p><p>Current I = 2 A</p><p>Length L = 10 cm = 0.01 m</p><p>Magnetic field B = 0.6 T</p><p>Angle θ = 30°</p><p>REQUIRED</p><p>Magnetic force F_B = ?</p><p>SOLUTION</p><p>The force on a current-carrying wire in a magnetic field is given by</p><p>F_B = ILB sin θ</p><p>Putting values,</p><p>F_B = 2 A × 0.1 m × 0.6 N/(A m) × sin 30°</p><p>therefore</p><p>F_B = 0.06 N</p><p>Answer</p>",
    "theory": "EXAMPLE 13.1 — MAGNETIC FORCE ON WIRE\nA wire carrying 2 A current and has length of 10 cm between the poles of a magnet is kept at an angle of 30° to the uniform field of 0.6 T. Find the force acting on the wire?\nGIVEN\nCurrent I = 2 A\nLength L = 10 cm = 0.01 m\nMagnetic field B = 0.6 T\nAngle θ = 30°\nREQUIRED\nMagnetic force F_B = ?\nSOLUTION\nThe force on a current-carrying wire in a magnetic field is given by\nF_B = ILB sin θ\nPutting values,\nF_B = 2 A × 0.1 m × 0.6 N/(A m) × sin 30°\ntherefore\nF_B = 0.06 N\nAnswer",
