    "title": "13.3 Magnetic flux — area orientation and curved surfaces",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>13.3 MAGNETIC FLUX</p><p>The scalar product of magnetic induction B and the area A is called magnetic flux Φ_B.</p><p>Φ_B = B⃗ · A⃗ = BA cos θ</p><p>Magnetic induction tells us how close together magnetic field lines are, as it tells us the strength of the magnetic field. The number of magnetic field lines passing through a surface placed perpendicular to it is called magnetic flux.</p><p>A. Maximum Flux: If area vector A is parallel to the magnetic field B, then the maximum number of magnetic field lines will pass through the surface. Therefore magnetic flux will be maximum.</p><p>Φ_B = BA cos 0°; since cos 0° = 1, therefore Φ_B = BA ...(1)</p><p>B. Minimum Flux: If area vector A is perpendicular to magnetic field B, then no magnetic field lines will pass through the surface. Therefore magnetic flux will be zero or minimum.</p><p>Φ_B = BA cos 90°; since cos 90° = 0, therefore Φ_B = 0 ...(2)</p><p>C. Flux at any Angle: If area vector A makes any angle with magnetic field B, then magnetic flux will depend upon the angle θ between them.</p><p>Φ_B = BA cos θ ...(3)</p><p>FIGURE 13.8 — Magnetic flux</p><p>MAGNETIC FLUX THROUGH CURVED OR NON-UNIFORM MAGNETIC FIELD</p><p>If the magnetic field is non-uniform or the area is curved, then we divide the whole curved surface into small patches, each of area ΔA, such that they are approximately flat and the magnetic field is almost uniform over each patch, as shown in Figure 13.9.</p><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 620 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Figure 13.8: maximum, zero and angle-dependent magnetic flux through surfaces.\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:620px;height:auto\"><rect width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" fill=\"#fff\"/><g stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"3\"><path d=\"M25 74H185M25 105H185M25 136H185M25 167H185M25 198H185\"/><path d=\"M225 74H385M225 105H385M225 136H385M225 167H385M225 198H385\"/><path d=\"M425 74H595M425 105H595M425 136H595M425 167H595M425 198H595\"/></g><path d=\"M80 82L142 75 155 198 92 205Z\" fill=\"#d8b4fe\" stroke=\"#6b21a8\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M300 112L355 115 350 169 295 166Z\" fill=\"#d8b4fe\" stroke=\"#6b21a8\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M490 82L555 96 528 198 463 184Z\" fill=\"#d8b4fe\" stroke=\"#6b21a8\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M155 140H202M323 140V80M515 140L556 83\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M194 132l12 8-12 8M315 92l8-12 8 12M546 88l13-7-2 15\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"22\" y=\"35\" font-size=\"14\">(a) Φ_B = BA</text><text x=\"221\" y=\"35\" font-size=\"14\">(b) Φ_B = 0</text><text x=\"420\" y=\"35\" font-size=\"14\">(c) Φ_B = BA cos θ</text><text x=\"25\" y=\"255\" font-size=\"13\">Magnetic field B</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"255\" font-size=\"13\">Area vector A</text><text x=\"455\" y=\"255\" font-size=\"13\">Magnetic flux</text></svg>",
    "theory": "13.3 MAGNETIC FLUX\nThe scalar product of magnetic induction B and the area A is called magnetic flux Φ_B.\nΦ_B = B⃗ · A⃗ = BA cos θ\nMagnetic induction tells us how close together magnetic field lines are, as it tells us the strength of the magnetic field. The number of magnetic field lines passing through a surface placed perpendicular to it is called magnetic flux.\nA. Maximum Flux: If area vector A is parallel to the magnetic field B, then the maximum number of magnetic field lines will pass through the surface. Therefore magnetic flux will be maximum.\nΦ_B = BA cos 0°; since cos 0° = 1, therefore Φ_B = BA ...(1)\nB. Minimum Flux: If area vector A is perpendicular to magnetic field B, then no magnetic field lines will pass through the surface. Therefore magnetic flux will be zero or minimum.\nΦ_B = BA cos 90°; since cos 90° = 0, therefore Φ_B = 0 ...(2)\nC. Flux at any Angle: If area vector A makes any angle with magnetic field B, then magnetic flux will depend upon the angle θ between them.\nΦ_B = BA cos θ ...(3)\nFIGURE 13.8 — Magnetic flux\nMAGNETIC FLUX THROUGH CURVED OR NON-UNIFORM MAGNETIC FIELD\nIf the magnetic field is non-uniform or the area is curved, then we divide the whole curved surface into small patches, each of area ΔA, such that they are approximately flat and the magnetic field is almost uniform over each patch, as shown in Figure 13.9.",
