    "id": "phys13-circular-motion-cyclotron-frequency104-105",
    "title": "Circular motion of a charged particle in a uniform magnetic field",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>C. CIRCULAR MOTION OF CHARGE PARTICLE IN UNIFORM MAGNETIC FIELD</p><p>The path of a charged particle moving in a plane perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field is a circle. Let us consider a positively charged particle moving in a uniform magnetic field with its initial velocity perpendicular to the field. Let us assume the direction of the magnetic field is into the page as in Figure 13.19. As the particle changes the direction of its velocity in response to the magnetic force, the magnetic force remains perpendicular to the velocity.</p><p>QUIZ?</p><p>1. Why is wheat flour usually passed near a magnet before being packed?</p><p>2. Why does a steel ship become magnetized as it is constructed?</p><p>FIGURE 13.19 — Circular trajectory of charged particle in magnetic field.</p><p>The particle moves in a circle because the magnetic force F_B is perpendicular to v and B and has a constant magnitude qvB. The centripetal force F_c is provided by magnetic force F_B; therefore,</p><p>F_c = F_B ...(1)</p><p>For a particle of mass m moving with velocity v in a circle of radius r, the centripetal force F_c is</p><p>F_c = mv²/r ...(2)</p><p>If the charge on the particle is +q and it is moving with velocity v perpendicular to a magnetic field of strength B, the magnetic force F_B is maximum, which is</p><p>F_B = qvB ...(3)</p><p>Putting equation 2 and equation 3 in equation 1, we get</p><p>mv²/r = qvB; therefore mv/r = qB ...(4)</p><p>The relation between linear and angular velocity is v = rω; therefore equation 4 can be written as</p><p>mrω/r = qB, or mω = qB; rearranging, ω = qB/m ...(5)</p><p>From the above equation, we can determine the time period T of the charged particle.</p><p>T = 2π/ω = 2π/(qB/m) = 2πm/qB, since f = 1/T</p><p>Therefore f = qB/(2πm) ...(13.8)</p><p>This is often called the cyclotron frequency of a particle in a field because this is the frequency at which particles revolve in a cyclotron.</p><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 680 360\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Figure 13.19: positively charged particle circular trajectory in a uniform magnetic field directed into the page.\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:680px;height:auto\"><rect width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" fill=\"#fff\"/><circle cx=\"330\" cy=\"180\" r=\"108\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#d4a72c\" stroke-width=\"3\" stroke-dasharray=\"7 4\"/><g stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\"><path d=\"M229 59l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M229 109l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M229 159l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M229 209l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M229 259l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M279 59l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M279 109l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M279 159l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M279 209l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M279 259l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M329 59l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M329 109l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M329 159l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M329 209l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M329 259l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M379 59l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M379 109l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M379 159l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M379 209l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M379 259l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M429 59l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M429 109l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M429 159l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M429 209l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/><path d=\"M429 259l12 12m0-12l-12 12\"/></g><g fill=\"#16a34a\" stroke=\"#166534\" stroke-width=\"2\"><circle cx=\"330\" cy=\"72\" r=\"15\"/><circle cx=\"424\" cy=\"235\" r=\"15\"/><circle cx=\"236\" cy=\"235\" r=\"15\"/></g><g fill=\"#fff\" font-size=\"14\"><text x=\"323\" y=\"77\">+</text><text x=\"417\" y=\"240\">+</text><text x=\"229\" y=\"240\">+</text></g><path d=\"M330 72L380 100M424 235L405 180M236 235L255 180\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M372 93l13 10-16 2M398 187l9-15 6 16M253 190l-4-17 14 9\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/><path d=\"M330 72L350 180M424 235L330 180M236 235L330 180\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M350 180L365 160M330 180L311 165M330 180L348 197\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M360 165l7-13 4 15M318 168l-14-5 10 13M345 194l7 13-15-6\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"354\" y=\"169\">r</text><text x=\"383\" y=\"92\">v</text><text x=\"368\" y=\"153\">F_B</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"325\" font-size=\"13\">B into the page · Circular trajectory of charged particle in magnetic field</text></svg>",
    "theory": "C. CIRCULAR MOTION OF CHARGE PARTICLE IN UNIFORM MAGNETIC FIELD\nThe path of a charged particle moving in a plane perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field is a circle. Let us consider a positively charged particle moving in a uniform magnetic field with its initial velocity perpendicular to the field. Let us assume the direction of the magnetic field is into the page as in Figure 13.19. As the particle changes the direction of its velocity in response to the magnetic force, the magnetic force remains perpendicular to the velocity.\nQUIZ?\n1. Why is wheat flour usually passed near a magnet before being packed?\n2. Why does a steel ship become magnetized as it is constructed?\nFIGURE 13.19 — Circular trajectory of charged particle in magnetic field.\nThe particle moves in a circle because the magnetic force F_B is perpendicular to v and B and has a constant magnitude qvB. The centripetal force F_c is provided by magnetic force F_B; therefore,\nF_c = F_B ...(1)\nFor a particle of mass m moving with velocity v in a circle of radius r, the centripetal force F_c is\nF_c = mv²/r ...(2)\nIf the charge on the particle is +q and it is moving with velocity v perpendicular to a magnetic field of strength B, the magnetic force F_B is maximum, which is\nF_B = qvB ...(3)\nPutting equation 2 and equation 3 in equation 1, we get\nmv²/r = qvB; therefore mv/r = qB ...(4)\nThe relation between linear and angular velocity is v = rω; therefore equation 4 can be written as\nmrω/r = qB, or mω = qB; rearranging, ω = qB/m ...(5)\nFrom the above equation, we can determine the time period T of the charged particle.\nT = 2π/ω = 2π/(qB/m) = 2πm/qB, since f = 1/T\nTherefore f = qB/(2πm) ...(13.8)\nThis is often called the cyclotron frequency of a particle in a field because this is the frequency at which particles revolve in a cyclotron.",
