    "id": "phys13-example134-electron-period108",
    "title": "Example 13.4 — electron orbital period",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>EXAMPLE 13.4 — TIME PERIOD FOR ELECTRON IN MAGNETIC FIELD</p><p>The path of an electron in a uniform magnetic field of flux density 1.0 × 10⁻² T in a vacuum is a circle of radius 1 cm. Calculate the period of its orbit.</p><p>GIVEN</p><p>Radius r = 1 cm = 1 × 10⁻² m; magnetic field B = 1.0 × 10⁻² T; electron charge q_e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C; electron mass m_e = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg.</p><p>REQUIRED — Time period T = ?</p><p>SOLUTION</p><p>From the circular trajectory of a charged particle, the time period is</p><p>T = 2πm/(qB)</p><p>Putting in the given values,</p><p>T = (2 × 3.14 × 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg)/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C × 1.0 × 10⁻² T)</p><p>T = 3.57 × 10⁻⁹ s = 3.57 ns</p>",
    "theory": "EXAMPLE 13.4 — TIME PERIOD FOR ELECTRON IN MAGNETIC FIELD\nThe path of an electron in a uniform magnetic field of flux density 1.0 × 10⁻² T in a vacuum is a circle of radius 1 cm. Calculate the period of its orbit.\nGIVEN\nRadius r = 1 cm = 1 × 10⁻² m; magnetic field B = 1.0 × 10⁻² T; electron charge q_e = 1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C; electron mass m_e = 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg.\nREQUIRED — Time period T = ?\nSOLUTION\nFrom the circular trajectory of a charged particle, the time period is\nT = 2πm/(qB)\nPutting in the given values,\nT = (2 × 3.14 × 9.11 × 10⁻³¹ kg)/(1.6 × 10⁻¹⁹ C × 1.0 × 10⁻² T)\nT = 3.57 × 10⁻⁹ s = 3.57 ns",
