    "id": "phys13-moving-coil-galvanometer-eq1313-112-113",
    "title": "Moving-coil galvanometer and current sensitivity",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>13.8 GALVANOMETER</p><p>A device used for detection and measurement of small electric currents is called a galvanometer.</p><p>The principle of moving-coil galvanometer is the torque on a current-carrying coil in a magnetic field.</p><p>Modern galvanometers are moving-coil galvanometers, which are also called d’Arsonval galvanometers.</p><p>A moving-coil galvanometer consists of a pivoted coil wrapped around a soft-iron core, placed in the concave poles of a permanent magnet as shown in Figure 13.25. The poles of the magnet are made concave to make the field stronger and radial, such that magnetic force is perpendicular and torque is always maximum. The soft-iron core concentrates the magnetic field (due to its high permeability), thus giving more inertia to the coil. A light pointer or mirror is used to measure this angular deflection on a well-calibrated scale.</p><p>The coil is also connected to a spring on the other side, which produces a restoring torque, bringing the pointer to its initial state when current flow stops.</p><p>The net torque on the coil is the sum of the torque produced by magnetic force and the restoring torque due to the spring. Let the magnetic torque be counterclockwise, while the spring-coil torque is opposite and is taken as negative. Thus τ⃗_net = τ⃗_mag + (−τ⃗_res) ...(1)</p><p>When the pointer comes to rest, equilibrium is achieved and the net torque is zero, τ⃗_net = 0 ...(2)</p><p>When current is applied, a magnetic field B sets up in the coil and iron-core assembly, which interacts with a permanent magnet, producing a torque.</p><p>τ⃗_mag = NIAB sin θ</p><p>Here N is the number of turns in the coil. Since the magnetic field is radial, the area vector is perpendicular to the field, θ = 90° and sin 90° = 1; therefore τ⃗_mag = NIAB ...(3)</p><p>The restoring torque in rotational form is τ⃗_res = cφ ...(4)</p><p>Here c is the spring constant (restoring torque per unit twist) in rotation and φ is the angular displacement. Putting equations 2, 3 and 4 in equation 1, we get 0 = NIAB − cφ, or NIAB = cφ.</p><p>I = cφ/(NAB) ...(13.13)</p><p>The factor c/NAB is constant, so galvanometer sensitivity can be adjusted by changing deflection angle φ, which is done by changing the factor c/NAB. Thus current I is proportional to angular displacement φ: I ∝ φ.</p><p>This relation shows that angular deflection can be used to detect and measure small currents. The cylindrical soft-iron assembly in the galvanometer core rotates in proportion to the current.</p><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 700 440\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Figure 13.25: moving-coil galvanometer with concave permanent-magnet poles, soft-iron core, coil, spring and pointer.\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:700px;height:auto\"><rect width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" fill=\"#fff\"/><path d=\"M100 95Q350 5 600 95L555 135Q350 70 145 135Z\" fill=\"#e2e8f0\" stroke=\"#64748b\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M145 135Q350 70 555 135\" stroke=\"#111827\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M170 105L195 102M210 96L232 91M250 88L270 83M290 80L310 76M330 75V96M370 75L390 76M410 80L430 84M450 88L470 93M490 97L515 104\" stroke=\"#111827\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"305\" y=\"160\" width=\"90\" height=\"155\" rx=\"38\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#b45309\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><rect x=\"331\" y=\"178\" width=\"38\" height=\"120\" rx=\"18\" fill=\"#a8a29e\" stroke=\"#57534e\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M350 170C300 120 400 120 350 170M350 308C300 358 400 358 350 308\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"6\"/><path d=\"M350 180L510 110\" stroke=\"#111827\" stroke-width=\"6\"/><circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"170\" r=\"9\" fill=\"#111827\"/><path d=\"M280 210Q260 240 280 270M420 210Q440 240 420 270\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"98\" y=\"60\">Scale</text><text x=\"224\" y=\"175\">spring</text><text x=\"500\" y=\"100\">pointer</text><text x=\"72\" y=\"325\">permanent magnet</text><text x=\"108\" y=\"265\">soft iron core</text><text x=\"405\" y=\"282\">pivoted coil</text><text x=\"485\" y=\"230\">magnetic field</text><text x=\"285\" y=\"420\">Galvanometer · Figure 13.25</text></svg>",
    "theory": "13.8 GALVANOMETER\nA device used for detection and measurement of small electric currents is called a galvanometer.\nThe principle of moving-coil galvanometer is the torque on a current-carrying coil in a magnetic field.\nModern galvanometers are moving-coil galvanometers, which are also called d’Arsonval galvanometers.\nA moving-coil galvanometer consists of a pivoted coil wrapped around a soft-iron core, placed in the concave poles of a permanent magnet as shown in Figure 13.25. The poles of the magnet are made concave to make the field stronger and radial, such that magnetic force is perpendicular and torque is always maximum. The soft-iron core concentrates the magnetic field (due to its high permeability), thus giving more inertia to the coil. A light pointer or mirror is used to measure this angular deflection on a well-calibrated scale.\nThe coil is also connected to a spring on the other side, which produces a restoring torque, bringing the pointer to its initial state when current flow stops.\nThe net torque on the coil is the sum of the torque produced by magnetic force and the restoring torque due to the spring. Let the magnetic torque be counterclockwise, while the spring-coil torque is opposite and is taken as negative. Thus τ⃗_net = τ⃗_mag + (−τ⃗_res) ...(1)\nWhen the pointer comes to rest, equilibrium is achieved and the net torque is zero, τ⃗_net = 0 ...(2)\nWhen current is applied, a magnetic field B sets up in the coil and iron-core assembly, which interacts with a permanent magnet, producing a torque.\nτ⃗_mag = NIAB sin θ\nHere N is the number of turns in the coil. Since the magnetic field is radial, the area vector is perpendicular to the field, θ = 90° and sin 90° = 1; therefore τ⃗_mag = NIAB ...(3)\nThe restoring torque in rotational form is τ⃗_res = cφ ...(4)\nHere c is the spring constant (restoring torque per unit twist) in rotation and φ is the angular displacement. Putting equations 2, 3 and 4 in equation 1, we get 0 = NIAB − cφ, or NIAB = cφ.\nI = cφ/(NAB) ...(13.13)\nThe factor c/NAB is constant, so galvanometer sensitivity can be adjusted by changing deflection angle φ, which is done by changing the factor c/NAB. Thus current I is proportional to angular displacement φ: I ∝ φ.\nThis relation shows that angular deflection can be used to detect and measure small currents. The cylindrical soft-iron assembly in the galvanometer core rotates in proportion to the current.",
