    "id": "phys13-galvanometer-to-ammeter-eq1314-114-115",
    "title": "Converting a galvanometer into an ammeter",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>A. CONVERSION OF GALVANOMETER INTO AMMETER</p><p>An ammeter is a measuring instrument used to measure electric current in a circuit.</p><p>A galvanometer is a very sensitive device; even a small current may show full-scale deflection. An ammeter is a modified form of galvanometer used to measure large current.</p><p>A galvanometer is converted into an ammeter by connecting a low resistance, called a shunt, in parallel with it. With the shunt connected, only a small fraction of current flows through the galvanometer; the remaining large portion passes through the shunt resistance, as shown in Figure 13.26.</p><p>FIGURE 13.26 — (a) Ammeter. (b) Internal circuit with shunt resistance R_s.</p><p>Let R_s represent the shunt resistance and R_g the internal resistance of the galvanometer. I_g is the current that produces full-scale deflection in the galvanometer, and I is the total current through the ammeter. Since the shunt is connected in parallel with the galvanometer, the potential is the same across both resistances.</p><p>V_s = V_g ...(1)</p><p>By Ohm’s law, the potential drop across R_g is V_g = I_g R_g ...(2). Similarly, the potential drop across R_s is V_s = I_s R_s ...(3).</p><p>Here I_s = I − I_g; therefore V_s = (I − I_g)R_s. Putting equations 2 and 3 in equation 1 gives (I − I_g)R_s = I_gR_g; hence R_s = I_gR_g/(I − I_g) ...(13.14).</p><p>The shunt converts a galvanometer into an ammeter that gives full-scale deflection when current I passes through the main circuit. An ammeter must have small resistance so that the current change caused by it is small. Ideally, an ammeter should have no resistance. Note: An ammeter must be connected in series with the circuit.</p><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 700 340\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Figure 13.26/13.27: translated ammeter or voltmeter internal circuit diagram.\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:700px;height:auto\"><rect width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" fill=\"#fff\"/><circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"165\" r=\"112\" fill=\"#fef08a\" stroke=\"#111827\" stroke-width=\"5\" stroke-dasharray=\"8 5\"/><circle cx=\"350\" cy=\"130\" r=\"29\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#111827\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"340\" y=\"137\" font-size=\"18\">G</text><path d=\"M200 130H320M380 130H490M200 130V235H490V130\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M270 235l15-10 15 20 15-20 15 20 15-20 15 10\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#111827\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"207\">R_s</text><text x=\"310\" y=\"87\">I_g</text><text x=\"400\" y=\"115\">I_s</text><text x=\"180\" y=\"115\">I</text><text x=\"300\" y=\"40\">Ammeter</text></svg>",
    "theory": "A. CONVERSION OF GALVANOMETER INTO AMMETER\nAn ammeter is a measuring instrument used to measure electric current in a circuit.\nA galvanometer is a very sensitive device; even a small current may show full-scale deflection. An ammeter is a modified form of galvanometer used to measure large current.\nA galvanometer is converted into an ammeter by connecting a low resistance, called a shunt, in parallel with it. With the shunt connected, only a small fraction of current flows through the galvanometer; the remaining large portion passes through the shunt resistance, as shown in Figure 13.26.\nFIGURE 13.26 — (a) Ammeter. (b) Internal circuit with shunt resistance R_s.\nLet R_s represent the shunt resistance and R_g the internal resistance of the galvanometer. I_g is the current that produces full-scale deflection in the galvanometer, and I is the total current through the ammeter. Since the shunt is connected in parallel with the galvanometer, the potential is the same across both resistances.\nV_s = V_g ...(1)\nBy Ohm’s law, the potential drop across R_g is V_g = I_g R_g ...(2). Similarly, the potential drop across R_s is V_s = I_s R_s ...(3).\nHere I_s = I − I_g; therefore V_s = (I − I_g)R_s. Putting equations 2 and 3 in equation 1 gives (I − I_g)R_s = I_gR_g; hence R_s = I_gR_g/(I − I_g) ...(13.14).\nThe shunt converts a galvanometer into an ammeter that gives full-scale deflection when current I passes through the main circuit. An ammeter must have small resistance so that the current change caused by it is small. Ideally, an ammeter should have no resistance. Note: An ammeter must be connected in series with the circuit.",
