    "title": "Example 13.6 — ammeter and voltmeter ranges",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>EXAMPLE 13.6 — CONVERSION OF GALVANOMETER</p><p>A galvanometer has a resistance of 100 ohms and gives full-scale deflection on 1 mA current. How can it be converted into (a) an ammeter of range 10 A, and (b) a voltmeter of range 10 V?</p><p>GIVEN — Galvanometer resistance R_g = 100 Ω; current through galvanometer I_g = 1 × 10⁻³ A; current range I = 10 A; voltage range V = 10 V.</p><p>REQUIRED — Shunt resistance R_s = ?; high resistance R_h = ?</p><p>(a) To convert the galvanometer into an ammeter of range I = 10 A, connect shunt resistance R_s in parallel. R_s = I_gR_g/(I − I_g). Putting values: R_s = (1 × 10⁻³ A × 100 Ω)/(10 A − 1 × 10⁻³ A). Therefore R_s = 0.0100 Ω. Answer.</p><p>(b) To convert the galvanometer into a voltmeter of range V = 10 V, connect a high resistance R_h in series. R_h = V/I_g − R_g. Putting values: R_h = 10 V/(1 × 10⁻³ A) − 100 Ω. Therefore R_h = 9,900 Ω. Answer.</p>",
    "theory": "EXAMPLE 13.6 — CONVERSION OF GALVANOMETER\nA galvanometer has a resistance of 100 ohms and gives full-scale deflection on 1 mA current. How can it be converted into (a) an ammeter of range 10 A, and (b) a voltmeter of range 10 V?\nGIVEN — Galvanometer resistance R_g = 100 Ω; current through galvanometer I_g = 1 × 10⁻³ A; current range I = 10 A; voltage range V = 10 V.\nREQUIRED — Shunt resistance R_s = ?; high resistance R_h = ?\n(a) To convert the galvanometer into an ammeter of range I = 10 A, connect shunt resistance R_s in parallel. R_s = I_gR_g/(I − I_g). Putting values: R_s = (1 × 10⁻³ A × 100 Ω)/(10 A − 1 × 10⁻³ A). Therefore R_s = 0.0100 Ω. Answer.\n(b) To convert the galvanometer into a voltmeter of range V = 10 V, connect a high resistance R_h in series. R_h = V/I_g − R_g. Putting values: R_h = 10 V/(1 × 10⁻³ A) − 100 Ω. Therefore R_h = 9,900 Ω. Answer.",
