    "id": "phys13-mri-information-and-coded-figure-118",
    "title": "Magnetic resonance imaging — information panel",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>FOR YOUR INFORMATION — MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING</p><p>Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) uses a combination of a strong magnetic field and radio waves to produce high-resolution images of the interior of the body.</p><p>The human body is composed primarily of fat and water and is made up mostly of hydrogen atoms. Hydrogen protons within the body align with the magnetic field.</p><p>By applying short radio-frequency (RF) pulses to a specific anatomical slice, the protons in the slice absorb energy at this resonant frequency, causing them to spin perpendicular to the magnetic field. As the protons relax back into alignment with the magnetic field, a signal is received by an RF coil. This signal acts as an antenna and is processed by a computer to produce diagnostic images of areas of the body.</p><p>FIGURE — Radio-frequency coil; gradient coil; magnet.</p><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 720 400\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Coded schematic of magnetic resonance imaging magnet, gradient coil and radio-frequency coil.\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:720px;height:auto\"><rect width=\"100%\" height=\"100%\" fill=\"#fff\"/><path d=\"M190 95H530V305H190Z\" fill=\"#ddd6fe\" stroke=\"#6d28d9\" stroke-width=\"24\"/><path d=\"M235 125H485V275H235Z\" fill=\"#fb923c\" stroke=\"#c2410c\" stroke-width=\"17\"/><rect x=\"120\" y=\"175\" width=\"440\" height=\"48\" rx=\"24\" fill=\"#e0f2fe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"6\"/><path d=\"M90 199H580M125 190V210M535 190V210\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"560\" y=\"100\">Magnet</text><text x=\"480\" y=\"145\">Gradient coil</text><text x=\"160\" y=\"165\">RF coil</text></svg>",
    "theory": "FOR YOUR INFORMATION — MAGNETIC RESONANCE IMAGING\nMagnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) uses a combination of a strong magnetic field and radio waves to produce high-resolution images of the interior of the body.\nThe human body is composed primarily of fat and water and is made up mostly of hydrogen atoms. Hydrogen protons within the body align with the magnetic field.\nBy applying short radio-frequency (RF) pulses to a specific anatomical slice, the protons in the slice absorb energy at this resonant frequency, causing them to spin perpendicular to the magnetic field. As the protons relax back into alignment with the magnetic field, a signal is received by an RF coil. This signal acts as an antenna and is processed by a computer to produce diagnostic images of areas of the body.\nFIGURE — Radio-frequency coil; gradient coil; magnet.",
