    "id": "phys13-unit13-key-points-121",
    "title": "Unit 13 key points",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>KEY POINTS</p><p>Force on a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field: A current-carrying wire has a magnetic field around it. When this field interacts with an external magnetic field, a force acts on the wire, given by F = I L × B.</p><p>Magnetic flux: The scalar product of magnetic induction vector B and area ΔA; mathematically ΔΦ_B = B⃗ · ΔA⃗ = B ΔA cos θ.</p><p>Ampere’s Law: This law relates the integrated magnetic field in a loop around a current-carrying wire to the current passing through the wire: ∑ B⃗ · Δl⃗ = μ₀ I.</p><p>The magnetic field of a solenoid: B = μ₀ n I.</p><p>Force on a charged particle in a magnetic field: The force on a charged particle results from interaction between the external magnetic field and the magnetic field created by the moving charge.</p><p>Velocity selector: A device that allows only particles with a certain specific speed to pass through it (thus permitting particles only with selected speeds).</p><p>Torque on a current-carrying coil in a magnetic field: τ = NIAB sin θ.</p><p>Galvanometer: A device used for detection and measurement of small electric currents.</p><p>Shunt resistance: The low resistance connected in parallel with a galvanometer, mathematically R_s = I_gR_g/(I − I_g).</p><p>High resistance: The high-value resistance connected in series with a galvanometer, mathematically R_h = V/I_g − R_g.</p><p>AVO meter: A single device that can measure current, voltage and resistance.</p>",
    "theory": "KEY POINTS\nForce on a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field: A current-carrying wire has a magnetic field around it. When this field interacts with an external magnetic field, a force acts on the wire, given by F = I L × B.\nMagnetic flux: The scalar product of magnetic induction vector B and area ΔA; mathematically ΔΦ_B = B⃗ · ΔA⃗ = B ΔA cos θ.\nAmpere’s Law: This law relates the integrated magnetic field in a loop around a current-carrying wire to the current passing through the wire: ∑ B⃗ · Δl⃗ = μ₀ I.\nThe magnetic field of a solenoid: B = μ₀ n I.\nForce on a charged particle in a magnetic field: The force on a charged particle results from interaction between the external magnetic field and the magnetic field created by the moving charge.\nVelocity selector: A device that allows only particles with a certain specific speed to pass through it (thus permitting particles only with selected speeds).\nTorque on a current-carrying coil in a magnetic field: τ = NIAB sin θ.\nGalvanometer: A device used for detection and measurement of small electric currents.\nShunt resistance: The low resistance connected in parallel with a galvanometer, mathematically R_s = I_gR_g/(I − I_g).\nHigh resistance: The high-value resistance connected in series with a galvanometer, mathematically R_h = V/I_g − R_g.\nAVO meter: A single device that can measure current, voltage and resistance.",
