    "content": "<p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS</p><p>1. A compass needle is deflected when a charged plastic rod is held near it. What is the origin of the force that produces the deflection?</p><p>2. What is the difference between permeability and permittivity?</p><p>3. Can objects in rotational motion create a magnetic field? Provide an example.</p><p>4. An electron and a proton are projected with the same velocity normal to a magnetic field. Which suffers greater deflection? Why?</p><p>5. A charged particle moves in a straight line through a particular region of space. Could there be a non-zero magnetic field in this region?</p><p>6. If a current is passed through an unstretched spring, will the spring contract or expand?</p><p>7. How can neutrons be accelerated in a cyclotron?</p><p>8. A current-carrying loop, free to turn, is placed in a uniform magnetic field B. What will be its orientation relative to B in equilibrium?</p><p>9. How does a current-carrying coil behave like a bar magnet?</p>",
    "theory": "CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS\n1. A compass needle is deflected when a charged plastic rod is held near it. What is the origin of the force that produces the deflection?\n2. What is the difference between permeability and permittivity?\n3. Can objects in rotational motion create a magnetic field? Provide an example.\n4. An electron and a proton are projected with the same velocity normal to a magnetic field. Which suffers greater deflection? Why?\n5. A charged particle moves in a straight line through a particular region of space. Could there be a non-zero magnetic field in this region?\n6. If a current is passed through an unstretched spring, will the spring contract or expand?\n7. How can neutrons be accelerated in a cyclotron?\n8. A current-carrying loop, free to turn, is placed in a uniform magnetic field B. What will be its orientation relative to B in equilibrium?\n9. How does a current-carrying coil behave like a bar magnet?",
