    "id": "phys13-exercise-numerical-problems1-10-124-125",
    "title": "Unit 13 exercise — numerical problems 1–10",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>NUMERICAL PROBLEMS</p><p>1. At what distance from a long straight wire carrying a current of 10 A is the magnetic field equal to Earth’s magnetic field of 5 × 10⁻⁵ T? (Answer: 0.04 m)</p><p>2. A long solenoid having 1000 turns uniformly distributed over a length of 0.5 m produces a magnetic field of 2.5 × 10⁻³ T at its center. Find the current in the solenoid. (Answer: 1 A)</p><p>3. A proton moving at right angles to a magnetic field of 0.1 T experiences a force of 2 × 10⁻¹² N. What is the proton’s speed? (Answer: 1.3 × 10⁸ m/s)</p><p>4. An 8 MeV proton enters perpendicularly into a uniform magnetic field of 2.5 T. (a) Find the force on the electron. (b) What is the radius of the path of the proton? (Answer: 1.6 × 10⁻¹¹ N, 0.17 m)</p><p>5. What is the time period of an electron projected into a uniform magnetic field of 10 mT that moves in a circle of radius 6 cm? (Answer: 3.6 ns)</p><p>6. A rectangular coil with 250 turns is 6.0 cm long and 4.0 cm wide. When placed in a magnetic field of 0.25 T, its maximum torque is 0.20 N m. What is the current in the coil? (Answer: 1.5 A)</p><p>7. Crossed electric and magnetic fields are established over a region. The magnetic field is 0.105 T and the electric field is 2.00 × 10⁵ V/m. An electron experiences zero net force and continues in a straight line. What is its speed? (Answer: 1.9 × 10⁶ m/s)</p><p>8. A galvanometer has full-scale deflection of 10 mA and resistance 100 Ω. How can it be converted into an ammeter of range 100 A? (Answer: R_s = 0.01 Ω)</p><p>9. How can a 5 mA, 100 Ω galvanometer be converted into a 20 V voltmeter? (Answer: R_h = 1900 Ω)</p><p>10. Two parallel wires 10 cm apart carry currents in opposite directions of 8 A. What is the magnetic field halfway between them? (Answer: 6.4 × 10⁻⁵ T)</p>",
    "theory": "NUMERICAL PROBLEMS\n1. At what distance from a long straight wire carrying a current of 10 A is the magnetic field equal to Earth’s magnetic field of 5 × 10⁻⁵ T? (Answer: 0.04 m)\n2. A long solenoid having 1000 turns uniformly distributed over a length of 0.5 m produces a magnetic field of 2.5 × 10⁻³ T at its center. Find the current in the solenoid. (Answer: 1 A)\n3. A proton moving at right angles to a magnetic field of 0.1 T experiences a force of 2 × 10⁻¹² N. What is the proton’s speed? (Answer: 1.3 × 10⁸ m/s)\n4. An 8 MeV proton enters perpendicularly into a uniform magnetic field of 2.5 T. (a) Find the force on the electron. (b) What is the radius of the path of the proton? (Answer: 1.6 × 10⁻¹¹ N, 0.17 m)\n5. What is the time period of an electron projected into a uniform magnetic field of 10 mT that moves in a circle of radius 6 cm? (Answer: 3.6 ns)\n6. A rectangular coil with 250 turns is 6.0 cm long and 4.0 cm wide. When placed in a magnetic field of 0.25 T, its maximum torque is 0.20 N m. What is the current in the coil? (Answer: 1.5 A)\n7. Crossed electric and magnetic fields are established over a region. The magnetic field is 0.105 T and the electric field is 2.00 × 10⁵ V/m. An electron experiences zero net force and continues in a straight line. What is its speed? (Answer: 1.9 × 10⁶ m/s)\n8. A galvanometer has full-scale deflection of 10 mA and resistance 100 Ω. How can it be converted into an ammeter of range 100 A? (Answer: R_s = 0.01 Ω)\n9. How can a 5 mA, 100 Ω galvanometer be converted into a 20 V voltmeter? (Answer: R_h = 1900 Ω)\n10. Two parallel wires 10 cm apart carry currents in opposite directions of 8 A. What is the magnetic field halfway between them? (Answer: 6.4 × 10⁻⁵ T)",
