  {
    "id": "phys14-moving-conductor-faraday-law-and-lenz-intro-130-131",
    "title": "Moving conductor, Faraday’s law and introduction to Lenz’s law",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "pdfPage": 71,
    "printedPage": 130,
    "sourcePdf": "Books/12th/12th Physics/PDF/Physics Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Let us consider a conducting rod L placed in a horizontal magnetic field as shown. Both ends of rod L are connected to a sensitive galvanometer, forming a loop. When the loop is held stationary in the field, the galvanometer indicates no deflection.",
      "When the loop is moved downwards across a magnetic field, the galvanometer deflects to one side. When the loop is moved upwards, the galvanometer deflects briefly to the opposite side. Moving the loop quickly through the field gives a greater current. If the loop is moved parallel to the field lines, there is no deflection on the galvanometer.",
      "A changing magnetic field through a loop induces an e.m.f. in the coil, which in turn causes current to flow.",
      "Thus an electromotive force or e.m.f. is said to be induced when the flux linking with a coil changes. This phenomenon is known as electromagnetic induction.",
      "14.1.1 FARADAY’S LAWS OF ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION",
      "The practical demonstration of electromagnetic induction in the previous discussion proves that the e.m.f. induced in a coil depends upon the following factors:",
      "1. The e.m.f. is directly proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux ΔΦ in time Δt. The greater the rate of change of magnetic flux through the coil, the greater the induced e.m.f.",
      "2. The e.m.f. is directly proportional to the number of turns N in the coil. The greater the number of turns, the greater the induced e.m.f.",
      "Induced e.m.f. ∝ N (ΔΦ/Δt)",
      "ε = kN (ΔΦ/Δt)",
      "Here k is a constant and its value is k = 1; therefore",
      "ε = N (ΔΦ/Δt) ...(14.1)",
      "The above equation is called Faraday’s law of induction.",
      "The induced e.m.f. always opposes the change in flux. The direction of induced e.m.f. is given by Lenz’s law:",
      "ε = −N (ΔΦ/Δt) ...(14.1)",
      "The negative sign in the equation represents Lenz’s law.",
      "14.3 LENZ’S LAW",
      "Lenz’s law is a convenient alternative method for determining the direction of an induced e.m.f. or current. To demonstrate Lenz’s law, consider the south pole of a magnet moving toward a coil as shown in Figure 14.3(a). As the south pole moves toward the coil, the flux through the coil increases.",
      "FIGURE 14.2 — A conducting rod moving through a magnetic field, connected to a galvanometer.",
      "FIGURE 14.3(a) — A south pole approaches a coil; magnetic flux linking the coil increases."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "phys14-lenz-law-conservation-energy-examples141-142-132-133",
    "title": "Lenz’s law, conservation of energy and Faraday-law examples",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "pdfPage": 72,
    "printedPage": 132,
    "sourcePdf": "Books/12th/12th Physics/PDF/Physics Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf",
    "paragraphs": [
      "The coil resembles a south pole and repels an approaching magnet. A changing magnetic flux induces current through the coil, which itself produces its own magnetic field. According to Lenz’s law, the direction of induced current opposes the change in flux that created it.",
      "On the other hand, if the south pole of the magnet is moved away from the coil, as in Figure 14.3(b), the magnetic flux linked with the coil decreases. The coil resembles a north pole and attracts the magnet when it is pulled away.",
      "The direction of induced current must be such that its own magnetic field is directed to oppose the changing flux caused by the field of the approaching magnet. The direction can be determined by applying the right-hand rule if the magnetic polarity of the coil is known.",
      "Similarly, when the north pole of the magnet is moved toward or away from the coil, repulsion and attraction take place between the coil and moving magnet and always oppose the magnet’s motion, as illustrated in Figure 14.3(c) and (d).",
      "Thus Lenz’s law states that the polarity of induced e.m.f. is such that it tends to produce a current which opposes the change in magnetic flux that produced it.",
      "FIGURE 14.3(b) — A south pole moves away from the coil; linked flux decreases.",
      "FIGURE 14.3(c)–(d) — A north pole moves toward or away from the coil; induced current opposes the magnet’s motion.",
      "14.3.1 LENZ’S LAW AND CONSERVATION OF ENERGY",
      "Lenz’s law is a consequence of the law of conservation of energy. For example, when the north pole of a magnet approaches the coil, the repulsive force acts on the bar magnet due to the current induced in the coil. The motion of the magnet is opposed. The mechanical energy spent overcoming this opposition appears in the coil as electrical energy. Thus Lenz’s law is consistent with the law of conservation of energy.",
      "EXAMPLE 14.1",
      "At what rate must a single conductor cut the flux so that a current of 1.2 mA flows through it when a 10 Ω resistor is connected across its ends?",
      "GIVEN — Current I = 1.2 mA; resistor R = 10 Ω; single conductor, N = 1.",
      "REQUIRED — Time rate of change of flux, ΔΦ/Δt = ?",
      "SOLUTION — ε = N (ΔΦ/Δt).",
      "Here ε = IR = 1.2 × 10⁻³ × 10 = 1.2 × 10⁻² V.",
      "ΔΦ/Δt = ε/N = (1.2 × 10⁻²)/1 = 1.2 × 10⁻² Wb/s.",
      "EXAMPLE 14.2",
      "A coil of mean area 500 cm² and having 1000 turns is held perpendicular to a uniform field of 0.4 gauss. The coil is turned through 180° in 1/10 s. Calculate the average induced e.m.f.",
      "FIGURE 14.3(b)–(d) — Induced-current direction and magnetic-flux response for a moving magnet."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "phys14-example142-motional-emf-and-example143-prompt-134-135",
    "title": "Example 14.2 solution, Assignment 14.1 and motional e.m.f.",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "pdfPage": 73,
    "printedPage": 134,
    "sourcePdf": "Books/12th/12th Physics/PDF/Physics Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf",
    "paragraphs": [
      "EXAMPLE 14.2 — GIVEN: When the plane of the coil is perpendicular to the field, θ = 0°. When the coil is turned through 180°, θ = 180°. Initial flux linked with the coil: Φ₁ = NBA cos θ = NBA. Flux linked when turned through 180°: Φ₂ = −NBA. Change in flux: ΔΦ = Φ₂ − Φ₁ = (−NBA) − (NBA) = −2NBA.",
      "N = 1000; B = 0.4 G = 0.4 × 10⁻⁴ T; A = 500 × 10⁻⁴ m²; Δt = 0.1 s.",
      "REQUIRED — induced e.m.f. ε = ?",
      "SOLUTION — Average induced e.m.f., ε = |ΔΦ|/Δt = 2NBA/Δt.",
      "ε = [2 × 1000 × (0.4 × 10⁻⁴) × 500 × 10⁻⁴]/0.1 = 0.04 V.",
      "ASSIGNMENT 14.1 — In state (1), magnetic flux changes by 5 Wb in 2 s on a coil with 20 loops. In final state (2), the same flux changes in 8 s. Determine the ratio of the initially induced e.m.f. to the final induced e.m.f. (Answer: 4:1)",
      "14.4.1 MOTIONAL e.m.f.",
      "Consider a conducting rod MN placed in a uniform magnetic field B directed into the page, as shown in Figure 14.4. The rectangle MNRS forms a closed circuit enclosing a varying area due to the motion of rod MN.",
      "When rod MN moves from position x₁ to x₂ in a small time interval Δt, the flux linked with the conductor changes and a current I starts to flow in the circuit. Therefore, an induced e.m.f. is produced. The distance travelled by the rod in time Δt is x₁ − x₂ = Δx.",
      "This induced e.m.f. due to motion of an electric conductor in a magnetic field is called motional e.m.f. To find its magnitude, use Faraday’s law of induction: ε = N (ΔΦ/Δt).",
      "Here N = 1 and flux Φ = BA cos θ. We have θ = 0° and cos θ = 1 because B is perpendicular to A. Since B is uniform, the change in flux is ΔΦ = Δ(BA) = BΔA. The area enclosed by circuit MNRS changes by ΔA = ℓΔx.",
      "Putting values into the equation: ε = BΔA/Δt = BℓΔx/Δt, since ΔA = ℓΔx.",
      "The velocity of the rod is Δx/Δt = v. Thus ε = Bℓv (B, ℓ and v are mutually perpendicular).",
      "The e.m.f. generated in the circuit by the moving rod is the product of magnetic-field strength, rod length and velocity. This equation is valid when B, ℓ and v are mutually perpendicular. Otherwise, their mutually perpendicular components are used. Motional e.m.f. is an example of dynamically induced e.m.f. because it is induced in a conductor in motion.",
      "When a conductor is moved in a stationary magnetic field so that the flux linking it changes in magnitude, the induced e.m.f. is called dynamically induced e.m.f.",
      "EXAMPLE 14.3 — A loop of resistance 0.1 Ω is placed in a magnetic field of 2 T. A conductor of length 0.2 m slides along the loop with velocity 0.2 m s⁻¹. Find (a) the e.m.f. produced if the conductor’s motion is perpendicular to the field; (b) current through the loop; (c) electrical power generated; (d) input mechanical power.",
      "GIVEN — Resistance R = 0.1 Ω; magnetic field B = 2 T; conductor length ℓ = 0.2 m; velocity v = 0.2 m s⁻¹.",
      "FIGURE 14.4 — A conductor moving in a magnetic field."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "phys14-motional-emf-power-railgun-example143-and-selfinduction-intro-136-137",
    "title": "Motional e.m.f. power, rail gun, Example 14.3 and self-induction introduction",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "pdfPage": 74,
    "printedPage": 136,
    "sourcePdf": "Books/12th/12th Physics/PDF/Physics Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Thus the power produced is Fv = BILv = Bℓv/R × Bℓv = B²ℓ²v²/R; therefore I = Bℓv/R.",
      "The power dissipated is P = B²ℓ²v²/R ...(14.2). In satisfying the principle of conservation of energy, the produced and dissipated powers are equal.",
      "A rail gun is an electromagnetic projectile launcher, as shown in Figure 14.5.",
      "FIGURE 14.5 — A rail gun: magnet field, driving current, armature current and force.",
      "EXAMPLE 14.3 — A loop of resistance 0.1 Ω is placed in a magnetic field of 2 T. A conductor of length 0.2 m slides along a loop at velocity 0.2 m s⁻¹. Find (a) the e.m.f. produced if the conductor’s motion is perpendicular to the field, (b) the current through the loop, (c) the electrical power generated, and (d) the input mechanical power.",
      "GIVEN — Resistance R = 0.1 Ω; magnetic field B = 2 T; conductor length ℓ = 0.2 m; velocity v = 0.2 m s⁻¹.",
      "REQUIRED — (a) e.m.f. ε; (b) current I; (c) power P₁; (d) input mechanical power P₂.",
      "SOLUTION (a) — Motional e.m.f. ε = vBℓ = 0.2 × 2 × 0.2 = 0.08 V.",
      "(b) Current through the loop is I = ε/R = 0.08 V/0.1 Ω = 0.8 A.",
      "(c) Electrical power generated is P₁ = IV = 0.8 A × 0.08 V = 0.064 W. The magnetic force on the current-carrying loop of length ℓ is F = B I ℓ = 2 T × 0.8 A × 0.2 m = 0.32 N.",
      "(d) The power needed to move the loop against magnetic force F is P₂ = Fv = 0.32 N × 0.2 m s⁻¹ = 0.064 W.",
      "14.4.2 SELF-INDUCED e.m.f.",
      "In the previous case, a changing magnetic flux was due to an external source, i.e. a magnet. Relative movement between magnet and coil caused induced current and e.m.f. in the coil. In this case, the e.m.f. induced in a coil is due to a change of current in the same coil. To explain self-induction, consider a coil connected to a battery through a rheostat, as shown in Figure 14.6. By changing the resistance in the circuit, the current through the coil changes and the magnetic flux through the coil also changes. As a result, an e.m.f. is induced in the coil itself. When current in a coil changes, the self-induced e.m.f. opposes the change of current in the same coil; this is known as self-inductance or inductance. The e.m.f. induced in a coil due to a change in its own linked flux is called self-induced e.m.f."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "phys14-self-inductance-derivation-factors-mutual-inductance-intro-138-139",
    "title": "Self-inductance equations and factors; mutual-inductance introduction",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "pdfPage": 75,
    "printedPage": 138,
    "sourcePdf": "Books/12th/12th Physics/PDF/Physics Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Consider a coil of N turns carrying current I. If the current changes, the flux linking the coil also changes and a self-induced e.m.f. is set up: ε = N ΔΦ/Δt = Δ(NΦ)/Δt. Since the flux linkage NΦ is proportional to current I, ε ∝ ΔI/Δt and ε = L ΔI/Δt = Δ(LI)/Δt (1), where L is the self-inductance (inductance) of the coil.",
      "The unit of inductance is the henry (H). From Eq. (1), an induced e.m.f. of 1 V for a current-change rate of 1 A s⁻¹ gives L = 1 H. Faraday’s law also gives ε = Δ(NΦ)/Δt (2). Comparing (1) and (2), LI = NΦ, so L = NΦ/I (14.3).",
      "Inductors are used in electronic, electrical, electromechanical and wireless circuits. The inductance of a coil (Figure 14.7) depends on (i) its shape and number of turns N, (ii) core material, and (iii) coil area and length. Greater magnetic permeability of the core gives greater inductance; replacing air with an iron core increases it.",
      "14.4.3 MUTUALLY INDUCED E.M.F. AND MUTUAL INDUCTANCE — To demonstrate mutually induced e.m.f., place coils A and B adjacent to each other as in Figure 14.8. Current in coil A produces a magnetic field; part of its flux passes through or links coil B. This flux common to both coils is called mutual flux Φₘ. A rheostat can change the current in A, inducing an e.m.f. in B. The e.m.f. in A is self-induced; the e.m.f. in B is mutually induced. This linking of flux from coil A with coil B is mutual induction. The property of two neighbouring coils to induce e.m.f. in one coil when the current in the other changes is mutual inductance.",
      "FIGURE 14.6 — Self-induced e.m.f. in a coil: magnetic flux linking a coil connected to a battery, rheostat and galvanometer.",
      "FIGURE 14.7 — A coil (inductor); core material, coil geometry and turns affect its inductance."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "phys14-mutual-induction-equations-static-emf-eddy-current-intro-140-141",
    "title": "Mutual inductance, statically induced e.m.f. and eddy-current introduction",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "pdfPage": 76,
    "printedPage": 140,
    "sourcePdf": "Books/12th/12th Physics/PDF/Physics Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf",
    "paragraphs": [
      "The mutually induced e.m.f. in coil B is directly proportional to the rate of change of current in coil A: ε ∝ ΔI/Δt; ε = −M ΔI/Δt = −Δ(MI)/Δt (1). M is a constant called mutual inductance. The minus sign expresses Lenz’s law: the induced e.m.f. sets up a current opposing the change of flux linking coil B. Its direction therefore opposes the cause producing it.",
      "The SI unit of mutual inductance is the henry (H), named in honour of Joseph Henry (1799–1878), an American scientist who discovered induced e.m.f. independently of Faraday. One henry is 1 H = 1 V·s/A; if ε = 1 V and ΔI/Δt = 1 A/s in Eq. (1), M = 1 H. Faraday’s law also gives ε = −Δ(NΦ)/Δt (2). Comparing (1) and (2), MI = NΦ, so M = NΦ/I (14.4).",
      "Like capacitance, mutual inductance is a geometric quantity. It depends on the shapes and relative positions of the two coils, and is independent of the currents in the coils.",
      "STATICALLY INDUCED E.M.F. — The e.m.f. induced in a coil due to a change of flux linked with it is called statically induced e.m.f. The coils are stationary and a magnetic field moving around the conductor produces e.m.f.; a transformer is an example.",
      "ASSIGNMENT 14.2 — Current in the first coil changes from 2 A to 10 A in 0.4 s. Find the mutual inductance if an e.m.f. of 60 mV is induced in the second coil. Also find the induced e.m.f. when the current in the first coil changes from 4 A to 16 A in 0.03 s; consider magnitude only. Printed answers: 3 × 10⁻³ H; 1.2 V.",
      "14.4.4 EDDY CURRENTS — Eddy currents are loops of electric current induced within conductors by a changing magnetic field in the conductor, according to Faraday’s law. They are currents induced in a conductor moving in a magnetic field or in metals exposed to a changing magnetic field.",
      "FIGURE 14.8 — Two neighbouring coils A and B linked by mutual magnetic flux; changing current in A induces e.m.f. in B."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "phys14-eddy-current-brake-ac-generator-and-frequency-intro-142-143",
    "title": "Eddy-current braking and alternating-current generator introduction",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "pdfPage": 77,
    "printedPage": 142,
    "sourcePdf": "Books/12th/12th Physics/PDF/Physics Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Eddy currents arise when a conductor passes through a magnetic field. They create opposing forces that circulate inside the conductor and flow in planes perpendicular to the magnetic field. In Figure 14.9, the increasing field at C induces a counterclockwise current, while the decreasing field at D induces a second eddy current clockwise.",
      "By Lenz’s law an eddy current produces a magnetic field opposing the field that produced it. Eddy currents therefore oppose the source-field change. The fields repel and a braking force results; this braking system is used in high-speed trains and roller coasters.",
      "PROJECT — A bar magnet is dropped inside a long vertical tube. If the tube is metal, the magnet quickly approaches terminal speed; if the tube is cardboard, it falls with constant acceleration. Explain why the magnet falls differently in the metal tube.",
      "14.5 GENERATING ELECTRICITY — Alternating-current generator. Changing the magnetic flux through a coil produces e.m.f. and is used to generate electricity; this is an important application of electromagnetic induction. A generator converts mechanical energy into electrical energy.",
      "An A.C. generator has two poles, north and south, of a magnet to provide a uniform magnetic field. It has a rectangular coil CDEF called the armature, and slip rings that produce alternating current. As coil CDEF rotates between the magnet poles, an e.m.f. is induced.",
      "When side CD moves upward, Fleming’s right-hand rule gives current from C to D and E to F. After half a revolution, FE occupies the position previously held by CD and the current reverses, flowing F to E and D to C. Thus the induced e.m.f. and current reverse every half revolution.",
      "The graph of e.m.f. against time is shown in Figure 14.11. The time for one revolution T is the period; frequency f is the number of revolutions per second, so f = 1/T. Domestic electricity has frequency 50 Hz, meaning the generator makes 50 revolutions per second. Figure 14.11 shows coil positions corresponding to points on the graph.",
      "FIGURE 14.9 — Eddy-current brake: changing magnetic flux through a conducting disc induces circulating currents and braking force.",
      "FIGURE 14.10 — A.C. generator: rotating armature CDEF between N and S poles, axle, slip rings A₁/A₂, brushes B₁/B₂ and alternating e.m.f. output."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "phys14-ac-generator-rotation-emf-equations-assignment143-ac-motor-intro-144-145",
    "title": "AC-generator rotation, sinusoidal e.m.f., Assignment 14.3 and AC-motor introduction",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "pdfPage": 78,
    "printedPage": 144,
    "sourcePdf": "Books/12th/12th Physics/PDF/Physics Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf",
    "paragraphs": [
      "At position 1 the coil is at 0°. It moves parallel to the magnetic field; flux is zero and no e.m.f. is induced.",
      "At position 2 the coil is at 90°. It moves perpendicular to the magnetic field; magnetic flux is maximum and the induced e.m.f. is maximum.",
      "At position 3 the coil is at 180°. It moves parallel to the field, flux is zero and no e.m.f. is induced.",
      "At position 4 the coil is at 270°. It moves perpendicular to the field, so induced e.m.f. is maximum. Its direction is opposite to that at position 2.",
      "At position 5 the coil is at 360°. It moves parallel to the field, so no e.m.f. is induced. It has completed one full revolution and returned to its starting point.",
      "The induced e.m.f. depends on the rate at which the number of flux linkages changes. Greater angular velocity ω makes the coil cut flux lines faster and produces greater e.m.f. If the coil rotates with angular velocity ω and makes angle θ with field B, the flux through a coil of N turns and area A is Φ = BAN cos θ = NAB cos ωt, where θ = ωt.",
      "The induced e.m.f. is ε = −ΔΦ/Δt = −Δ(NAB cos ωt)/Δt. Taking the limit as Δt→0 gives ε = NAB(ω sin ωt) (14.5). Since sin ωt has maximum value 1, εmax = NABω; therefore ε = εmax sin ωt = εmax sin(2πft) (14.6).",
      "ASSIGNMENT 14.3 — An electric generator has 100 turns of wire formed into a 50 cm × 30 cm rectangular loop, entirely in a uniform 3.50 T magnetic field. Find the maximum e.m.f. when it is spun at 1000 rev/min about an axis perpendicular to B. Printed answer: 5500 V.",
      "14.6 A.C. MOTOR — An electric motor converts electrical energy into mechanical energy. An A.C. motor has two basic electrical parts: a stator and a rotor. The stator is stationary and the rotor is the rotating part. The stator consists of electromagnets arranged to form a hollow cylinder, with one pole of each magnet facing the group’s centre.",
      "FIGURE 14.11 — A coil’s five orientations during one revolution in a magnetic field and the corresponding sinusoidal induced-e.m.f. graph at 0°, 90°, 180°, 270° and 360°."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "phys14-ac-motor-construction-back-emf-example144-146-147",
    "title": "A.C. motor construction, back e.m.f. and Example 14.4 prompt",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "pdfPage": 79,
    "printedPage": 146,
    "sourcePdf": "Books/12th/12th Physics/PDF/Physics Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf",
    "paragraphs": [
      "FIGURE 14.12 — A.C. motor: the stator is stationary around the rotor; the rotor turns inside the stator.",
      "The rotor is inside the stator and mounted on the motor shaft. The components make the rotor turn and thereby rotate the shaft. The stator has electromagnets arranged around a cylinder, with their poles facing the stator poles. When stator windings connect to an A.C. supply, flux is produced in the air gap and rotates at synchronous speed, inducing voltages in the stator and rotor windings.",
      "If the rotor circuit is closed, current flows in its winding and interacts with the rotating flux to produce torque. In steady state, the rotor runs close to synchronous speed. Eddy currents in iron motor cores cause energy losses; thin, electrically insulated iron laminations reduce them. The rotor coils are wound on a laminated iron armature mounted on an axle. Motors are used in fans, power tools, appliances, electric vehicles and hybrid cars.",
      "14.6.1 BACK e.m.f. — When a motor coil rotates in a magnetic field, an induced e.m.f. ε is produced in a direction opposing the battery e.m.f. V; it is called back e.m.f. or counter e.m.f. Its magnitude is proportional to the product of magnetic flux and speed.",
      "The back e.m.f. ε is less than the applied voltage V. Their difference drives current through the armature resistance: V = ε + IₐRₐ (14.7), where Rₐ is armature-circuit resistance and Iₐ is current. The textbook calls the motor output the difference between supply voltage and back e.m.f.",
      "EXAMPLE 14.4 — An A.C. motor has internal resistance 4 Ω. On a 50 Hz A.C. supply with peak voltage 120 V, it draws peak current 5 A. Find (1) peak back e.m.f., (2) peak power delivered by the A.C. supply, and (3) peak rate of energy loss as heat in the motor.",
      "GIVEN — Rₐ = 4 Ω; f = 50 Hz; peak current Iₐ = 5 A; peak applied voltage V = 120 V. REQUIRED — peak back e.m.f. ε; peak power Ppeak; peak heat-loss rate Pheat.",
      "MOTOR STARTING AND LOAD — At start, back e.m.f. is zero and the motor draws maximum current; starting a water pump can briefly dim lights because of the I R drop. As speed rises, eddy currents and back e.m.f. increase, reducing coil current. A mechanical load, such as an elevator moving uphill, opposes rotation and slows the motor, reducing back e.m.f. If the coil is prevented from turning, there is no back e.m.f.; the applied voltage can drive enough current to overheat the wires and destroy the motor."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "phys14-example144-solution-transformer-principle-ratios-and-lamination-148-149",
    "title": "Example 14.4 solution and transformer operation, ratios and laminated cores",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "pdfPage": 80,
    "printedPage": 148,
    "sourcePdf": "Books/12th/12th Physics/PDF/Physics Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf",
    "paragraphs": [
      "EXAMPLE 14.4 SOLUTION — Peak back e.m.f.: ε = V − IₐRₐ = 120 − (5 × 4) = 100 V. Peak power supplied: Ppeak = VI = 120 × 5 = 600 W. Peak rate of energy loss as heat: Pheat = I²R = (5)² × 4 = 100 W.",
      "14.7 TRANSFORMERS — A transformer changes electrical power from one voltage and current level to another and operates on alternating current. Its three main parts are a primary coil, secondary coil and laminated iron core. The primary coil connects to the A.C. supply; its changing current creates a changing magnetic field concentrated in the iron core.",
      "The primary and secondary coils are wound around a high-permeability core, such as iron, so most magnetic flux links both coils. Changing flux through the primary induces e.m.f. in the secondary. By Faraday’s law, Vs = Ns ΔΦ/Δt. The same flux passes through both coils, so Vp = Np ΔΦ/Δt and Vs/Vp = Ns/Np (14.8).",
      "Vp is primary voltage, Vs secondary voltage and Np primary turns. If Ns > Np, then Vs > Vp and the transformer is step-up; if Ns < Np, then Vs < Vp and it is step-down. The text states that the amount of energy cannot be stepped up or down. An ideal transformer has 100% efficiency, so VsIs = VpIp and Vs/Vp = Ip/Is (14.9). Stepping voltage up steps current down, and vice versa.",
      "For high voltage a step-up transformer is used; for high current a step-down transformer is used. Transformer cores are made of many thin iron sheets pressed together but separated by thin insulating layers. This confines eddy-current circulation to each lamination rather than the whole core and reduces overall heating.",
      "FIGURE 14.13 — Step-up transformer with primary and secondary windings on a laminated iron core.",
      "FIGURE 14.14 — Laminated transformer core assembled from individual insulated iron sheets.",
      "FIGURE 14.15 — A solid iron core permits large eddy currents; a laminated core reduces eddy currents and heating."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "phys14-transmission-lines-and-unit-key-points-150-151",
    "title": "Transformer transmission applications and Unit 14 key points",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "pdfPage": 81,
    "printedPage": 150,
    "sourcePdf": "Books/12th/12th Physics/PDF/Physics Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf",
    "paragraphs": [
      "Long, thick transmission-line cables are made of copper or aluminium because their resistance is low. High-voltage lines carry electricity long distances to substations. Lines go to grid stations near factories and towns, where transformers change very high voltage to lower levels. The source gives 220 V for larger appliances such as stoves and clothes dryers, and 110 V for lights, TVs and smaller appliances.",
      "FIGURE 14.16 — Electricity transmission: generator at a dam, long-distance high-voltage lines, grid station and transformer supplying a town.",
      "KEY POINTS — Self-induction: change of magnetic flux due to variation in current flowing in the same circuit.",
      "Mutual induction: change of magnetic flux due to variation in current flowing in another circuit.",
      "Lenz’s law: induced current flows in a direction that opposes the cause producing it.",
      "Dynamically induced e.m.f.: produced when a conductor moves in a stationary magnetic field so that the flux linking it changes in magnitude.",
      "Statically induced e.m.f.: produced when the conductor is stationary and the magnetic field is moving or changing.",
      "Eddy currents: currents induced in a conductor moving in a magnetic field or in metals exposed to a changing magnetic field.",
      "Generator: a device that converts mechanical energy into electrical energy.",
      "Transformer: a device used to transform electrical power from one voltage and current level to another."
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "phys14-exercise-multiple-choice-1-14-and-conceptual-1-8-152-153",
    "title": "Unit 14 exercise: multiple-choice questions 1–14 and conceptual questions 1–8",
    "kind": "exercise",
    "pdfPage": 82,
    "printedPage": 152,
    "sourcePdf": "Books/12th/12th Physics/PDF/Physics Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf",
    "paragraphs": [
      "UNIT 14 EXERCISE — MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS (choose the best possible answer). 1. For inducing e.m.f. in a coil the basic requirement is: (A) flux should link the coil; (B) change in flux should link the coil; (C) coil should form a closed loop; (D) both (B) and (C) are true. 2. A single-turn coil rotates in a uniform magnetic field at uniform speed; induced e.m.f. is: (A) alternating; (B) steady; (C) pulsating; (D) zero. 3. Dynamically induced e.m.f. is used in: (A) choke; (B) transformer; (C) generator; (D) thermocouple. 4. In Fleming’s left-hand rule the thumb represents: (A) current flow; (B) induced e.m.f.; (C) magnetic field; (D) mechanical force. 5. The field at any point on the axis of a current-carrying coil is: (A) perpendicular to the axis; (B) parallel to the axis; (C) at 45° to the axis; (D) zero. 6. When a coil rotates in a magnetic field, the induced current: (A) continuously changes; (B) remains the same; (C) becomes zero; (D) becomes maximum. 7. In an A.C. generator, increasing the number of turns in the coil: (A) decreases e.m.f.; (B) leaves e.m.f. the same; (C) increases e.m.f.; (D) makes e.m.f. zero. 8. A device in which induced e.m.f. is statically induced is: (A) transformer; (B) A.C. generator; (C) alternator; (D) dynamo. 9. Find mutual-inductance coefficient when magnetic flux changes by 2×10⁻² Wb and current changes by 0.01 A: (A) 2 H; (B) 3 H; (C) 1/2 H; (D) zero. 10. Induced e.m.f. in a coil is proportional to: (A) magnetic flux through the coil; (B) rate of change of magnetic flux through the coil; (C) coil area; (D) product of flux and area. 11. In a coil current changes from 2 to 4 A in 0.05 s. If average induced e.m.f. is 8 V, self-inductance is: (A) 0.2 H; (B) 0.1 H; (C) 0.8 H; (D) 0.04 H. 12. Which quantity remains constant in a step-up transformer? (A) current; (B) voltage; (C) power; (D) heat. 13. A step-up transformer has transformation ratio 3:2. What is secondary voltage if primary voltage is 30 V? (A) 45 V; (B) 15 V; (C) 90 V; (D) 300 V. 14. Eddy current is produced when: (A) a metal is kept in a varying magnetic field; (B) a metal is kept in a steady magnetic field; (C) a circular coil is placed in a steady magnetic field; (D) current is passed through a circular coil.",
      "CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS — 1. What factors limit the size of the back e.m.f.? 2. Why does back e.m.f. tend to decrease as the rate of doing work increases? 3. In Faraday’s experiments, what is the advantage of using coils with many turns? 4. Show that ε = −ΔΦ/Δt is dimensionally correct. 5. Give the formulae for flux linkage in terms of angular orientation. 6. How does an electromagnetic brake work? 7. How can eddy currents be minimized in a transformer? 8. How is electromagnetic induction used in cooktops in electric ranges?"
    ]
  },
  {
    "id": "phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-and-numericals-1-9-154-155",
    "title": "Unit 14 exercise: conceptual 9–10, comprehensive questions and numerical problems 1–9",
    "kind": "exercise",
    "pdfPage": 83,
    "printedPage": 154,
    "sourcePdf": "Books/12th/12th Physics/PDF/Physics Book for 12 class KPTBB.pdf",
    "paragraphs": [
      "CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (continued) — 9. What is meant by the term back e.m.f. in operation of an electric motor? 10. Why is it advantageous for the armature to rotate in a radial magnetic field rather than a uniform field? TRANSFORMER CORE: (a) Which material would you prefer: iron, solid soft iron, laminated soft iron or aluminium? (b) Why is this material used?",
      "COMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS — 1. Describe electromagnetic induction with simple experiments; explain factors affecting magnitude and direction of induced e.m.f. 2. State Faraday’s law; explain statically and dynamically induced e.m.f. with experiments. 3. Describe Lenz’s law and show that it manifests conservation of energy. 4. Explain mutual induction; define its coefficient and unit. 5. Explain self-induction; define its coefficient and unit. 6. Explain motional e.m.f. and show ε = Bℓv. 7. What is a D.C. generator? Explain how an alternating e.m.f. is generated by a loop of wire rotating in a magnetic field. 8. What is an A.C. motor? Explain its construction and operation. 9. What is a transformer? Give its principle and mathematical relationship; explain why a laminated iron core is used instead of a solid core. 10. Explain back e.m.f. in an electric motor.",
      "NUMERICAL PROBLEMS — 1. A wheel has 12 metal spokes, each 0.6 m long, and rotates at 180 rpm in a plane normal to Earth’s magnetic field. If B = 0.6 G, find induced e.m.f. between axle and rim. Printed answer: 2.035 × 10⁻⁴ V. 2. A 1000-turn circuit encloses a magnetic circuit of cross-section 20 cm². At 4 A, B = 1 Wb m⁻²; at 9 A, B = 1.4 Wb m⁻². Find mean inductance between these current limits and e.m.f. if current falls from 9 A to 4 A in 0.05 s. Printed answers: 0.16 H, 16 V. 3. A 100 Ω coil with 100 turns is in a 1 mWb field; a 400 Ω galvanometer is connected in series. Find average e.m.f. and current if the coil moves in 0.1 s from the given field to 0.2 mWb. Printed answer: 1.6 mA. 4. A horizontal east–west wire 10 m long falls at 5.0 m s⁻¹ perpendicular to the horizontal component of Earth’s field, 0.30 × 10⁻⁴ Wb m⁻². Find (a) instantaneous e.m.f., (b) direction, and (c) which end has higher potential. Printed answer: 1.5 × 10⁻³ V.",
      "5. Current in a circuit falls from 5.0 A to 0 A in 0.1 s. If average induced e.m.f. is 200 V, estimate self-inductance. Printed answer: 4 H. 6. A long solenoid has 15 turns per cm. A small loop of area 2.0 cm² is inside, normal to its axis. Solenoid current changes steadily from 2.0 A to 4.0 A in 0.1 s. Find induced e.m.f. in the loop. Printed answer: 7.54 × 10⁻⁶ V. 7. A rectangular loop of sides 8 cm and 2 cm, with a small cut, moves out of a uniform 0.3 T magnetic field normal to the loop at 1 cm s⁻¹. Find e.m.f. and duration when velocity is normal to (a) the longer side, (b) the shorter side. Printed answers: 2.4 × 10⁻⁴ V, 2 s; 0.6 × 10⁻⁴ V, 8 s. 8. A 90 mm wire moves upward at 35 m s⁻¹ between magnet poles. B = 80 mT to the right and wire resistance is 5.00 mΩ. Find induced-current magnitude and direction. Printed answer: 50.4 A. 9. Adjacent coils have mutual inductance 1.5 H. Current in one changes from 0 to 20 A in 0.5 s. Find change of flux linkage with the other coil. Printed answer: 30 Wb."
    ]
  }
