    "id": "phys14-unit-opening-outcomes-and-induced-emf-intro-126-127",
    "title": "Unit 14 Electromagnetic Induction — outcomes and introduction",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>UNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION</p><p>How does electromagnetism work in a metal detector?</p><p>LEARNING OUTCOMES</p><p>After studying this Unit, students will be able to:</p><p>describe the production of electricity by magnetism.</p><p>explain that induced e.m.f.s can be generated in two ways: (i) by relative movement (the generator effect); (ii) by changing a magnetic field (the transformer effect).</p><p>infer the factors affecting the magnitude of the induced e.m.f.</p><p>state Faraday’s law of electromagnetic induction.</p><p>account for Lenz’s law to predict the direction of an induced current and relate it to the principle of conservation of energy.</p><p>apply Faraday’s law of electromagnetic induction and Lenz’s law to solve problems.</p><p>explain the production of eddy currents and identify their magnetic and heating effects.</p><p>explain the need for laminated iron cores in electric motors, generators and transformers.</p><p>explain motional e.m.f. and, for a rod or wire moving through a magnetic field, compute the potential difference across its ends.</p><p>define mutual inductance (M) and self-inductance (L), and state their unit, the henry.</p><p>describe the main components of an A.C. generator and explain how it works.</p><p>describe the main features of an A.C. electric motor and the role of each feature.</p><p>explain the production of back e.m.f. in electric motors.</p><p>describe the construction of a transformer and explain how it works.</p><p>identify the relationship between the ratio of the number of turns in primary and secondary coils and the ratio of primary to secondary voltages.</p><p>describe how step-up and step-down transformers ensure efficient transfer of electricity along cables.</p><p>In 1831, English scientist Michael Faraday discovered that when the magnetic flux linking a conductor changes, an e.m.f. is induced in the conductor. This phenomenon is known as electromagnetic induction.</p><p>The interplay of electric and magnetic forces is the basis for electric motors, generators and many other modern technologies. Faraday’s Law of Induction describes how an electric current produces a magnetic field and, conversely, how a changing magnetic field generates an electric current in a conductor. In this Unit, we study various aspects of electromagnetic induction.</p><p>14.1 INDUCED E.M.F. AND MAGNETIC FLUX</p><p>To produce an induced e.m.f., consider a coil C of wire connected to a sensitive galvanometer G, as shown in Figure 14.1(a). The galvanometer detects any current induced in the coil. Near the coil is a stationary bar magnet. When the magnet is stationary, the galvanometer shows no current. If the magnet is moved towards coil C, the galvanometer shows a deflection in one direction.</p>",
    "theory": "UNIT 14 ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION\nHow does electromagnetism work in a metal detector?\nLEARNING OUTCOMES\nAfter studying this Unit, students will be able to:\ndescribe the production of electricity by magnetism.\nexplain that induced e.m.f.s can be generated in two ways: (i) by relative movement (the generator effect); (ii) by changing a magnetic field (the transformer effect).\ninfer the factors affecting the magnitude of the induced e.m.f.\nstate Faraday’s law of electromagnetic induction.\naccount for Lenz’s law to predict the direction of an induced current and relate it to the principle of conservation of energy.\napply Faraday’s law of electromagnetic induction and Lenz’s law to solve problems.\nexplain the production of eddy currents and identify their magnetic and heating effects.\nexplain the need for laminated iron cores in electric motors, generators and transformers.\nexplain motional e.m.f. and, for a rod or wire moving through a magnetic field, compute the potential difference across its ends.\ndefine mutual inductance (M) and self-inductance (L), and state their unit, the henry.\ndescribe the main components of an A.C. generator and explain how it works.\ndescribe the main features of an A.C. electric motor and the role of each feature.\nexplain the production of back e.m.f. in electric motors.\ndescribe the construction of a transformer and explain how it works.\nidentify the relationship between the ratio of the number of turns in primary and secondary coils and the ratio of primary to secondary voltages.\ndescribe how step-up and step-down transformers ensure efficient transfer of electricity along cables.\nIn 1831, English scientist Michael Faraday discovered that when the magnetic flux linking a conductor changes, an e.m.f. is induced in the conductor. This phenomenon is known as electromagnetic induction.\nThe interplay of electric and magnetic forces is the basis for electric motors, generators and many other modern technologies. Faraday’s Law of Induction describes how an electric current produces a magnetic field and, conversely, how a changing magnetic field generates an electric current in a conductor. In this Unit, we study various aspects of electromagnetic induction.\n14.1 INDUCED E.M.F. AND MAGNETIC FLUX\nTo produce an induced e.m.f., consider a coil C of wire connected to a sensitive galvanometer G, as shown in Figure 14.1(a). The galvanometer detects any current induced in the coil. Near the coil is a stationary bar magnet. When the magnet is stationary, the galvanometer shows no current. If the magnet is moved towards coil C, the galvanometer shows a deflection in one direction.",
