    "id": "phys14-magnetic-flux-change-experiment-seismometer-128-129",
    "title": "Changing magnetic flux, electromagnetic induction and seismometer application",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>If the magnet is moved away from the coil, the galvanometer again shows a deflection, but in the opposite direction. If the magnet is held steady and the coil of wire is moved toward or away from the magnet, an e.m.f. is again induced and a current flows.</p><p>If the magnet is held stationary inside the coil, although flux links the coil, there is no change in flux and hence no e.m.f. is induced. Therefore the galvanometer shows zero deflection. If the magnet is moved faster, a greater deflection is observed.</p><p>In each case, an e.m.f. is induced in a circuit when the magnetic flux through the circuit changes with time. It does not matter whether the magnet or the coil moves. Their relative motion is what counts.</p><p>TIP — The basic requirement for electromagnetic induction is a change in flux linking the conductor (or coil).</p><p>When the conductor is stationary and the magnet is moving, the e.m.f. induced in this way is called statically induced e.m.f.</p><p>A similar effect can be produced using the apparatus shown in Figure 14.1(b). It consists of a coil connected to a switch and a battery. This is the primary coil and its circuit is the primary circuit. A second coil, the secondary coil, is connected to an ammeter. Its circuit is the secondary circuit. There is no source of e.m.f. in the secondary circuit.</p><p>When the switch in the primary circuit is suddenly closed, the ammeter measures a current in the secondary circuit and then returns to zero. The two coils are magnetically linked by a common magnetic flux. When the switch is opened again, the ammeter reads a current in the opposite direction and again returns to zero.</p><p>Whenever there is a steady current in the primary circuit, the ammeter reads zero. Current in the secondary circuit occurs only for an instant while the magnetic field through the secondary coil is changing. Closing and opening the switch induces the current. It is the change in magnetic field that creates the current; a changing magnetic field induces an e.m.f.</p><p>APPLICATIONS OF ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION — To measure earthquakes, a device called a seismometer is used.</p><p>An electromagnetic seismometer works on the principle of electromagnetic induction. It transforms received vibration energy into electrical voltage. Relative motion between a magnet and a coil (one attached to the inertial mass and one to the frame) induces an e.m.f.</p><p>FIGURE 14.1 — (a) Magnet and coil connected to a galvanometer. (b) Electromagnetic induction in linked primary and secondary coils.</p><p>FIGURE — Electromagnetic seismometer: coil, suspension springs and permanent magnet.</p>",
    "theory": "If the magnet is moved away from the coil, the galvanometer again shows a deflection, but in the opposite direction. If the magnet is held steady and the coil of wire is moved toward or away from the magnet, an e.m.f. is again induced and a current flows.\nIf the magnet is held stationary inside the coil, although flux links the coil, there is no change in flux and hence no e.m.f. is induced. Therefore the galvanometer shows zero deflection. If the magnet is moved faster, a greater deflection is observed.\nIn each case, an e.m.f. is induced in a circuit when the magnetic flux through the circuit changes with time. It does not matter whether the magnet or the coil moves. Their relative motion is what counts.\nTIP — The basic requirement for electromagnetic induction is a change in flux linking the conductor (or coil).\nWhen the conductor is stationary and the magnet is moving, the e.m.f. induced in this way is called statically induced e.m.f.\nA similar effect can be produced using the apparatus shown in Figure 14.1(b). It consists of a coil connected to a switch and a battery. This is the primary coil and its circuit is the primary circuit. A second coil, the secondary coil, is connected to an ammeter. Its circuit is the secondary circuit. There is no source of e.m.f. in the secondary circuit.\nWhen the switch in the primary circuit is suddenly closed, the ammeter measures a current in the secondary circuit and then returns to zero. The two coils are magnetically linked by a common magnetic flux. When the switch is opened again, the ammeter reads a current in the opposite direction and again returns to zero.\nWhenever there is a steady current in the primary circuit, the ammeter reads zero. Current in the secondary circuit occurs only for an instant while the magnetic field through the secondary coil is changing. Closing and opening the switch induces the current. It is the change in magnetic field that creates the current; a changing magnetic field induces an e.m.f.\nAPPLICATIONS OF ELECTROMAGNETIC INDUCTION — To measure earthquakes, a device called a seismometer is used.\nAn electromagnetic seismometer works on the principle of electromagnetic induction. It transforms received vibration energy into electrical voltage. Relative motion between a magnet and a coil (one attached to the inertial mass and one to the frame) induces an e.m.f.\nFIGURE 14.1 — (a) Magnet and coil connected to a galvanometer. (b) Electromagnetic induction in linked primary and secondary coils.\nFIGURE — Electromagnetic seismometer: coil, suspension springs and permanent magnet.",
