    "id": "phys14-lenz-law-conservation-energy-examples141-142-132-133",
    "title": "Lenz’s law, conservation of energy and Faraday-law examples",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>The coil resembles a south pole and repels an approaching magnet. A changing magnetic flux induces current through the coil, which itself produces its own magnetic field. According to Lenz’s law, the direction of induced current opposes the change in flux that created it.</p><p>On the other hand, if the south pole of the magnet is moved away from the coil, as in Figure 14.3(b), the magnetic flux linked with the coil decreases. The coil resembles a north pole and attracts the magnet when it is pulled away.</p><p>The direction of induced current must be such that its own magnetic field is directed to oppose the changing flux caused by the field of the approaching magnet. The direction can be determined by applying the right-hand rule if the magnetic polarity of the coil is known.</p><p>Similarly, when the north pole of the magnet is moved toward or away from the coil, repulsion and attraction take place between the coil and moving magnet and always oppose the magnet’s motion, as illustrated in Figure 14.3(c) and (d).</p><p>Thus Lenz’s law states that the polarity of induced e.m.f. is such that it tends to produce a current which opposes the change in magnetic flux that produced it.</p><p>FIGURE 14.3(b) — A south pole moves away from the coil; linked flux decreases.</p><p>FIGURE 14.3(c)–(d) — A north pole moves toward or away from the coil; induced current opposes the magnet’s motion.</p><p>14.3.1 LENZ’S LAW AND CONSERVATION OF ENERGY</p><p>Lenz’s law is a consequence of the law of conservation of energy. For example, when the north pole of a magnet approaches the coil, the repulsive force acts on the bar magnet due to the current induced in the coil. The motion of the magnet is opposed. The mechanical energy spent overcoming this opposition appears in the coil as electrical energy. Thus Lenz’s law is consistent with the law of conservation of energy.</p><p>EXAMPLE 14.1</p><p>At what rate must a single conductor cut the flux so that a current of 1.2 mA flows through it when a 10 Ω resistor is connected across its ends?</p><p>GIVEN — Current I = 1.2 mA; resistor R = 10 Ω; single conductor, N = 1.</p><p>REQUIRED — Time rate of change of flux, ΔΦ/Δt = ?</p><p>SOLUTION — ε = N (ΔΦ/Δt).</p><p>Here ε = IR = 1.2 × 10⁻³ × 10 = 1.2 × 10⁻² V.</p><p>ΔΦ/Δt = ε/N = (1.2 × 10⁻²)/1 = 1.2 × 10⁻² Wb/s.</p><p>EXAMPLE 14.2</p><p>A coil of mean area 500 cm² and having 1000 turns is held perpendicular to a uniform field of 0.4 gauss. The coil is turned through 180° in 1/10 s. Calculate the average induced e.m.f.</p><p>FIGURE 14.3(b)–(d) — Induced-current direction and magnetic-flux response for a moving magnet.</p>",
    "theory": "The coil resembles a south pole and repels an approaching magnet. A changing magnetic flux induces current through the coil, which itself produces its own magnetic field. According to Lenz’s law, the direction of induced current opposes the change in flux that created it.\nOn the other hand, if the south pole of the magnet is moved away from the coil, as in Figure 14.3(b), the magnetic flux linked with the coil decreases. The coil resembles a north pole and attracts the magnet when it is pulled away.\nThe direction of induced current must be such that its own magnetic field is directed to oppose the changing flux caused by the field of the approaching magnet. The direction can be determined by applying the right-hand rule if the magnetic polarity of the coil is known.\nSimilarly, when the north pole of the magnet is moved toward or away from the coil, repulsion and attraction take place between the coil and moving magnet and always oppose the magnet’s motion, as illustrated in Figure 14.3(c) and (d).\nThus Lenz’s law states that the polarity of induced e.m.f. is such that it tends to produce a current which opposes the change in magnetic flux that produced it.\nFIGURE 14.3(b) — A south pole moves away from the coil; linked flux decreases.\nFIGURE 14.3(c)–(d) — A north pole moves toward or away from the coil; induced current opposes the magnet’s motion.\n14.3.1 LENZ’S LAW AND CONSERVATION OF ENERGY\nLenz’s law is a consequence of the law of conservation of energy. For example, when the north pole of a magnet approaches the coil, the repulsive force acts on the bar magnet due to the current induced in the coil. The motion of the magnet is opposed. The mechanical energy spent overcoming this opposition appears in the coil as electrical energy. Thus Lenz’s law is consistent with the law of conservation of energy.\nEXAMPLE 14.1\nAt what rate must a single conductor cut the flux so that a current of 1.2 mA flows through it when a 10 Ω resistor is connected across its ends?\nGIVEN — Current I = 1.2 mA; resistor R = 10 Ω; single conductor, N = 1.\nREQUIRED — Time rate of change of flux, ΔΦ/Δt = ?\nSOLUTION — ε = N (ΔΦ/Δt).\nHere ε = IR = 1.2 × 10⁻³ × 10 = 1.2 × 10⁻² V.\nΔΦ/Δt = ε/N = (1.2 × 10⁻²)/1 = 1.2 × 10⁻² Wb/s.\nEXAMPLE 14.2\nA coil of mean area 500 cm² and having 1000 turns is held perpendicular to a uniform field of 0.4 gauss. The coil is turned through 180° in 1/10 s. Calculate the average induced e.m.f.\nFIGURE 14.3(b)–(d) — Induced-current direction and magnetic-flux response for a moving magnet.",
