    "paragraphs": [
      "In P-type material, a neighboring valence electron can acquire energy and form a bond, leaving a hole. A boron atom that acquires an additional electron becomes a negative ion; the hole can move through the valence band, but the impurity ion is immobile. Impurity atoms that accept valence-band electrons are acceptor atoms. They produce holes without simultaneously producing conduction-band electrons. Intrinsic excitation also produces electrons and holes at ordinary temperatures, but holes outnumber electrons. Holes are majority carriers and electrons minority carriers, so the extrinsic semiconductor is P-type (P denotes the positive sign of its majority carriers).",
      "17.4 PN JUNCTION — A junction diode is formed by placing a P-type crystal in contact with an N-type crystal under high pressure so that they become a single piece. At the junction, electrons from N fill vacancies in P; negative and positive ions form. Their electric field points from the N side to the P side and opposes further diffusion of electrons and holes, maintaining charge separation. The junction region is free of mobile charges and is called the depletion region or layer. Its width depends on carrier concentration. A potential wall or barrier is formed at the junction. Figure 17.6 shows the diode junction and symbol; Figure 17.7 shows the depletion width and potential barrier.",
      "When the P region is connected to positive potential relative to the N region (forward bias), the potential drop is increased slowly and the junction barrier decreases. At the knee voltage, majority carriers cross the junction and forward current flows. If P is negative relative to N (reverse bias), no majority carriers cross; only a small current due to minority carriers flows, called reverse current, generally of the order of a few microamperes. Figures 17.8 and 17.9 show forward- and reverse-bias junctions and their current behavior."
    ]
