    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>The electrons completely occupy the valence band, leaving the conduction band vacant. The semiconductor energy-band structure has a valence band and a conduction band separated by the band-gap energy E_g (Figure 17.2a). Valence-band electrons do not possess enough energy to jump into the conduction band. Figure 17.1 represents the silicon or germanium crystal as a two-dimensional lattice: +4 symbols indicate the inner cores and the neighboring atoms share covalent bonds with bonding electrons. Therefore, an externally applied electric field cannot cause current in a semiconductor near 0 K; it behaves as an insulator.</p><p>17.2 SEMICONDUCTORS AT HIGH TEMPERATURE — Above 0 K, covalent bonds begin to break as they absorb thermal energy, producing mobile electrons and holes. The band-gap energy is E_g=0.72 eV for Ge and E_g=1.1 eV for Si at room temperature (300 K). An electron that gains at least E_g is excited from the valence band to the conduction band; E_g is the minimum energy required for this transition. Breaking a covalent bond leaves an empty state, called a hole, in the valence band (Figure 17.3). A neighboring valence electron can fill a hole, leaving a hole at its former position, so holes move opposite to electrons and participate in electrical conduction. Electrons move in the conduction band and holes in the valence band.</p><p>An intrinsic semiconductor is one in which free electrons and holes are created only by excitation of electrons from the valence band to the conduction band. At the bottom of printed page 233 the source continues, “If N denotes the number density of …”; the definition is continued on the next page.</p>",
