    "content": "<p>For an intrinsic semiconductor, if N is the number density of electrons in the conduction band and P the number density of holes in the valence band, then N=P.</p><p>17.3 DOPING OF IMPURITIES — Intrinsic semiconductors have low conductivity. Adding a small amount of impurity greatly increases conductivity. Adding an impurity to an intrinsic semiconductor is called doping; the impurity is a dopant. A doped semiconductor is extrinsic. Pentavalent Group V atoms or trivalent Group III atoms are used as dopants. Their atoms are nearly the same size as silicon or germanium atoms and substitute for host atoms without distorting the crystal structure. The two kinds of extrinsic semiconductor are N-type and P-type, depending on the impurity group.</p><p>17.3.1 N-TYPE SEMICONDUCTOR — An N-type semiconductor is made by doping a pure semiconductor with a pentavalent impurity, normally phosphorus or arsenic. Suppose a phosphorus atom substitutes for a silicon atom. Phosphorus has five valence electrons. Only four participate in bonding; the fifth remains loosely bound to the atom (Figure 17.4).</p><p>The impurity atoms are donor atoms because they contribute electrons to the conduction band without producing holes in the valence band. At very low temperature, donors are not ionized and the conduction band is empty. At slightly higher temperature, donor electrons populate the conduction band. At ordinary temperatures, some valence-band electrons are also excited by the intrinsic process, but the holes are fewer. Electrons are the majority carriers and holes the minority carriers; because the majority carriers are negative, this is an N-type extrinsic semiconductor (N denotes their negative sign).</p><p>17.3.2 P-TYPE SEMICONDUCTOR — A P-type semiconductor is made by doping an intrinsic semiconductor with a trivalent element such as boron or aluminum. When a trivalent impurity atom replaces a silicon atom, it lacks one electron to complete four covalent bonds with its neighbors. Replacing a host silicon atom with boron does not disturb the neutral environment around the boron atom. Figure 17.5 illustrates the acceptor impurity and the hole.</p>",
    "theory": "For an intrinsic semiconductor, if N is the number density of electrons in the conduction band and P the number density of holes in the valence band, then N=P.\n17.3 DOPING OF IMPURITIES — Intrinsic semiconductors have low conductivity. Adding a small amount of impurity greatly increases conductivity. Adding an impurity to an intrinsic semiconductor is called doping; the impurity is a dopant. A doped semiconductor is extrinsic. Pentavalent Group V atoms or trivalent Group III atoms are used as dopants. Their atoms are nearly the same size as silicon or germanium atoms and substitute for host atoms without distorting the crystal structure. The two kinds of extrinsic semiconductor are N-type and P-type, depending on the impurity group.\n17.3.1 N-TYPE SEMICONDUCTOR — An N-type semiconductor is made by doping a pure semiconductor with a pentavalent impurity, normally phosphorus or arsenic. Suppose a phosphorus atom substitutes for a silicon atom. Phosphorus has five valence electrons. Only four participate in bonding; the fifth remains loosely bound to the atom (Figure 17.4).\nThe impurity atoms are donor atoms because they contribute electrons to the conduction band without producing holes in the valence band. At very low temperature, donors are not ionized and the conduction band is empty. At slightly higher temperature, donor electrons populate the conduction band. At ordinary temperatures, some valence-band electrons are also excited by the intrinsic process, but the holes are fewer. Electrons are the majority carriers and holes the minority carriers; because the majority carriers are negative, this is an N-type extrinsic semiconductor (N denotes their negative sign).\n17.3.2 P-TYPE SEMICONDUCTOR — A P-type semiconductor is made by doping an intrinsic semiconductor with a trivalent element such as boron or aluminum. When a trivalent impurity atom replaces a silicon atom, it lacks one electron to complete four covalent bonds with its neighbors. Replacing a host silicon atom with boron does not disturb the neutral environment around the boron atom. Figure 17.5 illustrates the acceptor impurity and the hole.",
