    "content": "<p>In a center-tap full-wave rectifier, diodes D1 and D2 operate alternately. During the first half-cycle, point A is positive relative to B and B is positive relative to C; D1 is forward biased and D2 reverse biased, so load current is due to D1. In the second half-cycle, C is positive relative to B and B positive relative to A; D2 conducts and D1 is off. Current therefore passes through the load during both half-cycles and the full wave is rectified. One disadvantage is the need for a transformer with a center-tapped secondary winding (Figure 17.12).</p><p>17.6 TRANSISTOR — A transistor consists of three regions of doped semiconductors in which current flow is modulated by voltage or current applied to one or more electrodes. Modern transistors are bipolar or unipolar; a bipolar transistor&#x27;s operation involves both majority and minority carriers, while a unipolar transistor depends on majority carriers. This unit studies bipolar junction transistors (BJTs). Two PN junctions form either a PNP or NPN transistor. Electrons are majority carriers in NPN and holes in PNP.</p><p>The first region is the emitter, heavily doped to emit charges. The last region collects charges and is called the collector. The middle region is thin and lightly doped and is called the base (Figure 17.13). The symbol arrow indicates current direction. There are two junctions: the emitter junction and collector junction. In operation, the emitter junction is forward biased and the collector junction reverse biased. The forward-biased emitter junction has low resistance; emitted charges are attracted toward the collector. The reverse-biased collector junction has a large potential gradient that attracts charges. If the collector is open (not connected to a supply), charges return via the base. Most charges are collected by the collector, so collector current I_C is almost equal to emitter current I_E. Some charges cannot reach the collector and move via the base, constituting the small base current I_B. Therefore,</p>",
    "theory": "In a center-tap full-wave rectifier, diodes D1 and D2 operate alternately. During the first half-cycle, point A is positive relative to B and B is positive relative to C; D1 is forward biased and D2 reverse biased, so load current is due to D1. In the second half-cycle, C is positive relative to B and B positive relative to A; D2 conducts and D1 is off. Current therefore passes through the load during both half-cycles and the full wave is rectified. One disadvantage is the need for a transformer with a center-tapped secondary winding (Figure 17.12).\n17.6 TRANSISTOR — A transistor consists of three regions of doped semiconductors in which current flow is modulated by voltage or current applied to one or more electrodes. Modern transistors are bipolar or unipolar; a bipolar transistor's operation involves both majority and minority carriers, while a unipolar transistor depends on majority carriers. This unit studies bipolar junction transistors (BJTs). Two PN junctions form either a PNP or NPN transistor. Electrons are majority carriers in NPN and holes in PNP.\nThe first region is the emitter, heavily doped to emit charges. The last region collects charges and is called the collector. The middle region is thin and lightly doped and is called the base (Figure 17.13). The symbol arrow indicates current direction. There are two junctions: the emitter junction and collector junction. In operation, the emitter junction is forward biased and the collector junction reverse biased. The forward-biased emitter junction has low resistance; emitted charges are attracted toward the collector. The reverse-biased collector junction has a large potential gradient that attracts charges. If the collector is open (not connected to a supply), charges return via the base. Most charges are collected by the collector, so collector current I_C is almost equal to emitter current I_E. Some charges cannot reach the collector and move via the base, constituting the small base current I_B. Therefore,",
