    "id": "phys17-transistor-current-and-common-base-244-245",
    "title": "Transistor current relation and common-base configuration",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>The transistor base is thin and lightly doped, so fewer charges recombine and carrier transit time is short. PNP and NPN transistors operate in the same way except for bias polarity. NPN transistors are often preferred because electron mobility is about three times that of holes, so operation is faster. The transistor current relation is I_E=I_B+I_C (Equation 17.2).</p><p>17.7 TYPES OF CONFIGURATIONS — In a transistor circuit, one electrode is common to both input and output. The three configurations are common base (CB), common emitter (CE) and common collector (CC) (Figure 17.14).</p><p>17.7.1 COMMON BASE CONFIGURATION — In common-base operation the base is common to input and output; the emitter is the input terminal and the collector the output terminal. The base is grounded. Supply voltage between base and emitter is denoted V_BE; voltage between collector and base is V_CB. Figure 17.14 shows the circuit and NPN layer arrangement.</p><p>The common-base output characteristic has three operating regions (Figure 17.15). It is active when the base-emitter junction is forward biased and the collector-base junction reverse biased. It is cut-off when both junctions are reverse biased; output current is zero. It is saturation when both junctions are forward biased. In common-emitter configuration, the emitter is common to input and output; the base is input and collector output. The emitter is grounded, so this is also called grounded-emitter configuration.</p>",
    "theory": "The transistor base is thin and lightly doped, so fewer charges recombine and carrier transit time is short. PNP and NPN transistors operate in the same way except for bias polarity. NPN transistors are often preferred because electron mobility is about three times that of holes, so operation is faster. The transistor current relation is I_E=I_B+I_C (Equation 17.2).\n17.7 TYPES OF CONFIGURATIONS — In a transistor circuit, one electrode is common to both input and output. The three configurations are common base (CB), common emitter (CE) and common collector (CC) (Figure 17.14).\n17.7.1 COMMON BASE CONFIGURATION — In common-base operation the base is common to input and output; the emitter is the input terminal and the collector the output terminal. The base is grounded. Supply voltage between base and emitter is denoted V_BE; voltage between collector and base is V_CB. Figure 17.14 shows the circuit and NPN layer arrangement.\nThe common-base output characteristic has three operating regions (Figure 17.15). It is active when the base-emitter junction is forward biased and the collector-base junction reverse biased. It is cut-off when both junctions are reverse biased; output current is zero. It is saturation when both junctions are forward biased. In common-emitter configuration, the emitter is common to input and output; the base is input and collector output. The emitter is grounded, so this is also called grounded-emitter configuration.",
