    "content": "<p>KEY POINTS — Semiconductors have conductivity between conductors and insulators (e.g. silicon and germanium). An intrinsic semiconductor is chemically pure. In N-type material electrons are majority carriers; in P-type material holes are majority carriers. N-type material is formed by doping a pure semiconductor with a pentavalent impurity; P-type with a trivalent impurity. Rectification converts AC into DC; a rectifier is the device that performs it. Transistor types are PNP and NPN. Amplification increases voltage or current level.</p><p>MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS (1–8). 1. In N-type silicon, which is true? (a) electrons majority, trivalent dopants (b) electrons minority, pentavalent dopants (c) holes minority, pentavalent dopants (d) holes majority, trivalent dopants. 2. Silicon diode barrier potential at room temperature: (a) 0.3 V (b) 0.7 V (c) 0.9 V (d) 1.0 V. 3. Depletion-region width: (a) increases under forward bias (b) independent of applied voltage (c) increases under reverse bias (d) remains same. 4. NPN common-emitter current gain: (a) I_C/I_B (b) I_C/I_E (c) I_E/I_B (d) I_B/I_E. 5. Reverse saturation current is due to (a) majority carriers (b) minority carriers (c) acceptor ions (d) donor ions. 6. A reverse-biased PN junction acts as (a) capacitor (b) ON switch (c) inductor (d) OFF switch. 7. Improper transistor biasing produces (a) heavy loading of emitter current (b) distortion in output signal (c) excessive heat at collector terminal (d) faulty location of load line. 8. Transistors in digital circuits usually operate in (a) active region (b) breakdown region (c) saturation and cut-off regions (d) linear region.</p><p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (1–9). 1. Why is a transistor base thin and lightly doped, and the collector large? 2. Why is base current weak compared with collector current? 3. Why is the emitter-base junction forward biased and collector-base junction reverse biased? 4. How do NPN and PNP transistors work? 5. Distinguish N-type and P-type semiconductors. 6. A P-type semiconductor has many holes but is electrically neutral. Why? 7. Why is CE configuration widely used in amplifier circuits? 8. Why is a transistor called a current-amplification device? 9. A doped semiconductor has 10^10 silicon atoms and 10 trivalent atoms. At 25°C, how many free electrons and holes are inside it?</p><p>COMPREHENSIVE QUESTION 1 — Describe the energy-band structure of an insulator, semiconductor and conductor.</p>",
    "theory": "KEY POINTS — Semiconductors have conductivity between conductors and insulators (e.g. silicon and germanium). An intrinsic semiconductor is chemically pure. In N-type material electrons are majority carriers; in P-type material holes are majority carriers. N-type material is formed by doping a pure semiconductor with a pentavalent impurity; P-type with a trivalent impurity. Rectification converts AC into DC; a rectifier is the device that performs it. Transistor types are PNP and NPN. Amplification increases voltage or current level.\nMULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS (1–8). 1. In N-type silicon, which is true? (a) electrons majority, trivalent dopants (b) electrons minority, pentavalent dopants (c) holes minority, pentavalent dopants (d) holes majority, trivalent dopants. 2. Silicon diode barrier potential at room temperature: (a) 0.3 V (b) 0.7 V (c) 0.9 V (d) 1.0 V. 3. Depletion-region width: (a) increases under forward bias (b) independent of applied voltage (c) increases under reverse bias (d) remains same. 4. NPN common-emitter current gain: (a) I_C/I_B (b) I_C/I_E (c) I_E/I_B (d) I_B/I_E. 5. Reverse saturation current is due to (a) majority carriers (b) minority carriers (c) acceptor ions (d) donor ions. 6. A reverse-biased PN junction acts as (a) capacitor (b) ON switch (c) inductor (d) OFF switch. 7. Improper transistor biasing produces (a) heavy loading of emitter current (b) distortion in output signal (c) excessive heat at collector terminal (d) faulty location of load line. 8. Transistors in digital circuits usually operate in (a) active region (b) breakdown region (c) saturation and cut-off regions (d) linear region.\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (1–9). 1. Why is a transistor base thin and lightly doped, and the collector large? 2. Why is base current weak compared with collector current? 3. Why is the emitter-base junction forward biased and collector-base junction reverse biased? 4. How do NPN and PNP transistors work? 5. Distinguish N-type and P-type semiconductors. 6. A P-type semiconductor has many holes but is electrically neutral. Why? 7. Why is CE configuration widely used in amplifier circuits? 8. Why is a transistor called a current-amplification device? 9. A doped semiconductor has 10^10 silicon atoms and 10 trivalent atoms. At 25°C, how many free electrons and holes are inside it?\nCOMPREHENSIVE QUESTION 1 — Describe the energy-band structure of an insulator, semiconductor and conductor.",
