    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS (9–12). 9. Most electrons in the base of an NPN transistor flow (a) out of the base lead (b) into the collector (c) into the emitter (d) into the base supply. 10. In a transistor, collector current is controlled by (a) collector voltage (b) base current (c) collector resistance (d) all of the above. 11. The minimum number of diodes required for full-wave rectification is (a) 3 (b) 4 (c) 2 (d) 1. 12. If both collector-base and base-emitter junctions are reverse biased, the transistor is in (a) active region (b) cut-off region (c) saturation region (d) Q-point.</p><p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (1–9). 1. Why is the transistor base thin and lightly doped, and collector large? 2. Why is base current weak compared with collector current? 3. Why is emitter-base junction forward biased and collector-base junction reverse biased? 4. How do NPN and PNP transistors work? 5. Distinguish N-type and P-type semiconductors. 6. A P-type semiconductor has many holes but is electrically neutral. Why? 7. Why is CE configuration widely used in amplifier circuits? 8. Why is a transistor called a current-amplification device? 9. A doped semiconductor has 10^10 silicon atoms and 10 trivalent atoms. At 25°C, how many free electrons and holes are present?</p><p>COMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (1–8). 1. Describe energy-band structure of insulators, semiconductors and conductors. 2. Explain the significance of the depletion layer at equilibrium in a PN junction and give an energy-band diagram. 3. Explain how a PN junction acts as a half-wave rectifier. 4. Explain transistor operation as an amplifier. 5. Draw half-wave and full-wave rectifier circuits. 6. Compare advantages and disadvantages of full-wave and half-wave rectifiers. 7. Derive the relation between α and β of a transistor. 8. Discuss extrinsic-semiconductor conductivity and its band-gap energy.</p><p>NUMERICAL PROBLEMS (1–5). 1. A transistor has I_C=10 mA and I_B=40 μA. Find current gain. Printed answer: 250. 2. I_B=100 μA and β=100. Find I_C, I_E and I_C/I_E. Printed answers: 10 mA, 10.1 mA, 0.99. 3. A CE transistor has R_C=3 kΩ and voltage drop 6 V; α=0.97. Find base current. Printed answer: 2.06×10⁻³ A. 4. In a CB circuit α=0.96 and I_C=60 μA. Find emitter current. Printed answer: 62.5 μA. 5. In a transistor I_E=3.5 A and I_C=2.35 A. Find base current. Printed answer: 1.15 A.</p>",
    "theory": "MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS (9–12). 9. Most electrons in the base of an NPN transistor flow (a) out of the base lead (b) into the collector (c) into the emitter (d) into the base supply. 10. In a transistor, collector current is controlled by (a) collector voltage (b) base current (c) collector resistance (d) all of the above. 11. The minimum number of diodes required for full-wave rectification is (a) 3 (b) 4 (c) 2 (d) 1. 12. If both collector-base and base-emitter junctions are reverse biased, the transistor is in (a) active region (b) cut-off region (c) saturation region (d) Q-point.\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (1–9). 1. Why is the transistor base thin and lightly doped, and collector large? 2. Why is base current weak compared with collector current? 3. Why is emitter-base junction forward biased and collector-base junction reverse biased? 4. How do NPN and PNP transistors work? 5. Distinguish N-type and P-type semiconductors. 6. A P-type semiconductor has many holes but is electrically neutral. Why? 7. Why is CE configuration widely used in amplifier circuits? 8. Why is a transistor called a current-amplification device? 9. A doped semiconductor has 10^10 silicon atoms and 10 trivalent atoms. At 25°C, how many free electrons and holes are present?\nCOMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (1–8). 1. Describe energy-band structure of insulators, semiconductors and conductors. 2. Explain the significance of the depletion layer at equilibrium in a PN junction and give an energy-band diagram. 3. Explain how a PN junction acts as a half-wave rectifier. 4. Explain transistor operation as an amplifier. 5. Draw half-wave and full-wave rectifier circuits. 6. Compare advantages and disadvantages of full-wave and half-wave rectifiers. 7. Derive the relation between α and β of a transistor. 8. Discuss extrinsic-semiconductor conductivity and its band-gap energy.\nNUMERICAL PROBLEMS (1–5). 1. A transistor has I_C=10 mA and I_B=40 μA. Find current gain. Printed answer: 250. 2. I_B=100 μA and β=100. Find I_C, I_E and I_C/I_E. Printed answers: 10 mA, 10.1 mA, 0.99. 3. A CE transistor has R_C=3 kΩ and voltage drop 6 V; α=0.97. Find base current. Printed answer: 2.06×10⁻³ A. 4. In a CB circuit α=0.96 and I_C=60 μA. Find emitter current. Printed answer: 62.5 μA. 5. In a transistor I_E=3.5 A and I_C=2.35 A. Find base current. Printed answer: 1.15 A.",
