    "id": "phys17-electronics-opening-and-intrinsic-semiconductor-230-231",
    "title": "Unit 17 opening, learning outcomes and intrinsic semiconductor",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>UNIT 17 ELECTRONICS — Opening question: How are semiconductors an essential part of electronics? Electronics studies circuits that control electron flow or other charged particles; semiconductor technology has made devices smaller, faster and more reliable. Learning outcomes: distinguish intrinsic/extrinsic semiconductors; distinguish P- and N-type materials; explain holes and electrons and their motion across a junction; describe PN junction and forward/reverse bias; explain rectification and half-/full-wave diode rectifiers; distinguish PNP/NPN transistors; describe transistor operation and current equation; solve transistor problems; explain transistor use as switch and amplifier.</p><p>17.1 SEMICONDUCTOR — Electronics controls electron/charge flow through non-metal conductors called semiconductors. Semiconductors have electrical conductivity between conductors and insulators. A perfect pure crystal without impurities is an intrinsic semiconductor; the text considers a semiconductor pure if it has fewer than one impurity atom per billion host atoms. In silicon at low temperature, covalent bonds are intact (Figure 17.1). Figure 17.2 shows the valence band E_v, conduction band E_c and band gap E_g. Near 0 K, electrons are bound in the valence band and an external field cannot cause current, so the semiconductor behaves as an insulator. On PDF page 121 the next visible text ends mid-sentence: “If N denotes the number density of …”; the next page begins its band explanation.</p><p>INTEGRATED CIRCUIT INFORMATION — An integrated circuit (IC), chip or microchip is a semiconductor wafer carrying thousands or millions of tiny resistors, capacitors and transistors. An IC can function as amplifier, oscillator, timer, counter, computer memory or microprocessor. Digital devices and their electronic networks are called digital circuits/electronics; calculators, watches, communication systems and computers are common applications.</p>",
    "theory": "UNIT 17 ELECTRONICS — Opening question: How are semiconductors an essential part of electronics? Electronics studies circuits that control electron flow or other charged particles; semiconductor technology has made devices smaller, faster and more reliable. Learning outcomes: distinguish intrinsic/extrinsic semiconductors; distinguish P- and N-type materials; explain holes and electrons and their motion across a junction; describe PN junction and forward/reverse bias; explain rectification and half-/full-wave diode rectifiers; distinguish PNP/NPN transistors; describe transistor operation and current equation; solve transistor problems; explain transistor use as switch and amplifier.\n17.1 SEMICONDUCTOR — Electronics controls electron/charge flow through non-metal conductors called semiconductors. Semiconductors have electrical conductivity between conductors and insulators. A perfect pure crystal without impurities is an intrinsic semiconductor; the text considers a semiconductor pure if it has fewer than one impurity atom per billion host atoms. In silicon at low temperature, covalent bonds are intact (Figure 17.1). Figure 17.2 shows the valence band E_v, conduction band E_c and band gap E_g. Near 0 K, electrons are bound in the valence band and an external field cannot cause current, so the semiconductor behaves as an insulator. On PDF page 121 the next visible text ends mid-sentence: “If N denotes the number density of …”; the next page begins its band explanation.\nINTEGRATED CIRCUIT INFORMATION — An integrated circuit (IC), chip or microchip is a semiconductor wafer carrying thousands or millions of tiny resistors, capacitors and transistors. An IC can function as amplifier, oscillator, timer, counter, computer memory or microprocessor. Digital devices and their electronic networks are called digital circuits/electronics; calculators, watches, communication systems and computers are common applications.",
