    "content": "<p>UNIT 18 DAWN OF THE MODERN PHYSICS — Tardigrades (water bears) grow only up to 1.5 mm. How are high-resolution images of such small creatures possible?</p><p>distinguish between inertial and non-inertial frames of reference.</p><p>describe the significance of Einstein’s assumption of the constancy of the speed of light.</p><p>identify that if c is constant then space and time become relative.</p><p>explain qualitatively and quantitatively the consequences of special relativity in relation to:</p><p>the relativity of simultaneity</p><p>the equivalence between mass and energy</p><p>length contraction</p><p>time dilation</p><p>mass increase</p><p>explain the implications of mass increase, time dilation and length contraction for space travel.</p><p>describe the concept of black body radiation.</p><p>describe how energy is distributed over the wavelength range for several values of source temperature.</p><p>describe Planck’s hypothesis that radiation emitted and absorbed by the walls of a black-body cavity is quantised.</p><p>elaborate the particle nature of electromagnetic radiation.</p><p>describe the phenomenon of photoelectric effect.</p><p>solve problems and analyse information using E = hf and c = fλ.</p><p>identify data sources, gather, process and present information to summarise the use of the photoelectric effect in solar cells and photocells.</p><p>describe the confirmation of de Broglie’s proposal by the Davisson–Germer experiment, in which diffraction of electrons by the surface layers of a crystal lattice was observed.</p><p>describe the impact of de Broglie’s proposal that any kind of particle has both wave and particle properties.</p><p>explain the particle model of light in terms of photons with particular energy and frequency.</p><p>describe the Compton effect qualitatively.</p><p>explain the phenomena of pair production and pair annihilation.</p><p>explain how the very short wavelength of electrons, and the ability to use electric and magnetic fields to focus them, allows an electron microscope to achieve very high resolution.</p><p>describe the uncertainty principle.</p><p>At the end of the 19th century, scientists thought they had discovered nearly everything about physics. Newton’s laws of motion and universal gravitation, Maxwell’s theoretical work combining electricity and magnetism, and the laws of thermodynamics effectively explained all known physics.</p>",
