    "id": "phys18-reference-frames-and-relativity-context-256-257",
    "title": "Reference frames, GPS clock example and inertial frame",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>At the turn of the 20th century, two major concepts improved our understanding of nature. In 1900 Planck provided basic ideas leading to quantum theory; in 1905 Einstein formulated special relativity. These theories inspired developments in atomic, nuclear and condensed-matter physics.</p><p>GPS INFORMATION — According to relativity, a moving clock appears to run slowly compared with a similar clock at rest. GPS satellites move constantly relative to observers on Earth, which causes them to run at a slightly faster rate than clocks on Earth’s surface. General-relativity calculations predict that GPS satellite clocks should get ahead of ground-based clocks by 45 microseconds per day. The special theory of relativity is therefore important in GPS.</p><p>18.1 REFERENCE FRAMES — A reference frame is a coordinate system in which an experimenter measures the position and time of physical events. In special relativity, an event is a physical happening at a particular place and time. To record it, the experimenter uses a frame consisting of x, y and z axes and a time-measuring device such as a clock. Reference frames specify the relationship between a moving observer and an event. In some situations, it is impractical to choose a frame with a fixed origin.</p><p>A jet landing on an aircraft carrier at full throttle illustrates a moving reference frame. To describe the plane’s motion, use a coordinate system fixed to the carrier even while it moves. This is useful because the plane must come to rest relative to the carrier at a fixed position on the deck (Figure 18.1).</p><p>A. INERTIAL REFERENCE FRAME — An inertial frame is one in which an object subjected to no forces moves in a straight line at constant speed. Figure 18.2 illustrates velocities observed in different reference frames.</p>",
    "theory": "At the turn of the 20th century, two major concepts improved our understanding of nature. In 1900 Planck provided basic ideas leading to quantum theory; in 1905 Einstein formulated special relativity. These theories inspired developments in atomic, nuclear and condensed-matter physics.\nGPS INFORMATION — According to relativity, a moving clock appears to run slowly compared with a similar clock at rest. GPS satellites move constantly relative to observers on Earth, which causes them to run at a slightly faster rate than clocks on Earth’s surface. General-relativity calculations predict that GPS satellite clocks should get ahead of ground-based clocks by 45 microseconds per day. The special theory of relativity is therefore important in GPS.\n18.1 REFERENCE FRAMES — A reference frame is a coordinate system in which an experimenter measures the position and time of physical events. In special relativity, an event is a physical happening at a particular place and time. To record it, the experimenter uses a frame consisting of x, y and z axes and a time-measuring device such as a clock. Reference frames specify the relationship between a moving observer and an event. In some situations, it is impractical to choose a frame with a fixed origin.\nA jet landing on an aircraft carrier at full throttle illustrates a moving reference frame. To describe the plane’s motion, use a coordinate system fixed to the carrier even while it moves. This is useful because the plane must come to rest relative to the carrier at a fixed position on the deck (Figure 18.1).\nA. INERTIAL REFERENCE FRAME — An inertial frame is one in which an object subjected to no forces moves in a straight line at constant speed. Figure 18.2 illustrates velocities observed in different reference frames.",
