    "id": "phys18-noninertial-frames-postulates-simultaneity-258-259",
    "title": "Non-inertial frames, Einstein’s postulates and simultaneity",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>B. NON-INERTIAL REFERENCE FRAME — An accelerating frame of reference is called a non-inertial frame. Any frame moving at constant velocity relative to an inertial system is also inertial; therefore, there is no single preferred inertial frame (Figure 18.2).</p><p>The Earth-based frame is not exactly inertial because Earth has centripetal acceleration as it spins on its axis and revolves around the Sun. In most situations these accelerations are small enough to neglect, so Earth’s surface can be considered an inertial frame. For a cricket ball, Earth’s rotation has little effect; for a meteor falling from a great distance, Earth’s rotation must be considered.</p><p>18.2 SPECIAL THEORY OF RELATIVITY — In 1905 Albert Einstein proposed a theory that changed our views of space and time. He based special relativity on two postulates. 1. PRINCIPLE OF RELATIVITY: The laws of physics are the same in all inertial reference frames. Mechanical, thermal, optical or electrical experiments performed in a laboratory at rest must give the same results in a laboratory moving at constant speed past it. For example, pouring tea in an aeroplane moving at constant speed is the same as on Earth.</p><p>2. PRINCIPLE OF CONSTANCY OF THE SPEED OF LIGHT — In vacuum, light has the same speed c=2.99792458×10⁸ m/s in all inertial reference frames, regardless of the velocity of the observer or the light source. This second postulate is difficult to accept because it seems to conflict with everyday experience. Figure 18.3 shows that observers at torches A and B find that light speed is constant and independent of their relative motion; each measures approximately 3×10⁸ m/s.</p><p>18.3 CONSEQUENCES OF SPECIAL THEORY OF RELATIVITY — Some consequences of relativity contradict basic concepts. A. SIMULTANEITY: Two events simultaneous in one frame are generally not simultaneous in a second frame moving relative to the first. Simultaneity is not absolute; it depends on the observer’s motion. Einstein’s thought experiment considers a boxcar moving uniformly while lightning bolts strike its front and back. The marks left on the boxcar are A′ and B′; marks on the ground are A and B (Figure 18.4).</p>",
    "theory": "B. NON-INERTIAL REFERENCE FRAME — An accelerating frame of reference is called a non-inertial frame. Any frame moving at constant velocity relative to an inertial system is also inertial; therefore, there is no single preferred inertial frame (Figure 18.2).\nThe Earth-based frame is not exactly inertial because Earth has centripetal acceleration as it spins on its axis and revolves around the Sun. In most situations these accelerations are small enough to neglect, so Earth’s surface can be considered an inertial frame. For a cricket ball, Earth’s rotation has little effect; for a meteor falling from a great distance, Earth’s rotation must be considered.\n18.2 SPECIAL THEORY OF RELATIVITY — In 1905 Albert Einstein proposed a theory that changed our views of space and time. He based special relativity on two postulates. 1. PRINCIPLE OF RELATIVITY: The laws of physics are the same in all inertial reference frames. Mechanical, thermal, optical or electrical experiments performed in a laboratory at rest must give the same results in a laboratory moving at constant speed past it. For example, pouring tea in an aeroplane moving at constant speed is the same as on Earth.\n2. PRINCIPLE OF CONSTANCY OF THE SPEED OF LIGHT — In vacuum, light has the same speed c=2.99792458×10⁸ m/s in all inertial reference frames, regardless of the velocity of the observer or the light source. This second postulate is difficult to accept because it seems to conflict with everyday experience. Figure 18.3 shows that observers at torches A and B find that light speed is constant and independent of their relative motion; each measures approximately 3×10⁸ m/s.\n18.3 CONSEQUENCES OF SPECIAL THEORY OF RELATIVITY — Some consequences of relativity contradict basic concepts. A. SIMULTANEITY: Two events simultaneous in one frame are generally not simultaneous in a second frame moving relative to the first. Simultaneity is not absolute; it depends on the observer’s motion. Einstein’s thought experiment considers a boxcar moving uniformly while lightning bolts strike its front and back. The marks left on the boxcar are A′ and B′; marks on the ground are A and B (Figure 18.4).",
