    "id": "phys18-photoelectric-effect-experiments-and-stopping-potential-270-271",
    "title": "Photoelectric emission, stopping potential and two experiments",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>18.6 PHOTOELECTRIC EFFECT — When light hits a metal surface and electrons are emitted, the phenomenon is called the photoelectric effect; the emitted electrons are photoelectrons. Light with sufficiently short wavelength directed onto a metal ejects electrons (Figure 18.9). Heinrich Hertz discovered the effect in 1887; Einstein explained it using the photon concept in 1905. In the experimental arrangement (Figure 18.10), light of frequency f strikes a metallic emitter and ejects electrons. A potential difference V is maintained between emitter and collector, producing photocurrent I measured by a sensitive ammeter A.</p><p>FIRST PHOTOELECTRIC EXPERIMENT — Adjust the potential difference V so the collector is slightly negative relative to the emitter; this slows the ejected electrons. V is varied until a value called the stopping potential V₀ makes the sensitive ammeter reading just zero. At V=V₀, the most energetic electrons are turned back before reaching the collector. Their maximum kinetic energy is KE_max=eV₀ (Equation 18.9), where e is elementary charge. For a given light frequency, KE_max does not depend on light intensity.</p><p>Figure 18.11 plots photocurrent I against potential difference V for two light intensities. Doubling intensity doubles current because twice as many photoelectrons are emitted, but stopping potential V₀ remains the same. This is a puzzle for classical wave physics: increasing wave amplitude should give each ejected electron more energy, but that is not observed.</p><p>SECOND PHOTOELECTRIC EXPERIMENT — Vary incident-light frequency f and measure stopping potential V₀ or maximum kinetic energy KE_max. Figure 18.12 plots KE_max against f. The photoelectric effect does not occur below a threshold frequency f₀, or equivalently when wavelength is greater than threshold wavelength λ₀=c/f₀.</p>",
    "theory": "18.6 PHOTOELECTRIC EFFECT — When light hits a metal surface and electrons are emitted, the phenomenon is called the photoelectric effect; the emitted electrons are photoelectrons. Light with sufficiently short wavelength directed onto a metal ejects electrons (Figure 18.9). Heinrich Hertz discovered the effect in 1887; Einstein explained it using the photon concept in 1905. In the experimental arrangement (Figure 18.10), light of frequency f strikes a metallic emitter and ejects electrons. A potential difference V is maintained between emitter and collector, producing photocurrent I measured by a sensitive ammeter A.\nFIRST PHOTOELECTRIC EXPERIMENT — Adjust the potential difference V so the collector is slightly negative relative to the emitter; this slows the ejected electrons. V is varied until a value called the stopping potential V₀ makes the sensitive ammeter reading just zero. At V=V₀, the most energetic electrons are turned back before reaching the collector. Their maximum kinetic energy is KE_max=eV₀ (Equation 18.9), where e is elementary charge. For a given light frequency, KE_max does not depend on light intensity.\nFigure 18.11 plots photocurrent I against potential difference V for two light intensities. Doubling intensity doubles current because twice as many photoelectrons are emitted, but stopping potential V₀ remains the same. This is a puzzle for classical wave physics: increasing wave amplitude should give each ejected electron more energy, but that is not observed.\nSECOND PHOTOELECTRIC EXPERIMENT — Vary incident-light frequency f and measure stopping potential V₀ or maximum kinetic energy KE_max. Figure 18.12 plots KE_max against f. The photoelectric effect does not occur below a threshold frequency f₀, or equivalently when wavelength is greater than threshold wavelength λ₀=c/f₀.",
