        "text": "18.6 PHOTOELECTRIC EFFECT — When light hits a metal surface and electrons are emitted, the phenomenon is called the photoelectric effect; the emitted electrons are photoelectrons. Light with sufficiently short wavelength directed onto a metal ejects electrons (Figure 18.9). Heinrich Hertz discovered the effect in 1887; Einstein explained it using the photon concept in 1905. In the experimental arrangement (Figure 18.10), light of frequency f strikes a metallic emitter and ejects electrons. A potential difference V is maintained between emitter and collector, producing photocurrent I measured by a sensitive ammeter A.",
