    "id": "phys18-electron-microscope-resolution-sem-components-286-287",
    "title": "Electron wavelength results, assignment and electron microscope",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>EXAMPLE 18.5 CONTINUED — Substituting v=√(2qₑΔV/mₑ) into λ=h/(mₑv) and rearranging gives λ=h/√(2mₑqₑΔV). For ΔV₁=50 V, λ₁=0.173×10⁻⁹ m=0.173 nm. For ΔV₂=200 V, λ₂=0.087×10⁻⁹ m=0.087 nm.</p><p>ASSIGNMENT 18.4 — Compute the de Broglie wavelength for: (a) a cricket ball of mass 160 g travelling at 45 m/s; (b) a smoke particle of mass 10⁻⁹ g moving at 1 cm/s; (c) an electron moving at a typical speed of 10⁶ m/s. Printed answers: (a) 9.2×10⁻²⁵ m; (b) 6.6×10⁻²⁰ m; (c) 7.3×10⁻¹⁰ m.</p><p>18.11 ELECTRON MICROSCOPE — An electron microscope (EM) uses an electron beam with wave-like properties to illuminate a specimen and produce a magnified image; it is based on electron wave characteristics. Its resolving power is greater than an optical microscope because electron wavelengths are about 100,000 times shorter than visible-light photon wavelengths. EMs can achieve better than 50 pm resolution and magnification up to about 10,000,000×. Ordinary light microscopes are limited by diffraction to about 200 nm resolution and useful magnifications below 2000×.</p><p>A scanning electron microscope is shown in Figure 18.22 and produces images with three-dimensional quality. An electron gun beam is accelerated by an anode. Condenser lenses (electromagnetic coils) control and define beam size, which sets resolution; the objective lens focuses the beam onto the sample. Back-scattered electrons (BSE) and secondary electrons (SE) used for imaging are collected by corresponding detectors. X-rays generated by electron–matter interaction are also collected. A computer compiles data from all detectors to obtain a magnified object image (Figure 18.23).</p>",
    "theory": "EXAMPLE 18.5 CONTINUED — Substituting v=√(2qₑΔV/mₑ) into λ=h/(mₑv) and rearranging gives λ=h/√(2mₑqₑΔV). For ΔV₁=50 V, λ₁=0.173×10⁻⁹ m=0.173 nm. For ΔV₂=200 V, λ₂=0.087×10⁻⁹ m=0.087 nm.\nASSIGNMENT 18.4 — Compute the de Broglie wavelength for: (a) a cricket ball of mass 160 g travelling at 45 m/s; (b) a smoke particle of mass 10⁻⁹ g moving at 1 cm/s; (c) an electron moving at a typical speed of 10⁶ m/s. Printed answers: (a) 9.2×10⁻²⁵ m; (b) 6.6×10⁻²⁰ m; (c) 7.3×10⁻¹⁰ m.\n18.11 ELECTRON MICROSCOPE — An electron microscope (EM) uses an electron beam with wave-like properties to illuminate a specimen and produce a magnified image; it is based on electron wave characteristics. Its resolving power is greater than an optical microscope because electron wavelengths are about 100,000 times shorter than visible-light photon wavelengths. EMs can achieve better than 50 pm resolution and magnification up to about 10,000,000×. Ordinary light microscopes are limited by diffraction to about 200 nm resolution and useful magnifications below 2000×.\nA scanning electron microscope is shown in Figure 18.22 and produces images with three-dimensional quality. An electron gun beam is accelerated by an anode. Condenser lenses (electromagnetic coils) control and define beam size, which sets resolution; the objective lens focuses the beam onto the sample. Back-scattered electrons (BSE) and secondary electrons (SE) used for imaging are collected by corresponding detectors. X-rays generated by electron–matter interaction are also collected. A computer compiles data from all detectors to obtain a magnified object image (Figure 18.23).",
