    "content": "<p>MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 8 — In Compton scattering, at what angle does the electron carry maximum energy? Initially the target electron is at rest. (A) 0° (B) 90° (C) 135° (D) 180°</p><p>MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 9 — If X-rays scatter from protons instead of electrons, the wavelength change for a given scattering angle: (A) increases (B) decreases (C) is unchanged (D) is one radian</p><p>MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 10 — Pair production cannot take place in vacuum because: (A) mass is not conserved (B) momentum is not conserved (C) energy is not conserved (D) charge is not conserved</p><p>MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 11 — According to de Broglie’s equation, which has the smallest associated wavelength? (A) proton (B) neutron (C) alpha particle (D) electron</p><p>MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 12 — An electron and a proton have the same de Broglie wavelength. The electron’s kinetic energy is: (A) zero (B) infinite (C) equal to the proton’s kinetic energy (D) greater than the proton’s kinetic energy</p><p>MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 13 — In an electron microscope, the light source is replaced by a beam of very fast-moving: (A) electron (B) neutron (C) proton (D) photon</p><p>MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 14 — According to Heisenberg, if momentum-measurement uncertainty is reduced by a factor of 2, by what factor does position uncertainty change? (A) 1/2 (B) 1 (C) 2 (D) 4</p><p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTION 1 — Imagine a world in which the speed of light is only 50 m/s. How would everyday events appear to us?</p><p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTION 2 — Some stars appear reddish and some blue. Which stars have the higher surface temperature? Explain.</p><p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTION 3 — If the photoelectric effect is observed for one metal, can you conclude it will also occur for another metal under the same conditions?</p><p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTION 4 — Does a light bulb at 2500 K produce as white a light as the Sun at 6000 K?</p><p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTION 5 — A red-light beam and a blue-light beam have exactly the same energy. Which contains more photons?</p><p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTION 6 — Why must the rest mass of a photon be zero?</p><p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTION 7 — Why do we not observe the Compton effect with visible light?</p><p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTION 8 — If an electron and proton have the same de Broglie wavelength, which particle has greater speed?</p><p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTION 9 — An electron and a proton are accelerated from rest through the same potential difference. Which particle has the longer wavelength?</p><p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTION 10 — Why is ultraviolet radiation harmful to skin while visible light is not?</p><p>COMPREHENSIVE QUESTION 1 — What are inertial and non-inertial reference frames? Explain with examples.</p><p>COMPREHENSIVE QUESTION 2 — State Einstein’s postulates of special relativity and discuss their various results.</p><p>COMPREHENSIVE QUESTION 3 — What are the main features of thermal radiation from a black body? Discuss Planck’s quantum theory and its importance in physics.</p>",
    "theory": "MULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 8 — In Compton scattering, at what angle does the electron carry maximum energy? Initially the target electron is at rest. (A) 0° (B) 90° (C) 135° (D) 180°\nMULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 9 — If X-rays scatter from protons instead of electrons, the wavelength change for a given scattering angle: (A) increases (B) decreases (C) is unchanged (D) is one radian\nMULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 10 — Pair production cannot take place in vacuum because: (A) mass is not conserved (B) momentum is not conserved (C) energy is not conserved (D) charge is not conserved\nMULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 11 — According to de Broglie’s equation, which has the smallest associated wavelength? (A) proton (B) neutron (C) alpha particle (D) electron\nMULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 12 — An electron and a proton have the same de Broglie wavelength. The electron’s kinetic energy is: (A) zero (B) infinite (C) equal to the proton’s kinetic energy (D) greater than the proton’s kinetic energy\nMULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 13 — In an electron microscope, the light source is replaced by a beam of very fast-moving: (A) electron (B) neutron (C) proton (D) photon\nMULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTION 14 — According to Heisenberg, if momentum-measurement uncertainty is reduced by a factor of 2, by what factor does position uncertainty change? (A) 1/2 (B) 1 (C) 2 (D) 4\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTION 1 — Imagine a world in which the speed of light is only 50 m/s. How would everyday events appear to us?\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTION 2 — Some stars appear reddish and some blue. Which stars have the higher surface temperature? Explain.\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTION 3 — If the photoelectric effect is observed for one metal, can you conclude it will also occur for another metal under the same conditions?\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTION 4 — Does a light bulb at 2500 K produce as white a light as the Sun at 6000 K?\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTION 5 — A red-light beam and a blue-light beam have exactly the same energy. Which contains more photons?\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTION 6 — Why must the rest mass of a photon be zero?\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTION 7 — Why do we not observe the Compton effect with visible light?\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTION 8 — If an electron and proton have the same de Broglie wavelength, which particle has greater speed?\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTION 9 — An electron and a proton are accelerated from rest through the same potential difference. Which particle has the longer wavelength?\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTION 10 — Why is ultraviolet radiation harmful to skin while visible light is not?\nCOMPREHENSIVE QUESTION 1 — What are inertial and non-inertial reference frames? Explain with examples.\nCOMPREHENSIVE QUESTION 2 — State Einstein’s postulates of special relativity and discuss their various results.\nCOMPREHENSIVE QUESTION 3 — What are the main features of thermal radiation from a black body? Discuss Planck’s quantum theory and its importance in physics.",
