      "The D.C. voltage remains constant; D.C. sources include batteries and D.C. generators. In an A.C. generator, electrons first flow in one direction and then the other. A source whose potential difference changes polarity with time is an alternating source; voltage that changes polarity at regular intervals is alternating voltage. Figure 15.1 shows an A.C. source connected to resistance R, with its upper terminal positive and lower terminal negative so current flows.",
      "After some time the source polarities reverse and current flows in the opposite direction; this is alternating current. A.C. voltage has a time-varying sinusoidal waveform (Figure 15.2). The instantaneous sine-wave voltage at any rotation angle is V = Vₘ sin ωt (15.1), where V is instantaneous voltage, Vₘ is maximum voltage and ω is angular velocity of the coil. A sinusoidal voltage or current changes direction regularly and its magnitude changes continuously: the transition between polarities is smooth, changing fastest at zero and slowest at its peak.",
      "15.3 A.C. TERMINOLOGIES — Alternating voltage or current continuously changes magnitude and reverses direction at regular time intervals. It rises from zero to a positive maximum, falls to zero, reaches a maximum in the reverse direction, then returns to zero.",
      "CYCLE — One complete set of positive and negative values of an alternating quantity is a cycle. One cycle of alternating voltage corresponds to 360° or 2π radians.",
