      "15.6.1 POWER LOSS IN A RESISTOR — In a pure resistive circuit the power curve is the product of instantaneous voltage and current. Power is positive except at 0°, 180° and 360°, where it momentarily becomes zero. The source continuously delivers power consumed by the circuit. Average power over one cycle is P = ⟨VI⟩ = ⟨Vₘ sin ωt × Iₘ sin ωt⟩ = VₘIₘ⟨sin²ωt⟩ = VₘIₘ/2 (15.10) = (Vₘ/√2)(Iₘ/√2) = VᵣₘₛIᵣₘₛ (15.11).",
      "EXAMPLE 15.1 — A pure 20 Ω resistance is connected across a 220 V, 50 Hz A.C. supply. Calculate (a) current, (b) power consumed, and (c) equations for voltage and current. GIVEN: f = 50 Hz, R = 20 Ω, V = 220 V. REQUIRED: I, P and voltage/current equations.",
      "SOLUTION — Vₘ = √2V = √2 × 220 = 311.1 V. (a) I = V/R = 220/20 = 11 A. (b) Average power P = VI = 220 × 11 = 2420 W. Iₘ = √2I = 15.55 A and ω = 2πf = 314 rad s⁻¹. (c) V = 311.1 sin(314t) and I = 15.55 sin(314t).",
      "ASSIGNMENT 15.1 — A 60 Ω resistor is connected across a 220 V A.C. supply. Calculate the current drawn and power consumed by the heating element. Printed answer: 3.66 A, 860 W.",
      "15.7 A.C. THROUGH AN INDUCTOR — An inductor is a two-terminal component that resists changes in current and usually consists of a conductor wound into a coil. Apply alternating voltage to a pure inductance L as in Figure 15.10. A sinusoidal current I flowing with time t induces a back e.m.f. because of the coil’s inductance; it opposes the change in current. With no potential drop, the applied voltage must overcome this back e.m.f. The required energy for building up current in L is returned during current decay. Let alternating current be I = Iₘ sin ωt (i). A changing current sets up a back e.m.f. in the coil.",
