    "paragraphs": [
      "For changing current, back e.m.f. magnitude is ε = L ΔI/Δt. To maintain constant current the applied e.m.f. must be continuously applied. With I = Iₘ sin ωt, V = L ΔI/Δt = L Δ(Iₘ sin ωt)/Δt = LIₘ Δ(sin ωt)/Δt (ii). Since Δ(sin ωt)/Δt = ω cos ωt (iii), V = ωLIₘ cos ωt, where ωLIₘ = Vₘ. Thus V = Vₘ cos ωt = Vₘ sin(ωt + π/2) (iv).",
      "Comparing the current and voltage equations, current lags voltage by π/2 radians or 90° in a pure inductance. Inductance opposes change in current and delays its increase or decrease. The opposition an inductor offers to A.C. is inductive reactance Xₗ. Vₘ = IₘXₗ and Xₗ = Vₘ/Iₘ = V/I = ωL; therefore Xₗ = ωL (15.12) = 2πfL (15.13). Inductive reactance depends on A.C. frequency; for D.C., Xₗ = 0.",
      "15.7.1 POWER LOSS IN AN INDUCTOR — Figure 15.11 shows power in a pure inductive circuit. During the first 90° of a cycle voltage is positive and current negative, so supplied power is negative and flows from coil to source. During the next 90° both are positive and power flows from source to coil; in the following quarter-cycle power flows coil-to-source, and in the last quarter-cycle source-to-coil. The power curve over one",
      "FIGURE 15.10 — A.C. source and inductor circuit with phasor and waveforms showing current lagging voltage by 90°.",
      "FIGURE 15.11 — Instantaneous voltage, current and power in a pure inductor; power alternates between source-to-coil and coil-to-source flow."
    ]
