    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>UNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT — How are ultraviolet (UV) radiations important to us?</p><p>LEARNING OUTCOMES — After studying this unit students will be able to: (1) describe time period, frequency, instantaneous peak value and root-mean-square value of alternating current and voltage; (2) represent sinusoidally alternating current or voltage by x = x₀ sin ωt; (3) describe A.C. phase and phase lag/lead in A.C. circuits; (4) identify inductors used as chokes that present high resistance to alternating current; (5) explain A.C. flow through resistors, capacitors and inductors; (6) calculate capacitor and inductor reactances; and (7) describe impedance as the vector sum of resistance and reactances.</p><p>Further learning outcomes: (8) construct phasor diagrams and calculate series circuits with resistive and reactive components; (9) solve problems using A.C.-power formulae; (10) explain resonance and use the resonant-frequency formula; (11) describe maximum power transfer when source and load impedances match; (12) describe qualitatively Maxwell’s equations and production of electromagnetic waves; (13) become familiar with the electromagnetic spectrum from radio waves to γ-rays; (14) identify light as part of the continuous electromagnetic spectrum, whose waves travel through vacuum at the same speed; (15) describe information transmission by radio waves; (16) identify heating by certain-frequency microwaves absorbed by water and burns when absorbed by body tissues; (17) describe ultraviolet production by special lamps and the possibility that prolonged Sun exposure may cause skin cancer from UV radiation.</p><p>Most generators produce electricity as alternating current (A.C.). Although A.C. has no practical advantage over D.C. in some cases, it enables generators, motors and power-distribution systems that are far more efficient than D.C.; it is therefore used predominantly in high-power applications. In 1864 James Clerk Maxwell gave a theory relating accelerating charges, circuits and electric and magnetic fields. The theory predicts that accelerating electric charges radiate electromagnetic waves that propagate at the speed of light.</p><p>15.1 ALTERNATING VOLTAGE AND CURRENT — Electrical devices may receive current from a direct-current source (D.C.) or an alternating-current source (A.C.). In direct-current electricity, electrons flow continuously in one direction from the source of</p>",
    "theory": "UNIT 15 ALTERNATING CURRENT — How are ultraviolet (UV) radiations important to us?\nLEARNING OUTCOMES — After studying this unit students will be able to: (1) describe time period, frequency, instantaneous peak value and root-mean-square value of alternating current and voltage; (2) represent sinusoidally alternating current or voltage by x = x₀ sin ωt; (3) describe A.C. phase and phase lag/lead in A.C. circuits; (4) identify inductors used as chokes that present high resistance to alternating current; (5) explain A.C. flow through resistors, capacitors and inductors; (6) calculate capacitor and inductor reactances; and (7) describe impedance as the vector sum of resistance and reactances.\nFurther learning outcomes: (8) construct phasor diagrams and calculate series circuits with resistive and reactive components; (9) solve problems using A.C.-power formulae; (10) explain resonance and use the resonant-frequency formula; (11) describe maximum power transfer when source and load impedances match; (12) describe qualitatively Maxwell’s equations and production of electromagnetic waves; (13) become familiar with the electromagnetic spectrum from radio waves to γ-rays; (14) identify light as part of the continuous electromagnetic spectrum, whose waves travel through vacuum at the same speed; (15) describe information transmission by radio waves; (16) identify heating by certain-frequency microwaves absorbed by water and burns when absorbed by body tissues; (17) describe ultraviolet production by special lamps and the possibility that prolonged Sun exposure may cause skin cancer from UV radiation.\nMost generators produce electricity as alternating current (A.C.). Although A.C. has no practical advantage over D.C. in some cases, it enables generators, motors and power-distribution systems that are far more efficient than D.C.; it is therefore used predominantly in high-power applications. In 1864 James Clerk Maxwell gave a theory relating accelerating charges, circuits and electric and magnetic fields. The theory predicts that accelerating electric charges radiate electromagnetic waves that propagate at the speed of light.\n15.1 ALTERNATING VOLTAGE AND CURRENT — Electrical devices may receive current from a direct-current source (D.C.) or an alternating-current source (A.C.). In direct-current electricity, electrons flow continuously in one direction from the source of",
