    "id": "phys15-rl-example155-assignment154-and-rc-series-start-178-179",
    "title": "Example 15.5, Assignment 15.4 and R–C series A.C. circuit introduction",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>R–L POWER CONTINUATION — The positive waveform area is greater than the negative area, so net power over one cycle is positive and consumed in the R–L series circuit (Figure 15.15).</p><p>EXAMPLE 15.5 — A coil with resistance 7 Ω and inductance 31.8 mH is connected to a 220 V, 50 Hz supply. Find circuit current, phase angle, power factor and power consumed. GIVEN: L = 31.8 mH; V = 220 V; R = 7 Ω; f = 50 Hz. SOLUTION: X_L = 2πfL = 2π×50×31.8×10⁻³ = 10 Ω. Z = √(R²+X_L²) = √(7²+10²) = 12.2 Ω. I = V/Z = 220/12.2 = 18.03 A. tanφ = X_L/R = 10/7; φ = tan⁻¹(10/7) = 55° lag. Power factor cos55° = 0.573 lag. P = VI cosφ = 220×18.03×0.573 = 2272.8 W.</p><p>ASSIGNMENT 15.4 — A 220 V, 50 Hz A.C. source is connected to a 0.2 H inductance and 20 Ω resistance in series. Find circuit current. Printed answer: 3.33 A.</p><p>15.11 R.C. SERIES A.C. CIRCUIT — A pure resistance R is connected in series with a pure capacitor C. The applied r.m.s. voltage V is the phasor sum of component voltages V_R and V_C, where V_R = IR and V_C = I X_C. Current is common to both components and is used as the reference phasor. V_R is in phase with current; V_C lags current by 90° because current leads voltage by 90° in a pure capacitive circuit. Thus V² = V_R² + V_C² (15.24), V = √(V_R²+V_C²) = I√(R²+X_C²), and I = V/√(R²+X_C²) (15.25). Circuit impedance is Z = √(R²+X_C²) and I = V/Z (15.26). Figure 15.16 shows the R–C circuit and phasor diagram.</p>",
    "theory": "R–L POWER CONTINUATION — The positive waveform area is greater than the negative area, so net power over one cycle is positive and consumed in the R–L series circuit (Figure 15.15).\nEXAMPLE 15.5 — A coil with resistance 7 Ω and inductance 31.8 mH is connected to a 220 V, 50 Hz supply. Find circuit current, phase angle, power factor and power consumed. GIVEN: L = 31.8 mH; V = 220 V; R = 7 Ω; f = 50 Hz. SOLUTION: X_L = 2πfL = 2π×50×31.8×10⁻³ = 10 Ω. Z = √(R²+X_L²) = √(7²+10²) = 12.2 Ω. I = V/Z = 220/12.2 = 18.03 A. tanφ = X_L/R = 10/7; φ = tan⁻¹(10/7) = 55° lag. Power factor cos55° = 0.573 lag. P = VI cosφ = 220×18.03×0.573 = 2272.8 W.\nASSIGNMENT 15.4 — A 220 V, 50 Hz A.C. source is connected to a 0.2 H inductance and 20 Ω resistance in series. Find circuit current. Printed answer: 3.33 A.\n15.11 R.C. SERIES A.C. CIRCUIT — A pure resistance R is connected in series with a pure capacitor C. The applied r.m.s. voltage V is the phasor sum of component voltages V_R and V_C, where V_R = IR and V_C = I X_C. Current is common to both components and is used as the reference phasor. V_R is in phase with current; V_C lags current by 90° because current leads voltage by 90° in a pure capacitive circuit. Thus V² = V_R² + V_C² (15.24), V = √(V_R²+V_C²) = I√(R²+X_C²), and I = V/√(R²+X_C²) (15.25). Circuit impedance is Z = √(R²+X_C²) and I = V/Z (15.26). Figure 15.16 shows the R–C circuit and phasor diagram.",
