    "id": "phys15-spectrum-visible-infrared-microwave-radio-192-193",
    "title": "Electromagnetic spectrum, visible light, infrared, microwaves and radio",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>ELECTROMAGNETIC SPECTRUM — These radiations fundamentally differ in wavelength or frequency. The named regions shown in Figure 15.24 have no gaps or sharp boundaries; some ranges are assigned by convention for uses such as TV, AM and FM broadcasting.</p><p>VISIBLE LIGHT — The familiar visible region emitted by the Sun spans about 400 nm (violet) to 700 nm (red). Light is often emitted when atomic valence electrons change state of motion (optical transitions). Its color gives information about the emitting atom or object; light from the Sun and distant stars reveals their composition (Figure 15.25).</p><p>INFRARED — Infrared wavelengths are longer than visible light, from about 0.7 μm to 1 mm. Atoms or molecules emit it during rotational or vibrational changes. It transfers heat and is also called thermal radiation; objects at about 3–3000 K emit their strongest thermal radiation in the infrared. Home remote controls use invisible infrared pulses; Figure 15.26 shows an infrared image of the Milky Way.</p><p>MICROWAVES — Short radio waves with wavelengths about 1 mm to 1 m, commonly produced by electromagnetic oscillators, as in microwave ovens. They transmit telephone conversations and also arrive from space. Neutral hydrogen between stars emits microwaves at a wavelength of 21 cm.</p><p>RADIO WAVES — Wavelengths exceed 1 m. Terrestrial sources use electrons oscillating in antenna circuits. Antenna geometry controls the emitted radiation distribution; an antenna can transmit or receive. Expanding T.V. signals travel outward at light speed (Figure 15.27). Radio waves also come from extraterrestrial sources, especially the Sun; radio astronomy maps these emissions and reveals information not often available to optical telescopes. Figure 15.27 illustrates radiation from oscillating charges in a dipole antenna. The next unchecked section on PDF page 103 begins “5. Ultraviolet.”</p>",
    "theory": "ELECTROMAGNETIC SPECTRUM — These radiations fundamentally differ in wavelength or frequency. The named regions shown in Figure 15.24 have no gaps or sharp boundaries; some ranges are assigned by convention for uses such as TV, AM and FM broadcasting.\nVISIBLE LIGHT — The familiar visible region emitted by the Sun spans about 400 nm (violet) to 700 nm (red). Light is often emitted when atomic valence electrons change state of motion (optical transitions). Its color gives information about the emitting atom or object; light from the Sun and distant stars reveals their composition (Figure 15.25).\nINFRARED — Infrared wavelengths are longer than visible light, from about 0.7 μm to 1 mm. Atoms or molecules emit it during rotational or vibrational changes. It transfers heat and is also called thermal radiation; objects at about 3–3000 K emit their strongest thermal radiation in the infrared. Home remote controls use invisible infrared pulses; Figure 15.26 shows an infrared image of the Milky Way.\nMICROWAVES — Short radio waves with wavelengths about 1 mm to 1 m, commonly produced by electromagnetic oscillators, as in microwave ovens. They transmit telephone conversations and also arrive from space. Neutral hydrogen between stars emits microwaves at a wavelength of 21 cm.\nRADIO WAVES — Wavelengths exceed 1 m. Terrestrial sources use electrons oscillating in antenna circuits. Antenna geometry controls the emitted radiation distribution; an antenna can transmit or receive. Expanding T.V. signals travel outward at light speed (Figure 15.27). Radio waves also come from extraterrestrial sources, especially the Sun; radio astronomy maps these emissions and reveals information not often available to optical telescopes. Figure 15.27 illustrates radiation from oscillating charges in a dipole antenna. The next unchecked section on PDF page 103 begins “5. Ultraviolet.”",
