    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>UNIT 16 — PHYSICS OF SOLIDS. Opening prompt: What enables a floating bullet train to achieve speeds of 600 km/h? After studying this unit, students should be able to: distinguish crystalline, glassy, amorphous and polymeric structures; describe force-caused tensile and compressive deformation; describe spring load-extension behavior, Hooke’s law and spring constant; define/use Young’s, bulk and shear moduli; interpret force-extension graphs for ductile, brittle and polymeric materials; explain ultimate tensile stress and elastic/plastic deformation; describe energy bands; classify insulators, conductors and semiconductors by bands; discuss superconductors and uses; distinguish dia-, para- and ferromagnetism; describe magnetic domains and Curie point; classify hard/soft ferromagnets; describe hysteresis loss; and relate magnetic field strength to magnetizing current in a hysteresis loop.</p><p>STRUCTURE OF SOLIDS — Properties such as hardness, softness, magnetism and electrical conduction depend on atomic structure and bonding; crystal geometry is studied using X-ray diffraction (Laue and Bragg methods). Based on atomic arrangement, solids are crystalline, polycrystalline or amorphous. In a crystalline solid, atoms/ions/molecules form a definite three-dimensional geometric pattern with long-range periodic order. Crystals have smooth faces and straight edges and may cleave along preferred directions. Quartz, sucrose, diamond and rock salt (NaCl) occur as large single crystals (Figure 16.1).</p><p>POLYCRYSTALLINE SOLIDS — A polycrystalline material consists of many small single crystals (crystallites or grains), typically about 10³–10⁴ Å in size, separated by distinct boundaries and oriented in different directions. Each grain is a single crystal of irregular shape. PDF page 108 continues with the effect of their random orientations.</p>",
    "theory": "UNIT 16 — PHYSICS OF SOLIDS. Opening prompt: What enables a floating bullet train to achieve speeds of 600 km/h? After studying this unit, students should be able to: distinguish crystalline, glassy, amorphous and polymeric structures; describe force-caused tensile and compressive deformation; describe spring load-extension behavior, Hooke’s law and spring constant; define/use Young’s, bulk and shear moduli; interpret force-extension graphs for ductile, brittle and polymeric materials; explain ultimate tensile stress and elastic/plastic deformation; describe energy bands; classify insulators, conductors and semiconductors by bands; discuss superconductors and uses; distinguish dia-, para- and ferromagnetism; describe magnetic domains and Curie point; classify hard/soft ferromagnets; describe hysteresis loss; and relate magnetic field strength to magnetizing current in a hysteresis loop.\nSTRUCTURE OF SOLIDS — Properties such as hardness, softness, magnetism and electrical conduction depend on atomic structure and bonding; crystal geometry is studied using X-ray diffraction (Laue and Bragg methods). Based on atomic arrangement, solids are crystalline, polycrystalline or amorphous. In a crystalline solid, atoms/ions/molecules form a definite three-dimensional geometric pattern with long-range periodic order. Crystals have smooth faces and straight edges and may cleave along preferred directions. Quartz, sucrose, diamond and rock salt (NaCl) occur as large single crystals (Figure 16.1).\nPOLYCRYSTALLINE SOLIDS — A polycrystalline material consists of many small single crystals (crystallites or grains), typically about 10³–10⁴ Å in size, separated by distinct boundaries and oriented in different directions. Each grain is a single crystal of irregular shape. PDF page 108 continues with the effect of their random orientations.",
