    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>SPACE LATTICE AND CRYSTAL STRUCTURE — A space lattice is a skeleton for a crystal: atoms lie on or near lattice points. The distance between consecutive neighboring sites is the lattice constant; assigning directions gives lattice translation vectors. A plane lattice is generated by translations a and b; a space lattice by a, b and c (Figure 16.4). The smallest geometric unit whose periodic repetition forms a crystal is a unit cell. Crystal structure is obtained by adding an identical basis (same composition, arrangement and orientation) at every lattice point. A two-atom basis produces the illustrated structure; common unit cells include simple cubic (sc), face-centred cubic (fcc), body-centred cubic (bcc) and hexagonal close-packed (hcp) (Figure 16.5).</p><p>16.2 ELASTIC MODULI — An external force changes an object’s shape or size. Small, temporary deformation that disappears when the force is removed is elastic; if deformation remains, it is plastic. Deformation is described by stress and strain. Within the elastic range, stress/strain is constant and is the elastic modulus (Hooke’s law). The three moduli are Young’s modulus Y, shear/rigidity modulus S and bulk modulus B. The table lists values in Pa, in order Y, B, S: aluminium 7×10¹⁰, 7.5×10¹⁰, 2.5×10¹⁰; brass 9×10¹⁰, 6×10¹⁰, 3.5×10¹⁰; copper 11×10¹⁰, 14×10¹⁰, 4.4×10¹⁰; crown glass 6×10¹⁰, 5×10¹⁰, 2.5×10¹⁰; iron 21×10¹⁰, 16×10¹⁰, 7.7×10¹⁰; lead 1.6×10¹⁰, 4.1×10¹⁰, 0.6×10¹⁰; nickel 21×10¹⁰, 17×10¹⁰, 7.8×10¹⁰; steel 20×10¹⁰, 16×10¹⁰, 7.5×10¹⁰.</p><p>16.2.1 YOUNG’S MODULUS — Young’s modulus describes change in length under deforming force. Consider a rod of length L and cross-sectional area A, clamped at one end and pulled perpendicular to its cross-section; its length increases. Internal forces resist the change and, as the source sentence continues on PDF page 110, “but attain an equilibrium in which the length is (L+ΔL) where ΔL is the change in length.”</p>",
    "theory": "SPACE LATTICE AND CRYSTAL STRUCTURE — A space lattice is a skeleton for a crystal: atoms lie on or near lattice points. The distance between consecutive neighboring sites is the lattice constant; assigning directions gives lattice translation vectors. A plane lattice is generated by translations a and b; a space lattice by a, b and c (Figure 16.4). The smallest geometric unit whose periodic repetition forms a crystal is a unit cell. Crystal structure is obtained by adding an identical basis (same composition, arrangement and orientation) at every lattice point. A two-atom basis produces the illustrated structure; common unit cells include simple cubic (sc), face-centred cubic (fcc), body-centred cubic (bcc) and hexagonal close-packed (hcp) (Figure 16.5).\n16.2 ELASTIC MODULI — An external force changes an object’s shape or size. Small, temporary deformation that disappears when the force is removed is elastic; if deformation remains, it is plastic. Deformation is described by stress and strain. Within the elastic range, stress/strain is constant and is the elastic modulus (Hooke’s law). The three moduli are Young’s modulus Y, shear/rigidity modulus S and bulk modulus B. The table lists values in Pa, in order Y, B, S: aluminium 7×10¹⁰, 7.5×10¹⁰, 2.5×10¹⁰; brass 9×10¹⁰, 6×10¹⁰, 3.5×10¹⁰; copper 11×10¹⁰, 14×10¹⁰, 4.4×10¹⁰; crown glass 6×10¹⁰, 5×10¹⁰, 2.5×10¹⁰; iron 21×10¹⁰, 16×10¹⁰, 7.7×10¹⁰; lead 1.6×10¹⁰, 4.1×10¹⁰, 0.6×10¹⁰; nickel 21×10¹⁰, 17×10¹⁰, 7.8×10¹⁰; steel 20×10¹⁰, 16×10¹⁰, 7.5×10¹⁰.\n16.2.1 YOUNG’S MODULUS — Young’s modulus describes change in length under deforming force. Consider a rod of length L and cross-sectional area A, clamped at one end and pulled perpendicular to its cross-section; its length increases. Internal forces resist the change and, as the source sentence continues on PDF page 110, “but attain an equilibrium in which the length is (L+ΔL) where ΔL is the change in length.”",
