    "id": "phys16-young-shear-and-bulk-moduli-intro-208-209",
    "title": "Young’s modulus, shear modulus and bulk-modulus introduction",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>YOUNG’S MODULUS — The rod reaches equilibrium at length L+ΔL, where ΔL is its length change. Tensile stress is applied force per cross-sectional area: F/A (16.1). Tensile strain is change in length divided by original length: ΔL/L (16.2). Young’s modulus is stress/strain: Y=(F/A)/(ΔL/L) (16.3), SI unit N/m². Hooke’s law is valid within the elastic limit and for small strains.</p><p>16.2.2 SHEAR OR RIGIDITY MODULUS — A tangential force on one face while the opposite face is fixed deforms the object. Shear stress is F/A. Shear strain is displacement of the sheared face relative to the fixed face: Δx/y (16.4); for shear angle θ, tanθ=Δx/y and for small θ, tanθ≈θ (16.5). Shear modulus S = shear stress/shear strain = (F/A)/(Δx/y)=F/(A tanθ)≈F/(Aθ) (16.6–16.7). Figure 16.7 illustrates tangential deformation.</p><p>16.2.3 BULK MODULUS — Deforming forces applied at right angles to all surfaces produce a uniform change in a body’s volume (Figure 16.8). The printed page ends here before the volume-stress definition, which continues on PDF page 111.</p>",
    "theory": "YOUNG’S MODULUS — The rod reaches equilibrium at length L+ΔL, where ΔL is its length change. Tensile stress is applied force per cross-sectional area: F/A (16.1). Tensile strain is change in length divided by original length: ΔL/L (16.2). Young’s modulus is stress/strain: Y=(F/A)/(ΔL/L) (16.3), SI unit N/m². Hooke’s law is valid within the elastic limit and for small strains.\n16.2.2 SHEAR OR RIGIDITY MODULUS — A tangential force on one face while the opposite face is fixed deforms the object. Shear stress is F/A. Shear strain is displacement of the sheared face relative to the fixed face: Δx/y (16.4); for shear angle θ, tanθ=Δx/y and for small θ, tanθ≈θ (16.5). Shear modulus S = shear stress/shear strain = (F/A)/(Δx/y)=F/(A tanθ)≈F/(Aθ) (16.6–16.7). Figure 16.7 illustrates tangential deformation.\n16.2.3 BULK MODULUS — Deforming forces applied at right angles to all surfaces produce a uniform change in a body’s volume (Figure 16.8). The printed page ends here before the volume-stress definition, which continues on PDF page 111.",
