    "id": "phys16-conductors-semiconductors-superconductors-216-217",
    "title": "Conductors, semiconductors, superconductors and levitation",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>CONDUCTOR EXAMPLES AND SEMICONDUCTORS — Copper, silver, iron and aluminum are good electrical conductors. A semiconductor has a filled valence band and empty conduction band like an insulator, but its band gap is smaller. At low temperature it behaves as an insulator; at room temperature some electrons are thermally excited into the conduction band, leaving holes in the valence band. Holes act as positive charge carriers. Under a potential difference, holes and electrons drift in opposite directions and both contribute to current. A typical room-temperature semiconductor carrier concentration is about 10²¹ m⁻³; a metal’s is about ten million times greater. Conductivity lies between that of conductors and insulators.</p><p>16.6 SUPERCONDUCTORS — A superconductor conducts electricity with zero resistance below its critical temperature T_c; current then has no resistive heat loss. Most materials require extremely low temperatures. Figure 16.12a compares resistivity with temperature and shows a sharp transition to zero at T_c; high-temperature superconductors have different T_c values. Superconducting magnets carry large currents for MRI and transmission-line applications. The source reports that in 1988 a thallium-calcium-barium-copper-oxygen ceramic became superconducting at T_c=125 K, with research seeking higher T_c materials.</p><p>SUPERCONDUCTOR APPLICATIONS AND LEVITATION — Superconductors are used in MRI machines, maglev trains and particle accelerators. In maglev, superconducting magnets suspend a train car above a U-shaped concrete guideway; like ordinary magnets, matching poles repel. A small permanent magnet above a superconductor can levitate by repulsive force. The information box reports train speeds of 361 mph (581 kph) and notes conventional electromagnets waste much electrical energy as heat. The text explains that magnet-induced currents in the superconductor generate an equal and opposite field; page 114 ends mid-sentence at “generate an equal and”, continued on PDF page 115.</p>",
    "theory": "CONDUCTOR EXAMPLES AND SEMICONDUCTORS — Copper, silver, iron and aluminum are good electrical conductors. A semiconductor has a filled valence band and empty conduction band like an insulator, but its band gap is smaller. At low temperature it behaves as an insulator; at room temperature some electrons are thermally excited into the conduction band, leaving holes in the valence band. Holes act as positive charge carriers. Under a potential difference, holes and electrons drift in opposite directions and both contribute to current. A typical room-temperature semiconductor carrier concentration is about 10²¹ m⁻³; a metal’s is about ten million times greater. Conductivity lies between that of conductors and insulators.\n16.6 SUPERCONDUCTORS — A superconductor conducts electricity with zero resistance below its critical temperature T_c; current then has no resistive heat loss. Most materials require extremely low temperatures. Figure 16.12a compares resistivity with temperature and shows a sharp transition to zero at T_c; high-temperature superconductors have different T_c values. Superconducting magnets carry large currents for MRI and transmission-line applications. The source reports that in 1988 a thallium-calcium-barium-copper-oxygen ceramic became superconducting at T_c=125 K, with research seeking higher T_c materials.\nSUPERCONDUCTOR APPLICATIONS AND LEVITATION — Superconductors are used in MRI machines, maglev trains and particle accelerators. In maglev, superconducting magnets suspend a train car above a U-shaped concrete guideway; like ordinary magnets, matching poles repel. A small permanent magnet above a superconductor can levitate by repulsive force. The information box reports train speeds of 361 mph (581 kph) and notes conventional electromagnets waste much electrical energy as heat. The text explains that magnet-induced currents in the superconductor generate an equal and opposite field; page 114 ends mid-sentence at “generate an equal and”, continued on PDF page 115.",
