    "id": "phys16-exercise-mcq15-conceptual-comprehensive-and-numerical1-5-228-229",
    "title": "Exercise MCQ 15, conceptual and comprehensive questions, numerical problems 1–5",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>EXERCISE MCQ 15 — A cable breaks if stretched by more than 2 mm. It is cut into two equal parts. How far can either part be stretched without breaking? (a)0.25 m (b)1 mm (c)2 mm (d)0.5 m.</p><p>CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (1–11). 1. Why is steel’s coercive force greater than iron’s? 2. Evaluate the importance of strength and stiffness in design. 3. Discuss superconductivity of a conductor with a curve. 4. Distinguish crystalline, amorphous and polymer solids. 5. Differentiate elastic and plastic deformation. 6. Compare paramagnetic, diamagnetic and ferromagnetic materials with examples. 7. Distinguish soft and hard magnetic substances by drawing curves. 8. What is the difference between brittle and ductile materials? 9. How can semiconductor conductivity be increased? 10. Is there a difference in the length of a 20 m steel girder standing vertically and horizontally? 11. Why is it advantageous to use a superconductor in an MRI machine?</p><p>COMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (1–7). 1. Explain tensile and compressive forces and their effect on equilibrium in a structure. 2. Draw/describe a stress–strain graph and identify elastic region, plastic-flow region, yield stress and ultimate tensile stress. 3. Describe valence band, conduction band and forbidden gap using an energy-level diagram. 4. Discuss superconductivity with a curve. 5. Describe mechanical properties of solids. 6. Draw and describe the iron hysteresis loop for a ferromagnetic material. 7. Explain strain energy in a deformed wire using its graph.</p><p>NUMERICAL PROBLEMS (1–5). 1. A 1.50 m piano wire of diameter 0.25 cm is stretched by attaching a 10 kg mass. Find extension. Printed answer: 1.5×10⁻⁴ m. 2. A cylindrical steel rod, length 0.50 m and radius 1 cm, is under tensile force 1×10⁴ N. Find (a) tensile stress (b) tensile strain (c) extension. Answers: 0.31×10⁸ N m⁻²; 1.6×10⁻⁴; 0.8×10⁻⁴ m. 3. A 12 m cable stretches by 1.2×10⁻⁴ m under stress 6.4×10⁸ N m⁻². Find strain energy per unit volume. Answer: 3.2×10³ J m⁻³. 4. Wood has Y=1×10¹⁰ N m⁻². A chair has four legs, each 42 cm long with cross-section 2×10⁻³ m². Hamza’s mass is 100 kg. Find (a) stress on each leg (b) total shortening when he stands on the chair. Answers: 5×10⁵ N m⁻²; 2.1×10⁻⁵ m. 5. Force 1.5×10⁴ N produces strain 1.4×10⁻⁴ in a steel cable of area 4.8×10⁻⁴ m². Find (a) Young’s modulus (b) strain energy per unit volume at strain 1×10⁻⁴. Answers: 2.2×10¹¹ N m⁻²; 1.5×10³ J m⁻³.</p>",
    "theory": "EXERCISE MCQ 15 — A cable breaks if stretched by more than 2 mm. It is cut into two equal parts. How far can either part be stretched without breaking? (a)0.25 m (b)1 mm (c)2 mm (d)0.5 m.\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (1–11). 1. Why is steel’s coercive force greater than iron’s? 2. Evaluate the importance of strength and stiffness in design. 3. Discuss superconductivity of a conductor with a curve. 4. Distinguish crystalline, amorphous and polymer solids. 5. Differentiate elastic and plastic deformation. 6. Compare paramagnetic, diamagnetic and ferromagnetic materials with examples. 7. Distinguish soft and hard magnetic substances by drawing curves. 8. What is the difference between brittle and ductile materials? 9. How can semiconductor conductivity be increased? 10. Is there a difference in the length of a 20 m steel girder standing vertically and horizontally? 11. Why is it advantageous to use a superconductor in an MRI machine?\nCOMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS (1–7). 1. Explain tensile and compressive forces and their effect on equilibrium in a structure. 2. Draw/describe a stress–strain graph and identify elastic region, plastic-flow region, yield stress and ultimate tensile stress. 3. Describe valence band, conduction band and forbidden gap using an energy-level diagram. 4. Discuss superconductivity with a curve. 5. Describe mechanical properties of solids. 6. Draw and describe the iron hysteresis loop for a ferromagnetic material. 7. Explain strain energy in a deformed wire using its graph.\nNUMERICAL PROBLEMS (1–5). 1. A 1.50 m piano wire of diameter 0.25 cm is stretched by attaching a 10 kg mass. Find extension. Printed answer: 1.5×10⁻⁴ m. 2. A cylindrical steel rod, length 0.50 m and radius 1 cm, is under tensile force 1×10⁴ N. Find (a) tensile stress (b) tensile strain (c) extension. Answers: 0.31×10⁸ N m⁻²; 1.6×10⁻⁴; 0.8×10⁻⁴ m. 3. A 12 m cable stretches by 1.2×10⁻⁴ m under stress 6.4×10⁸ N m⁻². Find strain energy per unit volume. Answer: 3.2×10³ J m⁻³. 4. Wood has Y=1×10¹⁰ N m⁻². A chair has four legs, each 42 cm long with cross-section 2×10⁻³ m². Hamza’s mass is 100 kg. Find (a) stress on each leg (b) total shortening when he stands on the chair. Answers: 5×10⁵ N m⁻²; 2.1×10⁻⁵ m. 5. Force 1.5×10⁴ N produces strain 1.4×10⁻⁴ in a steel cable of area 4.8×10⁻⁴ m². Find (a) Young’s modulus (b) strain energy per unit volume at strain 1×10⁻⁴. Answers: 2.2×10¹¹ N m⁻²; 1.5×10³ J m⁻³.",
