      "19.1 ATOMIC SPECTRA — The spectrum of frequencies of electromagnetic radiation emitted or absorbed during transitions of electrons between energy levels within an atom is called an atomic spectrum. Each element has a characteristic spectrum by which it can be recognized. Heated matter emits light. Solids, liquids and very dense gases produce a continuous spectrum, with intensity changing continuously rather than in discrete lines.",
      "A. LINE EMISSION SPECTRUM — Radiation from electrically excited gases is discrete rather than continuous: an excited gas emits only specific light frequencies. Passing it through a spectroscope produces a bright-line emission spectrum containing the frequencies emitted by that gas. In Figure 19.1, an evacuated glass tube containing a gas such as helium, neon, mercury or barium emits light when a potential difference is applied across its electrodes. The emitted light passes through aligning slits and a prism; the observed discrete lines each correspond to a wavelength. Because every element has a unique set of lines, its spectrum can identify the substance. The figure shows mercury and barium emission lines.",
      "B. LINE ABSORPTION SPECTRUM — When white light passes through a sample, photons absorbed by its atoms are missing from the transmitted light. Dark lines or gaps therefore appear in the continuous spectrum; this is a line absorption spectrum."
