      "Figure 19.4 shows the Lyman, Balmer and Paschen hydrogen series and example wavelengths (91, 122, 365, 656, 820 and 1875 nm). The wavelengths and lines in these series are calculated from empirical formulae using R_H=1.0974×10⁷ m⁻¹. Lyman: 1/λₙ=R_H(1/1²−1/n²), n=2,3,4,… (ultraviolet). Balmer: 1/λₙ=R_H(1/2²−1/n²), n=3,4,5,… (visible). Paschen: 1/λₙ=R_H(1/3²−1/n²), n=4,5,6,… (infrared). Brackett: 1/λₙ=R_H(1/4²−1/n²), n=5,6,7,… (infrared). The general Rydberg formula is 1/λₙ=R_H(1/p²−1/n²), with p=1,2,3,4,… and n=p+1,p+2,p+3,… (Equation 19.2).",
      "19.3 BOHR MODEL OF THE HYDROGEN ATOM — Following Rutherford’s proposal that most atomic mass and positive charge are concentrated in a very small central region, Niels Bohr proposed in 1913 that the atom resembles a planetary system, with electrons orbiting the nucleus. Classical physics says an orbiting, accelerated electron continuously radiates electromagnetic energy. Losing energy, it would spiral into the nucleus and the atom would collapse (Figure 19.5).",
      "Bohr overcame this difficulty by proposing special motions called stationary states, in which an electron may exist without radiating electromagnetic energy. The source states that electron angular momentum L takes integral multiples of h: h, 2h, 3h,… but never 2.5h or 3.1h. It calls this quantization of angular momentum.",
      "BOHR POSTULATE 1 — The electron moves around the nucleus in a circular orbit. The required centripetal force is provided by the Coulomb force between the positively charged nucleus and negatively charged electron: F_centripetal=F_Coulomb, hence mv²/r=ke²/r² (Equation 1)."
