      "8. ENERGY OF ELECTRON IN QUANTIZED ORBITS — Both the kinetic and electric potential energies of an electron in an orbit depend on its distance from the nucleus, so the electron’s total energy is quantized; each orbit corresponds to an energy level. The total energy Eₙ is the sum of kinetic energy (K.E.) and potential energy (P.E.): Eₙ=K.E.+P.E. (Equation 6). K.E.=½mv² (Equation 7). Using mv²=ke²/rₙ from Equation 4 gives K.E.=½ke²/rₙ (Equation 8). The electrostatic potential energy is P.E.=−ke²/rₙ (Equation 9).",
