      "According to Bohr’s model, an atom raised to a high energy level is excited. In a hot gas, fast atoms can be excited by inelastic collisions with one another or with the container walls. An excited atom can transition to a lower level by emitting a photon. If Eₚ is the initial energy and Eₙ is the final energy, the photon energy is hf=Eₙ−Eₚ (Equation 1). Since Eₙ=−E₀/n² and Eₚ=−E₀/p², and f=c/λ, hc/λ=E₀/p²−E₀/n², or 1/λ=(E₀/hc)(1/p²−1/n²). The text calculates E₀/hc=(2.17×10⁻¹⁸ J)/(6.63×10⁻³⁴ J·s×3×10⁸ m/s)=1.0974×10⁷ m⁻¹ and sets it equal to R_H. Thus 1/λ=R_H(1/p²−1/n²) (Equation 19.7), agreeing with Balmer’s empirical value; the book says the Bohr model can therefore calculate hydrogen spectral energies and wavelengths.",
