    "title": "Sodium absorption, hydrogen spectrum and Balmer series",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>When white light falls on sodium vapour, some light is absorbed by sodium and the rest transmitted. Passing the transmitted light through a spectroscope shows a continuous spectrum with dark gaps at specific wavelengths, called a line absorption spectrum (Figure 19.2). The figure compares the sodium emission spectrum, which the caption says consists of several distinct lines, with its nearly continuous absorption spectrum.</p><p>19.2 SPECTRUM OF THE HYDROGEN ATOM — Atomic hydrogen has the simplest line spectrum. Much effort has been devoted to understanding its pattern of wavelengths.</p><p>When hydrogen gas is placed in a discharge tube and high voltage is applied across the tube, the gas becomes luminous and emits bluish-red light (Figure 19.3). A spectroscope resolves its visible emission spectrum into four lines. The figure also compares the hydrogen absorption and emission spectra.</p><p>BALMER SERIES — In 1885 Balmer showed that wavelengths of the visible hydrogen lines are accurately predicted by the empirical formula 1/λₙ=R_H(1/2²−1/n²) (Equation 19.1), for n=3,4,5,… and R_H=1.0974×10⁷ m⁻¹ (Rydberg constant). The longest wavelength is for n=3 and is predicted as 656.2 nm. The second and third lines are 486.1 nm and 434.1 nm; these agree with experiment. Balmer’s formula predicted other series beyond the visible region, later observed and grouped by discovery.</p>",
    "theory": "When white light falls on sodium vapour, some light is absorbed by sodium and the rest transmitted. Passing the transmitted light through a spectroscope shows a continuous spectrum with dark gaps at specific wavelengths, called a line absorption spectrum (Figure 19.2). The figure compares the sodium emission spectrum, which the caption says consists of several distinct lines, with its nearly continuous absorption spectrum.\n19.2 SPECTRUM OF THE HYDROGEN ATOM — Atomic hydrogen has the simplest line spectrum. Much effort has been devoted to understanding its pattern of wavelengths.\nWhen hydrogen gas is placed in a discharge tube and high voltage is applied across the tube, the gas becomes luminous and emits bluish-red light (Figure 19.3). A spectroscope resolves its visible emission spectrum into four lines. The figure also compares the hydrogen absorption and emission spectra.\nBALMER SERIES — In 1885 Balmer showed that wavelengths of the visible hydrogen lines are accurately predicted by the empirical formula 1/λₙ=R_H(1/2²−1/n²) (Equation 19.1), for n=3,4,5,… and R_H=1.0974×10⁷ m⁻¹ (Rydberg constant). The longest wavelength is for n=3 and is predicted as 656.2 nm. The second and third lines are 486.1 nm and 434.1 nm; these agree with experiment. Balmer’s formula predicted other series beyond the visible region, later observed and grouped by discovery.",
