    "id": "phys19-metastable-population-inversion-pumping-helium-neon-324-325",
    "title": "Metastable states, population inversion and helium-neon laser pumping",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>META-STABLE STATES AND POPULATION INVERSION — A short-lived excited state decays spontaneously in about 10⁻⁸ s to a lower excited state. In a meta-stable state, electrons remain longer, about 10⁻³ s, making stimulated emission possible by allowing more photons to pass atoms in that state. Population inversion is a collection in which more atoms are in an excited state than in the ground state (Figure 19.16). Under normal thermal equilibrium the lower state has the greater population; inversion is an unnatural situation that must be produced artificially and is essential for laser operation. The source’s information box says the Mars Orbiter Laser Altimeter (MOLA) produced a three-dimensional map of Martian topography from 27 million height measurements, each made by sending laser pulses to the surface and measuring their return times.</p><p>D. POPULATION INVERSION — Figure 19.16 compares normal population (more atoms in the ground state) with inverted population (more in the meta-stable state). E. LASER PUMPING — Transferring energy from an external source to produce excited atomic states is laser pumping. Pump energy is usually supplied as light (optical pumping) or electric current (electrical pumping). The text considers atoms in four states: ground E₀, short-lived excited E₁ and E₃, and long-lived meta-stable E₂. Pumping excites atoms to different levels; atoms excited to the meta-stable state remain longer and, after about 10⁻⁸ s, electrons in E₃ jump to meta-stable E₂, increasing its population relative to E₀ and achieving population inversion (Figure 19.17).</p><p>F. HELIUM-NEON LASER — This is the most common type of gas laser. A tube with partially reflecting mirrors is filled with 85% helium and 15% neon. Neon is the lasing or active medium. Helium and neon have identical meta-stable states at 20.61 eV and 20.66 eV, respectively. A high-voltage electric discharge excites some helium atoms to the 20.61 eV meta-stable state.</p>",
    "theory": "META-STABLE STATES AND POPULATION INVERSION — A short-lived excited state decays spontaneously in about 10⁻⁸ s to a lower excited state. In a meta-stable state, electrons remain longer, about 10⁻³ s, making stimulated emission possible by allowing more photons to pass atoms in that state. Population inversion is a collection in which more atoms are in an excited state than in the ground state (Figure 19.16). Under normal thermal equilibrium the lower state has the greater population; inversion is an unnatural situation that must be produced artificially and is essential for laser operation. The source’s information box says the Mars Orbiter Laser Altimeter (MOLA) produced a three-dimensional map of Martian topography from 27 million height measurements, each made by sending laser pulses to the surface and measuring their return times.\nD. POPULATION INVERSION — Figure 19.16 compares normal population (more atoms in the ground state) with inverted population (more in the meta-stable state). E. LASER PUMPING — Transferring energy from an external source to produce excited atomic states is laser pumping. Pump energy is usually supplied as light (optical pumping) or electric current (electrical pumping). The text considers atoms in four states: ground E₀, short-lived excited E₁ and E₃, and long-lived meta-stable E₂. Pumping excites atoms to different levels; atoms excited to the meta-stable state remain longer and, after about 10⁻⁸ s, electrons in E₃ jump to meta-stable E₂, increasing its population relative to E₀ and achieving population inversion (Figure 19.17).\nF. HELIUM-NEON LASER — This is the most common type of gas laser. A tube with partially reflecting mirrors is filled with 85% helium and 15% neon. Neon is the lasing or active medium. Helium and neon have identical meta-stable states at 20.61 eV and 20.66 eV, respectively. A high-voltage electric discharge excites some helium atoms to the 20.61 eV meta-stable state.",
