    "title": "Mass-energy, nuclear reaction example and fission",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>B. CONSERVATION OF MASS ENERGY</p><p>Since the mass number differ from atomic masses and difference provides binding energy to nucleons in the nucleus therefore energy conservation should also hold apart from conservation of nucleon number. This also include energy difference due to change in mass from Einstein mass energy relation.</p><p>For example in the reaction</p><p>\\({}^{4}_{2}\\mathrm{He}+{}^{14}_{7}\\mathrm{N}\\rightarrow{}^{17}_{8}\\mathrm{O}+{}^{1}_{1}\\mathrm{H}+Q\\)</p><p>nuclear reaction energy Q can be calculated as</p><p>LHS — RHS</p><p>\\({}^{4}_{2}\\mathrm{He}=4.00263\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\({}^{1}_{1}\\mathrm{H}=1.007825\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)</p><p>\\({}^{14}_{7}\\mathrm{N}=14.00307\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\({}^{17}_{8}\\mathrm{O}=16.999133\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)</p><p>\\(=18.005677\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\(=18.006958\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)</p><p>Since Q is the rest mass difference, therefore Q = 18.005677 u - 18.006958 u</p><p>\\(Q=-0.001281\\ \\mathrm{u}=-1.192\\ \\mathrm{MeV}\\)</p><p>As Q is negative therefore \\(\\alpha\\)-particle having kinetic energy 1.192 MeV can induce such a reaction.</p><p>EXAMPLE 20.3</p><p>NUCLEAR REACTION</p><p>What is the least energy does the proton have to make the following nuclear reaction possible?</p><p>\\({}^{1}_{1}\\mathrm{H}+{}^{13}_{6}\\mathrm{C}\\rightarrow{}^{14}_{7}\\mathrm{N}+{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}\\)</p><p>The mass of hydrogen \\({}^{1}\\mathrm{H}\\) is 1.007825 u, carbon \\({}^{13}\\mathrm{C}\\) is 13.003355 u, neutron \\({}^{1}\\mathrm{n}\\) is 1.008665 u and nitrogen \\({}^{13}\\mathrm{N}\\) is 13.005739 u.</p><p>GIVEN</p><p>Mass of hydrogen ‘\\({}^{1}\\mathrm{H}\\)’ = 1.007825 u</p><p>Mass of carbon ‘\\({}^{13}\\mathrm{C}\\)’ = 13.003355 u</p><p>Mass of neutron ‘\\({}^{1}\\mathrm{n}\\)’ = 1.008665 u</p><p>Mass of nitrogen ‘\\({}^{13}\\mathrm{N}\\)’ = 13.005739 u</p><p>REQUIRED</p><p>Proton energy Q = ?</p><p>SOLUTION</p><p>nuclear reaction energy Q can be calculated as</p><p>LHS — RHS</p><p>\\({}^{1}_{1}\\mathrm{H}=1.007825\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\({}^{13}_{7}\\mathrm{N}=13.005739\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)</p><p>\\({}^{13}_{6}\\mathrm{C}=13.003355\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\({}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}=1.008665\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)</p><p>\\(=14.011180\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\(=14.014404\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)</p><p>Since Q is the rest mass difference, therefore Q = 14.011180 u - 14.014404 u, this means that Q = - 0.003224 u, therefore</p><p>\\(Q=-0.003224\\ \\mathrm{u}\\times931.5\\ \\mathrm{MeV/u}=3.003156\\ \\mathrm{MeV}=3.00\\ \\mathrm{MeV}\\)     Answer</p><p>The negative sign shows that the reaction is endothermic, and proton would require more than 3.00 MeV energy to give excess energy to neutron and nitrogen after formation.</p><p>ASSIGNMENT 20.3</p><p>NUCLEAR REACTION</p><p>The following reaction requires an input of energy equal to 2.438 MeV.</p><p>\\({}^{1}_{1}\\mathrm{H}+{}^{18}_{8}\\mathrm{O}\\rightarrow{}^{18}_{9}\\mathrm{F}+{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}\\)</p><p>The mass of hydrogen \\({}^{1}\\mathrm{H}\\) is 1.007825 u, oxygen \\({}^{18}\\mathrm{O}\\) is 17.999160 u and neutron \\({}^{1}\\mathrm{n}\\) is 1.008665 u.</p><p>[Answer: 18.000937 u]</p><p>20.12 NUCLEAR FISSION</p><p>The process of splitting of nuclei into intermediate size nuclei is called nuclear fission. The fission process often produces free neutrons and photons (in the form of gamma rays), and releases a very large amount of energy.</p><p>Nuclear fission of heavy elements was discovered on December 17, 1938 by Otto Hahn and his assistant Fritz Strassmann, and explained theoretically in January 1939 by Lise Meitner and her nephew Otto Robert Frisch. Frisch named the process by analogy with biological fission of living cells.</p><p>They found that a uranium nucleus, after absorbing a neutron, splits into two fragments, of intermediate size. The general nuclear reaction of splitting of a massive nucleus into two less massive fragments is given below.</p><p>\\({}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+{}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U}\\rightarrow({}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U})^{*}\\rightarrow X+Y+\\) neutrons</p>",
    "theory": "B. CONSERVATION OF MASS ENERGY\nSince the mass number differ from atomic masses and difference provides binding energy to nucleons in the nucleus therefore energy conservation should also hold apart from conservation of nucleon number. This also include energy difference due to change in mass from Einstein mass energy relation.\nFor example in the reaction\n\\({}^{4}_{2}\\mathrm{He}+{}^{14}_{7}\\mathrm{N}\\rightarrow{}^{17}_{8}\\mathrm{O}+{}^{1}_{1}\\mathrm{H}+Q\\)\nnuclear reaction energy Q can be calculated as\nLHS — RHS\n\\({}^{4}_{2}\\mathrm{He}=4.00263\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\({}^{1}_{1}\\mathrm{H}=1.007825\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)\n\\({}^{14}_{7}\\mathrm{N}=14.00307\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\({}^{17}_{8}\\mathrm{O}=16.999133\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)\n\\(=18.005677\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\(=18.006958\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)\nSince Q is the rest mass difference, therefore Q = 18.005677 u - 18.006958 u\n\\(Q=-0.001281\\ \\mathrm{u}=-1.192\\ \\mathrm{MeV}\\)\nAs Q is negative therefore \\(\\alpha\\)-particle having kinetic energy 1.192 MeV can induce such a reaction.\nEXAMPLE 20.3\nNUCLEAR REACTION\nWhat is the least energy does the proton have to make the following nuclear reaction possible?\n\\({}^{1}_{1}\\mathrm{H}+{}^{13}_{6}\\mathrm{C}\\rightarrow{}^{14}_{7}\\mathrm{N}+{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}\\)\nThe mass of hydrogen \\({}^{1}\\mathrm{H}\\) is 1.007825 u, carbon \\({}^{13}\\mathrm{C}\\) is 13.003355 u, neutron \\({}^{1}\\mathrm{n}\\) is 1.008665 u and nitrogen \\({}^{13}\\mathrm{N}\\) is 13.005739 u.\nGIVEN\nMass of hydrogen ‘\\({}^{1}\\mathrm{H}\\)’ = 1.007825 u\nMass of carbon ‘\\({}^{13}\\mathrm{C}\\)’ = 13.003355 u\nMass of neutron ‘\\({}^{1}\\mathrm{n}\\)’ = 1.008665 u\nMass of nitrogen ‘\\({}^{13}\\mathrm{N}\\)’ = 13.005739 u\nREQUIRED\nProton energy Q = ?\nSOLUTION\nnuclear reaction energy Q can be calculated as\nLHS — RHS\n\\({}^{1}_{1}\\mathrm{H}=1.007825\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\({}^{13}_{7}\\mathrm{N}=13.005739\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)\n\\({}^{13}_{6}\\mathrm{C}=13.003355\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\({}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}=1.008665\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)\n\\(=14.011180\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\(=14.014404\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)\nSince Q is the rest mass difference, therefore Q = 14.011180 u - 14.014404 u, this means that Q = - 0.003224 u, therefore\n\\(Q=-0.003224\\ \\mathrm{u}\\times931.5\\ \\mathrm{MeV/u}=3.003156\\ \\mathrm{MeV}=3.00\\ \\mathrm{MeV}\\)     Answer\nThe negative sign shows that the reaction is endothermic, and proton would require more than 3.00 MeV energy to give excess energy to neutron and nitrogen after formation.\nASSIGNMENT 20.3\nNUCLEAR REACTION\nThe following reaction requires an input of energy equal to 2.438 MeV.\n\\({}^{1}_{1}\\mathrm{H}+{}^{18}_{8}\\mathrm{O}\\rightarrow{}^{18}_{9}\\mathrm{F}+{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}\\)\nThe mass of hydrogen \\({}^{1}\\mathrm{H}\\) is 1.007825 u, oxygen \\({}^{18}\\mathrm{O}\\) is 17.999160 u and neutron \\({}^{1}\\mathrm{n}\\) is 1.008665 u.\n[Answer: 18.000937 u]\n20.12 NUCLEAR FISSION\nThe process of splitting of nuclei into intermediate size nuclei is called nuclear fission. The fission process often produces free neutrons and photons (in the form of gamma rays), and releases a very large amount of energy.\nNuclear fission of heavy elements was discovered on December 17, 1938 by Otto Hahn and his assistant Fritz Strassmann, and explained theoretically in January 1939 by Lise Meitner and her nephew Otto Robert Frisch. Frisch named the process by analogy with biological fission of living cells.\nThey found that a uranium nucleus, after absorbing a neutron, splits into two fragments, of intermediate size. The general nuclear reaction of splitting of a massive nucleus into two less massive fragments is given below.\n\\({}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+{}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U}\\rightarrow({}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U})^{*}\\rightarrow X+Y+\\) neutrons",
