    "id": "phys20-fission-energy-chain-reaction-360-361",
    "title": "Fission fragments, energy and chain reaction",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>Where \\(({}^{236}\\mathrm{U})^{*}\\) is an intermediate excited state that lasts for only about \\(10^{-12}\\ \\mathrm{s}\\) before splitting into X and Y. The resulting nuclei X and Y are called fission fragments. There are many combinations of X and Y that satisfy the requirements of conservation of mass-energy, charge, and nucleon number as shown in figure 20.15.</p><p>The process results in the production of several neutrons, typically two or three. On the average, about 2.5 neutrons are released per event. A typical reaction of this type is</p><p>\\({}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+{}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U}\\rightarrow({}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U})^{*}\\rightarrow{}^{141}_{56}\\mathrm{Ba}+{}^{92}_{36}\\mathrm{Kr}+3\\,{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+Q\\)</p><p>where Q is the nuclear reaction energy.</p><p>Lise Meitner (1878 - 1968)</p><p>Lise Meitner (Austrian-Swedish) physicist who contributed to the discoveries of the element protactinium and nuclear fission.</p><p>FIGURE 20.15</p><p>\\({}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+{}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U}\\rightarrow{}^{141}_{56}\\mathrm{Ba}+{}^{92}_{36}\\mathrm{Kr}+3\\,{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+Q\\)</p><p>\\({}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+{}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U}\\rightarrow{}^{140}_{54}\\mathrm{Xe}+{}^{94}_{38}\\mathrm{Sr}+2\\,{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+Q\\)</p><p>The energy released in this fission can be calculated as</p><p>Reactants — Products</p><p>\\({}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}=1.0087\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\({}^{141}_{35}\\mathrm{Ba}=140.9139\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)</p><p>\\({}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U}=235.439\\ \\mathrm{u}\\quad+\\) — \\({}^{92}_{36}\\mathrm{Kr}=91.8973\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)</p><p>\\(=236.0526\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\(3\\,{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}=3.0261\\ \\mathrm{u}\\quad+\\)</p><p> — \\(=235.8373\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)</p><p>Since Q is the rest mass difference</p><p>\\(Q=236.0265\\ \\mathrm{u}-235.8373\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)</p><p>\\(Q=0.2153\\ \\mathrm{u}=200.44\\ \\mathrm{MeV}\\)</p><p>When one atom of \\({}^{235}\\mathrm{U}\\) undergoes this type of fission about 200 MeV energy is released. About 85% of this energy appears in the form of kinetic energy in the heavy fragments. This energy is very large compared to the energy released in chemical processes. For example, the energy released in the combustion of one molecule of octane used in gasoline engines is about one-millionth the energy released in a single uranium fission event.</p><p>A. FISSION CHAIN REACTION</p><p>When one nuclear reaction causes an average of one or more nuclear reactions, thus a self-propagating series of these reactions is achieved and is called Fission Chain Reaction.</p><p>In a sample of uranium, each of the neutrons emitted in fission can be absorbed by another nucleus of \\({}^{235}\\mathrm{U}\\) and thus induce another fission process, resulting in the emission of still more neutrons, followed by more fissions, and so forth as shown in figure 20.16.</p><p>As long as the average number of neutrons available to produce new fissions is greater than 1 per reaction, the number of fissions grows with time. This series of events is called fission chain reaction, each reaction release about 200MeV. Thus energy released is very high it is found that 1 kg of uranium delivers as much energy as 3000 tons of coal.</p>",
    "theory": "Where \\(({}^{236}\\mathrm{U})^{*}\\) is an intermediate excited state that lasts for only about \\(10^{-12}\\ \\mathrm{s}\\) before splitting into X and Y. The resulting nuclei X and Y are called fission fragments. There are many combinations of X and Y that satisfy the requirements of conservation of mass-energy, charge, and nucleon number as shown in figure 20.15.\nThe process results in the production of several neutrons, typically two or three. On the average, about 2.5 neutrons are released per event. A typical reaction of this type is\n\\({}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+{}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U}\\rightarrow({}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U})^{*}\\rightarrow{}^{141}_{56}\\mathrm{Ba}+{}^{92}_{36}\\mathrm{Kr}+3\\,{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+Q\\)\nwhere Q is the nuclear reaction energy.\nLise Meitner (1878 - 1968)\nLise Meitner (Austrian-Swedish) physicist who contributed to the discoveries of the element protactinium and nuclear fission.\nFIGURE 20.15\n\\({}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+{}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U}\\rightarrow{}^{141}_{56}\\mathrm{Ba}+{}^{92}_{36}\\mathrm{Kr}+3\\,{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+Q\\)\n\\({}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+{}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U}\\rightarrow{}^{140}_{54}\\mathrm{Xe}+{}^{94}_{38}\\mathrm{Sr}+2\\,{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}+Q\\)\nThe energy released in this fission can be calculated as\nReactants — Products\n\\({}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}=1.0087\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\({}^{141}_{35}\\mathrm{Ba}=140.9139\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)\n\\({}^{235}_{92}\\mathrm{U}=235.439\\ \\mathrm{u}\\quad+\\) — \\({}^{92}_{36}\\mathrm{Kr}=91.8973\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)\n\\(=236.0526\\ \\mathrm{u}\\) — \\(3\\,{}^{1}_{0}\\mathrm{n}=3.0261\\ \\mathrm{u}\\quad+\\)\n — \\(=235.8373\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)\nSince Q is the rest mass difference\n\\(Q=236.0265\\ \\mathrm{u}-235.8373\\ \\mathrm{u}\\)\n\\(Q=0.2153\\ \\mathrm{u}=200.44\\ \\mathrm{MeV}\\)\nWhen one atom of \\({}^{235}\\mathrm{U}\\) undergoes this type of fission about 200 MeV energy is released. About 85% of this energy appears in the form of kinetic energy in the heavy fragments. This energy is very large compared to the energy released in chemical processes. For example, the energy released in the combustion of one molecule of octane used in gasoline engines is about one-millionth the energy released in a single uranium fission event.\nA. FISSION CHAIN REACTION\nWhen one nuclear reaction causes an average of one or more nuclear reactions, thus a self-propagating series of these reactions is achieved and is called Fission Chain Reaction.\nIn a sample of uranium, each of the neutrons emitted in fission can be absorbed by another nucleus of \\({}^{235}\\mathrm{U}\\) and thus induce another fission process, resulting in the emission of still more neutrons, followed by more fissions, and so forth as shown in figure 20.16.\nAs long as the average number of neutrons available to produce new fissions is greater than 1 per reaction, the number of fissions grows with time. This series of events is called fission chain reaction, each reaction release about 200MeV. Thus energy released is very high it is found that 1 kg of uranium delivers as much energy as 3000 tons of coal.",
