    "content": "<p>FIGURE 20.16</p><p>A NUCLEAR CHAIN REACTION</p><p>The successive fissioning of ever-increasing numbers of uranium-235 atoms.</p><p>The minimum amount of mass required to sustain a fission chain reaction is called critical mass. If the amount of uranium is too small the chain reaction will stop. If a chain reaction is to continue it is necessary that the mass of uranium must be greater or equal to this critical mass.</p><p>The mass less than critical mass is called sub-critical and mass greater then critical mass is called super-critical as shown in figure 20.17.</p><p>FIGURE 20.17</p><p>(a) In a sub-critical mass, too many neutrons escape before colliding (b) in the critical mass, enough neutrons encounter other nuclei to maintain a chain reaction.</p><p>However Natural uranium consists of only about 0.7% of \\({}^{235}\\mathrm{U}\\) and 99.3% \\({}^{238}\\mathrm{U}\\), which means that most of the available uranium nuclei generally do not participate in the fission process but instead remove neutrons from being able to produce other fissions. To overcome this problem it is necessary to use enriched uranium, in which the abundance of \\({}^{235}\\mathrm{U}\\) is increased beyond its natural value of 0.7%.</p><p>Fission may take place in any of the heavy nuclei after capture of a neutron. However, low-energy neutrons are able to cause fission only in U-233, U-235, and Pu-239.</p><p>Fuel pellets contain enriched uranium-235. Each pellet contains the energy equivalent of 1 ton of coal.</p>",
    "theory": "FIGURE 20.16\nA NUCLEAR CHAIN REACTION\nThe successive fissioning of ever-increasing numbers of uranium-235 atoms.\nThe minimum amount of mass required to sustain a fission chain reaction is called critical mass. If the amount of uranium is too small the chain reaction will stop. If a chain reaction is to continue it is necessary that the mass of uranium must be greater or equal to this critical mass.\nThe mass less than critical mass is called sub-critical and mass greater then critical mass is called super-critical as shown in figure 20.17.\nFIGURE 20.17\n(a) In a sub-critical mass, too many neutrons escape before colliding (b) in the critical mass, enough neutrons encounter other nuclei to maintain a chain reaction.\nHowever Natural uranium consists of only about 0.7% of \\({}^{235}\\mathrm{U}\\) and 99.3% \\({}^{238}\\mathrm{U}\\), which means that most of the available uranium nuclei generally do not participate in the fission process but instead remove neutrons from being able to produce other fissions. To overcome this problem it is necessary to use enriched uranium, in which the abundance of \\({}^{235}\\mathrm{U}\\) is increased beyond its natural value of 0.7%.\nFission may take place in any of the heavy nuclei after capture of a neutron. However, low-energy neutrons are able to cause fission only in U-233, U-235, and Pu-239.\nFuel pellets contain enriched uranium-235. Each pellet contains the energy equivalent of 1 ton of coal.",
