    "id": "phys12-front-example111",
    "title": "Example 11.1 — Given and required",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>Three charges \\(q_1=-5\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\), \\(q_2=+10\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\) and \\(q_3=-12\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\) are placed in a line as shown in figure. Calculate the net electrostatic force on charge \\(q_2\\) due to the other two charges.</p><p>GIVEN</p><p>Charge \\((q_1)=-5\\,\\mu\\mathrm C=-5\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm C\\)</p><p>Charge \\((q_2)=+10\\,\\mu\\mathrm C=+10\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm C\\)</p><p>Charge \\((q_3)=-12\\,\\mu\\mathrm C=-12\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm C\\)</p><p>\\(k=9\\times10^9\\;\\mathrm{N\\,m^2\\,C^{-2}}\\)</p><p>The distance between charge \\(q_1\\) and \\(q_2\\) \\((r_{12})=6\\,\\mathrm{cm}=0.06\\,\\mathrm m=6\\times10^{-2}\\,\\mathrm m\\)</p><p>The distance between charge \\(q_2\\) and \\(q_3\\) \\((r_{23})=4\\,\\mathrm{cm}=0.04\\,\\mathrm m=4\\times10^{-2}\\,\\mathrm m\\)</p><p>REQUIRED</p><p>The magnitude of net electrostatic force on charge \\(q_2=F_{\\mathrm{net}}=?\\)</p><p>The direction of electrostatic force = ?</p><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 580 125\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Three collinear charges and their separations\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:580px;height:auto\"><defs><marker id=\"phys12-reviewed-example111\" markerWidth=\"8\" markerHeight=\"8\" refX=\"4\" refY=\"4\" orient=\"auto-start-reverse\"><path d=\"M0 0L8 4L0 8Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker></defs><circle cx=\"65\" cy=\"70\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#334155\"/><text x=\"59\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"20\">−</text><text x=\"56\" y=\"35\">q<tspan baseline-shift=\"sub\" font-size=\"11\">1</tspan></text><circle cx=\"335.0\" cy=\"70\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#f9a8d4\" stroke=\"#334155\"/><text x=\"329.0\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"20\">+</text><text x=\"326.0\" y=\"35\">q<tspan baseline-shift=\"sub\" font-size=\"11\">2</tspan></text><circle cx=\"515\" cy=\"70\" r=\"18\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#334155\"/><text x=\"509\" y=\"76\" font-size=\"20\">−</text><text x=\"506\" y=\"35\">q<tspan baseline-shift=\"sub\" font-size=\"11\">3</tspan></text><line x1=\"87\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"313.0\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#334155\" marker-start=\"url(#phys12-reviewed-example111)\" marker-end=\"url(#phys12-reviewed-example111)\"/><text x=\"182.0\" y=\"55\">6 cm</text><line x1=\"357.0\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"493\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#334155\" marker-start=\"url(#phys12-reviewed-example111)\" marker-end=\"url(#phys12-reviewed-example111)\"/><text x=\"407.0\" y=\"55\">4 cm</text></svg>",
    "theory": "Three charges \\(q_1=-5\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\), \\(q_2=+10\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\) and \\(q_3=-12\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\) are placed in a line as shown in figure. Calculate the net electrostatic force on charge \\(q_2\\) due to the other two charges.\nGIVEN\nCharge \\((q_1)=-5\\,\\mu\\mathrm C=-5\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm C\\)\nCharge \\((q_2)=+10\\,\\mu\\mathrm C=+10\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm C\\)\nCharge \\((q_3)=-12\\,\\mu\\mathrm C=-12\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm C\\)\n\\(k=9\\times10^9\\;\\mathrm{N\\,m^2\\,C^{-2}}\\)\nThe distance between charge \\(q_1\\) and \\(q_2\\) \\((r_{12})=6\\,\\mathrm{cm}=0.06\\,\\mathrm m=6\\times10^{-2}\\,\\mathrm m\\)\nThe distance between charge \\(q_2\\) and \\(q_3\\) \\((r_{23})=4\\,\\mathrm{cm}=0.04\\,\\mathrm m=4\\times10^{-2}\\,\\mathrm m\\)\nREQUIRED\nThe magnitude of net electrostatic force on charge \\(q_2=F_{\\mathrm{net}}=?\\)\nThe direction of electrostatic force = ?",
