    "id": "phys12-front-example113",
    "title": "Example 11.3",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>A metallic sphere of diameter 40 cm carries a charge of 600 μ C. Find the electric field intensity (a) at a distance of 60cm from the centre of the sphere and (b) at the surface of sphere.</p><p>GIVEN</p><p>Distance of a charge from the sphere \\(r=OB=60\\,\\mathrm{cm}=60\\times10^{-2}\\,\\mathrm m\\);</p><p>Charge on sphere \\(q=600\\,\\mu\\mathrm C=600\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm C\\)</p><p>Distance \\(r=\\frac d2=\\frac{OA}{2}=\\frac{40\\,\\mathrm{cm}}2=0.2\\,\\mathrm m\\)</p><p>REQUIRED</p><p>electric field intensity \\(=E=?\\)</p><p>SOLUTION</p><p>The electric field due to a charged sphere has spherical symmetry. Therefore, a charged sphere behaves for external points as if the whole charge is placed at its centre.</p><p>(a) Electric field intensity from the centre of the sphere \\(E=k\\frac q{r^2}\\)</p><p>\\(=9\\times10^9\\times\\frac{600\\times10^{-6}}{(60\\times10^{-2})^2}=1.5\\times10^9\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\)</p><p>(b) electric field intensity from the surface of a sphere \\(E=k\\frac q{r^2}\\)</p><p>\\(=9\\times10^9\\times\\frac{600\\times10^{-6}}{(0.2)^2}\\)</p><p>\\(=13.5\\times10^7\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\)</p><p>[Editorial clarification: the source prints \\(OA/2\\) in the radius calculation although OA is the radius in its diagram. Part (a) prints \\(1.5\\times10^9\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\); the displayed substitution evaluates to \\(1.5\\times10^7\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\). Both printed statements are retained.]</p><figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 420 160\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"O at sphere centre, A on surface, B at 60 cm from O\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:500px\"><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"65\" r=\"40\" fill=\"#facc15\" stroke=\"#334155\"/><path d=\"M100 65H220\" stroke=\"#7f1d1d\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"2 2\"/><circle cx=\"100\" cy=\"65\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"140\" cy=\"65\" r=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"220\" cy=\"65\" r=\"2\"/><text x=\"93\" y=\"55\">O</text><text x=\"139\" y=\"55\">A</text><text x=\"216\" y=\"55\">B</text><path d=\"M100 113V133M220 113V133M100 123H220\" stroke=\"#334155\"/><path d=\"M100 123L107.0 120.0L107.0 126.0Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/><path d=\"M220 123L213.0 126.0L213.0 120.0Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/><rect x=\"144\" y=\"113\" width=\"45\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#fff\"/><text x=\"146\" y=\"127\">60 cm</text></svg><figcaption>Field intensity due to a charged sphere</figcaption></figure>",
    "theory": "A metallic sphere of diameter 40 cm carries a charge of 600 μ C. Find the electric field intensity (a) at a distance of 60cm from the centre of the sphere and (b) at the surface of sphere.\nGIVEN\nDistance of a charge from the sphere \\(r=OB=60\\,\\mathrm{cm}=60\\times10^{-2}\\,\\mathrm m\\);\nCharge on sphere \\(q=600\\,\\mu\\mathrm C=600\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm C\\)\nDistance \\(r=\\frac d2=\\frac{OA}{2}=\\frac{40\\,\\mathrm{cm}}2=0.2\\,\\mathrm m\\)\nREQUIRED\nelectric field intensity \\(=E=?\\)\nSOLUTION\nThe electric field due to a charged sphere has spherical symmetry. Therefore, a charged sphere behaves for external points as if the whole charge is placed at its centre.\n(a) Electric field intensity from the centre of the sphere \\(E=k\\frac q{r^2}\\)\n\\(=9\\times10^9\\times\\frac{600\\times10^{-6}}{(60\\times10^{-2})^2}=1.5\\times10^9\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\)\n(b) electric field intensity from the surface of a sphere \\(E=k\\frac q{r^2}\\)\n\\(=9\\times10^9\\times\\frac{600\\times10^{-6}}{(0.2)^2}\\)\n\\(=13.5\\times10^7\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\)\n[Editorial clarification: the source prints \\(OA/2\\) in the radius calculation although OA is the radius in its diagram. Part (a) prints \\(1.5\\times10^9\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\); the displayed substitution evaluates to \\(1.5\\times10^7\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\). Both printed statements are retained.]",
