    "content": "<p>The electric flux through the end faces is EΔA, while flux through the curved surface is zero. Thus</p><p>\\(\\Phi_E=\\vec E\\cdot\\Delta\\vec A+\\vec E\\cdot\\Delta\\vec A+0\\)</p><p>Hence, \\(2EA=\\frac{\\delta A}{\\varepsilon_0}\\)</p><p>or \\(E=\\frac\\delta{2\\varepsilon_0}\\) ...(11.15)</p><p>and \\(\\vec E=\\frac\\delta{2\\varepsilon_0}\\hat r\\) ...(11.16)</p><p>Where \\(\\hat r\\) is the unit vector normal to the sheet and directed away from it.</p><p>[Editorial clarification: the source first uses \\(\\sigma\\) for surface charge density, then prints \\(\\delta\\) in the subsequent equations. Both source symbols are retained.]</p>",
    "theory": "The electric flux through the end faces is EΔA, while flux through the curved surface is zero. Thus\n\\(\\Phi_E=\\vec E\\cdot\\Delta\\vec A+\\vec E\\cdot\\Delta\\vec A+0\\)\nHence, \\(2EA=\\frac{\\delta A}{\\varepsilon_0}\\)\nor \\(E=\\frac\\delta{2\\varepsilon_0}\\) ...(11.15)\nand \\(\\vec E=\\frac\\delta{2\\varepsilon_0}\\hat r\\) ...(11.16)\nWhere \\(\\hat r\\) is the unit vector normal to the sheet and directed away from it.\n[Editorial clarification: the source first uses \\(\\sigma\\) for surface charge density, then prints \\(\\delta\\) in the subsequent equations. Both source symbols are retained.]",
