    "content": "<p>Like earth’s gravitational field every charge has electric field which theoretically expands upto infinity. Consider an isolated charge \\(+Q\\) fixed in space as shown in Fig11.20. The electric field intensity \\(E\\) is given by equation</p><p>\\(\\vec E=\\frac1{4\\pi\\varepsilon_o}\\frac Q{r^2}\\hat r\\)</p><p>As the test charge \\(q\\) is at infinity which is moved towards charge \\(+Q\\) a force of repulsion acts on it. Since external work is required to be done to bring charge from infinity to point A therefore negative sign is inserted in equation.</p><p>The work done is</p><p>\\(\\Delta W=-q\\vec E\\cdot\\Delta\\vec r=qE\\Delta r\\cos180^\\circ\\)</p><p>\\(\\Delta W=qE\\Delta r\\) ...(11.22)</p><p>[Editorial clarification: the printed middle equality omits the leading minus sign needed when expanding \\(-q\\vec E\\cdot\\Delta\\vec r\\). The printed expression and positive result in Eq11.22 are both retained.]</p>",
