    "id": "phys12-front-electronvolt33",
    "title": "THE ELECTRON VOLT (eV)",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>The electron-volt is a unit of energy or work which is defined as the amount of energy acquired or lost by an electron when it is displaced across two points between which potential difference is one volt.</p><p>Since the magnitude of the charge of an electron is about \\(1.602\\times10^{-19}\\mathrm C\\), it follows that an electron-volt is about \\(1.602\\times10^{-19}\\mathrm J\\).</p><p>By definition</p><p>\\(\\Delta(\\mathrm{KE})=1\\mathrm{eV}\\), when \\(q=e=1.602\\times10^{-19}\\mathrm C\\) and \\(\\Delta V=1\\mathrm V\\).</p><p>thus</p><p>\\(1\\mathrm{eV}=1.602\\times10^{-19}\\mathrm C\\times1\\mathrm V\\)</p><p>\\(=1.602\\times10^{-19}\\mathrm J\\)</p><p>This is the smaller unit of energy. Its bigger units are multiples of 1 eV that are in frequent use are given below.</p><p>1 Million electron volt \\(=1\\mathrm{MeV}=10^6\\mathrm{eV}\\)</p><p>1 Giga electron volt \\(=1\\mathrm{GeV}=10^9\\mathrm{eV}\\)</p>",
    "theory": "The electron-volt is a unit of energy or work which is defined as the amount of energy acquired or lost by an electron when it is displaced across two points between which potential difference is one volt.\nSince the magnitude of the charge of an electron is about \\(1.602\\times10^{-19}\\mathrm C\\), it follows that an electron-volt is about \\(1.602\\times10^{-19}\\mathrm J\\).\nBy definition\n\\(\\Delta(\\mathrm{KE})=1\\mathrm{eV}\\), when \\(q=e=1.602\\times10^{-19}\\mathrm C\\) and \\(\\Delta V=1\\mathrm V\\).\nthus\n\\(1\\mathrm{eV}=1.602\\times10^{-19}\\mathrm C\\times1\\mathrm V\\)\n\\(=1.602\\times10^{-19}\\mathrm J\\)\nThis is the smaller unit of energy. Its bigger units are multiples of 1 eV that are in frequent use are given below.\n1 Million electron volt \\(=1\\mathrm{MeV}=10^6\\mathrm{eV}\\)\n1 Giga electron volt \\(=1\\mathrm{GeV}=10^9\\mathrm{eV}\\)",
