    "content": "<p>Where, V = V1 - V2 is the potential difference and d is the separation between the plates. The strength of the electric field also depends on the number of charges on the plates. The charge density is the total charge per unit area of the plate, i.e.,</p><p>\\(\\sigma=\\frac QA\\)</p><p>By using Gauss’s law the electric field intensity E between the plates is</p><p>\\(E=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon_o}=\\frac Q{\\varepsilon_o A}\\) (B)</p><p>From Eq (A) &amp; (B) we have</p><p>\\(E=\\frac Vd=\\frac Q{\\varepsilon_o A}\\)</p><p>Or \\(Q=\\frac{\\varepsilon_o AV}d\\) ...(11.30)</p><p>By using \\(C_{vac}=\\frac QV\\), Eq(11.30) becomes</p><p>\\(C_{vac}=\\frac QV=\\frac{\\varepsilon_o A}d\\) ...(11.31)</p><p>When a dielectric is inserted between the plates of a capacitor, then it is seen that the charge storing capacity of a capacitor is enhanced by the dielectric which permits it to store εr times more charge for the same potential difference</p><figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 370 280\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Parallel capacitor plates A and B, battery connections, separation d\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:370px\"><path d=\"M90 50H280V240H90Z\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><rect x=\"112\" y=\"45\" width=\"24\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#334155\"/><rect x=\"190\" y=\"45\" width=\"24\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#334155\"/><text x=\"117\" y=\"60\">+</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"60\">−</text><text x=\"117\" y=\"78\">+</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"78\">−</text><text x=\"117\" y=\"96\">+</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"96\">−</text><text x=\"117\" y=\"114\">+</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"114\">−</text><text x=\"117\" y=\"132\">+</text><text x=\"195\" y=\"132\">−</text><path d=\"M159 220V260M179 232V248\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"149\" y=\"215\">+</text><text x=\"178\" y=\"215\">−</text><text x=\"106\" y=\"41\">A</text><text x=\"218\" y=\"41\">B</text></svg><figcaption>FIGURE 11.23: Plates of a capacitor separated by a distance d</figcaption></figure>",
    "theory": "Where, V = V1 - V2 is the potential difference and d is the separation between the plates. The strength of the electric field also depends on the number of charges on the plates. The charge density is the total charge per unit area of the plate, i.e.,\n\\(\\sigma=\\frac QA\\)\nBy using Gauss’s law the electric field intensity E between the plates is\n\\(E=\\frac{\\sigma}{\\varepsilon_o}=\\frac Q{\\varepsilon_o A}\\) (B)\nFrom Eq (A) & (B) we have\n\\(E=\\frac Vd=\\frac Q{\\varepsilon_o A}\\)\nOr \\(Q=\\frac{\\varepsilon_o AV}d\\) ...(11.30)\nBy using \\(C_{vac}=\\frac QV\\), Eq(11.30) becomes\n\\(C_{vac}=\\frac QV=\\frac{\\varepsilon_o A}d\\) ...(11.31)\nWhen a dielectric is inserted between the plates of a capacitor, then it is seen that the charge storing capacity of a capacitor is enhanced by the dielectric which permits it to store εr times more charge for the same potential difference",
