    "id": "phys12-front-discharge44",
    "title": "Capacitor discharging and CDI ignition",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>The capacitor will discharge itself through resistance R.</p><p>\\(t=RC\\quad q=q_0(0.368)\\)</p><p>The time constant of an RC circuit is also the time during which the charge on the capacitor falls from its maximum value to 36.8%. The corresponding graph in Figure 11.30 shows that discharging begins at t=0 when q=q₀, and decreases gradually.</p><p>The charging and discharging of a capacitor has many applications. Capacitor discharge ignition (CDI) is a type of automotive electronic ignition system widely used in motorcycles, lawn mowers, chain saws, small engines, turbine-powered aircraft, and some cars. It can enhance the capability of a power supply and make the spark much stronger.</p><p>TIP</p><p>Electronic flashguns for cameras have to be left for a short period of time between flashes. There is a capacitor inside that stores energy; it needs to be charged up again by the battery in the flashgun.</p><figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 410 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Open-loop capacitor discharging through resistance R, with switch K and decreasing current\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:410px\"><path d=\"M85 55H320V265H85V55\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#cbd5e1\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><circle cx=\"110\" cy=\"55\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#111827\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"135\" cy=\"55\" r=\"5\" fill=\"#111827\" stroke=\"#fff\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M110 55L132 45\" stroke=\"#f8fafc\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M320 105V130M320 130L302 140V172L320 182V212M300 142H340M300 170H340\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#f8fafc\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"349\" y=\"165\">R</text><text x=\"349\" y=\"210\">C</text><text x=\"116\" y=\"35\">K</text><path d=\"M85 100V160M73 160H97M77 171H93\" stroke=\"#f8fafc\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"38\" y=\"145\">−q</text><text x=\"38\" y=\"190\">+q</text><path d=\"M85 110V145M265 265H215\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#a)\"/><text x=\"68\" y=\"105\">i</text><text x=\"235\" y=\"251\">i</text></svg><figcaption>Figure 11.29 — discharging a capacitor</figcaption></figure><figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 450 315\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Capacitor charge q decays from q0 toward zero; 0.368 q0 occurs at time RC\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:450px\"><path d=\"M70 245V35M70 245H405\" stroke=\"#f8fafc\" stroke-width=\"3\" marker-end=\"url(#a)\"/><path d=\"M72 53C126 157 194 205 382 230\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M71 169H165V244M71 169H165\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"6 5\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"53\">q</text><text x=\"414\" y=\"250\">t</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"47\">q₀</text><text x=\"78\" y=\"162\">0.368q₀</text><text x=\"154\" y=\"270\">RC</text><text x=\"212\" y=\"300\">Discharging of a capacitor</text></svg><figcaption>Figure 11.30 — discharging a capacitor</figcaption></figure><figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 365 285\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Schematic CDI ignition path: battery, capacitor discharge unit, ignition coil, and spark plug\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:365px\"><rect x=\"25\" y=\"75\" width=\"92\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#334155\" stroke=\"#cbd5e1\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"41\" y=\"105\">CDI</text><text x=\"36\" y=\"126\">capacitor</text><path d=\"M117 110H170M220 110H278M278 110V170\" stroke=\"#38bdf8\" stroke-width=\"4\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M170 78C145 78 145 142 170 142C195 142 195 78 220 78M170 78C195 78 195 142 220 142\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#fbbf24\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><text x=\"173\" y=\"165\">coil</text><path d=\"M278 170L262 196L282 196L267 225L307 184L286 184L302 170Z\" fill=\"#f8fafc\"/><text x=\"245\" y=\"255\">spark plug</text><path d=\"M50 75V48H95V75M50 145V185H100\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"3\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"48\" y=\"40\">battery</text></svg><figcaption>Figure 11.31 — electronic ignition CDI (schematic)</figcaption></figure>",
    "theory": "The capacitor will discharge itself through resistance R.\n\\(t=RC\\quad q=q_0(0.368)\\)\nThe time constant of an RC circuit is also the time during which the charge on the capacitor falls from its maximum value to 36.8%. The corresponding graph in Figure 11.30 shows that discharging begins at t=0 when q=q₀, and decreases gradually.\nThe charging and discharging of a capacitor has many applications. Capacitor discharge ignition (CDI) is a type of automotive electronic ignition system widely used in motorcycles, lawn mowers, chain saws, small engines, turbine-powered aircraft, and some cars. It can enhance the capability of a power supply and make the spark much stronger.\nTIP\nElectronic flashguns for cameras have to be left for a short period of time between flashes. There is a capacitor inside that stores energy; it needs to be charged up again by the battery in the flashgun.",
