    "id": "phys12-front-keypoints45",
    "title": "KEY POINTS — Unit 11 Electrostatics",
    "kind": "lesson",
    "content": "<p>Coulomb’s Law: the magnitude of the force between two point charges is directly proportional to the product of the magnitudes of the charges and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.</p><p>Electric potential at any point: the work done in bringing a unit positive charge from infinity to that point without producing any acceleration.</p><p>Electric field: the region around a charge in which an electric test charge would experience an electric force.</p><p>The electric flux \\(\\Phi\\): the number of lines of force that pass through an area placed in the electric field. \\(\\Phi=E\\cdot A=EA\\cos\\theta\\). When area A is normal to the electric field E, the flux is maximum. When area A is parallel to E, the flux is minimum.</p><p>Gauss’s law: the electric flux through any closed surface is \\(\\frac{1}{\\epsilon_0}\\) times the total charge enclosed by it.</p><p>Capacitor: a device which is used for storing electric charges.</p><p>Electron volt (eV): the unit of energy related to the joule by \\(1\\,\\mathrm{eV}=1.602\\times10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm J\\).</p><p>Dipole: a system of two charges of equal magnitude but opposite sign separated by distance d.</p>",
    "theory": "Coulomb’s Law: the magnitude of the force between two point charges is directly proportional to the product of the magnitudes of the charges and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between them.\nElectric potential at any point: the work done in bringing a unit positive charge from infinity to that point without producing any acceleration.\nElectric field: the region around a charge in which an electric test charge would experience an electric force.\nThe electric flux \\(\\Phi\\): the number of lines of force that pass through an area placed in the electric field. \\(\\Phi=E\\cdot A=EA\\cos\\theta\\). When area A is normal to the electric field E, the flux is maximum. When area A is parallel to E, the flux is minimum.\nGauss’s law: the electric flux through any closed surface is \\(\\frac{1}{\\epsilon_0}\\) times the total charge enclosed by it.\nCapacitor: a device which is used for storing electric charges.\nElectron volt (eV): the unit of energy related to the joule by \\(1\\,\\mathrm{eV}=1.602\\times10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm J\\).\nDipole: a system of two charges of equal magnitude but opposite sign separated by distance d.",
