    "title": "Exercise 11 — Conceptual Questions",
    "kind": "exercise",
    "content": "<p>The electric potential is constant through a given region of space. Is the electric field zero or non-zero in this region?</p><p>If a point charge q of mass m is released in a non-uniform electric field with field lines pointing in the same direction, will it make a rectilinear motion?</p><p>What is the relationship between potential difference and electric potential energy?</p><p>How are the units of volts and electron volts related? How do they differ?</p><p>How can electric potential be high when the electric potential energy is relatively low?</p><p>Can the potential of a non-uniformly charged sphere be the same as that of a point charge?</p><p>Will the energy stored in three capacitors be greater when they are connected in series or in parallel?</p><p>Can different equipotential lines cross each other?</p><p>Water has a large dielectric constant, but it is rarely used in capacitors. Why?</p><p>How can you increase the capacitance of a capacitor?</p>",
