    "content": "<p>State and explain Coulomb’s law. Include the case when the charges are placed in dielectrics. Discuss how the unit of charge coulomb is defined.</p><p>Explain the concept of electric field and hence define electric field intensity. Discuss the direction as well as the unit of E.</p><p>Explain the concept of electric flux. Using mathematical expressions for electric flux, show how electric flux is maximum and minimum.</p><p>State and prove Gauss’s law for electrostatics. Also discuss its applications with daily-life examples.</p><p>Explain the concept of electric potential. Derive an expression for electric potential at a field point due to a source charge.</p><p>Describe the construction of a capacitor and derive an expression for the energy stored in a capacitor.</p><p>Describe the concept of equipotential surfaces and derive an expression for electric field as the negative of the potential gradient.</p><p>Explain the phenomenon of electric polarization. Discuss how polarization accounts for the increase in capacitance when a dielectric is inserted between the plates of a capacitor.</p><p>Derive an expression for the capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor when a dielectric is inserted between its plates.</p><p>Describe the process of charging and discharging a capacitor. Give diagrams and mathematical expressions for the growth and decay of charge on the capacitor.</p><p>Explain why capacitors are of little use for storing energy for normal domestic purposes such as lighting and heating.</p>",
    "theory": "State and explain Coulomb’s law. Include the case when the charges are placed in dielectrics. Discuss how the unit of charge coulomb is defined.\nExplain the concept of electric field and hence define electric field intensity. Discuss the direction as well as the unit of E.\nExplain the concept of electric flux. Using mathematical expressions for electric flux, show how electric flux is maximum and minimum.\nState and prove Gauss’s law for electrostatics. Also discuss its applications with daily-life examples.\nExplain the concept of electric potential. Derive an expression for electric potential at a field point due to a source charge.\nDescribe the construction of a capacitor and derive an expression for the energy stored in a capacitor.\nDescribe the concept of equipotential surfaces and derive an expression for electric field as the negative of the potential gradient.\nExplain the phenomenon of electric polarization. Discuss how polarization accounts for the increase in capacitance when a dielectric is inserted between the plates of a capacitor.\nDerive an expression for the capacitance of a parallel-plate capacitor when a dielectric is inserted between its plates.\nDescribe the process of charging and discharging a capacitor. Give diagrams and mathematical expressions for the growth and decay of charge on the capacitor.\nExplain why capacitors are of little use for storing energy for normal domestic purposes such as lighting and heating.",
