    "kind": "exercise",
    "content": "<p>Charges \\(2\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\), \\(-3\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\), and \\(4\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\) are placed in air at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of sides \\(10\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\). What is the magnitude of the resultant force acting on the \\(4\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\) charge?\n(Answer: \\(15.7\\,\\mathrm N\\))</p><p>Two equal and opposite charges of magnitude \\(2\\times10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm C\\) are placed \\(15\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) apart. Find the magnitude and direction of electric field intensity E at the midpoint. What force acts on a proton of charge \\(+1.6\\times10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm C\\) placed there?\n(Answer: \\(0.64\\times10^6\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\) along AB; \\(1.024\\times10^{-13}\\,\\mathrm N\\) along AB)</p><p>Two positive point charges of \\(15\\times10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm C\\) and \\(13\\times10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm C\\) are placed \\(12\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) apart. Find the work done in bringing the two charges \\(4\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) closer.\n(Answer: \\(7.31\\times10^{-8}\\,\\mathrm J\\))</p><p>A hollow sphere is charged to \\(14\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\). Find the potential (a) at its surface, (b) inside the sphere, and (c) at a distance of \\(0.2\\,\\mathrm m\\) from its surface. The radius is \\(0.3\\,\\mathrm m\\).\n(Answer: \\(42\\times10^4\\,\\mathrm V;\\ 42\\times10^4\\,\\mathrm V;\\ 25.2\\times10^4\\,\\mathrm V\\))</p><p>If \\(280\\,\\mathrm J\\) of work is done in carrying a charge of \\(2\\,\\mathrm C\\) from a place where the potential is \\(-12\\,\\mathrm V\\) to another place where the potential is V, calculate V.\n(Answer: \\(128\\,\\mathrm V\\))</p><p>The electric field at a point due to a point charge is \\(26\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\), and the electric potential there is \\(13\\,\\mathrm{J/C}\\). Calculate the distance from the charge and the magnitude of the charge.\n(Answer: \\(0.722\\times10^{-9}\\,\\mathrm C\\))</p><p>Two point charges of \\(8\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\) and \\(-4\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\) are separated by \\(10\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) in air. At what point on the line joining the charges is the electric potential zero?\n(Answer: \\(6.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) from \\(8\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\), and \\(3.3\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) from \\(-4\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\))</p><p>An electron with an initial speed of \\(29\\times10^5\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}\\) is fired in the same direction as a uniform electric field of magnitude \\(80\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\). How far does the electron travel before being brought momentarily to rest and turned back?\n(Answer: \\(0.299\\,\\mathrm m\\))</p><p>Three capacitors of capacitance \\(4\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\), \\(6\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\), and \\(8\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\), respectively, are connected in series to a \\(250\\,\\mathrm V\\) d.c. supply. Find (i) the total capacitance, (ii) the charge on each capacitor, and (iii) the potential difference across each capacitor.\n(Answer: \\(1.84\\,\\mu\\mathrm F;\\ 460\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm C;\\ 115\\,\\mathrm V,\\ 76.6\\,\\mathrm V,\\ 57.7\\,\\mathrm V\\))</p><p>Find the charge on the \\(5\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\) capacitor in the circuit shown in the figure.\n(Answer: \\(9\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\))</p><p>A \\(6\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\) capacitor is charged to a potential difference of \\(120\\,\\mathrm V\\) and then connected to an uncharged \\(4\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\) capacitor. Calculate the potential difference across the capacitors.\n(Answer: \\(72\\,\\mathrm V\\))</p><figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 440 300\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"3 microfarad capacitor in series with parallel 2 and 5 microfarad capacitors; 6 volt battery\" style=\"width:100%;max-width:440px\"><path d=\"M55 70H125M153 70H235V115M235 205V250H55V70M350 115V205M350 160H405V250H252M235 115H282M296 115H350M235 205H282M296 205H350\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"4\"/><path d=\"M125 55V85M153 55V85M282 105V125M296 105V125M282 195V215M296 195V215\" stroke=\"#0f172a\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"108\" y=\"45\">3 μF</text><text x=\"274\" y=\"96\">2 μF</text><text x=\"274\" y=\"238\">5 μF</text><circle cx=\"240\" cy=\"250\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#0f172a\"/><circle cx=\"252\" cy=\"250\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#0f172a\"/><text x=\"205\" y=\"280\">+ 6 V −</text></svg><figcaption>Numerical question 11 — 3 μF in series with a parallel 2 μF and 5 μF branch; 6 V source</figcaption></figure>",
    "theory": "Charges \\(2\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\), \\(-3\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\), and \\(4\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\) are placed in air at the vertices of an equilateral triangle of sides \\(10\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\). What is the magnitude of the resultant force acting on the \\(4\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\) charge?\n(Answer: \\(15.7\\,\\mathrm N\\))\nTwo equal and opposite charges of magnitude \\(2\\times10^{-7}\\,\\mathrm C\\) are placed \\(15\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) apart. Find the magnitude and direction of electric field intensity E at the midpoint. What force acts on a proton of charge \\(+1.6\\times10^{-19}\\,\\mathrm C\\) placed there?\n(Answer: \\(0.64\\times10^6\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\) along AB; \\(1.024\\times10^{-13}\\,\\mathrm N\\) along AB)\nTwo positive point charges of \\(15\\times10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm C\\) and \\(13\\times10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm C\\) are placed \\(12\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) apart. Find the work done in bringing the two charges \\(4\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) closer.\n(Answer: \\(7.31\\times10^{-8}\\,\\mathrm J\\))\nA hollow sphere is charged to \\(14\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\). Find the potential (a) at its surface, (b) inside the sphere, and (c) at a distance of \\(0.2\\,\\mathrm m\\) from its surface. The radius is \\(0.3\\,\\mathrm m\\).\n(Answer: \\(42\\times10^4\\,\\mathrm V;\\ 42\\times10^4\\,\\mathrm V;\\ 25.2\\times10^4\\,\\mathrm V\\))\nIf \\(280\\,\\mathrm J\\) of work is done in carrying a charge of \\(2\\,\\mathrm C\\) from a place where the potential is \\(-12\\,\\mathrm V\\) to another place where the potential is V, calculate V.\n(Answer: \\(128\\,\\mathrm V\\))\nThe electric field at a point due to a point charge is \\(26\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\), and the electric potential there is \\(13\\,\\mathrm{J/C}\\). Calculate the distance from the charge and the magnitude of the charge.\n(Answer: \\(0.722\\times10^{-9}\\,\\mathrm C\\))\nTwo point charges of \\(8\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\) and \\(-4\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\) are separated by \\(10\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) in air. At what point on the line joining the charges is the electric potential zero?\n(Answer: \\(6.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) from \\(8\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\), and \\(3.3\\,\\mathrm{cm}\\) from \\(-4\\,\\mu\\mathrm C\\))\nAn electron with an initial speed of \\(29\\times10^5\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}\\) is fired in the same direction as a uniform electric field of magnitude \\(80\\,\\mathrm{N/C}\\). How far does the electron travel before being brought momentarily to rest and turned back?\n(Answer: \\(0.299\\,\\mathrm m\\))\nThree capacitors of capacitance \\(4\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\), \\(6\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\), and \\(8\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\), respectively, are connected in series to a \\(250\\,\\mathrm V\\) d.c. supply. Find (i) the total capacitance, (ii) the charge on each capacitor, and (iii) the potential difference across each capacitor.\n(Answer: \\(1.84\\,\\mu\\mathrm F;\\ 460\\times10^{-6}\\,\\mathrm C;\\ 115\\,\\mathrm V,\\ 76.6\\,\\mathrm V,\\ 57.7\\,\\mathrm V\\))\nFind the charge on the \\(5\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\) capacitor in the circuit shown in the figure.\n(Answer: \\(9\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\))\nA \\(6\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\) capacitor is charged to a potential difference of \\(120\\,\\mathrm V\\) and then connected to an uncharged \\(4\\,\\mu\\mathrm F\\) capacitor. Calculate the potential difference across the capacitors.\n(Answer: \\(72\\,\\mathrm V\\))",
