      "term": "Ohm’s law",
      "definition": "This law was presented by German scientist George Simon Ohm in (1826). It relates the three fundamental electrical quantities: voltage (V), the current (I) and resistance (R). If a voltage V is applied across a conductor and current (I) flows through it then according to ohm law The magnitude of the current in a conductor is proportional to the applied voltage as long as the temperature of the conductor is kept constant.",
