      "id": "phys14-exercise-conceptual-9-10-comprehensive-numericals-1-9",
      "exercise": "Unit 14 exercise",
      "title": "Conceptual questions 9–10, comprehensive questions and numerical problems 1–9",
      "questions": "CONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS (continued) — 9. What is meant by the term back e.m.f. in operation of an electric motor? 10. Why is it advantageous for the armature to rotate in a radial magnetic field rather than a uniform field? TRANSFORMER CORE: (a) Which material would you prefer: iron, solid soft iron, laminated soft iron or aluminium? (b) Why is this material used?\n\nCOMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS — 1. Describe electromagnetic induction with simple experiments; explain factors affecting magnitude and direction of induced e.m.f. 2. State Faraday’s law; explain statically and dynamically induced e.m.f. with experiments. 3. Describe Lenz’s law and show that it manifests conservation of energy. 4. Explain mutual induction; define its coefficient and unit. 5. Explain self-induction; define its coefficient and unit. 6. Explain motional e.m.f. and show ε = Bℓv. 7. What is a D.C. generator? Explain how an alternating e.m.f. is generated by a loop of wire rotating in a magnetic field. 8. What is an A.C. motor? Explain its construction and operation. 9. What is a transformer? Give its principle and mathematical relationship; explain why a laminated iron core is used instead of a solid core. 10. Explain back e.m.f. in an electric motor.\n\nNUMERICAL PROBLEMS — 1. A wheel has 12 metal spokes, each 0.6 m long, and rotates at 180 rpm in a plane normal to Earth’s magnetic field. If B = 0.6 G, find induced e.m.f. between axle and rim. Printed answer: 2.035 × 10⁻⁴ V. 2. A 1000-turn circuit encloses a magnetic circuit of cross-section 20 cm². At 4 A, B = 1 Wb m⁻²; at 9 A, B = 1.4 Wb m⁻². Find mean inductance between these current limits and e.m.f. if current falls from 9 A to 4 A in 0.05 s. Printed answers: 0.16 H, 16 V. 3. A 100 Ω coil with 100 turns is in a 1 mWb field; a 400 Ω galvanometer is connected in series. Find average e.m.f. and current if the coil moves in 0.1 s from the given field to 0.2 mWb. Printed answer: 1.6 mA. 4. A horizontal east–west wire 10 m long falls at 5.0 m s⁻¹ perpendicular to the horizontal component of Earth’s field, 0.30 × 10⁻⁴ Wb m⁻². Find (a) instantaneous e.m.f., (b) direction, and (c) which end has higher potential. Printed answer: 1.5 × 10⁻³ V.\n\n5. Current in a circuit falls from 5.0 A to 0 A in 0.1 s. If average induced e.m.f. is 200 V, estimate self-inductance. Printed answer: 4 H. 6. A long solenoid has 15 turns per cm. A small loop of area 2.0 cm² is inside, normal to its axis. Solenoid current changes steadily from 2.0 A to 4.0 A in 0.1 s. Find induced e.m.f. in the loop. Printed answer: 7.54 × 10⁻⁶ V. 7. A rectangular loop of sides 8 cm and 2 cm, with a small cut, moves out of a uniform 0.3 T magnetic field normal to the loop at 1 cm s⁻¹. Find e.m.f. and duration when velocity is normal to (a) the longer side, (b) the shorter side. Printed answers: 2.4 × 10⁻⁴ V, 2 s; 0.6 × 10⁻⁴ V, 8 s. 8. A 90 mm wire moves upward at 35 m s⁻¹ between magnet poles. B = 80 mT to the right and wire resistance is 5.00 mΩ. Find induced-current magnitude and direction. Printed answer: 50.4 A. 9. Adjacent coils have mutual inductance 1.5 H. Current in one changes from 0 to 20 A in 0.5 s. Find change of flux linkage with the other coil. Printed answer: 30 Wb.",
